# The unrowed guest-reachable exits: turn your own census into FILED ROWS, ranked, with the three best assessed

Nonce: CODEX_UNROWED_20260910.  Open with `VERDICT:` naming your top three ids in order, then echo the nonce.

Ordinary defect triage on an emulator. **Please do not simply ratify your own earlier census** -- it was a
by-product of a different question and nothing has re-checked it.  **The tree is
`C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\GXEMUL-SEC\`**; three unrelated 2021-vintage copies of this project sit
elsewhere under the project root, so line numbers off by hundreds mean you are in one of those.

## Where this comes from
Your queue triage (`regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_triage_20260910.txt`, committed) ended with a list
headed *"I found no specific open/held repair row for these remaining source-reachable paths"*, naming
roughly twenty files across `src/devices/` and `src/cpus/`.  **That list is the most valuable unworked
thing in this project right now and nobody has acted on it.**  This round turns it into filed rows.

## What "filed" means here, so the output is directly usable
This project's queue lives in two places that must agree: `tools/pipeline/ledger.json` (one row per id,
machine-read) and `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` (the human queue).  A filing needs: a short stable **id**
(lowercase, no spaces, in the style of `lcaisalane`, `sgirebufuninit`, `pcc2counter8`); a **severity**;
a one-sentence **title** naming the site and the consequence; and a body that states the defect, its
**reachability from a guest**, and the smallest honest **fix shape**.

## The standing severity rule, from the owner: "prioritise critical bugs and also easy resolvable ones"
So rank on TWO axes and keep them separate.  CRITICAL = a guest can end the emulator process, corrupt
guest memory or state, or be handed wrong data silently.  EASY = a one-to-five-line edit closes it and a
detector row can be measured against a mutant in one session, with the vehicle already existing.

## Questions
1. **RE-DERIVE THE CENSUS AT HEAD `4eac0b1` BEFORE USING IT.**  Your earlier figure was 370 literal
   `exit(` sites and 240 lexical calls after removing comments and strings.  Re-run it.  Then, and this is
   the part that matters, **separate three populations that the raw count conflates**: (a) sites reachable
   by an ordinary guest access or instruction; (b) sites reachable only at init time or from the debugger;
   (c) sites already covered by an OPEN or HELD ledger row.  Give the counts and the method, and say which
   files fall in (a) with no row.  A count nobody can re-derive is not a fact.
2. **RANK the (a)-with-no-row population**, critical list and easy list separately, with your reason per
   entry.  I expect the two lists to overlap and that is fine -- say where.
3. **FULLY ASSESS YOUR TOP THREE.**  For each: the exact site by grep anchor; what a guest must do to
   reach it, concretely, and whether any in-tree machine actually does; the option-set member you would
   choose from {delete, silent decline, decline + latched announce, accept, fault, backed accept,
   implement} AND whether that member is even AVAILABLE on that CPU (a fault needs the dispatcher to
   deliver one, which this tree does only for two architectures); the smallest fix; and whether a rung-3
   witness looks constructible with an existing probe as the vehicle, naming it.
4. **NAME WHAT YOU WOULD NOT FILE, and why.**  A row filed for a path no guest reaches is noise that
   outranks real work in a queue this size.  I would rather have eight real rows than twenty padded ones.
5. **THE TRAP I WANT YOU TO CHECK FOR EXPLICITLY**: a site that LOOKS guest-reachable but whose guarding
   condition cannot hold, or which a preceding check already excludes.  Your earlier answer caught several
   of these (a width tail with complementary preceding conditions, a command-count clear, an outer
   condition). Do that again and list the dead ones separately, because recording a path as dead WITH ITS
   WARRANT is a result, not an absence.
6. If you found something else while checking, say so.

Read-only: no builds, no probe runs, no edits, no patches.  Prose and tables only.
