   "hold": "R6 - its own round: the deepest data item",
   "witness": "detector:regress/diff_diskimage_io.c -- OWNER-ADOPTED TERM, 2026-08-27.  The fix shipped with a MEASURED detector and a kill table, and no guest-path witness.  `detector:` IS NOT A RUNG: it ranks below every access witness and does NOT discharge the rung-3 ATTEMPT the lifecycle ruling requires.  Before this term the row had to be written `none`, which asserts DOCUMENTED-ONLY and was false about a round that shipped a fix.  *** THIS FIELD CITED `regress/diff_fb_clear.c`, WHICH DOES NOT EXIST AND NEVER DID. Corrected 2026-08-27 by a sibling grep. ***  The real detector is `regress/diff_diskimage_io.c` (its section is `section_overlay_unaligned`, tagged b115 in the file).  Found while correcting `gdromexitsix`, whose `probe:` field cited two scratchpad scripts that are also gone -- the adjudicating seat pointed out that the grep-for-siblings rule had been skipped, and doing it turned up these two.  **The `detector:` term was owner-adopted on 2026-08-27, used on three rows that day, and TWO of the three cited a file that does not exist** -- a plausible-looking filename written from intent rather than from the tree.",
   "state_note": "*** TWO CORRECTIONS OF MINE, BOTH SERIOUS, FROM THE OPUS PASS-2 SEAT. ***\n\n*** 1. FLOOR CLAUSE (d) WAS BREACHED AND I HAD ARGUED THE WRONG CLAUSE. *** The fix INTRODUCES the predicate `len > remain`, and clause (d) says weaken it to the least-restrictive form that still READS like the same check. **Two such mutants passed all six rows:**\n```\n  len > OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE ? remain : len     bound relaxed\n  remain = inblock ? 1 : OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE   remainder faked\n```\nBoth survived because the section only produced **inblock in {0,511} and len > 512.**\n\n**AND THE UNCOVERED CALLER IS REAL, AND THIS ROUND'S OWN CENSUS HAD NAMED IT:** `bootblock_iso9660.c:281-282` reads **256 bytes** at a `dirofs` snapped only to 2048 (`:275-276`) -- arbitrary mod 512. *The census named the arm and the rows did not cover it.*\n\n**I had argued L1 (*these rows are not vacuous*) and declined a fixture change. That was the WRONG CLAUSE.** This is (d)/L2, which the carrier says to replace IN-ROUND however late it is found. Fixed in-round.\n\n**ONE ROW KILLS BOTH, and the kill is MEASURED, not reasoned:**\n```\n  arm        short-unaligned row (510+3, want 2)   total\n  fixed      ok                                    53 rows, 0 failures\n  pristine   FAIL                                  2 failures\n  relaxed    FAIL   <- was GREEN before this row   1 failure\n  faked      FAIL   <- was GREEN before this row   1 failure\n```\n\n*** 2. I MISREAD MY OWN PANEL AND REPORTED IT AS THREE RATIFICATIONS. *** I wrote *'three seats have already said GO/RATIFY/sound'* -- into the brief I then handed a later seat. **The panel was NOT unanimous:** codex **GO WITH CHANGES**; grok **'PRODUCT DIFF IS SOUND; DETECTOR IS NOT DONE'**; minimax **'NO RATIFICATION'**, stated twice; the kimi substitute challenged the severity framing. **Only agy and deepseek ratified.**\n\n> **This is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape with an extra step, and worse than the usual form: I did not merely fail to read three dissents -- I propagated the misreading into a brief, so a later seat was told the panel agreed.** The seats that said DETECTOR IS NOT DONE were RIGHT, and their finding is exactly the clause-(d) breach above.\n\n*The lesson is the carrier's own, which I quoted at other rows today and did not apply here: **read the BODY of every seat whose verdict merely agrees** -- and, it turns out, of every seat whose verdict I have not actually read at all.*\n\n*** THE L1 QUESTION ON THE GEOMETRY GAP, CONSIDERED AND ANSWERED -- recorded so the next round does not re-argue it. ***\n\n**The case FOR fixing it in-round (L1):** the detector is still UNCOMMITTED, and L1 licenses in-round repair of *the row is part of the deliverable still being built... a row measured vacuous before commit is unfinished, not a reopening*. The tree also carries a MEASURED precedent that single-overlay fixtures are inadequate -- `diff_diskimage_sync.c:570`: *\"A SECOND OVERLAY IS WHAT MAKES THE ROW WORK, and it is not decoration... MEASURED: a single-overlay version of this row caught the [nothing]\"*. And a second overlay would strengthen EVERY row rather than adding one.\n\n*** THE CASE AGAINST, WHICH WINS: L1 IS ABOUT VACUITY, AND THESE ROWS ARE NOT VACUOUS. *** They have a measured kill table -- they redden on the pre-fix build and on the escaping mutant, and go green on the fix. The vacuity floor asks whether a detector fails on mutants OF THE FIX. **A layer-selection bug is not a mutant of this fix; it is a DIFFERENT DEFECT** that this fixture happens to be unable to see. Calling that L1 would make L1 mean *any adjacent class a detector does not cover*, which is unbounded -- and unbounded in-round strengthening is precisely what `#392` was.\n\n> **DISPOSITION: FILED, with the precedent cited so whoever closes it knows the shape is already proven.** It ranks ABOVE the `lenread` escape among the debts, because a second overlay strengthens the whole section while a short-read row adds one path.\n\n*The distinction worth carrying: an ORACLE gap (the rows cannot see a state they do reach) is closer to vacuity than a FIXTURE gap (the rows never reach the state at all). The first is sometimes L1. The second is coverage, and coverage is filed.*\n\n*** PASS-2, THE KIMI SUBSTITUTE SEAT: no break found, and THREE findings the other seats missed. The ask-again has now paid on every pass. ***\n\n**1. THE SIGNEDNESS SAFETY IS CONDITIONAL, and the condition is the same range invariant that proves termination.** `len > remain ? remain : len` compares a `size_t` against a signed `off_t`, so `remain` converts to unsigned -- **safe ONLY because `remain` is in `[1, BLOCK]`.** So the range proof is not merely about the loop terminating; **it is load-bearing for the comparison itself.** Had `remain` ever been <= 0 the conversion would produce an enormous unsigned value and the clamp would silently stop clamping. *Two independent consequences of one invariant, and only one of them was noticed by the other seats.*\n\n**2. A PROVABLY-EQUIVALENT EDIT THAT IS NOT COVERAGE DEBT:** `buf += lentoread` -> `buf += remain`. On every non-final iteration `len > remain`, so the two are equal; on the final iteration the loop exits before `buf` is read again. **Undetectable AND harmless** -- correctly classified as an equivalent mutant rather than an escape, which is a distinction this project has had to make before (`#449`'s guard-only widening measured EQUIVALENT).\n\n*** 3. A THIRD ESCAPE CLASS, AND IT IS ABOUT MY DETECTOR'S GEOMETRY RATHER THAN ITS ORACLE: *** a plausible 'optimization' that starts iteration k's overlay scan from iteration k-1's `overlay_nr` **passes every row I wrote** -- because my fixture pins exactly ONE geometry: a single overlay with only block 0 marked. With one overlay there is no layer ORDER to get wrong.\n\n> **That is a different kind of debt from the `lenread` escape.** That one is an oracle gap on an unexercised PATH (short reads). This is a FIXTURE gap -- the rows cannot distinguish layer-selection bugs because the fixture has only one layer to select. **A second overlay, with a different block marked, closes it** -- and the tree already knows this shape: `diff_diskimage_sync.c:570` says *'A SECOND OVERLAY IS WHAT MAKES THE ROW WORK, and it is not decoration.'*\n\n**FILED, not fixed** -- same reasoning as the `lenread` escape: not a floor breach, not a measured false pass against a shipped claim. **But it is the more valuable of the two to close**, because a second overlay would strengthen every row rather than adding one.\n\n*Fair criticism of my brief that I accept: the seat noted rows 3-5 were not inlined, so its answer to 'smallest wrong edit' had to reason from two rows rather than five. **A pass-2 brief should inline the DETECTOR, not just the product diff** -- the same inline-every-fact rule the carrier already states for packet-fed seats, applied to the artifact under review.*\n\n*** PASS-2 COVERAGE DEBT, FILED NOT FIXED (the stopping rule): a ONE-IDENTIFIER mutant escapes all five rows. ***\n\n**The mutant (deepseek, pass 2): `buf += lentoread;` -> `buf += lenread;`.**\n\n`lenread` is what `fread` actually RETURNED; `lentoread` is what was REQUESTED. **In every path the five rows exercise they are identical**, so the whole detector stays green. They diverge only on a SHORT READ, where `buf` would advance by less than `len` was decremented -- desynchronising the buffer from the offset for every later iteration.\n\n**WHY THIS IS FILED AND NOT FIXED IN-ROUND, stated so nobody re-argues it:**\n  * it is **not a floor breach** -- but the ground I first gave was CIRCULAR and is corrected here. I wrote *'it nulls nothing in any path the rows reach'*, which **every escape satisfies by definition**, including `#462`'s M11 that was ruled a floor breach. *A wrong reason gets reused.* **The correct, checkable ground:** outside a short read `lenread == lentoread` is an IDENTITY, so the swap preserves the repair across the entire defect class this round closes; it diverges only in the short-read state, which the contract excludes -- and which is **not silent**, because the pre-existing `INCOMPLETE READ` `fatal()` announces it.\n  * it is **not a measured false pass** against a shipped claim, and not a wrong record. The stopping rule files everything else, and `#392` is what happens when it does not.\n  * it lands squarely on the **short-read path this round already declared out of scope and pre-existing** -- so it is the same debt seen from the mutant side rather than a new one.\n\n**What would close it:** a row that forces a short read in a NON-FINAL iteration -- an overlay data file truncated mid-range -- and asserts the bytes land at the right offsets afterwards. That row would also cover the pre-existing zero-fill concern, so **one row discharges both**.\n\n*Two seats, two different answers to the same question, and the sharper one won: another seat answered that the smallest passing edit is an INLINED equivalent of the same arithmetic -- true, but behaviourally identical, so it is readability debt rather than coverage debt. **The ask-again is worth the invocation precisely because seats differ on what counts as an escape.***\n\n*** #472 IN FLIGHT 2026-08-27: REPRODUCED, FIXED, AND MEASURED -- pass 2 running, nothing committed yet. ***\n\n**TEST-FIRST, on the committed build, before any edit:**\n```\n  ok    [LIVE] aligned read of block 0 is all overlay        = 512\n  ok    [LIVE] aligned read of block 1 is all base           = 512\n  FAIL  overlay unaligned: wrong-layer bytes in 1024 @ 511   = 512, want 1\n  ok    overlay unaligned: reports SUCCESS despite corruption = 1\n```\nExactly ONE real failure, both liveness controls green, on the PREDICTED numbers. The last row is the silent-corruption evidence: it returns success while handing back wrong bytes.\n\n*** THE KILL TABLE, and pass 1 is why the second row exists: ***\n```\n  arm                        wrong-layer row      never-wrote row\n  PRISTINE (committed)       FAIL 512, want 1     ok 0\n  MUTANT   (clamp only)      ok 1  <- ESCAPES     FAIL 511, want 0\n  FIXED    (clamp + stride)  ok 1                 ok 0\n```\n**Two seats independently named that mutant** -- clamp the length, keep the fixed stride. Under it the wrong-layer row reads 1, **which is the CORRECT answer**, because the buffer receives HOLES rather than wrong bytes. The rows are measurably non-redundant.\n\n*Measured in a COPIED tree, so no repo file was ever mutated and no sentinel was needed.*\n\n**THE FIX:** clamp `lentoread` to the current block's remainder; advance `buf` by what was consumed. Same shape as `diskimage_access__cue()` in the same file.\n\n**A seat supplied the algebra I had not derived:** `curofs + remain` **is** `(curofs | (OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE-1)) + 1`, identically -- so the clamp is *provably consistent* with the loop's existing advance rather than merely compatible with it.\n\n*** THREE THINGS CAUGHT ME, ALL BY GUARDS: ***\n  1. **`buf += OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE` appears TWICE.** The other is in `fwrite_helper`, where the fixed stride is CORRECT because that path refuses unaligned access. **Patching the first occurrence would have broken the write path**; the anchor-count guard stopped it.\n  2. **I reformatted 6 lines I never touched** -- a whitespace pass removed 7 trailing-tab lines when I had introduced 1. Reverted and re-applied; the diff is now 17+/3- with `-w` giving an identical stat.\n  3. **The mutant script's anchor went to 0 after the fix landed** and said so, rather than silently patching nothing.\n\n**Post-fix:** `52 rows, 0 failures, DISKIMAGE_IO_PASS`. All 11 offline differentials green. Twin trees and ALL THREE build trees byte-identical (an earlier draft said 'both' and was MEASURED FALSE -- it forgot the WSL arc tree, which was stale).\n\n**FILED, NOT FIXED:** the short-read path is unchanged -- on a short read in a non-final iteration `len` decrements by the requested amount while `totallenread` grows by the actual, so the caller's zero-fill can cover the wrong region. Pre-existing; the patch neither helps nor worsens it.\n\n*** CONFIRMED 2026-08-27 by an adversarial opus pass that tried to break all four steps and could not. Three refinements, and the first is a REAL NARROWING I had missed. ***\n\n**PRECONDITION: the defect requires `d->nr_of_overlays > 0`.** With no overlay the function takes a fast path (`:1347`) that is a single `fseek`+`fread` and is correct. My record did not state this. **It is not a get-out:** overlays come from the `V` prefix (`:1810`) and from `R:` (`:2095`), and `R:` is routine in this project's own rigs (`gate_ab.sh:82` uses `LUNA_IMG=\"R:...\"`, and `drive_guest.py` and several probes do the same).\n\n**UNALIGNED OFFSETS ARE GUEST-REACHABLE, with a census rather than an example:**\n  * `promemul/dec_prom.c:167-170` -> `:148` -- `lseek` stores an arbitrary 64-bit guest value as `current_file_offset`; `read()` passes it straight through with a guest length up to 64 MB. **Gives both faults.**\n  * `promemul/arcbios.c:1923` -> `:1761` -- ARC `Seek` stores an arbitrary 64-bit offset from guest memory; `Read` uses it with a length up to 64 MB. **Gives both faults.**\n  * `diskimage_scsicmd.c:725`/`:830` -- variable-length tape READ via `tape_offset`.\n  * `bootblock_iso9660.c:271-282` -- unaligned, but a fixed 256-byte length means one iteration, so **wrong-layer only**, no misalignment.\n\n*** THE STRONGEST EVIDENCE IS AN ASYMMETRY I HAD NOT LOOKED FOR: THE WRITE SIDE HAS THE GUARD THE READ SIDE LACKS. *** `fwrite_helper` explicitly refuses a non-multiple length (`:1273-1280`) and an unaligned offset (`:1281-1286`), both returning 0, both under `#164`. `fread_helper` has **no such guard anywhere**. The read path was simply never given the check the write path was.\n\n**THE MECHANISM IS SHARPER THAN I STATED, in a way that matters for the detector:** iteration 1 is **positionally CORRECT** (`buf[k] = file[offset+k]`); its only fault is the LAYER. The positional corruption begins at iteration 2 and is caused by the fixed `buf += OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE` stride paired with a variable-start read. **So the two halves have different trigger conditions:** wrong-layer needs only an unaligned offset; duplication additionally needs `len > 512 - (offset & 511)`. A detector must cover both.\n\n**AND IT IS NOT A MEMORY-SAFETY ISSUE, which was checked explicitly:** `buf` advances 512 only when `lentoread == 512`, and any short `lentoread` zeroes `len` and ends the loop; `len` cannot underflow. **Silent wrong data, no overrun.**\n\n**The cue idiom is confirmed as the fix template** (`:1091-1144`): `chunk = CDROM_SECTOR_SIZE - intra; if (chunk > len) chunk = len;` with `buf + total_copied`. It clamps to bytes-remaining-in-sector AND indexes the buffer by bytes actually delivered -- **both halves of what the overlay loop lacks.**\n\n**ADJACENT, not claimed by this row:** on a short read the loop does `len -= lentoread` while `totallenread += lenread`, so a short read in a NON-FINAL iteration makes the caller's `memset(buf + lendone, ...)` zero the wrong region. Worth a row of its own if this is worked.\n\n*Honest limit the seat stated and I keep: no rig was measured actually issuing an unaligned >512-byte read against an overlaid disk. Reachability is READ, not MEASURED.*\n\n*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE, GUEST-REACHABLE, AND IT CORRUPTS READ DATA. The most severe bare-title row worked so far. Verified by reading the loop; the mechanism is worse than the title says. ***\n\n`src/disk/diskimage.c:1358-1402`, the overlay read loop:\n\n```c\nfor (curofs=offset; len != 0;\n    curofs = (curofs | (OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE-1)) + 1) {\n        for (overlay_nr = d->nr_of_overlays-1; overlay_nr >= 0; overlay_nr --)\n                if (overlay_has_block(d, overlay_nr, curofs))   /* layer chosen for THIS block */\n                        break;\n\n        lentoread = len > OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE? OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE : len;   /* NOT clamped to the\n                                                       block's REMAINDER */\n        ... fread(buf, 1, lentoread, <chosen layer>) ...\n\n        len -= lentoread;\n        buf += OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE;          /* fixed stride */\n}\n```\n\n**`lentoread` is clamped to the BLOCK SIZE, never to the bytes left in the CURRENT block.** So when `offset` is not block-aligned, the loop picks a layer for the block containing `curofs` and then reads up to a full block from it -- **running past that block's end into the next one or two blocks, which may live in a different layer.**\n\n**Worked example, offset 511, len 1024** (`OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE` = 512):\n  * iteration 1: `curofs = 511` -> layer chosen for **block 0**; `lentoread = 512`; reads file offsets **511-1022** into `buf[0..511]`.\n  * `curofs = (511 | 511) + 1 = 512`; `buf += 512`.\n  * iteration 2: layer chosen for **block 1**; reads file offsets **512-1023** into `buf[512..1023]`.\n\n**The guest asked for offsets 511-1534 and receives: wrong-layer bytes in the first half, and file offsets 512-1022 DUPLICATED at two different buffer positions.** The tail it asked for (1023-1534) is never read at all. This is silent read corruption, not a refusal.\n\n*** THE LOOP'S OWN ADVANCE EXPRESSION PROVES UNALIGNED OFFSETS WERE ANTICIPATED, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS AN OVERSIGHT AND NOT AN UNDOCUMENTED PRECONDITION. *** `curofs = (curofs | (OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE-1)) + 1` rounds UP to the next block boundary. If `offset` were always block-aligned that expression would simply be `curofs + OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE`. **The author wrote alignment-handling into the advance and omitted it from the length.**\n\n**GUEST-REACHABLE** (seat's sequence, structurally consistent with the code, which carries no alignment guard anywhere): an overlaid ARC disk, guest writes blocks 0 and 2 so they land in different layers, then a byte-granular `Seek(511)` + `Read(1024)`. ARC exposes byte-granular seek/read, so the unaligned offset reaches this loop. **Not verified by execution -- needs a build.**\n\n**Observable for a detector:** the guest-visible read payload. Write distinct sentinel bytes into blocks that land in different layers, then read across the boundary from an unaligned offset; broken returns the wrong layer's sentinel and a duplicated range, fixed returns the requested bytes in order.\n\n*** THE FIX IDIOM IS ALREADY IN THIS FILE. *** `diskimage_access__cue()` (`:1083`) does exactly the right thing for the same shape of problem -- it clamps each step to the remainder of the current sector and writes at `buf + total_copied` rather than on a fixed stride (see its `memset(buf + total_copied, 0, chunk)` at the same offset discipline). **Transferred warrant: clamp `lentoread` to the current block's remainder, and advance `buf` by the amount actually consumed rather than by a constant.**\n\n**A SECOND, SEPARABLE HALF reported by the seat and NOT verified here:** a short `fread` on the flat (non-overlay) path is said to return zero even after reading bytes, causing the caller to zero the whole requested buffer. Recorded as the seat's claim; check it before it rides along with the fix, because a row covering two defects buries one when the other closes.\n\n\n\n*** THE SENTENCE ABOVE PREDICTED THAT ONE ROT-PACKET RUN 'reds `stale quiet` and discharges\nBOTH'.  THE RUN HAPPENED AND REFUTED THE SECOND HALF.  Corrected 2026-08-29. ***\n\n`regress/artifacts/gdromexitsix/rot_20260829/` -- post-fix binary, one-line frozen-probe edit\n(packed format byte 0x20 -> 0x21):\n\n    base.log   GDROM_DEF_RESULT=60/60    stale quiet  0 vs 0      ok\n    rot.log    GDROM_DEF_RESULT=59/60    stale quiet  1 vs 0    **FAIL**\n\nThe two `GDROM_DEF_VECTOR` lines differ in EXACTLY ONE POSITION -- discriminating, not merely\ndisruptive, and proven by the probe's own vector rather than asserted.\n\n**`stale quiet` is DISCHARGED.  `stale how` is NOT**, and structurally cannot be by a rotted\npacket: the fix DECLINES rather than dying, so the session still exits cleanly.  Worse, it is\nthe WANTED-EQUALS-FAILURE shape -- the row wants `exit0` and a BROKEN `how` capture also\nyields `exit0` -- so only a measured red separates a working oracle from an inert one.  It\nneeds a mutant that exits AFTER the last budget check.  **Treat it as UNPROVEN until then.**\n\n*Found by a supplementary pass-2 seat applying the grep-for-siblings rule: the same predicted\nsentence sat in the probe comment AND here, and the measurement refuted both at once.*"
  },
