     "note": "*** VERDICT: NO SEPARATE ROUND, BUT `b120u` DOES NOT COVER IT -- KEEP AS A RIDER REQUIRING ITS OWN HUNK AND ITS OWN TEST. DO NOT CLOSE DOCUMENTED-ONLY. *** MODE SENSE is a KNOWN-command arm, not the unknown-opcode default: it inflates `retlen` by 100, restores only `data_in_len`, then writes the still-inflated value into the payload's one-byte Mode Data Length field, and it already installs GOOD status. So a `b120u` default-arm repair, or a generic missing-status -> CHECK CONDITION repair, leaves this untouched. Replaying the arithmetic: allocation 156 -> internal retlen 256 -> reported byte **0**, while `data_in_len` is 156 -- a guest asking for 156 bytes gets a non-empty response whose header says the following length is zero. *** AND IT REFUTED A CLAIM IN THE CARRIER, WITH THE EVIDENCE SITTING IN THIS ROW'S OWN LEDGER ENTRY. *** CLAUDE.md ranked this row using 'a MEASURED guest-visible symptom of NIL (two transcripts byte-identical)'. The row says NIL for `told-98` vs `told-35/11` -- and that **`told-0` FLIPS CONTROL FLOW: 32 MODE SENSE commands instead of 11, including MODE SENSE(10) fallbacks and page 5, because OpenBSD 7.7 branches on that byte.** NIL was measured for a PAIR and restated as a property of the PARAMETER. Corrected in both carrier copies; the RULE (rung measures reachability-proof strength, not harm) is unchanged and the EXEMPLAR is weaker. It kept the priority honest in the other direction too: no recorded rig naturally requests allocation length 156, and the full chain was not measured end to end, so this stays LOW priority -- 'that keeps priority low; it does not make the defect equivalent or covered.'"
