     "note": "PASS 2 ON THE DIFF, AND IT KILLED MY DETECTOR. Verdict SHIP-WITH-CHANGES.\n* *** THE DETECTOR WAS STILL VACUOUS: --wrap counted HOW MANY calls and never WHICH FILE, so FOUR mutants that restore real data loss all passed green *** -- v1 rename f_bitmap to f_data (bitmap NEVER synced), v2 invert the data/bitmap order (the rule the code itself calls load-bearing), v3 delete the ordering continue, v4 sync d->f instead of the bitmap. Every one keeps the totals at 2 and 2. Smallest defeating edit: v1, ONE identifier. And the row NAMES overclaimed -- 'fflushes data and bitmap' passed with the bitmap untouched, and the gate grepped those exact names as its evidence.\n* *** AND THE FIXTURE HAD NEVER WRITTEN A BYTE. *** SCSICMD_WRITE is 0x0a = WRITE(6) driven with a 10-byte CDB, so the length parsed as 256 blocks; and the handler requires data_out_offset == size, which the fixture never set. Both overlay files were 0 bytes after every run -- corroborated by identical 0-byte leftovers already in the repo from an earlier run. THAT is why the file-blind mutants lived: with nothing written there is no durability property to check, only call counts.\n* Q1 THE `continue` IS RIGHT -- it tried and could not build a state where skipping is worse; a stale bitmap makes a read fall through to the base (stale but real) rather than to the overlay's hole (zeros). TWO CORRECTIONS AROUND IT: the comment overclaims, because after 4 writes with NO sync an independent reader already sees bitmap[0]=0x07 -- the per-block fseeks already pushed both to the page cache, so the continue declines to FORCE publication rather than preventing it; and a multi-overlay disk reports CHECK CONDITION when a non-last overlay's fsync fails even though every guest byte is durable.\n* ADVERSARIAL: THE DURABILITY CLAIM IS FALSE UNDER R:. diskimage_add_overlay(d, tmpname, true) unlinks BOTH overlay files right after opening (:177-184; measured fstat().st_nlink == 0), so they are anonymous temporaries no post-crash reader can open. Not introduced by the diff -- R: is meant to be ephemeral -- but the new comment presents R: as the motivating case, which it should not.\n* Q2 safe for luna88k (d == NULL returns before the switch, so CHECK CONDITION needs #134's NULL d->f or a real error). But it MEASURED an exposure the diff introduced: for r:/CD-ROM the new FLAT branch fflushes and fsyncs a handle opened \"r\" -- precisely what the function's own comment says it avoids. The comment reasons its way to the right rule for the overlay branch and then does not apply it to the branch it wrote. FIXED in this round.\n* Q5 FOUR COMMENTS MEASURED FALSE, two of them mine from pass 1: the gate's 'drop the --wrap flags and the rows still pass against nothing' (they FAIL CLOSED -- 7 rows/4 failed without both, 2 failed without --wrap=fflush); the selfmutant comment's -fno-optimize-sibling-calls analogy (omitting the flags yields SELFMUTANT_SETUP, which selfmutant.py's own header says is NEVER scored as a detection, so it taught the wrong hazard class); the diskimage.c comment attributing the fflush-first note to fwrite_helper when it lives in overlay_set_block_in_use at :1207-1211; and the fixture's 'Issue a WRITE(10)'.\n* Residual filed, not fixed: IDE FLUSH CACHE has no durability path at all (grep -i flush dev_wdc.c is empty).  [panel_20260818_095322]"
