   "hold_question": "SYNCHRONIZE CACHE does not flush overlay and bitmap files. Work SHIPPED as 4d0981d. QUESTION: is the residual real, and may the row close? The hold calls it 'the honest residual of b120's stale third'. Two things to settle: (a) does the shipped fix leave any path where a guest-issued SYNCHRONIZE CACHE returns success while overlay or bitmap data is still unflushed; (b) this row's assess, research AND review stages are ALL held for you -- so answer whether the shipped work is complete, not merely whether the code is correct.",
   "state_note": "*** ADJUDICATED 2026-08-27 (fable): the collapse onto [[b120]] is CONFIRMED **MEASURED**, but it is a collapse of the SAFETY HALF ONLY -- do NOT read it as 'drop ovsync'. ***\n\nb120's fix makes opcode 0x91 an honest CHECK CONDITION. **It does not implement SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(16), so it does not make the flush happen.** That residual -- implement 0x91, or formally decline it -- is NOT covered by b120 and is what this row should carry from now on.\n\nSo: keep this row OPEN and NARROWED to the implement-or-decline question. The false-success danger is b120's; the missing flush is this row's.\n\n*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE, BUT NARROWER THAN THE TITLE -- and the live half turns out to be AN INSTANCE OF [[b120]], which neither row could show on its own. ***\n\n**HALF THAT IS ALREADY FIXED:** SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(10) is `SCSICMD_SYNCHRONIZE_CACHE = 0x35` (`diskimage.h:254`), handled at `diskimage_scsicmd.c:915`, and it **does** flush -- `:932` calls `diskimage_sync(d)`, with `:925` recording that this was the TODO carried out. *So the title, read as covering all synchronize-cache commands, is FALSE for the one the tree implements.*\n\n*** THE LIVE HALF, AND ITS DIAGNOSIS: SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(16) IS OPCODE `0x91`, AND `0x91` APPEARS **ZERO** TIMES IN THE DISPATCHER. *** It therefore falls into the `default:` arm at `:1380` -- which logs through a verbosity-gated call, never sets a status, and returns the same value a successful command returns. **The guest asks for a cache flush, is told GOOD, and nothing is flushed.**\n\n> **SO THIS IS NOT AN INDEPENDENT DEFECT: IT IS [[b120]] WEARING A SPECIFIC OPCODE.** `b120`'s fix -- refuse an unknown opcode with CHECK CONDITION / ILLEGAL REQUEST via the existing `diskimage__return_check_condition()` helper -- would convert this from a SILENT FALSE SUCCESS into an honest refusal, letting a guest fall back to CACHE(10). **A full `ovsync` fix additionally implements `0x91`**; the two are separable and should be ranked separately.\n\n**Rank `b120` first.** It is the general case, it is cheaper, and it makes this row's damage visible instead of silent. Implementing `0x91` without fixing `b120` would close one opcode and leave every other unimplemented one lying about success.\n\n*Method note: the seat reported the CACHE(10)/CACHE(16) split and the existing `diskimage_sync()` idiom; the `0x91`-falls-to-default connection was found here by grepping the dispatcher for the opcode and getting zero hits. **A count of zero was the whole finding** -- and it is only evidence because the denominator was checked: the file really does dispatch by opcode, and `0x35` is present in the same census.*\n\n\n\n**RESIDUAL-REMAINS.**  `4d0981d` fixed CACHE(10) -- data and bitmap now get ordered flush/sync with\nfailure reporting.  **CACHE(16) is still unimplemented** and falls through the generic default,\nwhich neither flushes nor explicitly refuses it.\n\n*And a founding overstatement corrected: the row asserted the base was ALREADY flushed pre-fix.  It\nused `fsync()` without the stdio `fflush()`, which is not the same claim.*"
