     "note": "MEMORY / DEVICE-ACCESS CLUSTER TRIAGE: six rows, each filed by ONE seat and never reviewed. Brief generated mechanically from the ledger.\n\n*** THE CLEAREST DROP OF THE CAMPAIGN: `mrwgrind`, FOUR SEATS (agy, glm, deepseek, minimax). ***  minimax's reason is the one that decides it -- 'unreachable' -- and unlike the constblind drop this one does NOT rest on a claim inside the row that everyone read back to me. It rests on the shape of the code path.\n\nWHAT TO DO FIRST -- three-way split:\n  mrwstore2  glm and minimax, and their reason is the strongest severity claim in the packet: 'real guest-reachable DATA CORRUPTION via double device write' on a straddling store whose tail faults. Under this project's ordering rule -- severity and reachability, never rung -- that outranks the others on its face.\n  ns16550    agy, for IMMEDIATE fix. A guest-writable divisor dividing by zero is UB, though the row itself concedes it is 'not in the shipped path'.\n  pcchalt    deepseek, and agy would schedule it as a DETECTOR round rather than a fix -- which is the right category: #431's deleted PPC page-crossing halt has no detector, so the question is whether its removal can be noticed, not whether to remove it again.\n\nALSO DROPPED: `devexit`, two seats (glm, deepseek). Note the tension worth recording -- devexit is the GENERAL question behind correction #438, which fixed one instance (a guest store to luna88k's INT_ST_MASK ended the emulator process), and this project already shipped census_exits.py counting 398 exit sites with 120 inside device handlers. A drop here means 'the general row is not the unit of work', NOT 'the exits are fine'.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable. Codex RATE-LIMITED, eighth panel today.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: KEPT. No seat prioritised it and none argued to drop it. It is an AUDIT row -- device handlers that ignore the access length -- and audits are the shape most likely to go stale, as constblind demonstrated tonight when a seven-differential audi