     "note": "*** MERGE WITH devexit: THEY INTERSECT AT 23 LINES AND NEITHER FILING SEAT SAW THE OTHER'S ROW. ***  Census of DEVICE_ACCESS handlers that test access LENGTH and then exit(1):\n    dev_pcc2.c 11 | dev_sgi_re.c 6 | dev_sh4.c 2 | gdrom/osiop/pvr 1 each\nA handler that cannot service a width (hlen) whose failure mode is process termination (devexit) is ONE DEFECT ON ONE LINE. Genuine merge: one fix design (the #218-#223/#438 shape), one detector shape. What survives separately is only a coverage ledger, not a round.\n\nRANKED FIRST OF THE CLUSTER on severity, not ease: exit(1) in a handler is the only outcome in the packet that destroys ALL guest state -- every process, plus whatever the disk overlay had not flushed. First round is [[sh4bcr]], witnessed at rung 3 today.\n\nThe filed witness is real and independently confirmed: ARM does emit 8-byte accesses (cpu_arm_instr_loadstore.c:101 `const int datalen = 8;` for LDRD/STRD, force-aligned at :229, handed to memory_rw at :233/:367), and DEV_IRQC_IRQ=0x0 / MASK=0x4 / UNMASK=0x8 means an 8-byte access at offset 0 spans two registers with OPPOSITE legal directions.\n\nA NUMBER TO CITE FROM THE INSTRUMENT, NOT THE PROSE: census_exits.py on HEAD gives 397 sites (214 direct + 183 macro), 119 ACCESS, 30 device files -- not the 398/120 in circulation. The delta is #438 (cf4b083). The instrument is current by construction; the prose is not.",
