     "ev": "stage coverage and verdict re-derived from the ledger by the main loop",
     "outcome": "CLOSURE-READY-NOT-CLOSED",
     "note": "Stage coverage checked: **assess 9/9, full roster** (agy, codex, deepseek, fable5, glm, grok,\nkimi, minimax, opus5), and an explicit CLOSE verdict is on the row.\n\n**The row has been sitting in `held` with an answered question.**  That is the\nstate-vs-substance mismatch this ledger's own `state_semantics` block calls the es438 class:\na reader who trusts states reads `held` as 'waiting on someone', which is false here.\n\n**DELIBERATELY NOT CLOSED BY ME.**  Closure is a judgement, and earlier today I closed a row\non thin coverage and the gate refused it four times, correctly.  What is recorded here is the\nMECHANICAL part -- coverage complete, verdict present -- so the decision can be made from\nfacts rather than re-derived."
    },
    {
     "seat": "fable5",
     "phase": "review",
     "d": "OK",
     "date": "2026-08-29",
     "grade": "CONFIRMED",
     "ev": "ledger state flip only; no new evidence",
     "outcome": "CLOSED-ON-EXISTING-ADJUDICATION",
     "note": "CLOSED 2026-08-29.  **No new decision was taken here.**  The row already carried assess 9/9 with the full roster NAMED (agy, codex, deepseek, fable5, glm, grok, kimi, minimax, opus5), an explicit CLOSE verdict, and a fable5 adjudication of the same date recording it as CLOSURE-READY and NOT CLOSED.  Flipping the state is the only thing that was owed.  *** IT SAT IN `held` WITH AN ANSWERED QUESTION, which is the state-vs-substance mismatch this project has recorded before: a future reader who trusts states reads `held` as 'waiting', and that is false. ***  It is the unread-review shape one level up -- the seat work was done, delivered and never banked.  Found while triaging the 75 held rows for the owner: only THREE of them were closure-ready, which itself corrected a claim I had just made that 43% of the backlog was seat-blocked (only 2 of 75 carry a seat marker)."
    }
   ],
   "hold_question": "CLOSURE-READY, recorded 2026-08-29.  Assess is 9/9 with the full roster.  The verdict is **THE FILE IS THE WRONG UNIT -- MEASURED, NOT ARGUED: close as documented-only with the census as the standing answer**, and the killer number is that 107 of 118 `.c` files in src/devices/ carry at least one terminating statement, so a per-FILE tally would report 91% and mean nothing.  That ANSWERS the hold question verbatim, which asked whether the useful unit is the FILE, the guest-reachable ACCESS site, or the de-fatalised PREDICATE.  The witness field already carries the census as a `probe:` artefact.  NOT CLOSED HERE.  (Prior question, provenance: POLICY row: device files that stop the host on a guest value have no per-file coverage record. QUESTION: is a per-FILE record the right unit? `exitsweep`'s census was just re-measured at 360 SITES on )",
   "witness": "probe:the committed census (`--by-file`) is a MEASURED artefact for the COUNT; the guest-reachable instance named below is rung-3 shaped (unmodified `-E cats`, a real guest `ldr` through real decode) but was NOT run, so no witness is claimed for it.",
   "state_note": "*** L4 CORRECTION 2026-08-27: I RECORDED FOOTBRIDGE EVIDENCE ON THIS ROW AND IT DOES NOT BELONG HERE. ***\n\nThis is a held **POLICY** row -- *'Device files that stop the host on a guest value: no per-file coverage record'* -- and **two seats (agy, grok) already recorded DROP RECOMMENDED** on the reason that *the general row is not the unit of work*. The footbridge-specific row is **[[n6]]** (*'The record says the footbridge host-halts were made graceful; nine of them are not'*).\n\nSo the one-instruction-kill evidence I wrote here has been moved to [[n6]], where it is the unit of work. **What legitimately belongs on THIS row is the census and nothing else.**\n\n*** THE CENSUS IS CONFIRMED MEASURED, re-run read-only at HEAD `9c59973` by a second seat: ***\n\n```\n  kind        ACCESS  HELPER    INIT   UNKNOWN   total\n  direct          85      67      25         0     177\n  macro            5      34     144         0     183\n  TOTAL           90     101     169         0     360\n```\n\n**Deriver: `python GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/census_exits.py`, DEFAULT invocation.** One precision correction to how I wrote it: `--by-file` prints a per-file table with **no totals row**, so citing `--by-file` for 360/90 names an invocation that does not print them. Sum the columns or use the default.\n\n**What it counts, so the number is not over-read:** `exit|abort(` plus `CHECK_ALLOCATION|FAILURE(` over `src/devices/*.c`, with comments and string literals blanked first, bucketed by walking back to the enclosing function. **It counts SITES, not defects, and it does NOT resolve HELPER reachability.**\n\n*** AND THIS ROW CARRIES TWO SUPERSEDED CENSUSES -- the frozen-count class, in the row whose whole subject is counting. *** Its closure note says *'271 exit(1) calls in 50 of the 118 files'* and its 2026-08-20 seat entries say *'398 exit sites with 120 inside device handlers'*. **Neither matches today's deriver-backed 360/90.** Both are left in place, superseded rather than deleted, so the drift is visible.\n\n*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: the CENSUS is MEASURED, and the row has a concrete guest-reachable instance. ***\n\n**MEASURED:** the committed census implements `--by-file`; run read-only against this tree it found **360 terminating sites, 90 of them inside `DEVICE_ACCESS`**. That is a re-derivation on today's HEAD, not a transcription of an older number.\n\n**Guest-reachable instance, named concretely:** on CATS, disable the guest MMU, then a guest `ldr` from footbridge base `0x42000000 + 0x188` reaches the `IRQ_ENABLE_SET` **read** arm and `exit(1)`. **One instruction ends the host process.** The existing detector records that exact one-instruction kill.\n\n**Observable:** post-access guest progress -- a serial marker emitted after the triggering load. Broken never reaches it.\n\n*** FIX IDIOM EXISTS AND IS NAMED: `m8820x_unimplemented()` is explicitly the same-class complain-once-and-continue helper that replaced former `exit(1)` sites. *** That is a transferred warrant AND it matches the house latch idiom (N=1 per instance) the carrier records, so a de-fatalisation here has a template rather than a design question.\n\n*Note the seat answered fully but emitted no VERDICT line -- a FORMAT miss, not a seat failure. All four questions were answered and it closed cleanly with 'No build or writes performed.'*\n\n"
  },
