   "id": "fbdiv",
   "title": "fbdiv  footbridge: a guest-written zero timer load divides by zero (SIGFPE, host core dump)",
   "state": "closed",
   "commits": "213b56d 35302f2 84f442d",
   "hold": "",
   "closure": {
    "by": "fable5",
    "badge": "A",
    "note": "SHIPPED 35302f2. Nine seats on the design -- the first full roster since #420. The crash is PRE-EXISTING (measured on 863d238^); this project's own hardening added a second, faster route, and the round corrected its own filing (213b56d) before fixing the code. The 21285 DATASHEET WAS OBTAINED MID-ROUND and withdrew one of the round's own arguments -- the 'devinit resets to TIMER_MAX_VAL' support was citing a model bug, since hardware resets both LOAD and CONTROL to 0. The zero-load mapping stays UNKNOWN, now MEASURED-absent rather than assumed.  [A: the diff was mine.]"
   },
   "entries": [
    {
     "seat": "fable5",
     "phase": "assess",
     "d": "✓",
     "date": "2026-08-16",
     "note": "OPENED BY THE R4 REVIEW, and it is the highest-priority thing the panel produced. dev_footbridge.c:141 does `random() %% d->timer_load[i]`, and TIMER_1_LOAD at :417 accepts a guest-written 0 (`idata & TIMER_MAX_VAL`). The measuring seat REPRODUCED the SIGFPE and core dump directly. *** R4'S OWN CLAMP MADE IT REACHABLE SOONER: *** before #427/#428 a zero load gave freq=+INF, interval=0.0 and a catch-up loop that never advanced -- a guest-reachable HANG inside SIGALRM, so the machine froze before the division could run. The clamp bounds the loop, the callback bumps pending 2^20 times in the first signal, and the guard opens. Hang -> crash. Reachability is MANUAL (a booted netwinder would settle it), but the division itself is the subject and an offline driver in diff_timer.c's shape reaches it, so this is test-first-able TODAY without a rig.",
     "grade": "CONFIRMED",
     "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260815_202720/"
    },
    {
     "seat": "opus5",
     "phase": "research",
     "d": "✓",
     "date": "2026-08-15",
     "grade": "CONFIRMED",
     "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260815_221232/",
     "note": "CORRECTS THIS ROW'S OWN FILING. The row said '#427/#428 convert a hang into a SIGFPE'. Half wrong. TIMER_1_LOAD does not clear pending_timer_interrupts (CONTROL at :447 does), so a LEGAL timer accrues a backlog and a later write of load=0 divides -- no +INF, no clamp. MEASURED against 863d238^, the pre-#427/#428 core: it crashes there too, exit 136. DEVICE_TICK is dispatched from machine_run(), the MAIN LOOP, not the SIGALRM handler. The crash is PRE-EXISTING; our hardening added a second, faster route."
    },
    {
     "seat": "kimi",
     "phase": "research",
     "d": "✓",
     "date": "2026-08-15",
     "note": "REVIVED MID-PANEL with no calendar signal -- its second unannounced return -- and it did not merely answer, it REPRODUCED the SIGFPE through shipped code (rc=136). Independently reached the same Q5 conclusion (instance, not family) by the same two measurements, and independently found the `int cycles` / `cycles <<= 8` UB, making THREE seats on that defect arrived at separately. Recommends int64_t as a one-word change inside the hunk the fix already touches. Also catalogued the exit(1) cluster -- its line numbers were off by 1-2, as were Fable's, and the main loop settled it by counting: ELEVEN calls, NINE guest-reachable in DEVICE_ACCESS (342,351,366,381,390,399,442,468,493) and TWO init-time in DEVINIT (574,610) that are arguably legitimate.",
     "grade": "CONFIRMED",
     "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260815_221232/"
    },
    {
     "seat": "codex",
     "phase": "research",
