.txt   ->  (nothing)\n    ITBASM at :2927   \"ITBASM  --  1  7  WO  PALmode only\"   -- a HARDWARE REGISTER\n    ITBASM at :3940   \"A write operation to the instruction translation buffer ASM\n                       (ITBASM) register ... is written only in PALmode.\"\n\n**`TBIAP` DOES NOT APPEAR IN THIS MANUAL AT ALL.**  `ITBASM` is an internal processor register that\nPALcode writes; `TBIAP` is an OSF/1 PALcode ENTRY POINT, defined by the Alpha architecture and its\nPALcode specification, not by this chipset manual.\n\n> **THE HONEST STATEMENT: the manual documents the ASM MECHANISM that a PALcode implementation of\n> TBIAP would USE.  It is strong supporting evidence about how ASM-based selective invalidation\n> works on this part; it is NOT a definition of the selector's architected scope.**\n\n**THE CONCLUSION IS UNAFFECTED AND RESTS ON BETTER EVIDENCE ANYWAY.**  That ASM selectivity has no\nrepresentation here does not come from the manual at all -- codex established it from the DATA\nSTRUCTURE: the cache entry carries validity, writeability, addresses and a host pointer, with no ASM\nor ASN metadata.  *Only the citation's strength changes, and it changes downward.*\n\n**AND glm EXTENDED ONE OF MY OWN RULES AGAINST ME, correctly:** I had leaned on\n`grep -rn ALPHA_PTE_ASM src/` returning one hit.  glm: *\"don't assume ABSENCE from a constant's\nnon-use either.\"*  A constant being unreferenced shows the constant is unused; it does not by itself\nshow the CAPABILITY is absent.  The structural finding is what closes that gap, which is why the\nround should cite the structure and not the grep.\n\n*The irony is worth recording: I fetched this manual precisely so I would not cite what I had not\nread, and then over-read what I had. Reading a document is not the same as reading it for the claim\nyou are about to make.*",
