     "hold_question": "`alphatbinegsel` HAS A WITNESS (5/7 -> 7/7, both selectors) AND A SIX-SEAT FIX-DESIGN PANEL THAT RATIFIES THE GUARD -- so the questions are about what the round OWES, not whether the fix is right.  grok: ship `{1,2,3} -> INVALIDATE_VADDR, else INVALIDATE_ALL'.  codex: retain the bounded guard, recognise -1/-2 explicitly.  deepseek, kimi, minimax, glm concur.  THREE QUESTIONS.  (1) THE ACCEPT ROWS: minimax found the witness drives only a0 in {-2,-1,2} -- never a0 == 1 or a0 == 3, the BOUNDARIES the fix introduces.  I enumerated it: EVERY bound-moving mutant (`>= 2', `<= 2', `<= 4') differs on NONE of the driven values and ESCAPES.  That is the de-escalation clause -- the detector must pin the accept side of the predicate the fix ADDS.  The rows are subtle: at a0 == 1 pre- and post-fix behave IDENTICALLY, so they are ACCEPT rows that cannot red on HEAD, and their kill is a bound-moving mutant observed as an UNRELATED page's translation SURVIVING.  Does the round owe both rows before shipping, and is `an unrelated cached translation survives' the right oracle?  (2) A CITATION OF MINE WAS OVER-READ AND A SEAT CAUGHT IT: I cited the 21066 manual as documenting ALPHA_TBIAP()'s selectivity.  `TBIAP' appears ZERO times in that manual; the quoted sentence describes writes to the ITBASM HARDWARE REGISTER -- the mechanism a PALcode TBIAP would use, not the selector's architected scope.  The conclusion survives on the cache entry's STRUCTURE (codex: no ASM or ASN metadata in it).  Is the disclosure sentence now `the finer semantics have no representation in this translation cache', with the manual cited only for the ASM MECHANISM?  (3) DISPOSITION FOR UNDOCUMENTED SELECTORS: a0 == 0 and a0 >= 4 are not among the five wrappers.  The guard sends them to INVALIDATE_ALL.  glm argues the stakes are performance-only, since over-invalidation cannot serve stale data and TB contents are never architected state -- so the fix `can't be wrong, only conservative'.  Is a debug-level announce for an undocumented selector worth its line, or is silence right?",
