  {
   "id": "alphactxfail",
   "sev": "med",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "2026-09-07",
   "title": "cpu_alpha_palcode.c swpctx: the context save and load ignore every memory-access failure, and this fork zeroes read buffers on a failed translation -- so an ignored failure becomes committed zero-valued PCB state",
   "witness": null,
   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 from the `swpctx' design pass (regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/codex_design_20260907.txt).\n\ncodex: *\"SAVE discards store return values; the load helpers discard read status.  `ctx' changes before\nloading finishes, with no failure check before committing the PCB.  In this fork, failed translations zero\nread buffers, so ignored failure can become committed zero-valued state.  'Simultaneously' does not\nestablish failure atomicity.\"*\n\n*** THE INTERACTION WITH `#95' IS THE SHARP PART, AND IT IS THE THIRD TIME TONIGHT THAT FIX HAS TURNED UP\nWITH AN UNCONSIDERED EDGE. ***  `#95' zeroes the caller's buffer on a device READ so a partial fill cannot\nleak host memory -- unambiguously right.  Here that same zeroing means **a failed load returns a\nwell-formed zero instead of an error**, and the caller, which checks nothing, commits it to the PCB.  A\nsafety fix converting a detectable failure into a plausible value is worth knowing about generally.\n\nThe comment above the assignments says *\"Update all variables in the pcb simultaneously\"* -- and as codex\nnotes, simultaneity of ASSIGNMENT is not atomicity under FAILURE.  That comment now has three separate\nthings wrong with it across this row, [[alphaswpctxptbr]] and this one.\n\nNOT INDEPENDENTLY VERIFIED BY ME -- two further concerns from the same trace are recorded with it and are\nalso unverified: the helpers request translated accesses without `PHYSICAL', and `store_64bit_word'\nsign-extends the address where the matching loads and 32-bit stores do not.\n\n*** CLUSTER SCOPING, codex, 2026-09-08 (ev: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_verify_20260908.txt).\nSIX SEPARATE ROUNDS -- no merger has sufficient evidence. ***\n\n    one fix design : propagate context-memory failures through a defined failed-switch outcome\n    one oracle     : an injected memory failure produces that outcome without reporting a successful switch\n\n**The ordering ruling matters more than the grouping:** codex put **`alphaswpctxreg` FIRST**, on the\nground that it is the only one with verified behaviour AND a decisive witness (a returned register\nvalue), and it labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential\nseverity\"* -- which is the rung-versus-severity distinction the ladder already insists on, arrived at\nindependently.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED A GROUPING HYPOTHESIS I HAD FLOATED: \"the claim that `alphapteprot` prevents all\nguest paging is not established by this review.\" ***  I had suggested the page-walk row might be a\nprerequisite for testing the others; that is not supported, and scheduling on it would have been\nscheduling on an assumption.  `alphafaultclaim` and `alphavmkill` may be reconsidered together only\nafter proving they share a file, one fault-handling repair and one fault-delivery oracle --\n*\"correcting a comment and preventing termination do not establish that grouping.\"*\n\n*** codex ASSESS, 2026-09-08.  THE CLAIM SPLITS ACROSS TWO LAYERS, AND ONLY ONE HALF IS THIS\nHANDLER'S FAULT. ***\n\n**THE STORE HALF IS TRUE.**  `store_64bit_word` returns `int` -- it is `return cpu->memory_rw(...)`\n-- and `swpctx` discards that status at every save site.\n\n*** THE LOAD HALF IS NOT THIS HANDLER'S TO IGNORE. ***  `load_64bit_word` returns `uint64_t` DATA\nand has **no failure indication at all**: it calls `memory_rw`, discards the result, byte-swaps, and\nreturns the assembled word.  **A caller cannot check what the helper does not report.**  So \"the\nload ignores every memory-access failure\" mis-assigns responsibility -- the defect there is the\nhelper's ABI, and `load_64bit_word` **erases** the failure information rather than the caller\ndropping it.\n\n**Neither helper passes `NO_EXCEPTIONS`** -- both use `CACHE_DATA`, so my Q2 premise was wrong.\n**The consequence codex states: failed translations can become committed zero-valued PCB state.**\n\n## *** MY OPTION SET WAS INCOMPLETE, AND THE MISSING MEMBER IS THE RIGHT ONE ***\n\nI offered three dispositions: real defect with an observable consequence; fidelity gap with no\nchannel; or not a defect at all.\n\n> *\"Your three possibilities omit the present combination: **observable behavior is proved, but\n> architectural incorrectness is not yet proved.**\"*\n\n*\"This is not 'nothing to ignore': stores expose failure, and loads erase underlying failure\ninformation.  It is also not 'no observable channel': PCB saveback and PTBR supply one.  But\nchanging behavior for invalid input requires establishing what that input permits or requires.  **I\nwould not invent a fault or transactional switch rule and use that invention as the oracle.**\"*\n\n**Provisional severity: LOW, affecting guest state integrity; no host compromise, no unprivileged\nroute.**  If promoted, the claim must narrow to *reported failures being discarded or hidden*.\n\n*This is the option-set-completeness rule landing on me, on a brief where I thought I had enumerated\nexhaustively -- the same failure the three-membered de-fatalisation set records.*\n\n## THE IN-TREE IDIOM -- and a SIBLING SITE WITH THE SAME DEFECT\n\n* **`wrfen` (`cpu_alpha_palcode.c`) ALSO discards `store_64bit_word`'s status.**  So this is a small\n  CLASS inside the PAL layer, not a single site.  *Filed as part of this row rather than separately:\n  same file, same helper, same fix design.*\n* **`debugger_cmds.c` DOES check it** -- assigns to `res` and tests `!res`, for both widths.  **That\n  establishes the status is meaningful**, which is what makes discarding it a defect rather than a\n  convention.\n* **`cpu_alpha_instr_loadstore.c` checks direct `memory_rw` calls** and returns on failure before\n  consuming read data or reporting store-conditional success -- *\"the strongest nearby\n  implementation idiom, though it is not a PAL context switch.\"*\n* **NO checking PAL context-switch sibling exists**, which is why the fix has no exact precedent.\n\n## *** A THIRD INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION OF [[alphafaultclaim]], UNASKED ***\n\n*\"The latter's comment saying the translator already signalled a fault is also unsupported by the\ninspected Alpha failure path.\"*  **Three separate briefs tonight -- alphatbinegsel Q3, alphapteprot,\nand this one -- have now reached that same conclusion from three different directions**, none of\nthem asked about it.\n\n## SCOPE: NO MERGER with [[alphaswpctxreg]], despite being lines apart\n\n    row              fix concern                          oracle concern\n    ---------------  -----------------------------------  ---------------------------------------\n    alphaswpctxreg   correct result-register assignment   the switch's return value appears in\n                                                          the required register\n    alphactxfail     preserve access status and apply a   a failed access produces the required\n                     VERIFIED failure policy              state and control flow\n\n*\"Either correction can exist while the other problem remains.\"*  **Proximity is not the test** --\nwhich is the round-scope rule stated exactly.\n\n**BLOCKED, like `alphapteprot`, on an Alpha architecture manual**: none of the sixteen PDFs in\n`_scratchpad/` is an Alpha reference, and the seat declined to certify fault, restartability or\nrollback semantics without one.  **That is now THREE rows blocked on absent primary sources**\n(`armbfcdecode` on DDI 0406; `alphapteprot` and this one on an Alpha ARM).",
   "entries": [
    {
     "seat": "codex",
     "phase": "assess",
     "d": "y",
     "date": "2026-09-08",
     "grade": "ANSWERED",
     "outcome": "store half TRUE; load half BELONGS TO A DIFFERENT LAYER; my option set was incomplete",
     "grader": "main-loop",
     "ev": "regress/artifacts/alphactxfail/codex_assess_20260908.txt",
     "note": "Quoted both helpers in full, split the claim across two layers, found a SIBLING site with the same defect, and refused to certify architecture without a manual."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "sgilinearnegidx",
   "sev": "low",
   "state": "closed",
   "opened": "2026-09-07",
   "title": "*** WITHDRAWN -- FALSE FINDING. *** dev_sgi_re.c case 4 does NOT admit a negative index: `tile_nr' is UNSIGNED, so a negative x converts to a huge value that the `>= 32' guard REJECTS.  Kept as a record of the error, not as a defect",
   "witness": "none -- WITHDRAWN as a false finding; the offsetof measurement stands but is about a path that cannot be reached",
   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **FOUND BY THE glm SEAT** -- a PACKET-FED transport, from the inlined function alone -- in\nan answer I had recorded one sentence from and not re-read (regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/ollama_glm.txt).  Verified here.\n\nglm: *\"`case 4' bounds `tile_nr' above only, so a negative `x' -- possible precisely because the sign check\nis skipped for linear modes -- indexes `re_linear_a[-1]'.\"*\n\n**THE THREE STEPS, EACH READ FROM THE SOURCE:**\n\n    if (!linear && (x < 0 || y < 0 || x >= 2048 || y >= 2048))   <- SKIPPED when linear\n            return;\n    ...\n    case 4: tile_nr = x >> 12;\n            if (tile_nr >= 32)          <- bounds ABOVE only\n                    return;\n            tileptr = 0x80000000 | (d->re_linear_a[tile_nr] << 12);\n\n    x = -1     -> tile_nr = -1   passes the >= 32 test  -> re_linear_a[-1]\n    x = -8192  -> tile_nr = -2   passes                 -> re_linear_a[-2]\n\n*** THE READ VALUE BECOMES A PHYSICAL ADDRESS, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THIS WORSE THAN A STRAY READ. ***\n`tileptr = 0x80000000 | (d->re_linear_a[tile_nr] << 12)' -- so whatever struct field precedes `re_linear_a'\nis shifted left 12 and used as the tile base, and the guest's pixel write then targets `tileptr + ofs'.  An\nout-of-bounds READ of the device struct selects the DESTINATION of a subsequent write.\n\n**THE REACHABILITY PATH IS COMPLETE AND GUEST-DRIVEN, and I traced it rather than assuming one exists:**\n`step_x' is read from the guest register `CRIME_DE_XFER_STEP_X' into an `int32_t'; `src_x += step_x' runs in\nthe XFER loop; and the sanity check at `:734' *announces and CONTINUES* rather than declining -- grok\nindependently noted that same check is the file's only cross-field test and that it does not reconcile.  So a\nguest writing a NEGATIVE step drives `src_x' negative into a linear-mode GET.\n\n**NOT A DUPLICATE of [[sgilinearoob]]**, which is the ABOVE bound (`re_linear_[ab][tlbi+1]' with `tlbi'\ncapped at 31) in a different function.  This is the BELOW bound in `horrible_getputpixel''s case 4.  Same\narray, opposite end, and the existing row's cap does not help here because the guard is a `>=' test that a\nnegative value passes.\n\n**WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED:** which field precedes `re_linear_a' in the struct, and therefore what value the\nOOB read actually yields -- that is layout-dependent and a round owes `offsetof' rather than a guess.  Also\nunestablished is whether any real driver writes a negative step; the path is open, not observed.\n\n*** THE SEAT NOTE WORTH KEEPING: A PACKET-FED SEAT FOUND THIS. ***  It cannot read the repo; it had the\nfunction inlined and nothing else, and it noticed that one guard's precondition excludes the very mode the\nother guard assumes.  The doctrine says packet-fed seats contribute attack REASONING rather than repo fact --\nthis is exactly that, and it is the strongest single finding any seat produced on this brief.\n\n*** ESCALATED THE SAME HOUR: THE OUT-OF-BOUNDS READ LANDS ON GUEST-WRITABLE MEMORY, SO THE TILE POINTER\nIS NOT MERELY STRAY -- IT IS CHOSEN.  Arithmetic and the write path both checked. ***\n\nThe declaration order is `re_tlb_a[256], re_tlb_b[256], re_tlb_c[256]` (uint16_t), then `re_tex[112]`\n(uint16_t), then `re_linear_a[32]` (uint32_t):\n\n    re_tlb_a..c   1536 bytes\n    re_tex         224 bytes   -> ends at offset 1760\n    1760 % 4 == 0              -> uint32_t needs no padding before it\n    re_linear_a[-1] = bytes 1756..1759 = re_tex[110] and re_tex[111]\n\n**And `re_tex` is written by the guest** -- `d->re_tex[tlbi] = data[hwi] * 256 + data[hwi+1];` in the\nCRIME_RE_TEX store arm.\n\n**THE COMPLETE CHAIN, each step read from the source rather than assumed:**\n\n    1. guest stores to CRIME_RE_TEX, filling re_tex[110] and re_tex[111]\n    2. guest sets CRIME_DE_XFER_STEP_X negative (int32_t; the :734 check ANNOUNCES and CONTINUES)\n    3. src_x += step_x drives src_x negative in the XFER loop\n    4. horrible_getputpixel with a LINEAR src_mode: the `x < 0' guard is skipped for linear\n    5. case 4: tile_nr = x >> 12 = -1, and `if (tile_nr >= 32)' does not reject a negative\n    6. tileptr = 0x80000000 | (re_linear_a[-1] << 12)   <- the two guest-written halfwords\n    7. tileptr &= ~0x80000000; memory_rw(..., tileptr + ofs, buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, PHYSICAL)\n\n**So the guest selects the PHYSICAL DESTINATION of a device write.**  The value is shifted left 12, so the\nreachable address space is large; `PHYSICAL' bypasses translation.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, AND A ROUND OWES EACH BEFORE ANY CLAIM IS MADE OUTSIDE THIS FILE: ***\n* **the layout arithmetic is a DERIVATION, not a measurement.**  It assumes no packing attribute and no\n  compiler-inserted padding.  `offsetof' settles it in one line and MUST be run -- this project has a\n  standing lesson that a struct-layout claim derived by hand was wrong, and the anchor rule's own exemplar\n  turned on exactly this.\n* **no witness.**  Every step is reachable by reading; none has been observed.\n* **[[sgitexindex]] complicates step 1**: TEX writes OVERLAP (the index advances four per eight-byte\n  register), so WHICH values land in `re_tex[110..111]' needs working out -- it affects controllability, not\n  reachability.\n* whether any real driver drives a negative step is unknown; the path is OPEN, not observed.\n\n**RELATION TO THE OTHER ROWS:** [[sgilinearoob]] is the same array's ABOVE bound in a different function and\nis unaffected.  [[sgirebufuninit]] shares the function but is about buffer CONTENTS; this is about the write\nDESTINATION, and a memset fixes nothing here.  **The two must not be folded into one round** -- different\ndefect, different oracle, different severity.\n\n*** THE `offsetof' THE ROW SAID MUST BE RUN HAS BEEN RUN.  ZERO PADDING -- THE ESCALATION HOLDS ON A\nMEASUREMENT RATHER THAN ON MY ARITHMETIC. ***\n\n    offsetof(re_tex)       = 1536\n    re_tex ends at         = 1760\n    offsetof(re_linear_a)  = 1760\n    padding between        = 0 byte(s)\n\nSo `re_linear_a[-1]' is bytes 1756..1759 = `re_tex[110]' and `re_tex[111]', both written by the guest through\nthe CRIME_RE_TEX store arm.  Source preserved at `regress/artifacts/sgilinearnegidx/offcheck.c'.\n\n*** AND THE FIRST ATTEMPT AT THIS MEASUREMENT PRODUCED A CONFIDENT WRONG ANSWER, BY A ROUTE THIS PROJECT HAS\nNOT RECORDED BEFORE. ***  I wrote the test to `/tmp/off.c' from a Bash tool call and compiled it with\n`wsl -- cc -o /tmp/off /tmp/off.c && /tmp/off'.  **git-bash's `/tmp' and WSL's `/tmp' are DIFFERENT\nFILESYSTEMS.**  The heredoc wrote the Windows one; `cc' read WSL's, which held a stale `off.c' from an\nearlier session; the compile SUCCEEDED, so `&&' could not catch it, and the program printed offsets for a\nstruct with `cmd' and `data' fields that appear nowhere in anything I wrote.\n\n**The tell was the FIELD NAMES, not the exit status** -- the same shape as the carrier's stale-binary rule\n(*a binary OLDER than its source*), reached by a different route: here the binary was fresh and the SOURCE\nwas the wrong file.  The existing rules do not cover it: no sentinel was owed, no tree was mutated, and every\nstatus was 0.\n\n**THE FIX IS THE ONE THE CARRIER ALREADY MANDATES FOR SCRIPTS, EXTENDED TO SOURCES: put the file on a\n`/mnt/c' path both sides can see.**  The re-run also `rm's the binary first and refuses if none is produced,\nso a stale artifact cannot answer for a failed build.\n\n*** THE LINK I FLAGGED AS DOUBTFUL IS CONFIRMED, AND THE ANSWER IS SHARPER THAN THE CHAIN I WROTE:\nONE PREDICATE DISABLES ALL THREE PROTECTIONS.  Verified by direct reading 2026-09-07, after the\nverification seat refused the brief. ***\n\nI wrote that I was least sure of step 3 -- whether `src_x' is masked the way the destination `x' is, saying\nthat if it were, the chain dies there.  It is not, and the masking is CONDITIONAL on the very mode that\ndisables the other two guards:\n\n    if (src_is_linear)\n            src_x += step_x;                  <- NO MASK\n    else\n            src_x = (src_x + dx) & 0x7ff;     <- masked\n\n**So `src_is_linear' simultaneously (a) removes the coordinate mask here, (b) skips the `x < 0' guard in\n`horrible_getputpixel' via `if (!linear && (x < 0 ...))', and (c) routes to `case 4', whose `tile_nr' bound\nis `>= 32' only.**  That is not three independent oversights lining up -- it is ONE predicate switching off\nthree protections at once, which is a much better description of the defect than my step list.\n\n**THE REMAINING LINKS, each read at its own line:**\n\n    :715   int32_t step_x = 0;                                  SIGNED\n    :719   step_x = d->de_reg[CRIME_DE_XFER_STEP_X ...];        straight from a guest register\n    :724   src_is_linear = ((src_mode & 0x00001c00) >> 10) > 3; guest-controlled via src_mode\n    :757   uint16_t saved_src_x = src_x;                        so the row STARTS non-negative\n    :912   if (src_is_linear) src_x += step_x;                  unmasked, per above\n\n**MINIMAL REACHABILITY, which the earlier note did not state:** each row restarts at `saved_src_x' (a\n`uint16_t', hence >= 0), the GET uses the CURRENT `src_x', and the increment happens after.  So with\n`saved_src_x = 0' and `step_x = -1', **the SECOND pixel of the rectangle already uses `src_x = -1'** -- a\ntwo-pixel-wide rectangle suffices.  No large geometry, no wraparound, no exotic value.\n\n**STATUS: every link is now verified by reading.**  What remains unestablished is unchanged and stated\nbefore: no witness has been run; [[sgitexindex]]'s overlapping TEX writes affect WHICH values land in\n`re_tex[110..111]'; and no real driver is known to drive a negative step.\n\n*** WITHDRAWN 2026-09-07.  THE FINDING IS FALSE.  `tile_nr' IS UNSIGNED, AND THE DECLARATION IS THREE\nLINES ABOVE THE CODE I QUOTED. ***\n\n    :204   int tile_nr_x = x / tilewidth_in_pixels;\n    :205   int tile_nr_y = y >> 7;\n    :207   unsigned int tile_nr = tile_nr_y * 16 + tile_nr_x;     <- UNSIGNED\n    ...\n    case 4: tile_nr = x >> 12;            <- converts to unsigned on assignment\n            if (tile_nr >= 32) return;    <- 4294967295 >= 32 is TRUE.  IT RETURNS.\n\n    x = -1     -> x >> 12 = -1  -> as unsigned = 4294967295  -> >= 32 -> REJECTED\n    x = -8192  -> x >> 12 = -2  -> as unsigned = 4294967294  -> >= 32 -> REJECTED\n\n**The guard I called `upper bound only' is a COMPLETE bound**, because the conversion folds every negative\nvalue into the rejected range.  There is no out-of-bounds read, no guest-chosen tile pointer, and no write\nprimitive.  Refuted by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/sgilinearnegidx/codex_siblings_20260907.txt).\n\n*** HOW BADLY I GOT THIS WRONG, RECORDED IN FULL BECAUSE THE PROCESS FAILURE IS THE ONLY THING OF VALUE\nLEFT IN THIS ROW. ***\n\n* I filed it HIGH, then ESCALATED it to a guest-chosen physical write address.\n* I wrote *\"every link is now verified by reading\"* -- and had verified the link I DOUBTED (whether `src_x'\n  is masked) while never checking the TYPE of the index variable, which is the most basic property of an\n  index and the one that decides the whole question.\n* I ran an `offsetof' to avoid trusting hand arithmetic on the struct layout.  **That measurement is\n  correct and it was aimed at the wrong link.**  Rigour on one step does not transfer to the step you did\n  not question.\n* **THE DECLARATION WAS ON MY SCREEN.**  I printed lines 184-215 of this function earlier in the same\n  session while checking the mode decode; `unsigned int tile_nr' is at 207, inside that range.  I read past\n  it because I was looking for the mode fields.\n* It is in commit `15633bb`'s MESSAGE as a headline claim, which cannot be edited.  **That message is\n  wrong** and this row is the correction a reader must reach.\n\n**WHAT SURVIVES:** the `offsetof' result (`re_tex` ends at 1760, `re_linear_a` begins at 1760, zero\npadding) is a true measurement, preserved at `regress/artifacts/sgilinearnegidx/offcheck.c'.  It is about a\npath nothing can reach, so it supports no finding.\n\n*** AND THE REFUSAL COST ME THE REFUTATION FOR HOURS, WHICH IS THE SHARPEST ARGUMENT YET ABOUT §C5. ***  I\nasked the seat to attack this chain link by link.  **That brief was REFUSED for being chain-shaped.**  The\nsame seat, asked the same subject WITHOUT the chain -- *which array indexes are bounded on one side only?*\n-- answered in one line: *\"the stated case-4 underflow is absent in this fork: `tile_nr' is unsigned.\"*\n**The refusal did not stop a bad question; it delayed a correct answer**, and in the interval I escalated a\nfalse finding, wrote it into a commit, and reported it as the session's most severe result.\n\n*The lesson is not that seats are unreliable.  I built this chain, I verified the parts I doubted, and I\nwas wrong about the part I never questioned.  The seat that could have caught it earlier was refused for\nthe SHAPE of my question rather than its content.*\n\n**WHAT THE SAME AUDIT DID FIND, and it is not this row's defect:** *\"There are missing checks on complete\nwrite SPANS in TLB_A/B/C and LINEAR_A/B, whose safety depends on caller alignment.\"*  A span check is a\ndifferent question from an index bound -- the index can be in range while the multi-byte write starting\nthere runs past the end.  **Filed separately as [[sgirespanchk]] rather than folded in here**, because a\nwithdrawn row is the worst possible place to hide a live one.",
   "entries": [],
   "commits": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "sgirespanchk",
   "sev": "low",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "",
   "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: the TLB_A/B/C and LINEAR_A/B write loops bound only the FIRST element of a multi-element span -- a handler ROBUSTNESS gap, with the alignment that makes it unreachable VERIFIED in the CPU load/store path rather than assumed",
   "witness": "none",
   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, SPLIT OUT OF THE WITHDRAWN [[sgilinearnegidx]] ROW.\n\n**Kept separate on purpose: a withdrawn entry is the worst possible place to hide a live finding.**  The\nsame codex audit that REFUTED the negative-index claim (`tile_nr` is unsigned, so the `>= 32` guard rejects\nevery negative x) continued in its verdict line:\n\n    \"There are missing checks on complete write SPANS in TLB_A/B/C and LINEAR_A/B, whose safety\n     depends on caller alignment.\"\n\n**A SPAN CHECK IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION FROM AN INDEX BOUND.**  A bound of the form `if (i >= n) return;`\nanswers *where does the access START*; it says nothing about where a multi-element access FINISHES.  An\nindex can be in range while the span running from it leaves the array.  So the refutation of the\nnegative-index chain does not touch this at all -- they are independent claims about the same arrays.\n\n*** STATUS: AN AUDIT VERDICT, NOT A VERIFIED FINDING, AND THE DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE POINT OF FILING IT\nTHIS WAY. ***  The verdict CONDITIONS the safety on caller alignment rather than asserting a defect -- the\nseat's own words are *\"whose safety depends on\"*, which is a statement about an unchecked assumption, not\nabout a reachable bug.  **An unchecked assumption that happens to hold is a different thing from a checked\none**, and this row must not be promoted until somebody says which of the two it is.\n\n**OWED, and the third is the load-bearing one:**\n  1. the arrays with their declared element counts, and every multi-element access site;\n  2. where each span's WIDTH comes from -- a constant, or a register-derived value;\n  3. **whether any CALLER can present an unaligned or oversized span.**  If every caller is bounded by\n     construction this is a robustness note; if one is not, it is a defect.\n\nA read-only audit is out (`regress/artifacts/sgirespanchk/SPAN_brief.md`, fired at the codex seat 2026-09-07\nunder the C5 scoping rules -- reading scoped to `src/`, no chain, no reachability question).  **The brief\nopens by telling the seat that my last brief stated a false premise about an index variable's TYPE and asks\nit to read the declarations**, which is the premise-audit instruction aimed at the exact error that produced\nthis row's parent.\n\n*Provenance worth keeping: this finding was in the SAME verdict line as the refutation, and I nearly lost it\n-- the refutation was the arresting half and the span sentence sat after it.  That is the under-harvest\nshape this session has already hit five times: take the finding, skip the rest of the sentence.*\n\n*** UPDATED WITHIN THE HOUR, AND IT IS A DOWNGRADE: THE SAME ANSWER ALREADY CONTAINED THE\nCALLER-ALIGNMENT CHECK I HAD JUST FIRED A SECOND BRIEF TO OBTAIN.  MED -> LOW. ***\n\n**I filed this row from the verdict LINE and had not yet read the body.**  The body enumerates the spans and\nthen answers the question the verdict left open:\n\n    | Each TLB write loop    | 253 | 253, 254, 255, **256**      |\n    | Each TLB write loop    | 254 | 254, 255, **256, 257**      |\n    | Each TLB write loop    | 255 | 255, **256, 257, 258**      |\n    | Each LINEAR write pair |  31 | 31, **32**                  |\n\n    \"These are HANDLER ROBUSTNESS GAPS, not established guest out-of-bounds writes.  For the normal\n     SGI CPU access paths checked, eight-byte accesses have eight-byte alignment.  Enumerating aligned\n     addresses gives TLB starting indexes 0,4,...,252, and LINEAR starting indexes 0,2,...,30; their\n     complete spans fit.  I VERIFIED THE ALIGNMENT HANDLING IN THE ORDINARY ACCESS CODE AND\n     UNALIGNED-ACCESS HELPER, RATHER THAN ACCEPTING THE DEVICE COMMENTS.\"\n\n-- `src/cpus/cpu_mips_instr_loadstore.c` and `src/cpus/cpu_mips_instr_unaligned.c`, both named.\n\n**THAT IS THE DIFFERENCE THIS ROW WAS FILED TO DEMAND, AND IT WAS ALREADY DISCHARGED.**  The row asked\nwhether the safety is a CHECKED property or an unchecked assumption that happens to hold.  The seat checked\nit, in the right files, and said so in the words that distinguish the two.  So: a robustness gap in the\ndevice handler, unreachable through the CPU paths that exist.\n\n**WHAT IS STILL OPEN, and it is narrow: the word \"checked\".**  The seat verified the MIPS load/store and\nunaligned paths.  It did NOT enumerate every route into `dev_sgi_re`'s access function -- a DMA engine, a\ndevice-to-device access, or the debugger's `put` reach a handler without going through a CPU load.  A\nsecond read-only audit is already running (`SPAN_brief.md`) whose Q4 asks exactly this, and it was written\nand fired BEFORE this body was read, so its answer is independent rather than a re-confirmation.\n\n*** THIS IS THE FIFTH UNDER-HARVEST OF THE SESSION AND THE FIRST WHERE IT COST A WHOLE INVOCATION. ***  The\nprevious four dropped a seat's REFUTATION while keeping its finding.  This one dropped the seat's own\nDISCHARGE of the obligation I then wrote into a row and spent a codex run re-asking.  **The verdict line is\na summary written against the brief's framing and is the LEAST likely part of an answer to carry the\nqualification** -- this file already says so about corrections, and it is equally true of discharges.\n\n*Also harvested from the same body, each a check I had not asked for: `de_reg[1024]` accessed at `[regnr]`\nand `[regnr+1]` reaches at most 512 (safe); the two static `rop_used[256]` arrays are masked `& 255`, which\nsupplies BOTH bounds; `zerobuf[4096]` is length-capped then page-shortened, never enlarged; and `buf[8]`\ntakes enumerated lengths 1/2/4/8 which fit -- with the seat noting unprompted that its INITIALISATION is a\nseparate defect, which is [[sgirebufuninit]], already filed.*\n\n**AND ONE FINDING THAT IS NEW AND IS NOT A SPAN QUESTION:** *\"the coordinate rectangle check does not by\nitself establish the TLB capacity bound.\"*  At depth field 3 (`bufdepth` 8, tile width 64) the pre-guard\n`tile_nr` reaches **271**, not 255 -- the `x,y < 2048` rectangle admits indexes past the end of a `[256]`\narray, and it is the LATER `tile_nr >= 256` guard that rejects them.  Both bounds are present so nothing is\nbroken; what is worth recording is that **the two guards are not redundant, and an edit removing the second\none as \"already covered by the rectangle check\" would open a 16-element overrun.**  That is a clause-(d)\nshaped hazard sitting in code nobody is currently editing -- noted here, not filed.",
   "entries": [],
   "commits": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "sgilinearnegidxpostmortem",
   "sev": "low",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "",
   "witness": "none",
   "title": "RULE QUESTION: a chain resting on an index or a bound should quote the DECLARATION of every variable in it -- raised by the withdrawal of a HIGH finding that was false because I never read a type three lines above the code I quoted",
   "hold_question": "",
   "state_note": "*** A RULE QUESTION FOR THE 2026-09-09 ADJUDICATION, RAISED BY THE FAILURE ITSELF.  See [[sgilinearnegidx]],\nwithdrawn at `a264a34`. ***\n\n**THE FAILURE, stated plainly:** I filed a HIGH finding, escalated it to *a guest-chosen physical write\naddress*, wrote it into a commit message as a headline claim, and reported it as the session's most severe\nresult.  It was FALSE.  `tile_nr` is declared `unsigned int` at `dev_sgi_re.c:207`, three lines above the\ncode I quoted, so a negative `x >> 12` converts to ~4294967295 and the `>= 32` guard REJECTS it.\n\n*** WHAT MAKES IT A RULE QUESTION RATHER THAN A LAPSE: I APPLIED THE DISCIPLINE AND IT DID NOT REACH. ***\nI identified the link I doubted (whether `src_x` is masked in the linear path), verified it by grep, and ran\nan `offsetof` rather than trust hand arithmetic about the struct layout.  **All three were aimed at links I\nhad already decided to question.**  The defeating fact sat in a declaration I never thought to read -- in a\nrange I had printed to my own screen earlier in the same session, while looking for something else.\n\n**THREE QUESTIONS, in the order I think they matter:**\n\n1. **Does the premise-audit instruction need a SELF-DIRECTED TWIN?**  It tells SEATS not to ratify my\n   premises, and it works -- the carrier records it finding four wrong premises in one brief.  Nothing tells\n   ME to enumerate the premises I never questioned, and that set is exactly where this lived.  **A premise\n   you did not notice you were making is not audited by an instruction aimed at your reader.**  I think this\n   is the more valuable of the two candidate remedies.\n2. **Is the narrow mechanical rule worth a template line?**  *\"A chain resting on an index or a bound\n   quotes the DECLARATION of every variable in it.\"*  It is cheap, it is mechanical, it would have caught\n   this in one line -- and it is minted from ONE instance, which this project's own freshly-minted-exemplar\n   rule says makes its shape a hypothesis rather than a class.  **Restricting it to chains whose conclusion\n   is a BOUND or an INDEX keeps the population small and recognisable**, which is the form I would propose.\n3. **What does this say about C5?**  The chain-shaped brief asking for this exact chain to be attacked was\n   REFUSED.  The same seat, asked the same subject WITHOUT the chain, put the refutation in its verdict\n   line.  **So the refusal did not stop a bad question -- it delayed a correct answer by hours, and in that\n   interval I escalated a false finding and committed it.**  That is new evidence [[codexbriefscoping]] did\n   not have when it was filed: does it change that row's disposition, and does it warrant splitting every\n   chain into LINK QUESTIONS by default rather than as a fallback?\n\n**WHAT I AM NOT ASKING, because it would be the wrong lesson:** whether packet-fed seats are unreliable.\nglm raised the asymmetry and was right that two guards disagree; what it could not do -- exactly as the\ndoctrine says -- is check a DECLARATION, because a type is a repo fact.  **The doctrine's warning is about\ncrediting such a seat with repo fact, and that is precisely the error I made on its behalf.**  The doctrine\ncame out of this correct; I did not.\n\n*Queued in `fable_queue.md` section 6 with the other four rule questions, written out in full so the\nadjudication can begin without reconstructing any of them.*",
   "entries": [],
   "commits": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "sgirgbscanoutrow",
   "sev": "low",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "",
   "title": "#505's arm-attribution warrant is a claim about dev_sgi_gbe.c's scanout that NO row observes -- the detector runs the drawing engine only and has no framebuffer oracle",
   "witness": "none",
   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 by `#505`'s own PRE-TABLE CLAIM->ROW check, which is what that check exists to produce.\n\n`#505` chose the GET arm as the defective one on a warrant from a DIFFERENT DEVICE: `dev_sgi_gbe.c`'s\nscanout composes a 32-bit tile pixel as `(buf[0]<<24)+(buf[1]<<16)+(buf[2]<<8)+buf[3]` and then\n`CRMFB_MODE_TYP_RGB8` takes R,G,B from bits 31..24 / 23..16 / 15..8 -- so byte 0 is R and byte 3 is\nunused.  **That is the strongest evidence in the round and NO ROW OBSERVES IT.**\n\nThe detector drives the drawing engine and reads the destination word back through the guest.  It never\nreaches scanout, so it cannot tell a tree where DE and GBE AGREE on the container from one where they\nhave both been changed consistently and wrongly.\n\n**WHAT A ROW WOULD NEED:** drive a DE draw in RGB mode, then make GBE scan the same tile out and compare\nframebuffer bytes -- i.e. an oracle on `dev_fb_access` output rather than on a guest load.  That is a\ndifferent probe SHAPE, not another row in this one, which is why it is filed rather than added: the\nround's own detector is above the floor without it.\n\n*Recorded as an explicit no-row sentence rather than omitted, because the CLAIM->ROW check's rule is that\nan added comment asserting an observable consequence owes a row OR an explicit statement that it has\nnone -- and an unrun check and an inapplicable one must not render the same.*",
   "entries": [],
   "commits": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "sgirebufshiftub",
   "sev": "med",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "",
   "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: FOUR of the five GET arms compute `buf[i] << 24` on a uint8_t, which promotes to signed int -- for any byte >= 128 that is a signed left-shift overflow, i.e. UB, on all four arms",
   "witness": "harness:regress/artifacts/sgirebufshiftub/ubsan_emulator_20260907.txt -- MEASURED 2026-09-07 under build-asan's -fsanitize=undefined, driving the shipped 10-row #505 probe through a stderr-capturing wrapper; positive AND negative controls in-band (ubsan_control_20260907.sh).  8 diagnostics, all left-shift.",
   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 by the **grok seat** under the found-something-else licence, on `#505`'s pass-1 panel\n(`_scratchpad/panel_20260907_051055`).  Verified by reading before filing.\n\n    uint8_t buf[8];\n    ...\n    *color = (buf[0] << 24) + (buf[1] << 16) + (buf[2] << 8);\n\n`buf[i]` is `uint8_t`, which the usual arithmetic conversions promote to **signed `int`** -- not to\n`unsigned int`, because `int` can represent every `uint8_t` value.  So `buf[0] << 24` for any byte\n>= 128 shifts a 1 into or past the sign bit of a 32-bit `int`:\n\n    0xaa = 170;  170 << 24 = 2,852,126,720  >  INT_MAX (2,147,483,647)\n\nwhich is **undefined behaviour** in C99 6.5.7p4 (E1 << E2 where E1 is signed and the result is not\nrepresentable).  In practice gcc/clang produce the wrapped value everyone expects, which is exactly\nwhy it has survived -- but `-fsanitize=undefined` reports it, and this tree HAS a UBSan sweep\n(`gate_asan_sweep.sh`).\n\n**IT IS PRE-EXISTING AND IT IS ON ALL FOUR ARMS**, not on the one `#505` edited: the RGB, RGBA, ABGR\nand default GET arms all use the same `buf[i] << 24` idiom, and the shipped pre-`#505` RGB arm used\n`buf[1] << 24` identically.  `#505` replaced one instance of the idiom with another instance of the\nsame idiom, which is the correct minimal-surgical choice: fixing the cast on ONE line would leave the\nfile internally inconsistent for no measured gain.\n\n**DISPOSITION: FILED, not fixed in-round, and the seat that raised it agrees** -- *\"Pre-existing on\nevery GET arm, not unique to RGB, not a reason to block.\"*  The stopping rule licenses an in-round fix\nonly for a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG RECORD, and this is neither.\n\n**THE FIX WHEN IT COMES IS ONE CAST PER TERM** -- `((uint32_t)buf[0] << 24)` -- across all four arms in\none diff, which is the class-closure form rather than a cell at a time.  **OWED FIRST: whether the\nUBSan sweep already reaches this function**, since if it does, the absence of a report is itself a\nfinding about the sweep rather than about the code.\n\n*Note the shape: a seat asked about byte ORDER volunteered a defect about integer PROMOTION in the same\nexpression.  The found-something-else licence is where three of this session's filed rows came from.*\n\n*** THE SAME DEFECT WAS RAISED AGAIN BY THE CODEX SEAT ON THE `sgirebufuninit` PANEL,\n2026-09-07, AND I FILED IT AS A SECOND ROW (`sgishift24ub`) WITHOUT CHECKING THIS ONE. ***\nThe duplicate is merged here and deleted.  **grok had it first, on `#505` pass 1, under the\nfound-something-else licence** -- so this is the unread-review class one more time: the finding was\nalready in the ledger, and the round that re-derived it paid for it twice.\n\n**WHAT THE SECOND RAISING ADDS, and it changes the disposition:** a CENSUS of the idiom, which\nneither raising had.  Keyed on whether the shifted element carries a cast, over\n`src/devices`, `src/cpus`, `src/core`:\n\n    <ident>[..] << 24     111 total\n      CAST                 25      e.g. cpu_alpha_instr_loadstore.c, `((uint64_t)data[3] << 24)`\n      BARE                 86      in 22 files\n        dev_px.c           20\n        tmp_arm_rc.c       15\n        dev_sgi_re.c        4      (a 5th grep hit is a comment, not code)\n\n> **THE BARE FORM IS THE HOUSE IDIOM BY 86 TO 25, AND IT IS UPSTREAM'S** -- it sits in the CPU\n> instruction decoders, and `cpu_mips_instr.c` carries both forms forty lines apart.  So\n> `dev_sgi_re.c` is NOT an outlier, and repairing four sites in one device would be arbitrary.\n> **The sibling-disposition check answers AGAINST a local fix**, exactly as it did for\n> `sgireunmapped`.\n\n**AND THE ROUND THIS DESERVES IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT AN EDIT.**  `build-asan/src/devices/Makefile:9`\nalready carries `-fsanitize=address,undefined`, and UBSan's shift check is in that set by default.\nSo *does it fire, on a path a gate already walks?* is answerable with a tool in the tree and no\nsource change at all.  If it fires, the finding is 86 sites and a tree-wide policy question for the\nowner (`-fwrapv`?  cast the idiom?  accept and document?).  If it does not fire, find out why\nbefore believing either answer.\n\n*Verified before recording: `src/devices/Makefile:9` is `-fstrict-aliasing -fomit-frame-pointer\n-fpeephole -fgnu89-inline -O3 -DNDEBUG` -- **no `-fwrapv`, no `-fno-strict-overflow`** -- so the\nbuild gives the idiom no cover, and `#505`'s probe drives `0xaa` through it on every run.*\n\n*Deriver, because my first TWO censuses were wrong -- one keyed on variable NAMES (a correlate,\nwhich silently counted already-cast sites) and one whose bare-form count returned **0 while its own\nsample output showed bare forms three lines below**:*\n\n    T=$(grep -rhoE '[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*\\[[^]]*\\] *<< *24' src/devices/*.c src/cpus/*.c src/core/*.c | wc -l)\n    C=$(grep -rhoE '\\)[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*\\[[^]]*\\] *<< *24' src/devices/*.c src/cpus/*.c src/core/*.c | wc -l)\n    echo \"total $T  cast $C  bare $((T-C))\"\n\n*** OWNER DECISION 2026-09-07, asked with the four options on the table: MEASURE WITH UBSan\nFIRST, THEN DECIDE.  The alternatives -- add `-fwrapv`, cast all 86 sites, or accept-and-document\n-- were all declined PENDING the measurement. ***\n\n**STEP 1, THE POSITIVE CONTROL, AND IT IS THE PART THAT MAKES THE MEASUREMENT MEAN ANYTHING.**\nA silent run is ambiguous between *the code does not do this* and *this toolchain does not check\nthis* -- the pattern-that-matches-nothing shape, which this project has met five times.  So the\nidiom was compiled STANDALONE with `build-asan`'s own flags, read out of its generated Makefile\nrather than retyped:\n\n    -fstrict-aliasing -fomit-frame-pointer -fpeephole -fgnu89-inline -O3 -DNDEBUG\n    -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g\n\nThe control takes its byte from `argv` so `-O3` cannot constant-fold the shift away.  Measured, both\ndirections:\n\n    byte 0xaa   ctl.c:13:18: runtime error: left shift of 170 by 24 places cannot be\n                represented in type 'int'                                     <- REPORTS\n    byte 0x02   stderr 0 lines                                                <- SILENT\n\n> **SO THE CHECK IS PRESENT AND DISCRIMINATING IN THIS TOOLCHAIN, and a silent real run now MEANS\n> the path did not execute the UB.**  Without this control it would have meant nothing, and the\n> honest disposition would have been *uninterpretable* rather than *not reached*.\n\n*Note what the control also settles: the UB is REAL rather than theoretical -- gcc's own sanitizer\ncalls it out by name at `-O3` with the fork's exact flags.  What remains open is REACHABILITY, which\nis exactly the axis the row said bounds the severity.*\n\n**STEP 2 is the shipped 10-row `#505` probe run against `build-asan/gxemul`** -- it drives\n`0xaabbccdd`, so `buf[0] = 0xaa`, the very value the control reports on.  Both build-tree clearance\nparts are checked before the run and it REFUSES to measure a stale or divergent ASAN tree.  Output\npreserved at `regress/artifacts/sgirebufshiftub/ubsan_real_20260907.txt` -- a durable path, because a\nnumber this row will cite must not live in `/tmp`.\n\n*** TAKE 1 OF THE REAL RUN RETURNED **ZERO** AND THE ZERO WAS AN APPARATUS ARTEFACT.  I had the\ncontrol that makes a zero interpretable and then measured the WRONG STREAM. ***\n\n    probe result   SGIRGB_RESULT=10/10  under the ASAN binary\n    'runtime error' lines                0\n    preserved file                    1310 bytes -- ten probe rows and nothing else\n\n**The probe spawns `gxemul` inside a PTY.**  UBSan writes to the EMULATOR's stderr, which never\nenters the probe's own stdout or stderr -- so what I captured could not have contained a diagnostic\nwhether or not one was emitted.  The 1310-byte file is the tell: an emulator run that had produced\nany console output at all would be larger.\n\n> **THE ZERO MEANT 'NO UBSAN OUTPUT ARRIVED HERE', NOT 'NO UB EXECUTED'.**  And it is worse than an\n> ordinary miss, because the CONTROL had just certified that a zero would be meaningful -- the\n> control certified the CHECK and said nothing about the PLUMBING, and I read its certificate as\n> covering both.\n\n*This is the carrier's own 'check that the apparatus selected something before reading what it says'\nrule, met from a new direction: the apparatus selected the right FILES and the wrong STREAM.  It also\nvery nearly produced the most expensive kind of wrong record here -- a MEASURED-LOOKING NEGATIVE,\nwhich would have closed the row as 'not reached' with a preserved artifact and a control behind it.*\n\n**TAKE 2 uses `UBSAN_OPTIONS=log_path=...`**, which writes diagnostics to `<path>.<pid>` files\nregardless of how the child's console is plumbed -- and it carries an **IN-BAND POSITIVE CONTROL**:\nthe known-UB binary is run through the SAME `log_path` mechanism first, and the script REFUSES to\ninterpret the emulator run if that control produces no log file.  *A measurement whose negative\nresult cannot be distinguished from a broken apparatus is not a measurement*, and take 1 is the\nproof.\n\n*** MEASURED: IT FIRES.  Owner decision was measure-then-decide; this is the measurement. ***\n\nTake 3 drove the shipped ten-row `#505` probe against `build-asan/gxemul` through a wrapper that\nappends the emulator's stderr to a file.  The probe itself scored **10/10** -- the emulator behaves\ncorrectly -- and the run emitted **EIGHT** UBSan diagnostics, every one a left shift:\n\n    5   dev_sgi_re.c:350:21   left shift of 170 by 24 places cannot be represented in type 'int'\n    1   dev_sgi_re.c:353:21   left shift of 170 by 24 places\n    2   dev_sgi_re.c:221:41   left shift of 32770 by 16 places\n\n`:350` is **the line `#505` ships**.  `170` is `0xaa`, the probe's own first byte.\n\n*** THE `:221` SITE WAS NOT IN MY CENSUS AT ALL, AND IT IS THE WORSE ONE. ***\n`32770` is `0x8002`.  `:221-225` are `tileptr = d->re_tlb_{a,b,c}[tile_nr] << 16;` over\n`uint16_t[256]` arrays -- and **bit 15 of a tile pointer is the VALID bit**.  So it is not that a\nlarge value occasionally appears: **every VALID tile pointer has bit 15 set, so every tiled access\nthrough this path is UB by construction.**  The RGB sites need a red channel >= 0x80; this one needs\nonly a tile that is marked present.\n\n*** AND MY CENSUS WAS WIDTH-BLIND, WHICH IS WHY I MISSED IT. ***  I keyed on `<< 24` because that is\nwhere a `uint8_t` overflows.  **The threshold moves with the shift**: a shift of N bits overflows\n`int` once the value reaches `2^(31-N)`, so\n\n    uint8_t  << 24    UB at >= 0x80        <- what I censused\n    uint16_t << 16    UB at >= 0x8000      <- the same defect, invisible to that census\n    uint8_t  << 16    max 0xff0000         <- SAFE, cannot overflow\n    uint8_t  << 8     max 0xff00           <- SAFE\n\n**So the class is defined by (operand's reachable maximum) x (shift width), not by the shift width\nalone** -- the deriver rule again, and the third time today a census of mine keyed on a correlate.\nRe-derived counts, bare (uncast) occurrences across `src/devices`, `src/cpus`, `src/core`:\n\n    << 8    total 207   cast   8   bare 199    (mostly SAFE -- a byte cannot reach 0x800000)\n    << 16   total 154   cast   8   bare 146    (UB wherever the operand is 16-bit and can set b15)\n    << 24   total 126   cast  25   bare 101\n\n*The `<< 8` population is large and mostly harmless, which is exactly why a raw shift-count census\nis the wrong instrument: it over-counts the safe family and under-counts the dangerous one.*\n\n> **SEVERITY SET TO `med`, and the axis is CONSEQUENCE rather than reachability.**  Reachability is\n> total -- it fires on an ordinary probe run and, at `:221`, on every tiled access.  What holds it\n> below `high` is that gcc emits the obvious shift at `-O3` today and no miscompilation has been\n> observed; the harm is a standards violation the optimiser is entitled to exploit, not a measured\n> wrong answer.  **That is a judgement about a compiler's future, which is exactly the kind of thing\n> this project says to hand to the adjudication rather than settle in a round.**\n\n**THE DISPOSITION IS NOW THE OWNER'S, WITH DATA.**  The three options the measurement was gating:\n`-fwrapv` (one flag, defines all of them, changes codegen tree-wide and therefore every gate oracle\nmeasured afterwards); cast the idiom (correct, matches the 25+8 sites that already do it, but a\nlarge diff across upstream CPU decoders); or accept-and-document (cheapest, and now harder to\njustify at `:221` than it was an hour ago).\n\n*Apparatus note, because the number is only worth what the plumbing is worth: takes 1 and 2 both\nreturned a false or refused zero -- take 1 measured the probe's streams rather than the emulator's,\ntake 2 tried `log_path`, which GCC's libubsan silently ignores (measured: zero files, diagnostic\nstill on stderr).  The in-band control refused take 2 rather than letting it report.  Take 3's\ncontrol fires on `0xaa` and is silent on `0x02`, so the eight is discriminating in both directions.*\n\n*** THE TITLE SAID \"every GET arm\" AND THAT IS FALSE.  Corrected 2026-09-07 after the `#509` pass-2 codex seat caught it while checking a DIFFERENT claim. ***  Read across all five arms:\n\n    CI    :323  *color = buf[0];                                  NO SHIFT AT ALL\n    RGB   :350  *color = (buf[0] << 24) + ...                     shifts buf[0]\n    RGBA  :353  *color = (buf[0] << 24) + ...                     shifts buf[0]\n    ABGR  :356  *color = (buf[3] << 24) + ...                     shifts buf[3], not buf[0]\n    dflt  :359  *color = (buf[3] << 24) + ...                     shifts buf[3], not buf[0]\n\nSo the UB population is **four arms, two of them shifting a different byte**, and the CI arm is not a member.  *The defect is unchanged in substance and the enumeration was wrong in two directions at once -- it over-counted CI and mis-named the byte for ABGR and the default arm.*",
   "entries": [],
   "commits": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "sgireput8tail",
   "state": "open",
   "title": "#505's detector has no row establishing that an 8-byte PUT's tail is initialised",
   "witness": null,
   "state_note": "Raised by the codex seat on the `sgirebufuninit` full panel, 2026-09-07, answering Q4 -- the\nquestion that asks which row stays green.\n\n`rgb depth64` and `rgb tiled64` widen the SOURCE READ to eight bytes.  Their DESTINATION is still a\nfour-byte RGBA write.  So the ten-row detector drives an eight-byte read and never an eight-byte\nwrite, and **nothing in it would redden if the PUT arms left `buf[4..7]` untouched at bufdepth 8.**\n\n*Coverage debt, not a floor breach: the shipped detector satisfies (a)-(f), and this is a cell that\nno enumerated mutant of `#505` distinguishes.  Row demand is per enumerated mutant, never per\nuncovered state -- so FILING is what the floor rules prescribe, not weakness.*  It becomes live the\nmoment [[sgirebufuninit]] ships, because a zero-fill fix's own ACCEPT SIDE is exactly this cell.\n\n*Evidence: `panel_20260907_130159/codex.txt`.*",
   "sev": "low",
   "opened": "2026-09-07",
   "entries": [],
   "commits": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "queueunrated",
   "sev": "med",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "2026-09-07",
   "title": "34%% of the OPEN queue carries no severity (65 of 189) and only 16 of those carry a real witness -- so 'prioritise critical bugs' is partly blind, but the blind part is overwhelmingly HARNESS DEBT, not concealed emulator criticals",
   "witness": "none",
   "entries": [
    {
     "seat": "codex",
     "phase": "research",
     "d": "✓",
     "date": "2026-09-07",
     "grade": "ANSWERED",
     "outcome": "partial-census-with-denominator",
     "grader": "main-loop",
     "ev": "regress/artifacts/queueunrated/codex_shiftub_20260907.txt",
     "note": "SHIFT-UB CENSUS.  *** VERDICT: `Partial census only.  Eight selected left-shift sites are positively cleared; the remaining shifts are not settled.' ***  It REFUSED the framing honestly -- *\"I can't supply a ranked discovery of guest-triggerable vulnerabilities and their triggering domains\"* -- which is worth more than a confident sweep.  DENOMINATOR GIVEN AND SCOPED: 224 C/H files screened lexically, 17,150 operator occurrences of which 7,067 are left shifts, and it says plainly these are NOT 17,150 semantic examinations; comments and literals excluded; macro expansion NOT evaluated.  Eight sites cleared with their guest-control domains (m88k clr/set `w' 0-31 under a ternary; three DEVINIT `1 << i' loops bounded 0-31; an ARM literal 27).  *** IT CORRECTED MY ARITHMETIC: `msb - lsb + 1 < 0' requires `lsb >= msb + 2' = 465 negative, 31 zero, 496 nonpositive.  Brute-forced here over all 1024 pairs: 465 / 31 / 496 -- the seat is exactly right and I was wrong. ***  Excluded `lcashiftub' and `sgirebufshiftub' from semantic review, and said so."
    },
    {
     "seat": "codex",
     "phase": "research",
     "d": "✓",
     "date": "2026-09-07",
     "grade": "ANSWERED",
     "outcome": "no-sibling;three-dead-guards;one-live-shift",
     "grader": "main-loop",
     "ev": "regress/artifacts/queueunrated/codex_bitsbytes_20260907.txt",
     "note": "BITS/BYTES SIBLING HUNT.  VERDICT: *\"No additional bits/bytes sibling confirmed.  I found three unrelated dead guards, one always-true test, and a separate ARM shift-count defect.\"*  224 files screened, 60 sites traced across 18 files.  A NEGATIVE RESULT WITH A DENOMINATOR is the useful shape here.  Its three by-products: the dec21143 dead clamps (-> `dec21143deadclamp'), the always-true `writeback_len > 1', and *** the ARM bit-field shift, which led to `armbfiwidth' -- a LIVE third site #510 missed. ***  Cleared two near-miss candidates by deriving their domains (sgi_gbe `8 << (field & 3)' -> {1,2,4,8} bytes; dev_px `bitdepth >> 3' -> {1,3})."
    },
    {
     "seat": "codex",
     "phase": "research",
     "d": "✓",
     "date": "2026-09-07",
     "grade": "ANSWERED",
     "outcome": "helpers-census",
     "grader": "main-loop",
     "ev": "regress/artifacts/queueunrated/codex_helpers_20260907.txt",
     "note": "HELPERS CENSUS, 12,560 bytes.  Recorded as ANSWERED; findings not yet mined into rows."
    }
   ],
   "commits": "",
   "state_note": "Derived 2026-09-07 while trying to obey the standing directive *prioritise critical bugs and also\neasy resolvable ones*.  **The directive is unexecutable over half the queue.**\n\n    open + in-work        187\n    carrying `sev`        100   (high 10, med 40, low 50)\n    UNRATED                87   = 47%\n    unrated AND WITNESSED  73\n\n**The 73 is the number that matters.**  A witness makes a row ELIGIBLE under the ladder, so those are\nrows that could be worked today and that nothing has ranked.  Ranking is what the carrier says orders\nthe queue -- *rung is a floor on admissibility, never a priority; SEVERITY and gate blind-spots order\nthe queue* -- so an unranked witnessed row is invisible to the only ordering rule this project has.\n\n*** THE FIX IS NOT A REGEX, AND I NEARLY SHIPPED ONE. ***  A keyword scan over titles buckets 7 rows\nunder host-kill language, 5 under memory-safety and 17 under wrong-data.  **Those buckets are\nCORRELATES.**  What makes a row HIGH here is *a guest can reach it and something bad happens*, and no\nkeyword tests reachability -- which is the deriver rule (*key on what DEFINES the class, not on a\ncorrelate of it*) applied to my own triage script.  The scan is kept only as a READING ORDER.\n\n**Candidates the scan surfaced, recorded as UNVERIFIED reading order and explicitly NOT as gradings:**\n`gdromcondavail` (COND_DATA_AVAIL permanently lies after an overread), `asicindexcase` (a deleted\nguard that was also a bounds check), `gdrompiooddlen` (guards one byte, reads two), `asickillsurvive`\n(four kill rows survive-only, so an OOB read is unobserved), `gdromdmawidth` (a tail store ignoring\n`len`), `sh4pcic15` (an interrupt line that can never be delivered), `asicpermute` (an index\npermutation).  **Each needs its reachability read before any `sev` is written.**\n\n> **DISPOSITION: a RATING pass, evidence-first, one row at a time, with the reachability sentence\n> quoted from the source.**  It is read-only, so it parallelises freely and belongs to the file-reading\n> seats -- the exact work the standing directive says to give codex.  It is NOT a round: nothing is\n> edited, and the deliverable is `sev` plus a one-line warrant per row.\n\n*Related but distinct from [[baretitle]], which counts rows with an EMPTY NOTE.  A row can be richly\ndocumented and still unranked -- most of these 73 are.*\n\n*** MY '73 WITNESSED' FIGURE IS WRONG, AND IT IS THE CORRELATE TRAP FOR THE THIRD TIME TODAY. ***\ncodex: *\"**37 of those 73 have witness strings beginning `none`** -- sometimes followed by\nhistorical measurements, sometimes explicitly denying reachability.  **The snippet counts strings,\nnot demonstrated witnesses.**\"*\n\nMy test was `r.get('witness') and r.get('witness') != 'none'` -- which passes any string that merely\nSTARTS with `none`, and this ledger's convention is to write `none -- <why>` with the reasoning\nattached.  **So the honest figure is about 36 witnessed-and-unrated rows, not 73.**\n\n*The paragraph above this one warns against keying on a correlate, and the snippet printed\nimmediately below it does exactly that.  Re-derive with a prefix test, and note that `none -- ...`\nrows are often the BEST documented ones, since a denial of reachability takes more evidence than an\nassertion of it.*\n\n*** A PATTERN IN MY OWN METHOD, WORTH RECORDING BECAUSE IT IS NOW FOUR FOR FOUR TODAY: EVERY\nCENSUS I RAN KEYED ON ONE SPELLING AND MISSED A VARIANT. ***\n\n    severity          the ledger's field is `sev`              -> 0 of 260 rows matched\n    witness != 'none' the convention is `none -- <why>`        -> 37 of 73 miscounted as witnessed\n    << 24             uint16_t << 16 is the same defect        -> the structural site missed entirely\n    (int)idata        the tree also writes `(int) idata`       -> 226 where the answer is 309\n\n**Each was found by someone else running a different derivation, never by me re-reading mine.**  The\ncarrier's rule says *when two derivers disagree, neither is trustworthy until you look at what each\nMATCHED* -- but all four of these had only ONE deriver, so there was nothing to disagree with, and a\nsingle method produces a confident number with no signal attached.\n\n> **THE PRACTICE THAT FOLLOWS: a census that will be written into a durable record runs TWO\n> derivations with different keys, and prints BOTH.**  Agreement is weak evidence (they can share a\n> blindness -- `sgirekills` measured that) but DISAGREEMENT is a free, immediate signal, and it is\n> the only one available before a seat reads the work.\n\n*** AND THE CLOSURE PASS CAUGHT A FIFTH COUNT ERROR OF MINE, IN THE BRIEF THAT ANNOUNCED THE OTHER\nFOUR. ***\n\nI stated \"note/witness cites a `#NNN` round: **40**\".  codex: *\"I cannot reproduce 40 from the\nstated fields.  `#\\s*\\d+` over top-level `note` and `witness` selects **51**; including `state_note`\nproduces **98 at HEAD / 104 in the working ledger**.  **The field selection needs to accompany that\ncount.**\"*  My KEY2 ran over `witness` plus `state_note[:400]` -- a truncation I never wrote down.\n\n**It also caught that my census MIXED SNAPSHOTS:** HEAD has 184 open/in-work rows, the working\n(uncommitted) ledger has 190.  Both contain the same fourteen commit-bearing candidates, so the\nKEY1 result stands -- but a count taken from a dirty tree and compared against HEAD is two\npopulations wearing one number.\n\n*The two-deriver practice adopted this morning would not have caught either: both are failures to\nstate the DOMAIN, not to vary the key.  **A count owes its field selection and its snapshot, not\njust its command.***\n\n*** ADDENDUM 2026-09-07: THE GAP IS NOT A CLOSED LEGACY BLOCK, SO THE ORDER OF THE FIX MATTERS. ***\n\nRe-derived from the other direction while filing a DUPLICATE of this row ([[ledgersevmissing]], now\nwithdrawn -- see it for the process lesson).  Counting rows that lack the `sev` KEY outright, rather\nthan rows that are unrated:\n\n    total ledger rows                       411\n    lacking a `sev` key entirely            261\n    of those, open or in work                78\n    by state: open 65, in work 13, held 72, closed 93, dropped 18\n\n**The set spans the whole history, not just the early rounds.**  It contains `d1`, `a4`, `n1`..`n6`\nfrom the first rounds -- and also `wdcdataoutdrain`, `gdromlatchcross`, `divratchetband` and others\nopened within the last fortnight.  So rows are STILL being created without the field.\n\n> **CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS ROW'S DISPOSITION: the writer-side check lands BEFORE the rating pass, not\n> after it.**  A backfill alone fixes the past and not the cause, and the queue refills behind it.\n> The check is the `check_cited_artifacts` idiom -- forward-only from a cutoff, so a NEW row without\n> a rating fails and the existing ones warn.  That ordering is the only thing the duplicate\n> contributed, and it is why it was folded here instead of being left as a second row.\n\n*Also observed while sorting: a row with no `sev` is not sorted LAST -- depending on the tool it is\nomitted entirely or it raises KeyError and kills the sort.  Both happened.  That is worse than a\nlow ranking, because nothing reports that the row was skipped.*\n\n*** THREE SUBSTANTIVE codex ANSWERS SAT ON DISK FOR THIS ROW WITH **ZERO** LEDGER ENTRIES, AND ONE\nOF THEM CONTAINED A LIVE DEFECT NOBODY HAD SEEN.  Recorded 2026-09-07. ***\n\nThe three (`codex_shiftub`, `codex_bitsbytes`, `codex_helpers`, all dated 2026-09-07) were verified\nby CONTENT and TAIL -- real answers, no wall text, no echo -- and cited nowhere.  **`codex_bitsbytes`\nnamed the `(1 << width) - 1` idiom in the ARM bit-field handlers.  Its UBFX/SBFX citations were\nSTALE (repaired by `#510` before it ran), but following the finding into the file found the THIRD\nsite, `X(bfi)`, still live with a WIDER undefined domain than either the round had fixed** -- now\nfiled as `armbfiwidth`.\n\n> **A STALE FINDING THAT LEADS TO A LIVE ONE IS NOT A WRONG ANSWER.**  It is the sibling grep the\n> round should have run itself, arriving a day late from a seat.  The carrier's mechanical rule 2\n> -- *when correcting a claim, grep for its siblings before calling it fixed* -- is the rule `#510`\n> missed, and it is recorded there with a prior instance of exactly this shape.\n\n*And the same batch corrected my own arithmetic in a way I then confirmed by brute force: 496\nnonpositive widths of 1024 pairs, not the figure I had supplied.*\n\n*** RE-DERIVED 2026-09-07, AND THE ROW'S OWN NUMBERS WERE WRONG -- WHICH IS THE DEFECT THIS ROW IS\nABOUT, COMMITTED BY THIS ROW. ***\n\n    claimed in the title    measured 2026-09-07\n    47% unrated             34%  (65 of 189 open rows)\n    73 unrated + witnessed  16\n\n**AND MY FIRST RE-MEASUREMENT WAS ALSO WRONG, IN THE WAY I HAD DOCUMENTED AN HOUR EARLIER.**  I\nfiltered `witness not in ('none', '')` and got **59** -- because the field holds prose like\n`none yet -- GUEST-REACHABLE and witnessable` and `n/a -- a harness gap`, which are not equal to\n`none` and are not witnesses either.  **That is the deriver rule's own failure: I keyed on a\ncorrelate (the string differs from a sentinel) instead of on what DEFINES the class (the field\nmatches the enforced schema).**  Keyed on `check_witness.py`'s own regex -- `^(boot:|probe:|\nconstruction:|harness:|detector:)` -- the answer is **16**.\n\n*Three counting methods, three answers (73, 59, 16), and only the one keyed to the enforced schema\nis defensible.  The tell was the same one this project keeps recording: when derivers disagree, look\nat what each MATCHED rather than choosing a number.*\n\n### *** THE CHARACTERISATION IS THE USEFUL PART, AND IT ANSWERS THE PRIORITISATION WORRY. ***\n\nOf the 16 unrated-and-witnessed rows, **14 carry `harness:` witnesses**, one `boot:` and one\n`detector:`.  **The unrated backlog is overwhelmingly TOOLING DEBT.**  So the rating gap is not\nconcealing critical emulator defects -- which is what the row's alarming headline implied and what a\nreader would reasonably fear.\n\n**All 16 were rated on 2026-09-07 by the MAIN LOOP** (2 of them raised on the day's own evidence;\nsee `panelcite0824`).  The remaining 49 unrated open rows carry NO witness of any kind, so they are\ncorrectly ranked below every witnessed row regardless of what severity they would eventually get --\n*a witness makes an item ELIGIBLE; severity orders what is already eligible.*"
  },
