   "title": "sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py's `lin_b diag once' row does not assert `(once)' -- removing the suppression marker alone leaves it green",
   "witness": "none",
   "entries": [],
   "commits": "",
   "state_note": "Volunteered by the codex seat under the found-something-else licence on the `sgirandomcolour` design\npass, 2026-09-07: *\"The sibling's `lin_b diag once` row does not assert `(once)`.  It counts\n`\"unimplemented dst_mode\"` occurrences.  Removing the required marker alone would leave that row\ngreen.\"*\n\n**This is the CLAIM->ROW check failing on SHIPPED material.**  The row's NAME asserts the latch\nmarker; its ORACLE counts a different string.  So the one row whose whole purpose is to pin the\nsuppression marker is blind to that marker's removal -- vacuous with respect to its own name.\n\n*The house idiom is `(once)`, which a census this fork ran on 2026-08-30 put at 24 occurrences in 4\nfiles against 4 and 5 for the two rival spellings -- so the marker is load-bearing convention, and a\nrow named for it that cannot see it is exactly the check-that-cannot-fail shape.*\n\n**NOT fixed in-round and not a floor breach of `sgirandomcolour`:** it is a row in a DIFFERENT,\nalready-shipped detector, above the floor, so it is coverage debt under the stopping rule.  It\nbecomes L3 the moment a round relies on that row as the sole carrier of a latch obligation.\n\nCheap repair when a round touches that probe: assert the marker text itself, not the message body."
  },
  {
   "id": "cxseatcheck",
   "sev": "med",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "2026-09-07",
   "title": "tools/cxlaunch.sh: a codex seat check that could report a REFUSED seat as a clean run, and a wall-grep over stderr that matched the project's own ledger text",
   "witness": "harness:controlled in four states on the day it landed -- refusal, echoed-source false positive, real wall, healthy answer; all four give the wanted disposition.",
   "entries": [],
   "commits": "",
   "state_note": "Built 2026-09-07 after the `lcaconfwidth` census was REFUSED by codex's content filter and my\nlauncher reported it as a completed run.  **Three defects, all mine, all found within an hour of\neach other, and all of one family: a check that answers the same whether or not the thing it tests\nis true.**\n\n1. **The refusal check read the wrong stream.**  A content-filter refusal writes NOTHING to stdout\n   and puts `ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.` on STDERR.  The check\n   grepped stdout and printed `refusal hits: 0` on a refusal that had already destroyed 108,432\n   tokens of completed work.\n2. **The script exited 0 while holding `rc=1`.**  It captured codex's status, printed it, and ended\n   on a successful command, so the harness reported the task COMPLETED.  Failure laundered into a\n   clean completion -- the fourth instance of that class recorded here.\n3. **The FIX introduced a third defect, measured on its first real run.**  Grepping stderr freely\n   for wall text reported `wall: 3` on a HEALTHY seat, because **codex streams the source it reads\n   into stderr** -- and all three hits were this project's own ledger prose about seat walls, one of\n   them the line *\"whose quotation of the pattern MATCHES\"*.  That is the carrier's\n   quoting-a-machine-read-marker rule, landing on a tool written by someone who had just read it.\n\n> **THE DISCRIMINATOR: codex's OWN diagnostics are `ERROR:` at line start; everything else on stderr\n> is echoed content.**  Anchor stderr tests on `^ERROR:`; grep stdout freely.  Exit 7 refusal, 8\n> wall, 6 empty-with-neither, else `rc`.\n\n**Controlled in four states**, and (b) is the one that matters: a stderr line containing *usage\nlimit* inside echoed source must NOT trip the wall check.\n\n    a  ERROR: flagged...           -> REFUSED   (want REFUSED)\n    b  echoed source w/ 'usage limit', good stdout -> answered  (want answered)\n    c  ERROR: usage limit          -> WALLED    (want WALLED)\n    d  ordinary reasoning, good stdout          -> answered  (want answered)\n\n*** THE CLAIM I ORIGINALLY CLOSED THIS ROW WITH WAS FALSE, AND I MADE IT IN A ROW ABOUT CHECKS THAT\nDO NOT CHECK.  Corrected within the hour, by reading `panel.sh` instead of assuming. ***\n\nI wrote that *\"`panel.sh` fires the same seat with its own check and has NOT been given this\ndiscriminator.\"*  **It has had one longer than mine, and a better one.**  Its `check()` anchors error\nsentinels to the **first 400 bytes** and its wall test to the **last 400 bytes**, with the reason in\nits own comment: *\"a review that merely quotes 'was retired' or 'usage:' gets mis-flagged (it did, on\nthe first live run).\"*\n\n**So the tree already held the lesson and I re-learned it by breaking it.**  The two discriminators\ndiffer and both are sound:\n\n    panel.sh    POSITION -- head/tail 400 bytes.  Transport-agnostic; works for any seat whose\n                own status lands at the edges of the file, which is all of them.\n    cxlaunch.sh FORMAT   -- codex's `^ERROR:` lines.  Sharper for codex, useless for the others.\n\n*The honest ordering: `panel.sh`'s is the general one and mine is a codex-specific refinement.  A\nfuture consolidation should take POSITION as the base test and add FORMAT where a seat's own error\nprefix is known -- not the other way round.*\n\n*Kept open for that consolidation, not for the phantom gap I invented.*\n\n*** A SECOND SHAPE OF THE SAME CLASS, MEASURED ON MYSELF 2026-09-08: THE HARNESS REPORTED\n\"completed (exit code 0)\" FOR A codex RUN THAT NEVER STARTED. ***\n\nA background launch used a RELATIVE artifacts path.  The background shell's cwd was not\n`GXEMUL-SEC`, so both halves failed:\n\n    cat: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/Q3_brief.md: No such file or directory\n    bash: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_q3_20260908.txt: No such file or directory\n    EXIT=1\n\nand the task notification said **completed (exit code 0)** -- the WRAPPER's status, not the command's.\n`codex` was never invoked; no `.txt` and no `.err` were created at all.\n\n**The tell is the carrier's own three-state rule, and it is the state people forget:** log MISSING =\nnever started; log 0 bytes = started, mid-run or dead; log growing = alive.  A REFUSED codex run\nleaves a 0-byte `.txt` AND a large `.err`.  **This left neither** -- which is how it is distinguished\nfrom a refusal, and why a check that only looks for the refusal text would have called it a clean run.\n\nTwo things follow, and the second is the cheap one:\n* **an explicit `echo \"EXIT=$?\"` after a backgrounded seat launch is what caught it.**  Without it\n  the missing answer would have looked like a refusal or a wall.\n* **use ABSOLUTE paths for every seat launch.**  The carrier already says the session cwd DRIFTS and\n  mandates absolute paths for `panel.sh`; the same applies to any hand-rolled seat invocation, and a\n  BACKGROUND shell does not reliably inherit the foreground `cd` at all."
  },
