   "commits": "c0c7f55",
   "state_note": "Filed 2026-09-07.  Full record in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md under the same id.\n\n*** THIS ROW EXISTS BECAUSE THE LEDGER<->BUGFILE SYNC IS ONE-DIRECTIONAL AND ITS WARRANT HAS DECAYED -- see [[bugfilereverse]].  It was filed into OUTSTANDING_BUGS and given no ledger row, which is precisely the failure check_bugfile_sync.py was written to stop, in the direction it does not check. ***\n\n*** 2026-09-07 FOLLOW-UP: TWO SBFX CELLS ADDED, AND A FALSE CLAIM SHIPPED IN THE COMMIT MESSAGE OF `c0c7f55'. ***  The round claimed the pc control held on EVERY row; only three rows asserted pc and ALL THREE WERE MOV/UBFX -- no SBFX row checked pc, which is the case whose failure value is also exactly zero.  `s w32 pc' and `s w31' added; 14/14.  A commit message cannot be amended, so the CHANGELOG block and the probe comment ARE the record, and both now carry the correction with the quote it refutes.  Also: my own CHANGELOG citation decayed within the evening when the driver reused `witness_FIXED12...raw' -- RECOVERED by `git checkout' because the artifact was committed, so all four scores stand on their own logs.  My first correction claimed it was lost and was written before I checked git; a wrong correction is worse than the error.  Source half proven COMMENT-ONLY by stripping comments and diffing (byte-identical).\n\n*** PASS 2 ON `01a0339`, 2026-09-07 -- THREE SEATS AGREED ON A POINT THAT IS MEASURABLY FALSE, AND\nMY BRIEF SUPPLIED THE FALSE PREMISE BY OMISSION. ***\n\nAll three landed seats independently called `s w32 pc` **a control, not a kill**, on the reasoning\nthat `s w32` already reds under non-execution because its expected value is NONZERO (`0x89abcdef`).\nminimax stated the load-bearing assumption outright: *\"r0 also retains its prior-row value (the row\nbefore `s w32` is a UBFX row, not 0x89abcdef).\"*\n\n**THERE IS NO PRIOR-ROW VALUE.**  `run_word()` is:\n\n    def run_word(iw, src=SRC):\n        cmds = [\"r1=0x%x\" % src, \"r0=0x0\",\n                \"put w 0x%x, 0x%08x\" % (CODE, iw),\n                \"pc=0x%x\" % CODE, \"step 1\", \"print r0\", \"print pc\"]\n        t, alive = session(cmds)\n\nand `session()` does `pty.fork()` + `os.execv(BIN, ...)` -- **a FRESH emulator per row, with `r0`\nexplicitly zeroed before every word.**  So a word that does not execute leaves `r0 == 0x0`, and\n`0x0` is EXACTLY the value `s w32` returned pre-fix.  The row is genuinely ambiguous between\n*executed and returned the defect* and *never executed at all*, which is the ambiguity a pc control\nexists to resolve.  **[THIS SENTENCE IS WRONG.  See the correction at the end of this note -- `s w32 pc` is an ATTRIBUTION control, the seats were right, and I refuted them on a mistake.]**, because all\nthree seats reasoned from the same false premise.\n\n**THE DEFECT IS THE BRIEF'S, NOT THE SEATS'.**  I inlined the diff and the row list and did NOT\ninline `run_word`.  That is the sketch rule -- *a brief that solicits evaluation of an edit must\ninline the enclosing context, every exit path included* -- applied to a probe instead of a function,\nand it is the second time this session a packet defect has produced confident agreement: the\ncarrier's rule is that **unanimity on a brief-supplied fact is worth nothing**, and here the fact\nwas supplied by ABSENCE, which is harder to notice than a wrong sentence.\n\n*** deepseek's CLAIMED FLOOR BREACH IS AN EQUIVALENT MUTANT, so it is a RESULT and not a breach --\nPREDICTED HERE, OWED A MEASUREMENT. ***  It proposes `width < 32` -> `width <= 32` and reports 14/14.\nThe score is almost certainly right and the DISPOSITION is wrong:\n\n    at width 32   mask      = 0xffffffff          (the `width == 32` ternary arm)\n                  1U << 31  = 0x80000000          DEFINED -- 1U is unsigned, 31 < 32, no UB re-opened\n                  x & that  = nonzero for SRC\n                  x |= ~mask  ==  x |= 0          NO OBSERVABLE CHANGE\n\nSo the mutant neither re-opens the defect (clause b) nor nulls the repair (clause d): it is\nsemantically equivalent, and this project has measured that exact shape before -- `#449`'s\nguard-only widening was EQUIVALENT because the latch argument still masked to the original bits.\n**A mutant that cannot fail is a result, not noise, and an equivalent mutant is not a floor breach.**\nRecorded as PREDICTED; it is one build to settle and it is owed before the chain closes, because\nthis round's own rule is that a zero must carry its denominator.\n\n**WHAT THE SEATS GOT RIGHT AND I ACCEPT:** `s w31` IS load-bearing (kimi and deepseek both, and it\nis the row that pins SBFX at width 31 at all); the comment-only claim on `cpu_arm_instr.c` is\nverified FROM THE DIFF by minimax (all four hunks inside comment blocks, the two `uint32_t mask =`\nlines unchanged context), which is an independent confirmation of the strip-and-diff proof; and\nkimi's warning that *\"filed is not run\"* for `ubsanshiftlane` is correct -- the disclosure claims a\nUBSAN differential WOULD pin the two definedness escapes and no such receipt exists.\n\n**NEXT: re-fire with `run_word` and `session` inlined.**  The adjudication packet carries the\ncorrection, names the three seats' shared premise, and asks the parties to adjudicate their own\nfinding -- specifically whether `s w32 pc` is load-bearing once they can see that r0 is zeroed.\n\n*** CORRECTION, SAME EVENING: I REFUTED THE PANEL AND THE PANEL WAS RIGHT.  grok -- the seat that\nREAD THE TREE -- settled it against me. ***\n\nI argued above that `s w32 pc` is load-bearing because `run_word()` zeroes `r0` before every word in\na fresh emulator, so a non-executing word leaves `r0 == 0x0`, which is exactly the pre-fix failure\nvalue.  **Every fact in that sentence is true and the conclusion does not follow.**  grok:\n\n> *\"A word that never ran leaves `r0 = 0` (the probe zeroes it).  That REDS `s w32`, it does not\n> green it.  So this row does not stop a false pass of the value oracle.  What it does is ATTRIBUTE\n> a red `s w32`: executed-and-wrong (pc advanced) versus never-ran (pc stuck or vectored).  `w32 pc`\n> already does that for UBFX.  This is sibling parity, and it is correctly labelled CONTROL in the\n> row note.\"*\n\n`s w32` WANTS `0x89abcdef`.  Under BOTH failure modes -- never-ran and executed-with-the-defect --\n`r0` is 0, so `s w32` is RED either way.  **Ambiguity in a row that is already failing cannot produce\na false pass**, and a detector row earns its place by turning red when something is wrong, not by\nexplaining why.  I conflated DIAGNOSTIC ATTRIBUTION with COVERAGE.\n\n**AND THE ROW'S OWN NOTE ALREADY SAID SO.**  `s w32 pc` is labelled `\"CONTROL: the SBFX word\nEXECUTED\"` in the probe.  The round shipped it correctly labelled and then argued in the ledger that\nit was something else.\n\n*** THE PROCESS LESSON, WHICH IS WORTH MORE THAN THE ROW: minimax's STATED PREMISE WAS FALSE AND ITS\nCONCLUSION WAS RIGHT. ***  It wrote *\"r0 also retains its prior-row value\"* -- there is no prior-row\nvalue, `run_word` zeroes it in a fresh session -- and it still reached the correct disposition, by a\ndifferent route than grok's.  I found the false premise, felt the refutation, and stopped.  **This\nproject's own rule is that a wrong reason can accompany a right conclusion and that neither credit\nnor dismissal may rest on the reason alone**; the same file records a case where a flagship seat's\ncorrection was itself false.  Checking the CONCLUSION costs one more paragraph and I did not spend it.\n\n*This is the third correction-of-a-correction this session, and the rule keeps holding: a correction\ncarries more authority than the error it corrects, so it earns a SECOND reading rather than less of\none.  All three were caught, none shipped -- but all three were mine.*\n\n**WHAT NOW STANDS, on five landed seats plus codex still streaming:**\n\n* `s w32 pc` -- **ATTRIBUTION CONTROL**, sibling parity with `w32 pc`.  Correctly labelled, correctly\n  shipped, and NOT a kill.  The round's prose implying otherwise is the thing to fix.\n* `s w31` -- **LOAD-BEARING and not a duplicate of `w31`** (grok: it pins a SBFX-only `>= 31`\n  bound-relax; kimi and deepseek independently).  This is the row that earned its place.\n* **THE FLOOR QUESTION: grok CLOSES THE CHAIN** -- *\"no in-rule clause-(d) mutant of `width == 32` or\n  `width < 32` sweeps the 14-row detector except the two definedness escapes already disclosed.\"*\n  That is a zero WITH its denominator, which is what the stop condition requires.\n* **A FOURTH FALSE PROSE CLAIM, caught independently by glm AND grok**: the diff says *\"the SBFX rows\n  are exactly the ones whose expected FAILURE value is also EXACTLY ZERO.\"*  Too wide.  **UBFX `w32`\n  also returned exactly `0x0` pre-fix** -- this round's own witness table says so -- and only\n  `s w32` has that property among the SBFX rows.  The asymmetry argument that justified adding a pc\n  control was therefore wrong in its stated form, while the row itself is fine as a control.\n* **deepseek's `width <= 32` \"floor breach\" is an EQUIVALENT MUTANT** (my analysis, and grok's\n  closure implies it): at width 32 the mask is `0xffffffff`, `~mask` is 0, so `x |= ~mask` is a\n  no-op and `1U << 31` is defined -- no UB re-opened, nothing observable changed.  Not a breach.\n* **glm's second finding is refuted by an EXISTING MEASUREMENT MY BRIEF FAILED TO INLINE.**  It says\n  deleting `x |= ~mask` is green against every row the packet names.  That mutant was built during\n  the previous pass with a pre-registered prediction and measured **10/11, killed by `s mid` alone**\n  (`regress/artifacts/armbfxwidth32/M_nosext.raw`) -- `s mid`'s field is `0xde`, whose bit 7 IS set.\n  glm could not know that; **the brief inlined the two disclosed escapes and not this measurement.**\n  Third packet defect of mine this session, same shape: a premise the seats needed and I withheld.",
   "hold_question": "What should `bfi` do when msb < lsb?  Its width = msb - lsb + 1 spans -30..32, so 465 of the 1024 pairs give a strictly NEGATIVE shift count (496 is the msb < lsb population, i.e. width <= 0; the other 31 give width 0, which is defined) -- undefined by the same C99 clause as the too-large one.  ARM calls that encoding UNPREDICTABLE, and this fork's standing position is that an unpredictable GUEST encoding must not produce undefined behaviour in the HOST.  A codex pass examined 13 comparable decisions and found the tree answers this class INCONSISTENTLY: no-op has bitfield precedent, conditional UND has decoder precedent, cpu_arm_instr_loadstore.c:204 picks another defined result for an UNPREDICTABLE halfword load, and #312's own comment argues AGAINST halting.  It said plainly that nothing examined settles bfi uniquely.  AND THE OBVIOUS ROUTE IS WRONG AS WRITTEN: X(und) expects arg[0] to hold a PC OFFSET and bfi's holds a REGISTER POINTER.  #510 therefore fixes the three WITNESSED sites and leaves bfi alone rather than inventing a policy."
  },
  {
   "id": "alphapteprot",
   "sev": "med",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "2026-09-07",
   "title": "memory_alpha.c: the page walk returns 2 for every successful walk, so memory_rw caches EVERY walked page WRITABLE -- flags and all seven protection bits are never tested",
   "witness": "none",
   "entries": [
    {
     "seat": "codex",
     "phase": "assess",
     "d": "y",
     "date": "2026-09-08",
     "grade": "ANSWERED",
     "outcome": "mechanism CONFIRMED, this row's FRAMING REFUTED, and the real defect is larger",
     "grader": "main-loop",
     "ev": "regress/artifacts/alphapteprot/codex_assess_20260908.txt",
     "note": "Found the documented contract, showed both 1 and 2 are cacheable so caching is not the defect, showed BOTH access paths accept a prohibited store, and cross-confirmed alphafaultclaim on the way."
    }
   ],
   "commits": "",
   "state_note": "Filed 2026-09-07.  Full record in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md under the same id.\n\n*** THIS ROW EXISTS BECAUSE THE LEDGER<->BUGFILE SYNC IS ONE-DIRECTIONAL AND ITS WARRANT HAS DECAYED -- see [[bugfilereverse]].  It was filed into OUTSTANDING_BUGS and given no ledger row, which is precisely the failure check_bugfile_sync.py was written to stop, in the direction it does not check. ***\n\n*** CLUSTER SCOPING, codex, 2026-09-08 (ev: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_verify_20260908.txt).\nSIX SEPARATE ROUNDS -- no merger has sufficient evidence. ***\n\n    one fix design : return/cache permissions according to the actual translation contract\n    one oracle     : observed access disposition matches independently specified page permissions\n\n**The ordering ruling matters more than the grouping:** codex put **`alphaswpctxreg` FIRST**, on the\nground that it is the only one with verified behaviour AND a decisive witness (a returned register\nvalue), and it labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential\nseverity\"* -- which is the rung-versus-severity distinction the ladder already insists on, arrived at\nindependently.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED A GROUPING HYPOTHESIS I HAD FLOATED: \"the claim that `alphapteprot` prevents all\nguest paging is not established by this review.\" ***  I had suggested the page-walk row might be a\nprerequisite for testing the others; that is not supported, and scheduling on it would have been\nscheduling on an assumption.  `alphafaultclaim` and `alphavmkill` may be reconsidered together only\nafter proving they share a file, one fault-handling repair and one fault-delivery oracle --\n*\"correcting a comment and preventing termination do not establish that grouping.\"*\n\n*** codex ASSESS, 2026-09-08: THE MECHANISM IS CONFIRMED AND THIS ROW'S FRAMING IS REFUTED.  The\ndefect is bigger than the one filed. ***\n\n**CONFIRMED:** every successful walk returns `2`.  `memory_alpha.c` ends the three-level walk with\n`// TODO.` then `return 2;`, and the kernel direct-map and zero-PTBR bootstrap arms return `2` as\nwell.  **The only PTE checks are `ALPHA_PTE_VALID`; `flags` is unused.**  There is no\nprotection-dependent successful return anywhere.\n\n*** BUT \"SO `memory_rw` CACHES EVERY WALK\" IS THE WRONG DIAGNOSIS, AND THE CONTRACT IS DOCUMENTED\nRATHER THAN UNWRITTEN. ***  `src/include/memory.h`:\n\n    0    MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED       denied or failed\n    1    MEMORY_ACCESS_OK           success, READ-ONLY\n    2    MEMORY_ACCESS_OK_WRITE     success, READ/WRITE\n    256  MEMORY_NOT_FULL_PAGE       suppresses whole-page caching\n\nwith the prose authority spelled out in `memory_arm.c`.  **Both 1 and 2 are cacheable** -- the\nconsumer passes `ok - 1` as the cache's write permission, so `1 -> host_store = NULL` and\n`2 -> host_store = host_page`.  *Caching a read-only mapping is legitimate; the defect is claiming\nREAD/WRITE for every page.*\n\n## *** AND CHANGING THE RETURN TO 1 WOULD NOT BE ENOUGH -- BOTH PATHS ACCEPT THE PROHIBITED STORE ***\n\n* **fast path:** `host_store` is installed, so the store never reaches a check;\n* **slow path:** `memory_rw` supplies `FLAG_WRITEFLAG` but **rejects only a ZERO translation\n  result**.  Alpha returns 2, so the RAM path performs the write; and the physical backing helper in\n  `core/memory.c` has no guest-PTE protection check either.\n\n> **So the repair is request-time ENFORCEMENT plus permission-dependent cache metadata -- not a\n> one-line return change.**  A round that only edited the return value would ship a fix that\n> measures clean on the fast path and still writes through the slow one.\n\n**THE IN-TREE IDIOM EXISTS AND WAS FOUND BY GREPPING, as the option-set rule requires:**\n\n    M88K   rejects writes when accumulated permissions contain PG_RO, then\n           return (accumulated_flags & PG_RO)? 1 : 2;          memory_m88k.c\n    ARM    computes permission from the descriptors and mode, requires access > writeflag,\n           returns that permission, else takes the permission-fault path.  memory_arm.c\n\n*** IT CROSS-CONFIRMED A DIFFERENT OPEN ROW ON THE WAY: [[alphafaultclaim]]. ***  *\"There is no\nimplemented Alpha MM-fault dispatch here.  `ALPHA_KENTRY_MM` and `ALPHA_MMCSR_*` have definitions\nbut no consumers; `kentry` entries are initialized and assigned, never used to dispatch.  The\ncomments claiming the translator or caller 'already signalled' a fault are unsupported by the\nimplementation.\"*  **That is `alphafaultclaim`'s claim, established independently by a seat that was\nnot asked about it.**\n\n**Q5 -- IT IS OBSERVABLE, so the documented-only escape does NOT apply:** the channel is guest RAM\nCONTENTS -- a prohibited write changes bytes that should not change.  *\"'Protection is unmodelled,\nso nothing can observe the error' does not follow: memory contents remain observable.\"*\n\n**Q6 -- ITS OWN SEVERITY WORDING, which is careful and which I am adopting rather than\nparaphrasing:** an *\"Alpha MMU protection-fidelity gap with observable guest-memory correctness\nconsequences\"*, substantial for software relying on page permissions -- and explicitly **NOT** host\nmemory corruption and **NOT** a host security-boundary failure.\n\n*** TWO CAUTIONS ON ITS OWN EVIDENCE, both of which I am recording rather than glossing: ***\n* **It corrected a premise I had inlined from another seat.**  The `DM_EMULATED_RAM` registration is\n  the RAM-MIRROR branch in `dev_ram.c`; base RAM is created through `memory_new` in `core/emul.c`.\n  The conclusion (2 is cacheable) is unaffected, but the premise as I stated it was wrong.\n* **IT CITED THE ALPHA ARCHITECTURE MANUAL BY URL, AND THAT MANUAL IS NOT IN `_scratchpad/`.**  The\n  fifteen PDFs there include no Alpha reference.  So the Table 17-3 citation is **NOT locally\n  verifiable**, and under this project's rule -- born from `#357`/`#362`/`#365` shipping page numbers\n  that came from model output -- it must not be quoted as an established architectural fact.  *The\n  SOURCE findings above stand on their own; only the architectural authority is unverified.*\n\n**A ROUND TAKING THIS OWES AN ALPHA ARCHITECTURE MANUAL**, exactly as `armbfcdecode` owes DDI 0406.\nThat is now TWO rows blocked on absent primary sources."
  },
