  {
   "id": "shiftubcensus",
   "sev": "low",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "2026-09-07",
   "title": "a tree-wide census of signed-1 shifts came back HONESTLY PARTIAL: 7,067 left shifts lexically, 8 examined semantically -- the class is surveyed at ~0.1%",
   "witness": "none",
   "entries": [],
   "commits": "",
   "state_note": "Filed 2026-09-07.  Full record in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md under the same id.\n\n*** THIS ROW EXISTS BECAUSE THE LEDGER<->BUGFILE SYNC IS ONE-DIRECTIONAL AND ITS WARRANT HAS DECAYED -- see [[bugfilereverse]].  It was filed into OUTSTANDING_BUGS and given no ledger row, which is precisely the failure check_bugfile_sync.py was written to stop, in the direction it does not check. ***\n\n*** TWO CONCRETE MEMBERS OF THIS CLASS, FOUND BY THE grok PASS-2 SEAT ON `#511` AND VERIFIED, 2026-09-07. ***\n\n    dev_uninorth.c:70-72   for (dev=11; dev<32; dev++)  if (idata & (1 << dev)) break;\n    dev_v3.c:164-166       for (dev=24; dev<32; dev++)  if (relative_addr & (1 << dev)) break;\n\nBoth reach `dev == 31`, so both evaluate `1 << 31` = 0x80000000 -- **a signed overflow in a 32-bit\nint, C99 6.5.7p4**, the same defect `#511` repaired in `dev_lca.c`.  Both are PCI IDSEL scanners:\nthe same idiom, in three chipsets, and only one had been noticed.\n\n**THE ONE-TOKEN REPAIR IS THE SAME (`1U << dev`)**, and each site needs the same check `#511`\nneeded: what is the type of the left-hand operand of the `&`, and does the truth value move?  In\n`#511` that operand was a plain `int`, so the `&` happened at 32 bits and nothing observable\nchanged; **neither of these two has been checked, and the answer may differ** -- `dev_uninorth.c`\nands against `idata`, which in that handler is likely 64-bit, and that is EXACTLY the case where the\nold sign-extension and the new zero-extension give different masks.  *Do not assume `#511`'s\nconclusion transfers; it turned on a type.*\n\n**WHY THIS ROW GAINED THEM RATHER THAN A NEW ROW:** it is already the filed home for the class and\nalready marked HONESTLY PARTIAL.  These make the partiality concrete.\n\n*Provenance worth keeping: my own sibling census for `#511` MISSED both, because it excluded\nliteral `1` as \"the ordinary bitmask idiom\".  `1` has the most headroom and is therefore the only\nliteral that overflows at the maximum shift a `< 32` loop produces -- the exclusion removed exactly\nthe population it should have kept.*\n\n*** THE TYPE CHECK EACH SITE OWED IS DONE, AND THE TWO SITES DIVERGE -- ONE REPAIR IS\nBEHAVIOUR-PRESERVING AND THE OTHER IS NOT.  A THIRD SITE WAS FOUND IN THE PROCESS, ON THE LINE\nABOVE, THAT NEITHER SEAT NAMED. ***\n\n**`#511`'s conclusion turned on `tag` being a 32-bit `int`, so the `&` happened at 32 bits and the\nbit pattern was unchanged.  That reasoning must be redone per site, and it does not give the same\nanswer twice.**\n\n**(A) `dev_uninorth.c:70-71` -- THE REPAIR CHANGES BEHAVIOUR, AND FIXES A REAL DEFECT.**\n\n    :56    uint64_t idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len | MEM_PCI_LITTLE_ENDIAN);\n    :70    for (dev=11; dev<32; dev++)\n    :71        if (idata & (1 << dev))\n\n`idata` is **`uint64_t`**, and it is the same variable at both lines (one block, declared at `:56`,\nused at `:71`).  So the `&` is `uint64_t & int`:\n\n    OLD at dev==31:  `1 << 31' is int, 0x80000000, NEGATIVE  ->  sign-extends to 0xffffffff80000000\n    NEW at dev==31:  `1U << 31' is unsigned, 0x80000000      ->  zero-extends to 0x0000000080000000\n\n**Those masks differ, and `idata` really can carry bits 32..63** -- it comes from\n`memory_readmax64` with the access width, and is stored whole into `d->cur_addr` (a `uint64_t`).\n**So under the CURRENT code a guest write with any bit in 32..63 set matches spuriously at dev==31\nand the scan breaks on the wrong device.**  The `1U` repair does not merely remove UB there; it\ncorrects a guest-visible mis-decode.\n\n*This is exactly the case the `#511` pass-2 seats warned about in the abstract -- glm, minimax,\ndeepseek and the kimi substitute all raised the wider-operand sign-extension scenario -- and it is\nREAL here even though it was not real in `#511`.*\n\n**(B) `dev_v3.c:164-165` -- THE REPAIR IS BEHAVIOUR-PRESERVING.**\n\n    :161   relative_addr &= 0xfffff;\n    :162   relative_addr |= ((d->lb_map0 & 0xfff0) << 16);\n    :164   for (dev=24; dev<32; dev++)\n    :165       if (relative_addr & (1 << dev))\n\n`relative_addr` is `uint64_t` (the `DEVICE_ACCESS` macro), so the same sign-extension happens -- but\nby `:164` its **bits 32..63 are all ZERO**: `:161` masks to 20 bits and `:162` ORs in a value that\ncannot exceed bit 31.  The extra ones in `0xffffffff80000000` therefore meet only zeros, and the\nresult is identical either way.  **Still worth repairing -- the shift itself is UB regardless of what\nthe `&` does with it -- but nothing observable moves.**\n\n*** (C) A THIRD SITE, ON `dev_v3.c:162` ITSELF, WHICH NEITHER SEAT NAMED AND MY OWN CENSUS ALSO\nMISSED. ***\n\n    :63    uint16_t  lb_map0;\n    :162   relative_addr |= ((d->lb_map0 & 0xfff0) << 16);\n\n`lb_map0` is `uint16_t`, which INTEGER-PROMOTES to `int`; `0xfff0` is an `int`; so the `&` yields an\n`int` and the `<< 16` is a signed shift.\n\n    0xfff0 << 16 = 0xfff00000 = 4293918720      INT_MAX = 2147483647      OVERFLOWS\n\n**It overflows whenever bit 15 of `lb_map0` is set** -- i.e. for half the values the register can\nhold -- and `lb_map0` is GUEST-WRITABLE at `:212` (`d->lb_map0 = idata`).  Repair: `0xfff0U`, or\ncast the operand.\n\n*My census could not see this one either, for a different reason than the `1 << 31` misses: the\nshift count here is a CONSTANT (16), so a filter keyed on \"shifted by a VARIABLE\" -- which mine was\n-- excludes it by construction.  **Two filters, two blind spots, both of my own choosing.**  The\nhonest form of this row remains what it already said: HONESTLY PARTIAL.*\n\n**STATUS: three verified members, none repaired.**  Each is a one-token change but (A) has a\nguest-visible consequence and therefore owes a witness of its own, while (B) and (C) are\nlanguage-level repairs.  They are NOT bundled: (A) is a defect round, (B)/(C) are hygiene."
  },
  {
   "id": "bugfilereverse",
   "sev": "med",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "2026-09-07",
   "title": "check_bugfile_sync.py is one-directional on a warrant that has since decayed: 33 OUTSTANDING_BUGS headings have no ledger row",
   "witness": "none",
   "entries": [],
   "commits": "",
   "state_note": "`check_bugfile_sync.py` verifies that every OPEN LEDGER ROW is findable in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md.  It does NOT verify the reverse, and its docstring says why:\n\n    \"The reverse direction is already clean (no OUTSTANDING_BUGS header lacks a ledger\n     row), which is what the es438 fix bought.  This closes the other direction.\"\n\n*** THAT SENTENCE IS NOW FALSE.  Measured 2026-09-07: 190 headings, 398 ledger rows, and **33 headings with no ledger row** -- TEN of them filed by me tonight. ***\n\n> This is the carrier's own **absence-record-is-time-indexed** class, on a checker rather than on a mutant: *an absence record must NAME ITS WARRANT, and any edit touching that warrant RE-RUNS THE DEATH MEASUREMENT.*  The warrant is named -- honestly and in the right place -- and **nothing re-runs it**, so the check's scope silently stopped matching its justification.\n\n**WHY IT MATTERS AND IS NOT SYMMETRY FOR ITS OWN SAKE.**  The checker's own reasoning applies unchanged in this direction: *\"ledger.json is the pipeline's source of truth -- per-seat, per-phase, machine-read by gen_dashboard.py and check_stage_panels.py.  A round with no ledger row is invisible.\"*  A row filed only in OUTSTANDING_BUGS gets no dashboard cell, no stage tracking, and no fable-queue visibility.  **`armbfxwidth32` -- an active round with a measured witness -- was in exactly that state until this row was written.**\n\n**SCOPE IF IT IS BUILT:** the same shape as the existing check -- open items only, forward-only from a dated cutoff, so the 23 PRE-EXISTING headings are reported and not failed.  A rule that retro-fails 23 rows blocks the next commit on reconstruction nobody scheduled, and *a rule that does that gets switched off*.\n\n*Not built here: it changes what every commit must satisfy, and owes two negative controls -- red on a heading with no row, green once the row exists.*"
  },
  {
   "id": "ubsanshiftlane",
   "sev": "med",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "2026-09-07",
   "title": "a UBSAN differential would pin the shift-UB class that a guest-value probe provably cannot see",
   "witness": "none",
   "entries": [],
   "commits": "",
   "state_note": "Filed 2026-09-07 from `#510`'s pass 2.  Full record in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md under the same id.\n\ngrok showed two one-line regressions that re-open real UB and score FULL MARKS on a 12-row guest-value probe, and the reason is STRUCTURAL: both are DEFINEDNESS repairs, and definedness has no guest-visible value on a non-trapping host.  `1 << 31' and `1U << 31' give the same mask after the `- 1'; with a correct 32-bit mask `x |= ~mask' is `x |= 0'.\n\n**build-asan already carries `-fsanitize=address,undefined`** -- established AFTER I wrote \"unchecked\" about it in the probe header, one grep from the answer.  So the class IS pinnable, as a DIFFERENTIAL: fixed source produces no shift diagnostic, the `1U'->`1' mutant produces one.\n\n**Not bolted onto #510**: a second binary and a STDERR oracle is a new measurement lane, and #510's pass 2 had already run.  A lane added after a pass is a lane nobody reviewed.\n\nCovers the same species at [[lcashiftub]], [[sgirebufshiftub]], m88k_rot:513, cpu_mips_instr.c:1757 and the two is_userpage sites -- EIGHT sites across three CPUs, none directly visible to a value oracle.\n\n*** THE FILED QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND THE ANSWER COMES WITH A FREE VEHICLE: THE PROBE SHIPPED FOR\n[[lcaconfbus1kill]] ALREADY EVALUATES THIS EXPRESSION ON EVERY RUN. ***\n\nThis row asks *\"whether a UBSan sweep already flags it, and if not, why not.\"*  Both halves settled\nby reading, 2026-09-07.\n\n**UBSAN IS PRESENT.**  `build-asan/Makefile:9` carries\n`-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g`.\n\n**BUT THE SWEEP CANNOT REACH THIS SITE, AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.**\n`gate_asan_sweep.sh` runs, per machine type and subtype:\n\n    timeout 25 stdbuf -o0 -e0 \"$bin\" -V -E \"$t\" -e \"$s\" \"$DUMMY\" </dev/null\n\nThat is a **CONSTRUCTION** sweep -- it builds each machine and lets it start.  The shift lives in\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s device-scan loop, which runs only when a guest performs a PCI\nCONFIG-SPACE ACCESS.  **Constructing `alphabook1` never executes that loop**, so no amount of\nsweeping flags it.  *This is the witness-ladder distinction one level down: rung 2 (construction)\ncannot reach an access-path defect, which is exactly what the ladder says about registration\nproving presence and not reachability.*\n\n*** AND THE VEHICLE ALREADY EXISTS, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH ACTING ON. ***\n`regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` (shipped 2026-09-07 as the witness for [[lcaconfbus1kill]]) drives a\nguest `ldq` into the PCI config window with `ioc_conf == 0`.  That takes the `bus == 0` branch:\n\n    for (dev=0; dev<21; dev++)\n            if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                    break;\n\nIts control read uses `relative_addr == 0`, so `tag = (relative_addr >> 5) & ~0xff` is **0**, the\n`if` is never true, and **the loop runs all 21 iterations** -- evaluating `0x800 << 20` at the last\none.  `0x800` is `2^11` and a plain `int`, so `0x800 << 20` is `2^31`: **signed overflow, C99\n6.5.7p4, undefined behaviour, on every single run of that probe.**\n\n> **SO THE MEASUREMENT IS: run the existing `lca_confbus_probe.py` against `build-asan/gxemul` and\n> read stderr for a UBSAN shift diagnostic.  No new probe, no new row, no new apparatus.**  The\n> expected diagnostic names `dev_lca.c` and a left-shift of a signed value.\n\n**NOT RUN YET** -- a probe run must not overlap the codex census currently in flight.  Queued.\n\n**IF IT FIRES**, this row gains a measured witness and its one-token repair (`0x800U << dev`) can be\ngraded properly.  **IF IT DOES NOT**, that is itself a finding about\n[[ubsanshiftlane]] -- it would mean the ASAN build is not reporting a shift UB that is provably\nevaluated, and the whole proposed UBSAN differential lane rests on an assumption that does not hold.\n**Either outcome is worth the one run**, which is the argument for doing it before designing the lane.\n\n*Found while answering this row's own filed question rather than by looking for it -- and note the\nirony the row already recorded: this loop is the file's OWN graceful-degradation precedent, cited by\n[[lcabadval]] as a transferable warrant.  The safe path contains the UB.*\n\n*** MEASURED 2026-09-07.  THE UBSAN DIAGNOSTIC FIRES, NAMING THE FILE, LINE, COLUMN, VALUE AND\nSHIFT COUNT.  Artifact: `regress/artifacts/lcashiftub/ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw`. ***\n\n    GXemul> step 5\n    fffffc0001040000:  243f0102    ldah  t0,0x102\n    fffffc0001040004:  a4410010    ldq   t1,16(t0)\n    fffffc0001040008:  a4810000    ldq   t3,0(t0)\n    fffffc000104000c:  a4210008    ldq   t0,8(t0)\n    fffffc0001040010:  a4620000    ldq   t2,0(t1)\n    dev_lca.c:125:21: runtime error: left shift of 2048 by 20 places cannot be\n                      represented in type 'int'\n    GXemul> print t2\n    0xffffffffffffffff\n\n**`2048` is `0x800`; `by 20 places` is the final loop iteration; `dev_lca.c:125` is exactly\n`if (tag & (0x800 << dev))`.**  The diagnostic lands on the guest `ldq t2,0(t1)` -- a real guest\ninstruction through real address decode on an unmodified `alphabook1`, with the empty-slot signature\n`0xffffffffffffffff` printed immediately after.\n\n**NO NEW APPARATUS WAS BUILT.**  This is `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py`, shipped earlier the same day\nas the witness for [[lcaconfbus1kill]], run against `build-asan/gxemul` instead of `build/gxemul`.\nThe control read takes the `bus == 0` branch with `tag == 0`, so the scan loop runs all 21\niterations and reaches the overflow every time.\n\n*** AND THE FIRST MEASUREMENT WAS A FALSE NEGATIVE OF MY OWN MAKING, WHICH IS WORTH MORE THAN THE\nRESULT. ***  Run 1 reported NO diagnostic and I nearly recorded that as a finding against\n[[ubsanshiftlane]].  The cause: **I grepped the PROBE's stdout, and a UBSAN message goes to the\nEMULATOR's stderr**, which lands in the probe's internal pty buffer and is never echoed.  That is\n*exactly* the defect I had fixed hours earlier for the `bus attrib` row -- a green row whose evidence\nnever reaches the artifact -- recurring one level out, on the harness rather than the row.\n\n> **A PROBE ONLY SHOWS YOU WHAT IT CHOOSES TO PRINT.  When you are looking for output the probe was\n> not written to surface, dump the buffer -- do not grep the summary.**  Re-run from a frozen COPY\n> in `/tmp` (the shipped probe is not edited for a measurement) with the full buffer echoed, and the\n> diagnostic was in the first ten lines.\n\n*The wrong answer REASSURED, which is this project's recurring signature: \"no diagnostic\" is exactly\nwhat a clean build looks like.*\n\n*** THE LANE'S PREMISE IS CONFIRMED BY MEASUREMENT: THE ASAN BUILD DOES REPORT SHIFT UB AT RUNTIME,\nWITH FILE, LINE AND COLUMN. ***  This row proposed a UBSAN DIFFERENTIAL to pin the shift-UB class\nthat no guest-value row can see, and rested on the untested assumption that `build-asan` would\nactually emit such a diagnostic.  **It does**, and the message is precise enough to key an oracle on\n(`dev_lca.c:125:21`, the value, the shift count, and the type).\n\n**So the differential shape is viable as filed:** fixed source emits no diagnostic for that site, the\n`1U`->`1` mutant emits one.  What this measurement does NOT establish is the other half -- that the\ndiagnostic DISAPPEARS once the site is repaired -- which needs the repaired build and is the\ntwo-direction control the lane owes.\n\n*One incidental observation, recorded and NOT filed: the same run ends with `LeakSanitizer: detected\nmemory leaks -- 3211582 byte(s) leaked in 21 allocation(s)`.  The session ends via `exit(1)` from the\nkill under test, so leaks at abrupt termination are expected and this is not evidence of a defect.*\n\n*** codex, `#511` PASS 2: \"INVOKING THE EXISTING PROBE ALONE IS INSUFFICIENT\" -- AND IT IS RIGHT,\nWHICH CHANGES THIS ROW'S DESIGN. ***\n\n*\"The shipped `lca_confbus_probe.py` `session()` and the top-level evidence-printing code captures\nemulator output but does not score or generally print shift diagnostics.  Future wiring must\nexplicitly expose and check that evidence.\"*\n\n**That is exactly the defect I hit by hand earlier the same day** and recorded as an apparatus\nlesson: the probe captures the whole pty buffer but PRINTS only the rows it chooses, so a UBSAN\nmessage is present in memory and absent from the artifact.  My working measurement used a frozen\nCOPY in `/tmp` with the buffer dumped -- **not the shipped probe.**\n\n> **SO THIS LANE'S RECIPE IS NOT \"point the existing probe at build-asan\".**  It owes an explicit\n> SCORING step: a row whose oracle is the presence or absence of a shift diagnostic in the captured\n> buffer, printed into the artifact so a later reader can check it.  Without that the lane would\n> pass by construction -- a green run that never looked.\n\n*That is this project's check-that-cannot-fail shape, and it would have been built into the lane's\nfirst version if codex had not named it.*"
  },
  {
   "id": "ledgersevmissing",
   "sev": "med",
   "state": "dropped",
   "opened": "2026-09-07",
   "title": "tools/pipeline/ledger.json: 261 rows carry NO `sev` field, 78 of them open or in work -- so a severity-sorted triage omits them or dies, and rows are STILL being created without it",
   "witness": "harness: the survey that found it -- sorting the open queue by severity raises KeyError",
   "entries": [],
   "commits": "",
   "state_note": "*** WITHDRAWN 2026-09-07, SAME DAY IT WAS FILED: THIS IS A DUPLICATE OF [[queueunrated]], WHICH WAS OPENED EARLIER ON THE SAME DATE AND IS THE BETTER ROW. ***\n\nI filed it after a severity sort of the ledger died on a KeyError, wrote it up with a deriver and a proposed fix, filed it in BOTH OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md and here -- and never checked whether the class was already open.  It was.  `queueunrated` had the same finding with sharper numbers and a better diagnosis.\n\n**WHAT THE ORIGINAL HAS THAT MINE DID NOT:** the count that actually matters is not rows-missing-a-field, it is **73 rows that are UNRATED AND ALREADY WITNESSED** -- rows that could be worked today and that nothing has ranked.  And it names the trap I was walking into: a keyword scan over titles is a CORRELATE, because what makes a row high here is *a guest can reach it and something bad happens*, and no keyword tests reachability.  That is the deriver rule -- key on what DEFINES the class -- applied to triage, and I had not thought of it.\n\n**WHAT MINE ADDED, folded into the original rather than kept here:** the gap is not a closed legacy block.  Rows are STILL being created without the field today, so a backfill alone refills -- which is why the writer-side check must land BEFORE the rating pass, not after.\n\n*** THE REAL FINDING IS THE PROCESS ONE, AND IT IS THE FOURTH INSTANCE IN ONE SESSION. *** Four times running I re-derived something already recorded: the twelve-of-sixteen colour census and the depth-8 uninitialised-byte disclosure (both in COMMITTED codex artifacts under regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/), the `lin_b diag once' marker gap (filed as [[randompixdiagonce]]), and now this.  **This project's unread-review class has always been about PANEL answers nobody opened; these were committed files and open queue rows.**  The cheap defence is one command before filing anything -- grep the ledger ids and OUTSTANDING_BUGS for the subject -- and I did not run it once."
  },
  {
   "id": "refilingclass",
   "sev": "med",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "2026-09-07",
   "title": "the main loop re-derived FIVE already-recorded findings in one session and filed one as a duplicate row -- the unread-review class, but the unread things are COMMITTED artifacts and OPEN queue rows, not panel files",
   "witness": "harness: the five instances, each with the committed artifact or open row that already held it",
   "entries": [],
   "commits": "",
   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 by the main loop, about the main loop.  **FIVE instances in ONE session**, every one\nof them work that was already recorded, and I found none of them by checking -- I found them by\naccidentally tripping over the record afterwards.\n\n    1  \"twelve of sixteen colour encodings reach the random arm\"   already in a COMMITTED artifact,\n                                                                   regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/\n                                                                   codex_design_20260907.txt, with a full table\n    2  \"depth 8 transmits uninitialised stack bytes\"               already in codex_pass1_20260907.txt AND\n                                                                   already an open row, [[sgirebufuninit]]\n    3  \"`lin_b diag once' does not assert the (once) marker\"       already an open row, [[randompixdiagonce]]\n    4  \"half the queue carries no severity\"                        already an open row, [[queueunrated]] --\n                                                                   and I FILED A DUPLICATE, [[ledgersevmissing]],\n                                                                   into the ledger AND OUTSTANDING_BUGS before\n                                                                   noticing.  Withdrawn same day.\n    5  \"the GET half consumes more bytes than it reads\"            already in [[sgirebufuninit]]'s own note,\n                                                                   almost verbatim\n\n**THIS IS THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, BUT NOT THE VERSION THE CARRIER DESCRIBES.**  That class is about\nPANEL ANSWERS NOBODY OPENED -- a seat file sitting in a panel dir.  Every instance here is different and\nworse: the knowledge was in **committed files** and **open queue rows**, i.e. in the two places this\nproject treats as its own memory.  Instance 4 is the sharpest, because the duplicate row PASSED THE\nPRECOMMIT GATE and would have sat in the queue indefinitely as a second entry for one defect.\n\n**THE COST IS NOT ONLY TIME.**  Instance 1 and 2 consumed a codex invocation on questions a committed\nartifact had already answered -- against a standing directive to spend codex on the queue.  And a\nduplicate row inflates the backlog the owner is trying to prioritise, which is the opposite of what\n[[queueunrated]] is for.\n\n*** WHAT MAKES IT A CLASS RATHER THAN CARELESSNESS: THE INVESTIGATION IS WHAT HIDES THE RECORD. ***\nIn every instance I reached the finding by READING SOURCE, and a source reading gives no signal that a\nrow exists.  The record lives under a row id I would only search for if I already suspected the answer.\nInstance 3 is the clean demonstration: I derived the `(once)` gap from the probe, wrote it up as new,\nand the row `randompixdiagonce` had been filed EARLIER THE SAME DAY under a name I would never have\nguessed from the code.\n\n**PROPOSED STRUCTURAL FIX, and it is deliberately two things because they catch different halves:**\n\n* **(a) WRITER-SIDE, mechanical -- a duplicate check on ADDED ledger rows.**  When a commit adds a row,\n  compare its title's content words against every open/in-work row's title and REPORT the nearest\n  matches for a human to dismiss.  It cannot auto-reject (titles are prose and near-misses are real\n  rows), so it warns; the value is that it fires at the moment of filing.  Forward-only from a cutoff,\n  the `check_cited_artifacts` idiom.  **Owes two negative controls -- a genuine near-miss pair must NOT\n  be reported as a duplicate, and an actual duplicate MUST be -- so it ships as its own harness round,\n  not as a rider.**\n* **(b) READER-SIDE, a habit with a command -- before investigating a device or a probe, list what is\n  already open against that FILE.**  This is the half that would have caught instances 1, 2 and 5,\n  which were never going to be caught at filing time because I never filed them.  The command is one\n  line and the artifacts directory is the other half of it:\n\n      grep -l \"<file>\" regress/artifacts/*/*.txt\n      python -c \"...print rows whose title or note names <file>...\"\n\n**HONEST LIMIT: (b) IS PROSE, AND THIS FILE'S OWN FINDING IS THAT PROSE DOES NOT FIRE.**  I am not\nclaiming it will.  It is recorded because (a) genuinely cannot catch instances 1, 2 and 5 -- there is no\nfiling event to hook -- and saying \"no structure reaches this half\" is more useful than pretending a\nhabit is a control.  If a later session finds a mechanical trigger for the reader side, that supersedes\nthis.\n\n*Severity med, not high: nothing shipped wrong.  Every instance was caught, four of them within the\nhour, and the one that reached the ledger was withdrawn the same day.  The cost is wasted effort and an\ninflated backlog, not a defect in the emulator.*\n\n*** A NEIGHBOURING RECORDS CLASS, MEASURED 2026-09-08: A MULTI-ROUND COMMIT LEFT ALL FOUR OF ITS\nROWS WITHOUT COMMIT ATTRIBUTION, AND ONE OF THEM ALSO KEPT A STALE STATE. ***\n\n`7a41b3f` shipped `#505 #506 #507 #508`.  Afterwards:\n\n    sgirergbroundtrip  #505   state STILL `open', commits None, witness None   <- worst\n    lcaconfwidth       #506   commits None\n    alphaswpctxptbr    #507   commits None\n    alphapfnoffset     #508   commits None\n\n**The `commits` gap is a records defect with no measured decision cost yet.**  The `state` gap DID\ncost one: I read `sgirergbroundtrip` as an open med defect while scoping the `sgirandomcolour`\nround in the same file, and began designing around a defect `#505` had already fixed.  *Those are\ndifferent fields and should not be conflated into one severity.*\n\n**HOW BOTH WERE FOUND, and the order matters:** the state gap by ACCIDENT (opening the source while\nscoping something else); the three commit gaps by then asking the general question --\n*\"which non-closed rows are named by a CHANGELOG round-block heading?\"* -- which is a precise query\nbecause the heading format is fixed (`# \\`#NNN\\` <rowid>: <title>`).  It returned **7 candidates,\nof which 4 were legitimate `in work` rows and 3 were real gaps**, so the query needs\nrow-by-row verification and is not a population claim.\n\n**NO CHECKER IS PROPOSED.**  The carrier's escalation discipline applies: the commit-attribution\ngap has consumed zero decisions, and a check that fires on legitimate `in work` rows would be\nswitched off.  *If a stale STATE ever consumes a second decision, that is the trigger to revisit --\nand this note exists so the count is findable.*"
  },
  {
   "id": "lcaconfbus1kill",
   "sev": "high",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "2026-09-07",
   "title": "dev_lca.c: writing 0x1 to LCA_IOC_CONF is ACCEPTED by its guard and arms a host kill in lca_pci_conf -- `bus != 0` is a bare fatal()+exit(1) five lines below the file's own graceful-degradation precedent",
   "witness": "probe:regress/lca_confbus_probe.py -- rung 3, MEASURED 3/4 on the committed build (witness_HEAD_20260907.raw); the RED row is the defect.  Post-fix half PENDING (no fix chosen yet).",
