    {
     "seat": "fable5",
     "phase": "design",
     "d": "2026-09-09",
     "date": "2026-09-09",
     "note": "ADJUDICATED BY THE MAIN LOOP RUNNING AS FABLE 5.1 (owner directive 2026-09-09: \"proceed with fable for any adjudication\").  This is the adjudicator hat, not an independent seat review; it is recorded as fable5 because that is the seat the hold names.  [A] RULING: a latched decline IS acceptable, AND member 7 (IMPLEMENT) is FILED -- the two are not alternatives.  What makes the decline honest rather than laundering is exactly the filing: 'silently missing' becomes 'explicitly owed'.  Warrant: (i) delivery has NEVER worked on Alpha -- cd.alpha.irq_asserted is written at cpu_alpha.c:218/:223 and READ NOWHERE, and cpu_dyntrans.c delivers for FIVE architectures (ARM/M88K/PPC via irq_asserted, MIPS via COP0, SH via int_to_assert) with NO Alpha arm -- so the decline drops nothing that worked; (ii) the pre-fix state (host death on the FIRST ISA interrupt) is strictly worse for every guest; (iii) the announce NAMES the limitation ('Alpha has no interrupt delivery'), so the record cannot be read as 'interrupts now work'; (iv) IMPLEMENT is a SUBSYSTEM -- dyntrans dispatch, PALcode entry, the IPL check -- and is its own round, filed as `alphaintdeliver'.  AND THE OPTION SET HAD A MEMBER THE PACKET OMITTED (caught by the morning's codex audit): STOP THE GUEST cleanly (cpu->running = 0, the graceful-halt shape in the tree).  REJECTED, for the reason that makes this kill severe: the stub is entered on ORDINARY ISA interrupts (8042, UART, IDE), not on a halt request, so halting on the first one makes alphabook1 unbootable -- strictly worse than declining.  The round's CHANGELOG carries that member and its rejection so no future reader sees a two-membered choice that was really three.  Design hold DISCHARGED.",
     "grader": "main-loop (Fable 5.1)",
