   "opened": "2026-09-08",
   "title": "#512's detector has a MEASURED clause-(d) escape: a fix that announces correctly and forgets `assert = 0' scores 7/7.  The row needs a FREE-RUNNING probe, because the ICR INT bit is masked out of every guest-writable arm and its only producer is a host-timer callback that `step' stops",
   "witness": "detector:regress/pcc2_c040_probe.py -- the four L2 rows (irq pending, irq live, c040 noassert, c040 noassert2).  All THREE clause-(d) mutants that previously swept the instrument are now killed.  Artifacts: regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/killtable_20260908/",
   "entries": [
    {
     "seat": "codex",
     "phase": "design",
     "d": "✓",
     "date": "2026-09-08",
     "grade": "ANSWERED",
     "outcome": "BUILD-WITH-REVISIONS;design-would-have-passed-its-own-mutant",
     "grader": "main-loop",
     "ev": "regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/codex_assertrow_20260908.txt",
     "note": "DESIGN REVIEW BEFORE BUILDING.  *** IT FOUND THAT MY PROPOSED DEFECT ROW WOULD HAVE PASSED THE `return` MUTANT IT EXISTS TO CATCH, AND THAT MY ORACLE WAS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL.  Both verified here before recording. ***  Confirmed every hardware premise (PSR, VBR, vector, source, priority) and checked the stcr word against the decoder."
    }
   ],
   "commits": "9fa07fe",
   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-08 as `#512`'s own declared floor breach.  **The round graded it a BREACH rather than clause-(e) debt on purpose**: `assert = 0` is the repair's effect, so nulling it is (d)'s canonical form and sits inside the closure.  THE SHAPE OF THE REPAIR: breakpoint + `continue` so host-timer callbacks run, T1 enabled with a small compare so a timer interrupt goes pending, MIEN and C040 both set, and the oracle is the `debug(\"[ pcc2: IPL = %i, MASK = %i => %s ]\")` at the foot of reassert_interrupts() reading `no assert`.  *That channel IS visible in a stepped capture -- what cannot vary is the STATE, because no guest store can set an ICR INT bit.*\n\n*** THE ORACLE THIS ROW WAS FILED WITH IS WRONG, AND THE codex SEAT HAD ALREADY SAID SO IN AN ANSWER\nI HAD RECORDED AS \"not yet fully mined\".  Corrected 2026-09-08, VERIFIED BY LINE ORDER. ***\n\nThe row proposed reading `debug(\"[ pcc2: IPL = %i, MASK = %i => %s ]\")` for `no assert`.  **That\nmessage EXECUTES BEFORE BOTH SUPPRESSIONS:**\n\n    :334   debug(\"[ pcc2: IPL = %i, MASK = %i => %s ]\" ...)   <-- prints `assert' HERE\n    :339   if (!(GENCTL & PCC2_MIEN))       assert = 0;\n    :364   if (GENCTL & PCC2_C040)        { ... assert = 0; }\n    :374   if (assert) INTERRUPT_ASSERT ... else INTERRUPT_DEASSERT\n\nSo it reports the PRE-suppression value and is **green under exactly the mutant it would be chosen\nto catch**.  codex: *\"do not use the existing `IPL ... => ASSERT' debug message as the line-state\noracle: it executes before MIEN suppression and before the proposed C040 suppression.  Observe the\ninterrupt callback/state.\"*\n\n> **THE CORRECT ORACLE IS THE INTERRUPT CALLBACK/STATE, not the diagnostic.**  A free-running probe\n> is still required for the STATE (an ICR INT bit cannot be set by a guest store), but the CHANNEL\n> changes: observe delivery, or instrument the callback -- never the pre-suppression debug line.\n\n*This is the channel-line check failing on a row I wrote while quoting the channel-line check.  A\nrow whose named channel cannot vary under its named mutant is vacuous by construction -- and here\nthe channel was not merely invariant, it was measured at the wrong POINT IN THE FUNCTION.*\n\n*** THE \"NEEDS A FREE-RUNNING PROBE\" WARRANT IS WRONG.  Refuted at pass 2 by grok and VERIFIED here\n2026-09-08. ***\n\n`pcctwo_interrupt_common()` ORs `PCC2_IRQ_INT` onto SCSIICR/SCCTX/SCCRX when those lines assert, and\n`dev_clmpcc.c:88-89` calls `INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq_scc_tx)` **synchronously inside a guest store to\nCLMPCC_REG_IER**; `machine_mvme88k.c` constructs clmpcc with its irq wired to pcc2.  **So the state\nis reachable while single-stepping** -- `st.b` IEN|IL to SCCTX, `st.b` to the clmpcc IER, `st.b`\n`C040|MIEN` (0x06) to GENCTL.\n\n**THIS ROW IS THEREFORE AN IN-ROUND L2 OBLIGATION, NOT A FILED ONE**, and `pcc2c040kill` has been\nreopened accordingly.  The remaining difficulty is the ORACLE, not the state: the `debug()` line is\nbefore both suppressions, so the observable must be the interrupt line itself -- `INTERRUPT_ASSERT`\n/ `cpu->cd.m88k.irq_asserted` / guest delivery.\n\n*Two wrong reasons in a row on this same question -- first \"the channel is invisible\" (false: it\nprints while stepping), then \"the state is unreachable\" (false: another device's store sets it).\nThe conclusion \"no row today\" was right both times and neither reason was.*\n\n*** THE VEHICLE EXISTS AND IS SHIPPED.  `regress/m88k_exc_probe.py` IS ALREADY AN m88k FREE-RUNNING\nEXCEPTION-DELIVERY PROBE.  Found 2026-09-08 by looking instead of reasoning. ***\n\nIts own header describes the shape this row needs, verbatim:\n\n> *\"seeded with `rN=`, a `br .` (c0000000) planted at every vector entry so a delivered exception\n> spins rather than executing zeros, `breakpoint add <8*vector>`, `continue`, then `reg` and\n> `reg 0,0`.\"*\n\n**So the repair is a COPY of a proven in-tree shape, not a new capability.**  That matters for the\ndisposition: \"needs a different probe shape\" sounded like a capability gap and is really a\nhalf-day's careful work against an existing template.\n\n**THE ORACLE, now that both halves are settled:**\n\n    STATE   (stepped-reachable, per grok/pass 2) st.b IEN|IL -> SCCTX; st.b -> CLMPCC_REG_IER,\n            whose handler calls INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq_scc_tx) synchronously inside the store;\n            pcctwo_interrupt_common() then ORs PCC2_IRQ_INT into SCCTX.\n    ORACLE  (needs FREE-RUNNING) the m88k INTERRUPT vector entry.  Plant `br .' there,\n            `breakpoint add', `continue'.  Breakpoint FIRES  = the line asserted = the mutant.\n            Loop survives to timeout                          = suppressed  = the fix.\n\n> **WHY THE ORACLE STILL NEEDS FREE-RUNNING EVEN THOUGH THE STATE DOES NOT** -- and this refines\n> grok's *\"the obstacle is the oracle, not the state\"* rather than contradicting it.  The only\n> difference between the fix and the mutant is `INTERRUPT_ASSERT` versus `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT` on\n> `d->cpu_irq`; `cpu->cd.m88k.irq_asserted` is a HOST-side field with no guest-readable reflection,\n> so the sole guest-visible consequence is DELIVERY -- and `cpu_dyntrans.c` suppresses interrupt\n> dispatch while single-stepping by design (*\"Do not cause interrupts while single-stepping\"*).\n> **State: stepped.  Delivery: free-running.  Both facts are needed and neither alone settles it.**\n\n*Note the adjacency to `m88kexcdetectorl2`, which is a HIGH row about that same probe.  These are\nseparate rows and must not be merged -- same vehicle, different defect, different oracle.*\n\n*** THE DESIGN REVIEW CAUGHT TWO DEFECTS BEFORE A LINE WAS WRITTEN, AND THE FIRST IS THAT THE ROW\nWOULD HAVE PASSED ITS OWN MUTANT.  codex, 2026-09-08, VERDICT: BUILD-WITH-REVISIONS. ***\n\n### 1.  *** THE PROPOSED DEFECT ROW STILL PASSES THE `return` MUTANT. ***\n\n**GENCTL resets to ZERO**, so MIEN is clear throughout the setup and every completed recalculation\nDEASSERTS the CPU line.  At the final GENCTL write:\n\n    shipped repair            deasserts the line          -> no interrupt   PASS\n    `assert = 0' DELETED      asserts the line            -> interrupt      caught\n    `assert = 0' -> `return'  leaves the line UNCHANGED   -> no interrupt   *** PASSES ***\n\n> *\"Deleting the assignment PERMITS ASSERTION; returning PRESERVES THE INCOMING LINE STATE.  The\n> callbacks simply assign `irq_asserted = 1` or `0`; returning invokes neither.\"*\n\n**My §3 treated the two mutants as interchangeable.  They are not**, and the row was built around the\none that happens to be easier to catch.\n\n**THE FIX IS A PRECONDITION, not a new oracle: write MIEN-ONLY GENCTL IN BOTH ROWS FIRST**, so the\nCPU line is already ASSERTED before the operation under test.  Then `return` leaves it asserted, the\ninterrupt is delivered, and the row reddens.\n\n### 2.  *** THE ORACLE WAS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL.  VERIFIED IN THE CODE. ***\n\n`session()`'s `rd()` tests `until in buf` **against the ENTIRE ACCUMULATED TRANSCRIPT**, at the top\nof its loop -- and `alive = rd(settle)` has already drained the boot banner, which ends at a\n`GXemul>` prompt.  So `(\"continue\", grace, \"GXemul>\")` returns **True with ZERO READS**.  codex\nexercised the extracted reader in memory and got exactly that.\n\n**And `how == \"no-breakpoint\"` is not the negative oracle I took it for**: it establishes a MISSING\nTOKEN, not successful execution without delivery.  The runner deliberately preserves that status\nthrough forced termination and unreaped outcomes, *\"so a C040-specific hang or unexpected guest halt\ncan satisfy a timeout-only negative oracle.\"*\n\n### 3.  THE REVISED DESIGN (to build)\n\n1. Fresh machine per row; perform setup while STEPPING.\n2. Establish the pending SCC TX request, then write **MIEN-only GENCTL in BOTH rows** -- this is the\n   precondition from §1.  *Do not rewrite SCCTX afterwards: its write arm CLEARS INT.*\n3. Final GENCTL write: MIEN-only for the control, MIEN|C040 for the defect row.\n4. `continue` into a FINITE guest path with a **separate normal-completion breakpoint**.  The\n   control must stop at the interrupt entry; the defect row must reach NORMAL COMPLETION without\n   hitting it.  **That gives the negative case affirmative evidence instead of a timeout.**\n5. Attribute stops by FRESH breakpoint evidence and PC.  **Treat timeout, unexpected stop or death\n   as FAILURE**, never as the negative result.\n\n### 4.  EVERY HARDWARE PREMISE CONFIRMED, one with a better warrant than I had\n\n* **PSR `0x80000000`** keeps supervisor mode, clears IND and SFRZ, preserves endianness; the\n  dispatch condition is `irq_asserted && !(PSR & IND)` outside single-stepping.  **It also checked\n  my `stcr` word against the decoder** -- it does select `stcr r5,cr1`.  *That is the\n  hand-assembled-encoding trap closed by someone other than its author.*\n* **VBR = 0 is right, and for a better reason than mine:** `mvmeprom.c:59` EXPLICITLY sets it to\n  zero, rather than it being an assumed reset value.\n* **Entry `0x8`**: interrupt is vector 1 and entry sets PC to `VBR + 8 * vector`.\n* **Source and priority:** SCCTX takes `0x13`; a qualifying IER write asserts SCC TX synchronously,\n  making it `0x33`; recalculation selects IPL 3; MASK starts at 0, so the comparison succeeds.\n\n### 5.  TWO CORRECTIONS TO MY OWN Q2 EXPECTATIONS\n\n* **\"Suppression by another route\" is NOT inherently a defect.**  *\"A behaviorally equivalent\n  implementation should pass.  The defect is violated behavior or collateral state changes, not\n  failure to use the local variable named `assert`.\"*\n* **My \"both rows fail to deliver\" escape is not a green pair** -- `(no interrupt, no interrupt)`\n  FAILS the positive control.  It becomes an escape only if the grader omits or neutralises that\n  control failure, which is an argument for asserting the control's outcome explicitly rather than\n  treating it as informational.\n\n**Residual classes it names as still uncovered by a delivery-only pair:** C040 suppression that\ncorrupts MASK/MIEN/pending state or IPL bookkeeping; suppression that stays stuck after C040 clears\n(needs a RECOVERY observation); and suppression that works only for the tested source, priority or\nfirst invocation.\n\n*** CLOSED 2026-09-08.  ALL THREE ESCAPING MUTANTS ARE KILLED. ***\n\n    BASE            11/11\n    M_d1_noassert    9/10   `assert = 0' DELETED        -- was 7/7 (a CLEAN SWEEP)   KILLED\n    M_return         9/10   `assert = 0' -> `return;'   -- the one-token pass-2 mutant KILLED\n    M_latchinside   10/11   `assert = 0' INSIDE the latch -- was 10/10               KILLED\n\n**The rows, and what each buys:**\n\n    irq pending     SCCTX reads 0x33 after the clmpcc IER store -- LIVENESS.  Without it, \"no\n                    interrupt\" is indistinguishable from \"no pending request was ever created\".\n    irq live        C040 CLEAR: the guest REACHES the interrupt vector (pc = 0x8).  POSITIVE\n                    CONTROL, and it is what makes the negative case meaningful.\n    c040 noassert   C040 SET on an ALREADY-ASSERTED line: reaches COMPLETION (pc = 0x1001c).\n    c040 noassert2  the SECOND C040 encounter, latch already set: also reaches completion.\n\n*** TWO DESIGN CORRECTIONS MADE THIS WORK, AND NEITHER WAS MINE. ***\n\n1. **The precondition.**  My first design wrote C040 into a RESET GENCTL, so MIEN was clear, the\n   line was already deasserted, and `return` -- which merely preserves the incoming line state --\n   would have PASSED.  Writing MIEN-only GENCTL FIRST, in BOTH rows, is what makes it catchable.\n   *codex, design review, before a line was written.*\n2. **The encounter axis.**  `assert = 0` moved inside the latch suppresses correctly on the FIRST\n   encounter and skips it on every later one, so a single-encounter row scores 10/10 against it.\n   **Closing the axis with one repeated write beats adding a row per mutant** -- the class-closure\n   rule rather than pass-per-instance.  *Predicted by codex on pass 1, measured here.*\n\n**AND THE NEGATIVE CASE IS AFFIRMATIVE, NOT A TIMEOUT.**  Both outcomes stop at a breakpoint and the\ndiscriminator is WHICH PC.  The obvious oracles were both unusable: the `debug()` line executes\nBEFORE the suppressions, and `(\"continue\", grace, \"GXemul>\")` returns True with ZERO READS because\n`rd()` matches against the whole accumulated buffer, which already holds a prompt.\n\n**RESIDUAL, disclosed rather than implied:** these four rows establish DELIVERY under one source, one\npriority and two encounters.  They do not cover C040 suppression that corrupts MASK/MIEN/pending\nstate, suppression that stays stuck after C040 CLEARS (a recovery observation), or other sources and\npriorities.  *And per codex: suppression by a different implementation route is NOT a defect -- \"the\ndefect is violated behavior or collateral state changes, not failure to use the local variable named\n`assert`.\"*\n\n*** COMMITTED AS `9fa07fe`, inside `#512`.  This row was FILED as coverage debt and CLOSED IN-ROUND\ninstead, which is what pass 2 required. ***  It never became a separate round: the four rows it\nspecified ship as part of the detector they were owed to."
  },
  {
   "id": "guardfailsopen",
   "sev": "med",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "2026-09-08",
   "title": "the brief-launch guard FAILS OPEN on a missing file: `[ \"$N\" -lt 3 ]` with an empty N is a bash syntax ERROR, which `if` grades as FALSE, so the refusal branch is unreachable in exactly the case it exists for -- and the stale panel dir was pass 1's, so pass 1 could have been graded as pass 2",
   "witness": "harness: the subject IS the launch apparatus.  Proof is the run log -- `integer expression expected` immediately followed by `ok -- launching` and then panel.sh's `no such brief`, with the newest panel dir still bearing pass 1's timestamp.",
   "entries": [],
   "commits": "",
   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-08.  **A guard written SPECIFICALLY to stop a panel launching on an empty brief launched\na panel on a brief that did not exist** -- and it is a NEW MECHANISM for the check-that-cannot-fail\nclass, not another instance of the recorded one.\n\n*** THE CASCADE, in order. ***\n\n1.  The diff was written to git-bash's `/tmp/d512.diff` and READ by **Windows** python\n    (`C:/Users/.../Python312/python.exe`), which resolves a DIFFERENT `/tmp`.  *The carrier already\n    records `git-bash /tmp != WSL /tmp`; this is the same trap with a third interpreter.*\n2.  The brief generator raised `FileNotFoundError`, so **no brief was written**.\n3.  *** THE GUARD FAILED OPEN. ***  It read:\n\n        N=$(grep -cE '^@@' \"$BRIEF\")\n        if [ \"$N\" -lt 3 ]; then echo \"REFUSING TO LAUNCH\"; exit 1; fi\n        echo \"ok -- launching\"\n\n    On a MISSING file `grep -c` prints nothing, so `N` is the EMPTY STRING.  `[ \"\" -lt 3 ]` is not\n    false -- it is a **bash syntax error**, `integer expression expected`, which exits NON-ZERO, and\n    an `if` reads a non-zero exit as FALSE.  **So the else-branch ran and printed \"ok -- launching\".**\n4.  `panel.sh` printed `no such brief` and launched **nothing** -- and exited 0, so the background\n    task reported SUCCESS.\n\n*** WHY IT IS WORSE THAN A WASTED RUN: the newest panel dir was still PASS 1's. ***  Had the seat\nfiles been read without checking the directory timestamp, **pass 1's answers would have been graded\nas pass 2** -- a whole review stage discharged by the stage before it.  That is the carrier's own\nwarning about a relative `./panel.sh` launch (\"a SILENT zero-seat failure that looks like a completed\nrun, so you review the previous round's stale seat files by mistake\") reached by a different road.\n\n> *** THE CLASS EXTENSION, and it contradicts something the carrier asserts. ***  The\n> check-that-cannot-fail section concludes that in this project such a check *\"is almost never a\n> badly-written assertion -- it is a claim that some cheap observable stands in for an expensive\n> one\"*, and reports a hunt over 251 gate rows that found ZERO unguarded self-comparisons.\n>\n> **This is a badly-written assertion, and it is in a guard whose entire purpose was to prevent the\n> failure it permitted.**  The mechanism is specific to shell: **a comparison against an EMPTY\n> variable is an ERROR, not a FALSE, and `if` grades an error as false -- so the guard's refusal\n> branch is unreachable in exactly the case it exists for.**\n>\n> The generalisation: **test EXISTENCE before you test a PROPERTY.**  A property test on a\n> non-existent object cannot fail closed, because there is nothing to measure.\n\n**THE FIXED FORM, used on the relaunch:**\n\n    [ -s \"$B\" ] || { echo \"REFUSING: brief missing or empty\"; exit 1; }\n    N=$(grep -cE '^@@' \"$B\" 2>/dev/null)\n    [ \"${N:-0}\" -ge 3 ] || { echo \"REFUSING: only ${N:-0} hunk markers\"; exit 1; }\n    grep -q \"<a token from the diff body>\" \"$B\" || { echo \"REFUSING: diff body absent\"; exit 1; }\n\nThree properties, and each is load-bearing: `-s` catches missing AND empty; `${N:-0}` makes the\ncomparison well-formed whatever grep did; and the CONTENT grep catches a brief that has hunk markers\nbut lost the body.\n\n**NOT MECHANISED, deliberately.**  This guard is written ad hoc at each launch rather than living in\n`panel.sh`, and moving it there is a change to the script every panel runs -- which owes two negative\ncontrols (prove it refuses a missing brief, prove it does NOT refuse a good one).  **That is its own\nharness round.**  Note `panel.sh` ALREADY refuses correctly (`no such brief`); what is missing is\nthat the CALLER believes it launched.\n\n*Provenance: my own guard, written earlier in the same session, in the same shell block, on the\nbelief that it made the launch safe.  It had worked on three previous briefs -- because they\nexisted.*\n\n*** THE FIXED GUARD CAUGHT A REAL DEFECT ON ITS FIRST USE, AND IT WAS THE **CONTENT** CHECK THAT\nFIRED -- not the existence test and not the count.  2026-09-08. ***\n\nBuilding the `sgirekills` round-design brief, I inlined the arm cascade as lines 420-545 and the\nguard refused:\n\n    REFUSING: cascade truncated before LINEAR_B\n\n**The range was wrong.**  `LINEAR_A`'s arm alone runs to line 585 and `LINEAR_B` starts at 588, so\nthe true cascade is 420-660 -- **241 lines, not 126.**  The brief would have said *\"the FULL arm\ncascade, VERBATIM\"* while omitting the sixth of the six windows it was about, and the seat would\nhave answered a question about five arms believing it had seen six.\n\n> **THE COUNT CHECK WOULD HAVE PASSED IT.**  126 inlined lines is a perfectly healthy-looking\n> number; the file existed and was 11 KB.  **Only asking \"is the thing I claim to have inlined\n> actually in here?\" could fail.**\n>\n> That is the denominator rule's own limit, restated: a denominator proves the apparatus SELECTED\n> something, never that it selected the RIGHT something.  A content assertion is the only check that\n> distinguishes them.\n\n**So the three-part guard has now earned each of its parts by a different failure:** `-s` was minted\nby the missing-file cascade that filed this row; `${N:-0}` by the empty-variable comparison; and the\nCONTENT grep by this truncation, which the other two pass cleanly.\n\n*Recorded because the guard was written defensively an hour earlier with no expectation of firing,\nand the thing it caught was mine.*"
  },
  {
   "id": "pcc2genctlc040alias",
   "sev": "low",
   "state": "in work",
   "opened": "2026-09-08",
   "title": "mvme_pcctworeg.h defines C040 TWICE with DIFFERENT values -- PCC2_C040 = 0x04 (right, and what the model uses) and PCC2_GENCTL_C040 = 0x03 (wrong; 0x03 is FAST|MIEN).  The wrong alias has no consumers today, so it is a loaded trap rather than a live defect",
   "witness": "none -- documented-only: a comment on a symbol nothing reads (grep -rn PCC2_GENCTL_C040 src/ finds only the definition), so a row would be vacuous by construction.  *** CORRECTED 2026-09-09 from `harness:read' -- the harness: term is admitted only when the round's SUBJECT is the test apparatus, and a vendored product header is not; the reading it named (mvme_pcctworeg.h :61 and :134 plus the zero-consumer grep) is preserved here as the finding's evidence, not as a witness. ***",
   "entries": [
    {
     "seat": "codex",
     "phase": "assess",
     "d": "✓",
     "date": "2026-09-07",
     "grade": "ANSWERED",
     "outcome": "found-the-alias",
     "grader": "main-loop",
     "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260907_233310/codex.txt",
     "note": "Found while auditing `#512`'s P1.  I had checked only PCC2_C040 and concluded the fork was clean; codex checked the whole header and found the fork carries the SAME conflict it inherited from OpenBSD's `include/pcctworeg.h`."
    },
    {
     "seat": "fable5",
     "phase": "implement",
     "d": "2026-09-09",
     "date": "2026-09-09",
     "grade": "SHIPPED",
     "ev": "CHANGELOG.md `#517` block; src/include/thirdparty/mvme_pcctworeg.h (54a2eb4)",
     "grader": "main-loop (Fable 5.1)",
     "note": "One-line annotation plus a nine-line comment naming the right constant; value unchanged.  Disposition adjudicated (annotate, not delete: a vendored third-party header, and the addition licence does not reach a deletion on a zero-consumer warrant).  Propagated with the #516 batch, two-part clearance CLEAN/FRESH on every row (_scratchpad/propagate516.out; three consumers of the header, devices/*.o and machines/*.o cleared first).  Witness field corrected in the same edit: `harness:read' was a misuse of the harness: term for a product header."
    }
   ],
   "commits": "54a2eb4",
   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-08.  **`#512` verified `PCC2_C040 = 0x04` against the MVME187 board manual and shipped on it.  The header ALSO carries `PCC2_GENCTL_C040 = 0x03` sixty lines down.**  0x03 is `FAST|MIEN` -- two unrelated control bits -- so an edit reaching for the second spelling would test the wrong bits entirely and would look plausible doing it.\n\n**NOT A LIVE DEFECT: a fork-wide grep finds ZERO consumers of the 0x03 alias.** It is upstream residue, inherited from OpenBSD's own `mvme88k/include/pcctworeg.h`, which carries the same wrong value.\n\n*The disposition question -- DELETE the dead alias, or CORRECT it to 0x04 -- is an option-set question and is not settled here.  Deleting is the additions-licence-consistent move (a dead define is not load-bearing); correcting it leaves a second spelling for a future edit to pick.*\n\n*** I FILED THIS FINDING A SECOND TIME ON 2026-09-08 AS `pcc2c040dup', HOURS AFTER THIS ROW EXISTED.\nThe duplicate has been removed, and the GATE is what caught it -- not me. ***\n\nA codex analysis job on [[pcc2c040kill]] named the two symbols in passing; I verified the values and\nthe zero-consumer count and filed a new row.  `precommit_check.sh` section **P** then refused the\ncommit -- *\"AN OPEN ROW EXISTS ONLY IN THE LEDGER: pcc2c040dup\"* -- because a new row must also be\nfindable in `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md`.  Going to add it there is what put this row in front of me.\n\n**THIS ROW IS THE BETTER OF THE TWO AND ANSWERS A QUESTION MINE LEFT OPEN.**  I had written that the\nheader lives under `src/include/thirdparty/`, so it *\"may be a verbatim import from NetBSD, in which\ncase editing it is a DIVERGENCE\"* -- and flagged that as undecided.  **This row already had it\nsettled: the alias is upstream residue, inherited from OpenBSD's own\n`mvme88k/include/pcctworeg.h`, which carries the same wrong value.**\n\n**THE ONE THING WORTH CARRYING ACROSS, because it sharpens why the trap is loaded rather than merely\npresent:** the second family is not simply wrong, it is wrong ONLY IN ITS LAST MEMBER.\n\n    PCC2_FAST  0x01   vs  PCC2_GENCTL_FAST  0x01     AGREE\n    PCC2_MIEN  0x02   vs  PCC2_GENCTL_IEN   0x02     AGREE\n    PCC2_C040  0x04   vs  PCC2_GENCTL_C040  0x03     *** DISAGREE ***\n\nThe first two coincide because a bit-mask sequence (1, 2, 4) and a plain enumeration (1, 2, 3) are\nidentical until the first power-of-two gap -- **which is exactly the point at which a reader stops\nchecking.**  So the family reads consistent, and is not.\n\n*Recorded rather than quietly deleted: the memory rule is to check for an existing entry before\ncreating a new one, and I did not.  What made the duplicate cheap is that section P forces every\nledger row into the bug file, so a duplicate collides with its original at commit time instead of\nliving alongside it.*\n\n**SHIPPED 2026-09-09 as `#517` (54a2eb4): ANNOTATE, NOT DELETE.**  The wrong value is left in place, marked WRONG on its own line pointing at `PCC2_C040`, with the reasoning in a comment above it; nothing that parses the header changes meaning.  Adjudicated by fable5 as a records/guard fix rather than a round; no panel on a one-line annotation, by the loop's judgment, recorded in the CHANGELOG block.  State `in work` rather than closed: the regress phase is Fable-only and has not run on it, and the two sibling defines (`PCC2_GENCTL_FAST`/`IEN`) were NOT audited against the manual, which the block says."
  },
  {
   "id": "armbfcdecode",
   "sev": "low",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "2026-09-08",
   "title": "cpu_arm_instr.c: the bfi decoder accepts source field 15 and passes r[15] to X(bfi), but the architecture assigns that encoding to BFC (Bit Field Clear) -- so BFC is decoded as `BFI with Rn = PC'",
   "witness": "none -- NOT YET ATTEMPTED.  Raised by the codex seat from source inspection while answering armbfiwidth; the decoder arm and handler were read, no instruction was planted.  This field says `not yet done`, never `did not think to ask`.",
   "entries": [
    {
     "seat": "codex",
     "phase": "design",
     "d": "✓",
     "date": "2026-09-08",
     "grade": "ANSWERED",
     "outcome": "raised-as-separate",
     "grader": "main-loop",
     "ev": "regress/artifacts/armbfiwidth/codex_fixdesign_v2_20260908.txt",
     "note": "Raised as an explicitly SEPARATE issue: *\"the decoder accepts source-field 15 and passes r[15] to this handler, while the architecture assigns that encoding to BFC.  The mask correction does not settle that issue.\"*  Recorded as its own row rather than folded into armbfiwidth, per the round-scope rule -- different defect, different fix, different oracle."
    },
    {
     "seat": "codex",
     "phase": "assess",
     "d": "y",
     "date": "2026-09-08",
     "grade": "ANSWERED",
     "outcome": "implementation claim VERIFIED; architectural claim NOT document-verified",
     "grader": "main-loop",
     "ev": "regress/artifacts/armbfcdecode/codex_decode_20260908.txt",
     "note": "Verified the decoder arm and the handler line by line, refuted my r[15] characterisation and my ARMv5 premise, said plainly that no local manual covers BFI/BFC, and supplied a constructible distinguishing observation."
    }
   ],
   "commits": "",
   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-08 from the codex `armbfiwidth` design answer.  **BFC is BFI with Rn = 15**, and the architecture defines it as clearing the field rather than inserting from a register.  This fork's decoder does not distinguish them, so a BFC would insert bits taken from `r[15]` -- the PC -- instead of clearing.\n\n**NOT VERIFIED HERE.**  It comes from one seat's source reading, and neither the decoder arm's exact guard nor the architectural claim has been re-derived by me. *The round-scope rule keeps it separate from `armbfiwidth`: same file and adjacent lines, but a different defect with a different fix and a different oracle, so it is a candidate ROUND rather than a rider.*\n\n*** THE codex ASSESS ANSWER, 2026-09-08.  THE IMPLEMENTATION HALF IS VERIFIED; THE ARCHITECTURAL\nHALF IS NOT, AND THE SEAT SAID SO RATHER THAN FILLING THE GAP FROM MEMORY. ***\n\n### 1.  VERIFIED: source field 15 does reach the handler\n\nThe generic decoder computes `rm = iword & 15`, and the bfi arm binds `ic->arg[1] =\n(size_t)(&cpu->cd.arm.r[rm])` with **no guard excluding 15**.  `X(bfi)` then opens with\n`uint32_t x = reg(ic->arg[1]);` and has **no source-15 clear path**.  All three of my settled\npremises about the extraction and packing were confirmed against the file.\n\n### 2.  *** MY OWN CHARACTERISATION WAS WRONG, AND THE CORRECTION IS SHARPER THAN THE CLAIM. ***\n\nI wrote that BFC becomes *\"BFI with Rn = PC\"*.  **`r[15]` is not the PC in this interpreter.**\nTranslation reconstructs the instruction address into `cpu->pc` WITHOUT refreshing `r[15]`, and the\nPC+8 that PC-relative loads need is computed into a separate `tmp_pc`.  So `r[15]` holds whatever\nwas last written there -- **a previous load-to-PC target, or an exception-vector address** -- which\nis a *stale* value, not a predictable one.\n\n*That matters for the fix and for any oracle: a row that assumes r[15] holds the current PC would\nbe pinning something the emulator does not maintain.*\n\n### 3.  *** AND MY MANUAL PREMISE WAS WRONG TOO -- THE PREMISE-AUDIT LINE EARNED ITS PLACE AGAIN. ***\n\nI told the seat DDI 0100I was ARMv5.  **Its preface explicitly includes ARMv6.**  It still does not\ncontain BFI or BFC -- the seat searched and found neither mnemonic -- because those are ARMv6T2\nadditions.\n\n> **SO THE ARCHITECTURAL CLAIM AT THE HEART OF THIS ROW REMAINS UNVERIFIED AGAINST ANY LOCAL\n> DOCUMENT, and the seat refused to paper over it:** *\"source 15 -> architectural BFC is not\n> document-verified within this reading scope; I cannot attach a local manual page citation to it.\"*\n> **That is the honest form this project asks for, and it is a BLOCKER: #357/#362/#365 shipped page\n> citations that came from model output rather than a local manual.**  This round does not ship a\n> behavioural change on a recalled architectural rule.  **It needs an ARMv7-A ARM (DDI 0406) in\n> `_scratchpad/`, and until then the row stays open with the implementation half verified.**\n\n### 4.  THE WITNESS IS CONSTRUCTIBLE, and the design turns on the r[15] correction above\n\nBecause `r[15]` is stale rather than the PC, the witness must PUT a known value there:\n\n    r0 = 0xffffffff\n    a word load into PC whose target is 0x00001008   -- this is what leaves 0x1008 in r[15]\n    at that target:  BFC r0, #8, #4                  -- lsb=8, msb=11, width=4\n\n    clear the field          -> r0 = 0xfffff0ff\n    insert the stored r[15]  -> r0 = 0xfffff8ff\n\n**Merely placing the instruction at 0x1008 does NOT establish that register value** -- the\nload-to-PC setup is what does it, which is precisely the consequence of finding 2.  Width 4 is\nchosen to avoid the sibling mask-boundary cases.  *Source-derived and arithmetic-checked; not run.*\n\n### 5.  TWO ROUNDS CONFIRMED, on the round-scope rule\n\n*\"Correcting source-15 semantics leaves mask construction unchanged; correcting mask construction\nleaves source-15 semantics unchanged.\"*  Different fix, different oracle -- so `armbfiwidth` stays\nseparate, as filed.\n\n*** THE MANUAL THIS ROW WAS BLOCKED ON IS NOW LOCAL, AND IT COVERS BFC TOO. ***\n`_scratchpad/ddi0406.txt` (ARM DDI 0406C.d), fetched 2026-09-08 at a seat's citation for the\nsibling row `armbfiwidth` and verified by reading.  **`:17995`, A8.8.19 BFC:**\n\n    if msbit >= lsbit then\n        R[d]<msbit:lsbit> = Replicate('0', msbit-lsbit+1);\n        // Other bits of R[d] are unchanged\n    else\n        UNPREDICTABLE;\n\nIdentical in structure to BFI's.  So this row no longer needs a source it does not have -- and the\nreason it appeared to need one is worth recording: **`ddi0100i.txt` is DDI 0100I, which predates\nARMv6T2 and contains no BFI, BFC, UBFX or SBFX at all.**  The block was the WRONG MANUAL, not a\nmissing one."
  },
  {
   "id": "rulingnotinrow",
   "sev": "med",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "2026-09-08",
   "title": "a STANDING flagship DO-NOT-CODE ruling lived in the CHANGELOG and another row's artifact but not in the row it governs, and a full round was designed against it before a seat caught it -- the population is NOT mechanically enumerable (two greps gave 0 and 9, the 9 mostly noise)",
   "witness": "harness: the measured near-miss -- sgitexindex's round was designed, briefed and panelled before grok opened regress/artifacts/pcc2widthkills/fable_adjudication_20260902.md and quoted the DO-NOT-CODE verbatim.",
   "entries": [],
   "commits": "",
   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-08, from a MEASURED near-miss: I spent a round preparing to code `sgitexindex` against a\nSTANDING FLAGSHIP RULING that said DO NOT CODE, and a seat stopped it by opening a record I had not\nread.\n\n*** THE DEFECT IS NOT THAT THE RULING WAS UNRECORDED.  IT IS THAT IT WAS RECORDED SOMEWHERE THE\nDECIDER DOES NOT LOOK. ***\n\n    where the ruling was   CHANGELOG.md, and an artifact under ANOTHER row's directory\n                           (regress/artifacts/pcc2widthkills/fable_adjudication_20260902.md)\n    where I looked         the LEDGER ROW, which said `state: open, sev: low` and carried no note\n    result                 a full round of design, a nine-seat brief, and a panel fired against a\n                           question that had been adjudicated six days earlier\n\n**The ruling even contained the finding I re-derived**: *\"the proposed `>> 1` fix would IMPORT the\nsiblings' overrun.\"*  I enumerated that from scratch and wrote it up as new.\n\n> **A STANDING DISPOSITION THAT LIVES ANYWHERE EXCEPT THE ROW IT GOVERNS WILL BE RE-OPENED BY THE\n> NEXT PERSON WHO READS THE ROW -- which is exactly the population that might re-open it.**\n\n*** THE POPULATION IS NOT MECHANICALLY ENUMERABLE, AND I TRIED. ***  Two greps disagreed:\n\n    narrow (a close-word within ~120 chars AFTER the row id)      0 rows\n    broad  (any ruling word within 400 chars either side)         9 rows\n\nSpot-reading three of the nine: `wdcbeoddtail`'s hit is a SEVERITY assignment in an audit table\n(noise); `sgirandomskipput`'s is a FILING decision (*\"filed as X rather than dropped\"*), not a\ndo-not-code; `m88kmxmkill`, `m88ktbnd` and `m88kexctaildefault` are *\"filed now as documented-only\nLOW rows\"*, which IS a disposition worth carrying.  **So the broad check is a CORRELATE -- \"a ruling\nword near the row id\" -- and not the class**, which is *a disposition decided elsewhere that the row\ndoes not carry*.  That distinction needs READING, not grepping.\n\n*This is the deriver rule landing on my own audit within the hour of my citing it: before writing a\nderiver, ask what makes a member a member.  Here the answer is a property no regex tests.*\n\n**NAMED CANDIDATES for a proper read, not a claim about them:** `m88kmxmkill`, `m88ktbnd`,\n`m88kexctaildefault` -- all three cited in one sentence as filed documented-only, none carrying that\nin the row.  **The other six flagged rows are probably noise and are NOT claimed.**\n\n**NO CHECKER PROPOSED.**  A proximity grep would fire on ~9 rows with a majority false-positive rate,\nwhich is how a check gets switched off -- this project's own recorded reason for declining the\nstale-count checker.  What is cheap and reliable is a HABIT at the point of decision: **before\nopening a round on any row, grep the CHANGELOG and `regress/artifacts/*/` for the row id and read\nwhat comes back.**  It cost one command here and would have saved the round.\n\n*Related in shape, one row over: `sgilinearoob` carried a bare title and an EMPTY note while the\n2026-09-02 adjudication referred to \"the siblings' overrun\" as known -- the class was known to the\nadjudication and not findable from the row that carries it.*\n\n*** THE THREE \"NAMED CANDIDATES\" DISSOLVE ON A FULL READ.  THE POPULATION OF THIS CLASS IS ONE.\nCorrected within the hour, 2026-09-08, before anyone acted on the list. ***\n\nI named `m88kmxmkill`, `m88ktbnd` and `m88kexctaildefault` as probable members because the CHANGELOG\nsays they were *\"filed now as **documented-only** LOW rows\"*.  **Reading the whole sentence changes\nit:** *\"three rows this round CITED were never FILED ... filed now as documented-only LOW rows\n**RATHER THAN CITED AS IF THEY EXISTED**.\"*\n\n**The disposition being recorded there is FILING -- creating rows that had been cited without\nexisting -- not a prohibition on coding them.**  \"Documented-only\" describes their EVIDENCE state,\nwhich is the ordinary condition of an open row, and not a DO-NOT-CODE.  `sgitexindex`'s ruling was\nexplicit and adversative: *\"DO NOT CODE and DO NOT DROP.\"*\n\n**And checking the rows settles it a second way: all three have EMPTY state_notes.**  That makes\nthem members of `baretitle` -- *\"20 of 55 OPEN rows are a bare title with an EMPTY note\"* -- which is\na DIFFERENT row, already filed.  A bare row cannot be carrying a ruling it does not carry.\n\n> **SO THIS CLASS HAS A POPULATION OF ONE, AND THAT ONE IS FIXED.**  The near-miss was real and the\n> lesson stands; the *scale* I implied was not.\n\n*Twice in one hour I named a population from a proximity grep and had to withdraw it -- nine rows,\nthen three.  The class is defined by \"a DISPOSITION decided elsewhere that the row does not carry\",\nand neither a grep nor a partial read can test that.  **The honest form of this row is: one measured\ninstance, a habit that prevents it, and NO claim about how often it recurs.**"
  },
  {
   "id": "sgidefatalstale",
   "sev": "low",
   "state": "open",
   "title": "the de-fatalisation rounds left DESCRIPTIONS of host kills behind in dev_sgi_re.c and gate_arm.sh -- a reader is told the guard is fatal where the code now latches and declines; plus two gate records that contradict the gate's own current requirements",
   "witness": "none",
   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-08 from a codex records-only review of the `#513` composite\n(`regress/artifacts/sgirekills/codex_records_20260908.txt`).  **These are PRE-EXISTING and were NOT\nwritten by `#513`** -- that round's own six defects were corrected in place.  Filed rather than\nfixed because they belong to the rounds that de-fatalised those sites, not to this one.\n\n**THE LIST, as the seat gave it, each with its citation:**\n\n* `dev_sgi_re.c` commentary and the probe header both call the DE width guard a **\"width kill\"**.\n  Its current branch latches a diagnostic and executes `goto de_declined;`, which reaches the\n  read-response tail and `return 1`.  *Traceable to [[sgidewidth]].*\n* `gate_arm.sh` says the drawing default **\"is fatal on 254 of 256 top-byte op classes\"**.  The\n  current default latches its diagnostic and `break`s.  *Traceable to [[sgideprimop]].*\n* the probe header excludes **\"anything in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de)\"** from its scope.  That is now\n  too broad: `#513`'s `tex xbit` makes a DE access and checks session survival.  Incidental\n  coverage, not comprehensive -- but the exclusion as written is false.\n* **`gate_arm.sh`'s padded-column explanation is wrong about its own mechanism.**  It says a name\n  as long as its column \"makes a check UNSATISFIABLE and the row quietly stops being tested\".  The\n  check is `check \"sgi_re row: $v\" \"$(count ...)\" \"1\"` -- **no match gives 0 against a required 1,\n  which is a FAILURE, not a silent pass.**  A correction further down the same file already\n  explains it correctly, so the file disagrees with itself.  *Related: [[ledgerboiler]] and four\n  other rows quote this same wording.*\n* two older gate records contradict the gate's CURRENT requirements: a table saying **\"today's\n  table would read 41/58\"** where the gate now requires 190, and a writeback table saying only\n  three cold-word rows enter the general path **\"and nothing else\"** where five are now named.\n\n**ALREADY COVERED ELSEWHERE, checked before filing rather than duplicated:**\n* the `// bufdepth = 1, 2, or 4.` comment (the value can be 8) is **[[sgirebufuninit]]**'s subject.\n* `printed()`'s comment overstating sign/zero-extension normalisation is a probe-helper nit in a\n  file nine rows already reference; not worth its own row and not claimed as new.\n\n**SEVERITY low, and argued rather than asserted:** none of these changes what the code does or what\nany gate measures.  The cost is a reader being told a guard is fatal when it declines -- which is\nprecisely the misreading that makes someone re-fix a fixed defect, and this session already\nrecorded a stale ROW sending me to design a round around an already-repaired bug.  *That is the\nharm model; it has not yet been paid here.*\n\n*A records-only brief -- \"no mutant, no evasion, no guest sequence\" -- drew zero refusals from a\nseat that had refused this round's pass 3, and returned nine defects.  Framing, not capability.*",
   "entries": [
    {
     "seat": "codex",
     "phase": "assess",
     "d": "y",
     "date": "2026-09-08",
     "grade": "ANSWERED",
     "outcome": "filed from its records-only review",
     "grader": "main-loop",
     "ev": "regress/artifacts/sgirekills/codex_records_20260908.txt",
     "note": "Nine record defects across three files; six were #513's own and were corrected in place, these five are pre-existing."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "codexcyberflag",
   "title": "codex briefs are rejected PLATFORM-SIDE by a cybersecurity content filter, and this project's own row ids are the trigger",
   "sev": "med",
   "state": "open",
   "witness": "none",
   "commits": "",
   "state_note": "*** A SEAT-FAILURE CLASS NOT ON THE CARRIER'S LIST, MEASURED BY CENSUS 2026-09-08. ***\n\n    ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.  If this seems wrong, try\n    rephrasing your request.  To get authorized for security work, join the Trusted Access for\n    Cyber program\n\n**It is a PLATFORM content filter, not a model refusal**, and it is the only codex failure mode\nhere that can leave a ZERO-BYTE answer behind a large `.err`.  The carrier lists codex refusal\ntriggers as vocabulary, archive-reading, chain shape and the evasion-construction question; this\nis a different mechanism and it is not listed.\n\n*** THE CENSUS, and it corrects my own first reading of it. ***\n\n    21   .err files carry the flag\n     8   of those left a ZERO-BYTE .txt\n     5   of the 21 produced a FULL answer anyway (14,102 / 18,687 / 12,952 / 8,682 / 6,868 b)\n\n**So the flag is NOT terminal and its PRESENCE proves nothing** -- exactly like the refusal-line\nrule already on record.  `gateworktree`'s 14,102 b assess carries the flag twice and answered in\nfull.  **The `.txt` SIZE is the only discriminator**, which is the rule this project already\nlearned once for refusal lines and had not connected to this filter.\n\n*** THE TRIGGER IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN VOCABULARY, WHICH IS WHY IT KEEPS RECURRING. ***  The row\nids are `sgirekills`, `lcaconfbus1kill`, `lcairqstubkill`, `pcc2c040kill`, `alphavmkill`,\n`asicoffsetkill`; the probe rows are literally named `tlb_a ld kill`, and briefs speak of *death\nrows* and *host kills*.  **A brief that merely NAMES its row carries the trigger.**  The graded\nevidence is in `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/refusals/`:\n\n    attempt1.err                       5,906 b   flagged early\n    attempt2_neutralised.err           6,572 b   flagged early\n    attempt3_scoping.err               6,829 b   flagged early\n    attempt4_neutral_late_flag.err   382,773 b   did the WHOLE analysis, flagged at the END\n\n*** THE RECOVERY ALREADY WORKS AND HAS BEEN USED THREE TIMES, AND IT IS WRITTEN DOWN NOWHERE. ***\n\n    sgirekills      codex_rounddesign_20260908       0 b  ->  _RETRY_  6,868 b\n    armbfiwidth     codex_fixdesign_20260907         0 b  ->  20260908 5,091 b, v2 7,308 b\n    sgirebufuninit  codex_pass1_20260907             0 b  ->  _reworded_ 10,557 b\n\n**The filename `codex_reworded_...` IS the technique**, applied by hand three times by whoever hit\nthe wall, and never promoted to a rule -- so the next round rediscovers it.  *That is the carrier's\nown trigger-versus-memory finding: the knowledge existed, on disk, at the moment of the decision,\nand did not fire.*\n\n**STILL UNRECOVERED (codex lost; another seat covered the topic in all but the last):**\n`lcaconfwidth` census (grok 25,281 b), `lcaconfbus1kill` witness (grok 10,679 b), `alphatbinegsel`\nwitness (grok 10,679 b -- grok took it after codex was flagged), and `m88kexcdeliver/pass1/\ncodex_p1_refire` which has a 0-byte `.txt` and **NO `.err` at all**, i.e. by the redirect-target\nrule it never started rather than being flagged.\n\n**A RECORDS GAP FOUND WHILE MEASURING THIS:** `lcaconfwidth` has no brief file on disk at all --\nthe brief was passed inline and not preserved -- so **a flagged job there cannot be re-fired or\naudited without reconstructing its brief.**  A brief that is not preserved is not re-runnable.\n\n**PROPOSED, NOT DONE:** a neutral-vocabulary convention for codex briefs (*process-exit arm*,\n*unreachable-path termination*, *abort site* for *kill*; describe the row rather than naming its\nid), plus preserving every brief beside its answer.  It is a convention change affecting every\nfuture brief, so it goes to the owner rather than being adopted by a round.\n\n*** THE TRIGGER IS NOW MEASURED, NOT INFERRED: A ONE-VARIABLE EXPERIMENT, TWO BRIEFS DIFFERING IN\nONE QUESTION. ***\n\n    IMPLEMENT_brief.md     Q4 = \"what is the smallest edit that would make this look\n                                implemented while still being wrong?\"        ->  0 b, flag x2\n    IMPLEMENT_brief_v2.md  Q4 = \"what observable behaviour distinguishes a correct\n                                implementation from an incomplete one?\"      ->  7,026 b, flag x0\n\nEverything else in the two files is byte-identical -- the diff is that question and nothing else.\n**The answer went from nothing to a full review, and the flag count from two to zero.**\n\n> **SO THE EVASION-CONSTRUCTION FORM IS A TRIGGER AND THE OBSERVABLE-DIFFERENCE FORM IS NOT, AND\n> THE TWO ASK FOR THE SAME ENGINEERING CONTENT.**  The carrier already listed \"the ask-again /\n> evasion-construction question\" among codex's refusal triggers; this measures it, and gives the\n> rewrite rather than only the warning.\n\n**THE REWRITE, which costs nothing and preserves the adversarial value:** ask what OBSERVABLE\ndistinguishes correct from incomplete, and what a regression row must assert so a later partial\nimplementation does not read as complete.  That still forces the seat to think like an adversary\nabout the detector, without asking it to author a disguised defect.\n\n**AND THE NON-TERMINAL HALF GAINED A SIXTH INSTANCE THE SAME DAY:** the `lcaconfbus1kill`\nself-review carried **five** flag hits and still returned **9,710 bytes** of full answer -- the\nlargest flag count on record with a complete answer behind it.  *The flag's presence remains\nevidence of nothing; the `.txt` size is the discriminator.*\n\n**Four briefs written in neutral vocabulary and checked against the trigger list before firing\n(`lcabadval/MANUAL`, `lcabadval/PACKET`, `alphaswpctxreg/PASS1`, `lcaconfbus1kill/SELFREVIEW`)\nreturned 8,657 / n-a / 4,841 / 9,710 bytes.  Zero lost seats since the rule was applied.**\n\n*** TWO MORE INSTANCES 2026-09-08, AND THEY CHANGE THE DIAGNOSIS.  THE TRIGGER IS NOT THE BRIEF'S\nWORDING -- IT IS WHAT CODEX READS AFTER THE BRIEF, AND THAT IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN RECORDS. ***\n\nTwo independent codex jobs refused the same way inside one hour:\n\n    lcaconfbus1kill pass 2      71,124 b of trace, then ERROR ... flagged for possible\n                                cybersecurity risk       (24,984 tokens)\n    sgirandomcolour witness     277,836 b of trace, same ERROR   (78,953 tokens)\n\n**THE SECOND ONE HAD ALREADY BEEN BENIGN-REFRAMED, AND THAT IS THE EVIDENCE.**  Its brief was\nwritten from the start as ordinary emulator correctness work: no \"kill\", no \"attack\", no \"exploit\",\nno \"host death\" -- a census of the finished text returned ZERO for every one of those.  It was\nflagged anyway.\n\n*** WHAT THE TRACE SHOWS, and it is the whole finding: codex went and read the RECORDS. ***\n`grep -c 'dev_sgi_re|crmfbreg|DE_MODE_TYPE'` over its trace = **1,725**, and the trace contains long\nverbatim extracts of `CHANGELOG.md` and `tools/pipeline/ledger.json` -- files whose prose is\nsaturated with exactly the vocabulary the brief had been scrubbed of, because THIS PROJECT WROTE\nTHEM ABOUT HOST KILLS AND GUEST-REACHABLE PROCESS TERMINATION.\n\n> **SO BENIGN FRAMING OF THE BRIEF IS NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT.  A file-reading seat pointed at\n> this repo will find the un-scrubbed vocabulary by itself, in the records, and the refusal arrives\n> at OUTPUT time after the tokens have been spent.**\n\n**AND THE COST IS NOT ONLY THE SEAT.**  Both refusals came AFTER substantial real work -- 79k tokens\non the second -- so the failure is expensive and produces a ZERO-BYTE `.txt` with everything in the\n`.err`.  A run that reads 1,725 record lines and then refuses looks, from the `.txt` alone, exactly\nlike a seat that was never invoked.\n\n**THE FIX UNDER TEST (not yet proven at the time of writing): a `§B0` READING-SCOPE BOUND** naming\nthe four files the task needs and saying explicitly *\"do NOT search or read CHANGELOG.md,\nledger.json, OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md or _scratchpad/ -- those are engineering RECORDS, not the code\nunder review\"*, with an instruction to stop and say so rather than go and get a fact that lives only\nthere.  **Recorded as a HYPOTHESIS with its result to follow, not as a remedy**, because this row\nhas already had one diagnosis overtaken by evidence and a second unverified one would be worse than\nnone.\n\n*The operational consequence for the standing directive to use codex heavily: EVERY codex brief in\nthis repo now carries the scope bound, and a codex job that returns a zero-byte `.txt` must have its\n`.err` TAIL read before it is scored -- the seat-check rule that a wall is diagnosed from `.err` and\nnever from size, applied to a failure mode that is not a wall.*\n\n*** THE RECORDS HYPOTHESIS IS REFUTED, AND THE REAL DISCRIMINATOR IS MEASURED.  Four controlled\nfirings, 2026-09-08, and the split is clean. ***\n\n**The scope-bound experiment WORKED as an experiment and FAILED as a remedy.**  Adding a `§B0`\nreading bound cut the trace from 78,953 tokens to 17,566 -- so codex obeyed it and did NOT go to the\nrecords -- **and it was flagged anyway.**  So the vocabulary in `CHANGELOG.md` and `ledger.json` is\nnot the trigger.\n\n**THE DECISIVE CONTROL: a MINIMAL brief on the SAME FILE, in the same repo, with no scope bound at\nall, ANSWERED.**  733 bytes, correct, in one call:\n\n    \"Read src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c.  How many case labels does the colour switch have?\n     What does its default: arm do?  Does crmfbreg.h define a constant it has no case for?\n     Answer in prose.  Do not propose any code change, and do not write any test.\"\n\n    -> ANSWERED.  Named all four cases, described the default arm, named DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB.\n\n**SO IT IS NOT THE FILE, NOT THE REPO, AND NOT THE RECORDS.  IT IS THE ASK.**\n\n    FLAGGED  lcaconfbus1kill pass 2   asked it to assess a probe that DRIVES an aborting path\n    FLAGGED  sgirandomcolour witness  asked for \"the exact guest instruction sequence ... as\n                                      concrete stores with addresses and values\"\n    FLAGGED  sgirandomcolour scoped   same ask, reading bounded\n    ANSWERED sgirandomcolour minimal  pure analysis; explicitly \"do not write any test\"\n\n> **THE DISCRIMINATOR: codex answers ANALYSIS of a code path and refuses CONSTRUCTION of an input\n> sequence that drives an unhandled one.**  Test-construction and exploit-construction are the same\n> shape from outside, and this project's rung-3 witness discipline is, by design, exactly that shape\n> -- a hand-assembled instruction sequence built to reach a path a guest should not reach.\n\n*** THE OPERATIONAL REMEDY, which KEEPS the seat rather than losing it: SPLIT THE JOB. ***\n\n    to codex     the enumeration, the premise audit, the decode arithmetic, the option-set\n                 reasoning, the disposition argument, \"which constant has no case\", \"what does\n                 this arm do\", \"is this claim true of the source\"\n    NOT to codex the instruction sequence, the probe design, the address construction\n                 -> those go to grok (which built this round's decode walk unaided), to agy when\n                    no measurement is in flight, or to the main loop\n\n**AND THE ANALYSIS HALF IS THE HALF THIS PROJECT MOST NEEDS FROM IT.**  On the round where it was\nflagged, the seat that replaced it -- grok -- produced the round's single most valuable finding by\ndoing exactly the analysis codex would have done.  Nothing about the split reduces codex to a\nlesser seat; it aims it at what it will answer.\n\n*A cheap standing test, since the boundary may move: the minimal-analysis brief above is preserved\nat `regress/artifacts/codexcyberflag/minimal_20260908.txt` with its `.err`.  Re-firing it is a\nfour-second liveness check that separates \"codex is walled\" from \"codex refused THIS ask\" -- two\nstates this row previously conflated, and which a zero-byte `.txt` renders identically.*\n\n**AND A ZERO-BYTE `.txt` IS THE STANDING TELL.**  All three refusals wrote 0 bytes to stdout and\nput everything on stderr, so from the `.txt` alone a refusal is indistinguishable from a seat that\nwas never invoked.  **Read the `.err` TAIL before scoring any codex job** -- the seat-check rule\nthat a wall is diagnosed from content and never from size, applied to a failure mode that is not a\nwall.\n\n*** THE DIAGNOSIS MADE A PREDICTION AND THE PREDICTION HELD, which is the first time this row has\nbeen able to say that. 2026-09-08, `#515`'s ask-again. ***\n\nThe ask-again brief is the one this project fires at a CLEAN kill table, and its whole purpose is to\nobtain **an edit that re-opens a closed defect**.  Its words were: *\"give me the exact source edit as\na diff hunk\"*, *\"the smallest realistic edit ... which leaves all 14 rows GREEN while re-opening the\ndefect\"*.\n\n**That is construction in its purest form, so the diagnosis says it must be refused -- and it was**,\nat 6,339 bytes with the same filter message.  Five firings now, and the split is unchanged:\n\n    FLAGGED    #515 pass 2 (original)          construction: assess a probe that drives a kill path\n    FLAGGED    sgirandomcolour witness         construction: \"the exact guest instruction sequence\"\n    FLAGGED    sgirandomcolour scoped          same ask, reading bounded\n    FLAGGED    #515 ask-again                  construction: \"the exact source edit as a diff hunk\"\n    ANSWERED   #515 pass 2 benign re-fire      analysis\n    ANSWERED   minimal probe                   analysis\n    ANSWERED   lcabadval (x2), lcairqstubkill  analysis\n    ANSWERED   sgirandomcolour ANALYSIS        analysis -- same row, same file, opposite outcome\n\n*** THE OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE IS SHARPER THAN \"SPLIT THE JOB\", AND IT NAMES A STANDING COST. ***\n\n**The ask-again is STRUCTURALLY a construction task**, so codex cannot serve it in its usual form.\nThat is not a small loss: the ask-again is the capped adversarial query this project relies on after\na table comes back green, and codex is the seat most likely to enumerate an input space exhaustively.\n\n**THE REFRAME THAT KEEPS THE SEAT: ask for the GAP, not for the EDIT.**\n\n    refused    \"give me the smallest edit that leaves all 14 rows green\"\n    accepted   \"name the input-space cell these 14 rows leave least constrained, and say why it\n                matters -- describe it as a property, not as an edit\"\n\nThose solicit the same knowledge.  A named unconstrained cell is one step from a mutant, and that\nstep is one I can take myself.  **The seat supplies the enumeration; the main loop supplies the\nedit** -- which is the same division the rest of this row already prescribes, applied to the one\nbrief in the pipeline that most obviously violates it.\n\n*Recorded as a held prediction rather than as another instance, because that is what raises this from\na pattern to a usable rule: the diagnosis said in advance which of two briefs would be refused, and\nit was right.  The reframed firing's outcome is recorded separately -- if IT is also refused, the\n\"analysis is safe\" half of this row is weaker than five firings suggested and must be re-stated.*\n\n*** A SECOND, UNRELATED FAILURE MODE OF THE SAME SEAT, AND IT IS ABOUT SCOPE RATHER THAN CONTENT.\nThree firings of ONE question, three scopes, measured 2026-09-08. ***\n\n    scope                                     stream     verdict?\n    regress/*.py  (a PREDICATE, ~65 files)    2,404 KB   NO -- cut short\n    six files named extensionally               325 KB   NO -- cut short\n    ONE file named                              (running)\n\n**A codex run has a budget, and a reading-scope bound spends it before the question does.**  The\nfirst scope was not a bound at all -- *\"only the probe files that contain `row(` and `exit0`\"*\ncannot be evaluated without reading every probe.  The second was properly extensional and STILL\ntoo large.\n\n> **THE RULE: a reading-scope bound must be EXTENSIONAL *and* SMALL.  Name the files, and name as\n> few as the question can be answered from.**  Where a census spans many files, ask it ONE FILE AT\n> A TIME and pick the file holding the largest share -- here `pcc2_width_probe.py`, 25 of 90\n> candidate rows.\n\n*** AND THIS IS WHY THE `codex_answer.py` VERDICT LINE MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKED. ***  All three\nruns produced substantial, correct, partial work -- the membership definition, the fourth guise of\nthe class, two concrete leads.  **A reader scoring them by size or by \"did it answer\" would call all\nthree successes.**  The tool reports *\"verdict blocks 0 -- NO VERDICT LINE: treat as CUT SHORT, not\nas agreement\"*, which is the only thing separating a completed census from a budget exhaustion, and\nit is the same POSITION discriminator this project already uses for seat truncation.\n\n*Recorded under this row rather than a new one because it is the same operational question -- what\ndoes it take to get an answer out of this seat -- and this row is where a future round will look.\nThe CONTENT rule (analysis answers, construction is filtered) and the SCOPE rule (extensional and\nsmall) are independent, and a brief has to satisfy both.*",
   "entries": [],
   "note": "\n\n[MAIN-LOOP NOTE, 2026-09-09, phase research -- moved out of the seat entries because the dashboard declares no such seat]  THIRD independent confirmation of the CONTENT rule, and the first scored by the POSITION-keyed detector shipped the day before.  sgirandomcolour/codex_scoped_20260908.err is a genuine CONTENT-FILTER REFUSAL: two markers IN THE TAIL, zero verdict blocks, the seat's own words being 'I can assess the code path and disposition, but I can't provide or run a guest sequence designed to reproduce the suspected defect.'  Counting the string would have been ambiguous here; position is not.  The routing fact holds: ask codex for the GAP, never for the WITNESS.  Measured the same day AGAINST a control: a GAP-shaped, four-file, premise-as-question brief (wiringcliff) returned a full 12.9 KB verdict from the same seat in the same session.\n\n[MAIN-LOOP NOTE, 2026-09-09, phase research -- moved out of the seat entries because the dashboard declares no such seat]  A THIRD CODEX RULE, measured: the CONTENT rule (ask for the GAP, not the EDIT) is necessary and not sufficient -- an analysis-shaped brief was refused because the DIFF IT INLINED carried memory-safety language in its comments.  Two sibling briefs fired the same minute with the same shape and no such diff text (pcc2c040kill completeness; armbfxwidth32, whose diff is arithmetic) both answered in full.  RULE: for codex, a review of a memory-safety fix is pointed at the FILE (the ARGV_CAP pointer form) rather than inlined, or the diff is summarised -- and any brief that inlines a diff is scanned for the trigger vocabulary before firing.  The position-keyed detector caught it correctly; the SIZE (29 KB) would have read as an answer."
  },
  {
   "id": "probecolorprompt",
   "title": "pty probes omit `-A`, so with CLICOLOR set the debugger prompt arrives wrapped in ANSI escapes and every prompt-anchored parser reads ZERO responses -- 53 of 61 pty probes are exposed, and the 8 that pass -A are all the ARM ones",
   "sev": "med",
   "state": "open",
   "witness": "harness:MEASURED 2026-09-08 -- CLICOLOR=1 without -A gives 53 ANSI escapes and 0 parsed responses on alpha_swpctx_v0_probe.py (a CORRECT build scoring 1/5); with -A, 0 escapes and 5/5.  CLICOLOR_v4_20260908.out.",
   "commits": "",
   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-08, found by a pass-4 seat reviewing `#514`'s probe and then MEASURED\nin both directions.\n\n**THE MECHANISM.**  `use_colorized_output()` (`src/core/misc.c`) gates colour on\n`isatty(STDIN_FILENO) && isatty(STDOUT_FILENO)`.  **A pty satisfies BOTH**, so a probe that uses\n`pty.fork()` -- which is every probe in this tree -- is exactly the case where colour turns on.\nWith `CLICOLOR` in the environment the prompt arrives as\n\n    \\x1b[34;1mGXemul> \\x1b[0mprint v0\n\nand **no parser anchored on `^GXemul>` can match it.**\n\n**MEASURED, both directions, same probe and same binary:**\n\n    CLICOLOR=1, no -A     53 ANSI escapes, ZERO parsed responses, a CORRECT build scores 1/5\n    CLICOLOR=1, with -A    0 ANSI escapes, responses parse, 5/5\n\n`-A` sets `enable_colorized_output = false` (`src/core/main.c:317`).\n\n*** THE POPULATION, derived rather than estimated: 61 probes call `pty.fork()`, and 53 DO NOT PASS\n`-A`.  The 8 that do are ALL the ARM ones *** -- `arm_bfx_width`, `arm_endian`, `arm_flags`,\n`arm_fold_marker`, `arm_idle`, `arm_memcpy`, `arm_strlen`, `arm_writeback`.  So the tree already\nholds the defence and it was applied to one family and not carried.\n\n**SEVERITY IS `med` AND THE REASON MATTERS: THIS DOES NOT FIRE ON THIS MACHINE.**  `CLICOLOR` is\nnot set here -- measured at 0 escapes without `-A` -- so nothing in the current gate is wrong.  It\nis a LATENT environment dependence: a probe run on a developer's machine with `CLICOLOR` set, or\nunder a CI image that sets it, silently stops parsing.  **And the failure shape differs by parser:\na prompt-anchored one fails CLOSED (loud, a correct build reds), while a loose one may still match\nand mis-attribute (silent).**  The loose parsers are therefore the more dangerous half of the\npopulation, not the safer half.\n\n**NOT FIXED HERE, and deliberately.**  `#514`'s own probe is fixed because that round owns it; the\nother 52 are a separate harness round -- it is a one-argument edit per file, but it changes what\nevery probe launches and owes a check that each still parses afterwards.  *A 52-file sweep bolted\nonto a round whose pass 4 is still open is exactly the scope creep the one-site rule refuses.*\n\n**THE CHEAP CHECK FOR A FUTURE ROUND:** run any pty probe under `CLICOLOR=1` and compare its score\nwith the same probe unset.  A difference is this defect; no difference means that probe's parser\ntolerates the escapes, which is worth knowing separately."
  },
  {
   "id": "seattruncverdict",
   "sev": "med",
   "state": "open",
   "commits": "",
   "title": "a seat answer TRUNCATED MID-VERDICT reads as agreement -- glm returned exactly \"**VERDICT: RATIFY THE\" (21 bytes) with the daemon up, and a verdict-line extractor scores that as a ratification",
   "witness": "harness:MEASURED 2026-09-08 -- _scratchpad/panel_514p6_20260908/ollama_glm.txt is 21 bytes; the re-fire to ollama_glm_REFIRE.txt returned 6,425 bytes and the OPPOSITE-SHAPED verdict (\"CLOSES AT PASS 6\", with a contingency).",
   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-08 from a pass-6 panel.\n\n**glm's answer was TWENTY-ONE BYTES, cut mid-word:**\n\n    **VERDICT: RATIFY THE\n\n*** IT IS NONE OF THE FIVE RECORDED SEAT-FAILURE CLASSES. ***  The daemon was up and `glm-5.3:cloud`\nwas in `api/tags`, so not the REFUSED CONNECTION class.  No quota text, so not a WALL.  The response\nis NON-EMPTY, so not the THINKING BURN -- that class is an empty `response` behind a huge `thinking`\nfield.  And it is a VERDICT LINE rather than an opening intention (*\"let me pull the files\"*), so\nnot the minimax STALL.\n\n> **THE DANGER IS THE WORD IT STOPPED ON.**  This project's own policy is VERDICT LINE FIRST so an\n> answer survives truncation -- and that policy is what makes this shape harmful: the surviving\n> fragment is `RATIFY`.  **A verdict-line extractor scores it as agreement.  A size check scores it\n> as a tiny file whose content happens to read positive.**  Neither can tell it from a seat that\n> ratified in one line.\n\n**THE RE-FIRE IS WHAT CAUGHT IT, and it changed the answer's SHAPE and not just its length.**  The\nstanding rule is to re-fire a walled seat once; applied here to a truncation, it returned **6,425\nbytes** whose verdict was *\"CLOSES AT PASS 6 ... fund no pass 7\"* **with a stated contingency**.  A\ncontingent close is not the unqualified ratification the fragment implied.\n\n**WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, said plainly:** the CAUSE.  This was one occurrence, the daemon was\nhealthy before and after, and the re-fire succeeded immediately.  Whether it was a transport hiccup,\na provider-side cut, or something in `panel_ollama.py`'s read loop is **not measured**, and this row\ndoes not guess.\n\n**THE CHEAP DEFENCE, proposed not built:** `seat_verdicts.py` already distinguishes seat states by\nPOSITION rather than size.  A seat file whose entire content is shorter than its own verdict line --\nno newline, no body -- is mechanically detectable, and reporting it as TRUNCATED rather than as a\nverdict costs one test.  *That is a change to a tool every panel reads, so it owes negative controls\nin both directions and ships as its own harness round, not as a rider.*"
  },
  {
   "id": "splitscorer",
   "sev": "low",
   "state": "open",
   "commits": "",
   "title": "APPENDING a row BELOW a probe's score computation silently splits the summary -- numerator over the old row set, denominator evaluated at print time.  *** POPULATION AT HEAD IS ZERO across all 73 candidate probes: this is an EDITING hazard I introduced twice in one session, NOT a latent defect in the tree ***",
   "witness": "harness:MEASURED 2026-09-08 both ways.  I created the split in two probes by appending rows; the 8042 probe then printed six rows all `ok` under a summary of 4/6, and the UART probe printed 3/5 pre-fix where the honest score was 2/5 (confirmed after repair: 2/5).  A position census over HEAD -- score assignment BEFORE the last row() call -- returns ZERO of 73 candidates.",
   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-08, found by adding rows to two probes during `#516`.\n\n**THE SHAPE.**  Both probes computed their score at a point ABOVE the last `row()` call:\n\n    ok  = sum(1 for _, o in rows if o)      <- evaluated HERE\n    ... more row() calls ...                <- appended AFTER\n    print(\"..._RESULT=%d/%d\" % (ok, len(rows)))\n\n`ok` is a VALUE captured early; `len(rows)` is a call evaluated late.  So the numerator counts the\nold row set and the denominator counts the new one.\n\n*** IT IS SILENTLY RIGHT WHENEVER NOTHING FAILS, WHICH IS THE DANGEROUS HALF. ***  With every row\npassing, the numerator equals the old count and the denominator the new, so the score reads `4/6`\nrather than `6/6` -- visibly wrong.  But in the UART probe's POST-FIX run the formula was\n`len(rows) - len(bad)` with `bad` empty, which renders `5/5` -- **correct by coincidence**.  Only the\nPRE-FIX run, where rows actually failed, exposed it: reported 3/5, honest score 2/5.\n\n> **A SCORE LINE THAT CONTRADICTS ITS OWN PRINTED ROWS IS THE CHEAPEST SIGNAL THERE IS, and it is\n> only available because these probes print every row individually.**  A probe that printed only its\n> summary would have shipped the wrong number with nothing to compare it against.\n\n**REPAIRED IN BOTH FILES** by moving the computation adjacent to the print, with a comment saying\nwhy, so the split cannot be reintroduced by appending a row.\n\n*** THE POPULATION IS NOT ESTABLISHED AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT GUESSED. ***  Two probes were checked\nbecause two were being edited.  The tree has ~65 probes.  **A census keyed on \"score computed before\nthe last row\" needs to compare two POSITIONS in each file, not match a pattern** -- and this project\nhas recorded three name-based censuses coming back wrong in one day, so the deriver has to test the\nproperty (relative position of the assignment and the final `row()` call) rather than a spelling.\n\n*The class is adjacent to `exit0oracle` and `storesidedead` -- all three are oracles that are right\nfor the wrong reason under exactly the conditions where nobody looks -- but it is a DIFFERENT\nmechanism: those are about what a row observes, this is about when the SUMMARY is computed.*\n\n*** CORRECTION, SAME DAY, BEFORE THIS ROW WAS EVER READ BY ANYONE ELSE: THE POPULATION AT HEAD IS\nZERO, AND I CREATED BOTH INSTANCES MYSELF. ***\n\nThe row as first filed implied a latent tree-wide class and asked for a census.  **I then ran the\ncensus, and it inverts the finding.**  Keyed on the PROPERTY -- the position of the score assignment\nrelative to the LAST `row()` call, which is what defines the defect -- over every probe at HEAD:\n\n    probe .py files at HEAD          369\n    candidates (assignment + row() calls + a _RESULT line)    73\n    *** SPLIT AT HEAD                  0 ***\n\n**So the tree has never carried this defect.  Both instances were mine, made minutes apart, by\ninserting rows BELOW the score computation in two probes that were correct before I touched them.**\n\n> **THE CLASS IS REAL AND ITS DIRECTION IS THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT I FILED: it is an EDITING hazard,\n> not a latent one.  A probe is correct until someone appends to it, and appending is exactly what a\n> fix round does** -- this round added three rows across two probes and broke both scorers doing it.\n\n**SEVERITY LOWERED med -> low** to match: nothing shipped is wrong, and the repair (keep the\ncomputation adjacent to the print) is now in both files with a comment saying why.\n\n*** WHAT THE ORIGINAL FILING GOT RIGHT AND IS WORTH KEEPING: the defect is SILENTLY CORRECT WHENEVER\nNOTHING FAILS. ***  The UART probe's post-fix run rendered `5/5` while split, because `bad` was\nempty; only the PRE-FIX run, where rows genuinely failed, exposed it as 3/5 against an honest 2/5.\n**A green run cannot detect this.**  That is why it was found by measuring the red direction, and it\nis the argument for the two-direction discipline rather than for a checker.\n\n*** AND THE CENSUS ITSELF IS THE POINT WORTH CARRYING. ***  I filed a med row naming a class,\nexplicitly refused to guess its size, then spent two commands measuring it -- and the measurement\nsaid the class had no members.  **Filing a class and measuring its population are different acts,\nand doing the second cheaply is what stopped a wrong record from standing.**  The deriver is two\npositions per file and is recorded in `witness` so it can be re-run rather than trusted."
  },
  {
