   "id": "sgiretlbshiftub",
   "sev": "med",
   "state": "open",
   "opened": "2026-09-10",
   "title": "dev_sgi_re.c horrible_getputpixel: the tile-base shift `re_tlb_[abc][tile_nr] << 16' is a SIGNED shift of a promoted uint16_t, and every tile pointer the model accepts as VALID (bit 15 set) overflows INT_MAX -- undefined behaviour on the only path that works",
   "witness": null,
   "entries": [
    {
     "seat": "codex",
     "phase": "assess",
     "d": "2026-09-10",
     "date": "2026-09-10",
     "grade": "FOUND-WHILE-VERIFYING",
     "ev": "regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_coverage_20260910.txt",
     "grader": "main-loop (Opus 5)",
     "note": "FILED 2026-09-10, found by the codex seat under the found-something-else licence while answering a TEST-DESIGN question about #519's row coverage -- it was checking whether a tiled PUT reaches a given physical word and noticed the shift on the way.  VERIFIED BY READING before filing.\n\n`dev_sgi_re.c` declares `uint16_t re_tlb_a[256]', `re_tlb_b[256]', `re_tlb_c[256]', and `horrible_getputpixel()' computes the tile base as `tileptr = d->re_tlb_a[tile_nr] << 16;' (anchor `case 0:\ttileptr = d->re_tlb_a[tile_nr] << 16;', and the same at the b/c arms).  A `uint16_t' promotes to `int' under the usual arithmetic conversions, so this is a SIGNED shift: any entry with bit 15 set gives at least 0x8000 << 16 = 2147483648, which exceeds INT_MAX (2147483647).  Signed left-shift overflow is UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR in C99 and C11.\n\n*** IT IS NOT AN EDGE CASE -- IT IS THE ONLY PATH THAT WORKS. ***  Two lines below, the model gates on `if (!(tileptr & 0x80000000)) return;' with the comment `The highest bit seems to be set for a \"valid\" tile pointer', and then clears it with `tileptr &= ~0x80000000;'.  Bit 31 of the shifted value can ONLY come from bit 15 of the uint16_t, so EVERY tile pointer the model accepts as valid is exactly a value that overflows, and every tile pointer that would not overflow is rejected as invalid.  The UB is on the taken path, not a corner.\n\nGUEST-DRIVABLE: the three arrays are written by ordinary guest register writes inside DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re) (the TLB register handlers, which require 8-byte writes), so the guest chooses the value that overflows.\n\nNOT the same defect at the fourth array: `re_linear_a' is `uint32_t[32]', so `re_linear_a[tile_nr] << 12' promotes to UNSIGNED int and wraps rather than overflowing -- defined, and out of scope for this row.\n\nFIX SHAPE: three one-token casts, `(uint32_t)d->re_tlb_x[tile_nr] << 16'.  No behaviour change on any compiler that today does the obvious thing; it removes the licence for one that does not.  EASY.\n\nWITNESS: none built, and the honest note is that a rung-3 witness may be IMPOSSIBLE for UB whose observable behaviour is the intended one on every compiler this project builds with -- the ATTEMPT is owed per the 2026-08-27 lifecycle ruling, and a build under `-fsanitize=undefined' in build-asan/ is the obvious first try, since that turns the UB into a diagnostic rather than into a wrong value.  Do not record `none' as `did not think to ask'."
    }
   ]
  }
