 succeeded in 590ms:
{"id": "n6", "sev": null, "state": "held", "title": "n6  The record says the footbridge host-halts were made graceful; the guest-reachable kills are not", "note": "  *** THE DESIGN IS DONE, THE TITLE'S COUNT IS STALE, AND AN ACCEPT ROUND WOULD RED THE GATE UNLESS ONE PROBE ROW MOVES FIRST.  codex seat, 2026-08-30, farmed read-only while agy was quota-walled; every load-bearing claim re-checked here. ***  **THE COUNT: THREE NUMBERS ARE IN CIRCULATION AND ALL THREE ARE RIGHT ABOUT DIFFERENT THINGS.**  A comment-aware census of `dev_footbridge.c` finds **10** live `exit(` calls.  **TWO of them are in `DEVINIT(footbridge)`** -- `footbridge unimpl machine type` and `footbridge: unimplemented machine type.` -- which are CONSTRUCTION-TIME and not guest-reachable, because no guest chooses its own machine type.  That leaves **EIGHT guest-access kills**, and this row's title says NINE.  The ninth was the divide-by-zero-on-a-guest-written-timer-encoding fixed by `#432` (`35302f2`), verified by `git log -S`.  **L4: the title is stale and is left unedited on purpose -- titles are identity strings other records grep -- but no future round may quote the nine.**  **TWO DISPOSITIONS, THREE ROUND-GROUPS.**  Not one round, and the split is by semantic class rather than by convenience:  (1) **ACCEPT, one site**: `read from ENABLE SET?` at `+0x188`.  Table 7-10 (`dc21285.txt:6821`) and the IRQEnable prose (`:6911`) make it a documented READ whose value can only be changed via EnableSet/EnableClear -- and **the model already computes `d->irq_enable` and then discards it by dying**.  So: delete the kill, KEEP the value, no latch and no message, because there is no event to announce.  This is the FOURTH option-set member, and a bounded-announce here would ship a wrong record in product code.  (2) **DECLINE + BOUNDED ANNOUNCE, five sites, one class**: the invalid-DIRECTION interrupt accesses -- WRITE to IRQStatus `+0x180` and FIQStatus `+0x280` (read-only, `:6886`), WRITE to IRQRawStatus `+0x184` and FIQRawStatus `+0x284` (read-only, `:6901`), and READ from ENABLE CLEAR `+0x18c` (write-only).  **`+0x18c` carries an extra obligation the others do not: the decline must NOT expose the currently assigned `d->irq_enable`**, which is the value a naive 'just return the field' fix would leak.  (3) **DECLINE + an ACCURATE announce, two sites, a DIFFERENT rationale**: the SDRAM Base Address Mask `+0x100` (`:5844`) and Offset `+0x104` (`:5897`) reads.  These registers are R/W and legal -- **the model simply does not implement them** -- so the message must say `unimplemented read`, NOT `invalid access`.  Getting that wording wrong is the same wrong-record defect as (1), one step milder.  *** THE OPERATIONAL TRAP, AND IT IS THE SHARPEST THING IN THIS FINDING: `gate_arm.sh` REQUIRES THE `+0x188` KILL TO KEEP HAPPENING. ***  Its row is `check \"footbridge: the one-instruction kill arm still dies\" ... '^K1 .*ok$' ... 1`, and `K1` is precisely the ENABLE-SET read the ACCEPT round would fix.  **So the ACCEPT round turns a GREEN GATE RED by succeeding**, and the fix is to REPOINT K1 at `+0x18c` in the same commit.  A round that does not know this reads the red as a regression in its own work.  Verified by opening the gate.  **AND THE PROBE'S FAILABILITY LEG RESTS ON A PRODUCT DEFECT**, which is fine until it is the LAST one: `footbridge_sites_probe.py` reaches exactly one fatal site (K1) and uses its death as the proof the guest code got that far.  Once the final live kill is repaired there is no product defect left to point at, and the leg must become a MUTATION/failability leg instead.  That is a real cost of finishing this cluster and it should be planned, not discovered.  DETECTOR DEBT the design already owes: seed `irq_enable` with a NONZERO write to `+0x188` before reading it, so the ACCEPT row cannot pass on the reset zero; a row per remaining live site checking survival, accurate bounded reporting AND no state mutation; and for `+0x18c`, that the invalid read neither clears `irq_enable` nor returns it.  **A PER-SITE LATCH BITMASK, not one shared latch** -- otherwise the first access kind to fire suppresses the first useful report for every other, which is `#490`'s measured `M3shared` shape.  *Provenance and its limit: the datasheet citations are `dc21285.txt` line anchors from a seat that read the file.  I verified the count reconciliation, the `#432` attribution and the gate coupling myself; I did NOT re-read all six datasheet sections, and this row says so rather than implying a completeness it does not have.*  *** TWO FARMED RUNS OF THE SAME SEAT ON THE SAME BRIEF AGREED ON EVERYTHING CHECKABLE AND SPLIT ON ONE DESIGN CALL.  Both are recorded; neither is picked, because picking is what the round is for. ***  (Both ran because a `${j}` in the launch command did not expand, so each job read all three briefs -- an accident that bought an unplanned replication.)  **AGREED, and these are the facts:** eight guest-access kills not nine, the ninth removed by `#432`; `+0x188` is ACCEPT; the four writes to read-only status registers are decline+announce; and WIDTH vs DIRECTION at the sgi_re sites are TWO rounds, not one, because they share an address and a probe but not a semantic class.  **SPLIT 1 -- how many round-groups.**  Run A said THREE (accept / five invalid-direction / two SDRAM).  Run B said FOUR, splitting `+0x18c` (IRQEnableClear, a READ of a write-only register) into its own group because it carries an obligation the other four do not: **the present code must also STOP EXPOSING `d->irq_enable`**, which a plain decline does not accomplish.  Run A recorded that obligation but kept the site in the same group.  **Run B's split is the better one on the round-scope rule's own text** -- a site needing a state-suppression the others do not is not covered by the same fix design -- but it is a judgement call and it is recorded as one.  *** SPLIT 2, AND IT PROPOSES A FIFTH OPTION-SET MEMBER: `IMPLEMENT`. ***  For the two SDRAM Base Address registers (`+0x100` mask, `+0x104` offset) Run A said decline + an ACCURATE announce (*'unimplemented read'*, not *'invalid access'*).  Run B said flatly **'none of the four offered options fits'** and proposed implementing them: reset-zero state with masked write/readback.  **The disagreement is real because these registers are LEGAL AND READ/WRITE.**  The existing four members all presume the access is either unserviceable (delete / decline / decline+announce) or already correctly computed (ACCEPT).  A legal R/W register the model simply never implemented is neither: declining it announces truthfully and still returns a wrong answer to a guest that wrote then read back.  **THE ADDITION LICENCE PUTS THE BURDEN ON `IMPLEMENT`, and that is the right way round:** additions to product code here are licensed by a MEASURED DELTA or a TRANSFERRED WARRANT, never by 'it cannot hurt'.  Implementing two registers is an addition, so it owes one of those -- a guest that actually reads them back, or an in-tree precedent for modelling an SDRAM sizing register.  **Until one exists, decline+accurate-announce is the licensed choice and IMPLEMENT is the filed alternative.**  A round taking these two sites must answer this explicitly rather than inheriting whichever note it reads first.  *Recorded now because the four-member option set is carrier doctrine, and a proposed FIFTH member is a rule question -- which this project's own splitting rule says goes to the adjudicating seat at the next round boundary rather than being settled in passing.*  *** THE K1 GATE DEPENDENCY IS VERIFIED INDEPENDENTLY, NOT TAKEN ON THE DESIGN'S WORD.  Main loop, 2026-08-30. ***  `gate_arm.sh` carries `check \"footbridge: the one-instruction kill arm still dies\"` and `footbridge_sites_probe.py` emits `K1  kill arm dies at 0x188, exit 1`.  **So the gate genuinely asserts that the kill KEEPS HAPPENING, and the ACCEPT round reddens it BY SUCCEEDING.**  A round that does not know this reads the red as a regression in its own work -- which is why it is written here rather than discovered at gate time.  **AND THE DEEPER PROBLEM IS THE ONE WORTH CARRYING FORWARD: the probe's failability leg RESTS ON A PRODUCT DEFECT.**  It reaches exactly one fatal site and uses that death as proof the guest code got that far.  That is sound while other kills remain and self-defeating when the round's whole purpose is to remove the last one.  *A test that proves its own liveness by reaching a crash is spending a defect as apparatus, and the bill comes due on the round that fixes it.*  **HOLD QUESTION SET 2026-08-30.**  This row was held with `hold_question: None` -- the exact defect `check_stage_panels.py` exists to catch, and it passed only because the hold predates the checker's cutoff.  *A stage held for a seat must carry the question it is held for; a seat fired against a disposition note has to GUESS what it is being asked, and a guessed question yields an answer to the wrong question that still looks like a discharged stage.*  *** THE HOLD QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND THE ANSWER MOVED THE ROUND'S SHAPE IN FOUR PLACES.  Adjudicated 2026-08-30; every load-bearing claim re-verified here by grep before recording. ***  **(1) THE REPOINT IS LEGITIMATE -- BUT NOT FOR THE REASON I ASKED ABOUT.**  I framed it as an oracle-correction: a shipped row pinning the opposite of the architecture.  **K1 pins nothing about `+0x188`'s correct behaviour.**  Its own header says so -- `+0x188` is *an UNFIXED site, used here rather than repaired, because a probe that reports SURVIVED for every row is vacuous unless something in it can still die*.  It is a **FAILABILITY CONTROL whose CONSUMABLE the fix consumes**, and retargeting a control to another live consumable is not re-pinning an oracle.  **THE ORDER DISCIPLINE TRANSFERS ANYWAY, and this is the laundering hazard that IS real:** a round that repointed the arms and shipped nothing else would change behaviour with **zero rows changing colour** -- no red ever measured, observationally identical to fitting the harness around the fix.  So on the committed PRE-FIX build, before any `exit(1)` is touched: the repointed arms must still DIE at the new site, and the new ACCEPT row must be RED **for the reason under test** (its fatals carrying the ENABLE SET text -- mechanical rule 4).  A dead replacement control is the check-that-cannot-fail shape.  *** (2) THE BLAST RADIUS IS UNDERCOUNTED BY A FACTOR OF THREE, AND IT CARRIES A THREE-WEEK FUSE.  VERIFIED BY GREP. ***  The design says *repoint K1*.  **THREE committed probes plant `0xE5907188`** -- `footbridge_sites_probe.py:103`, `fbpending_drain_probe.py:226`, `fbpending_witness.py:195` -- and the latter two score their K rows on `HOST-DIED` (`fbpending_drain_probe.py:659`).  **`fbpending_drain_probe.py` is dated-exempt from wiring only until 2026-09-20**, with `gate_arm.sh` named as its home -- so a round that repoints one arm leaves two probes broken on disk and a fuse that wires one into a gate where its K row reads as a regression.  **All three move in the same commit**, each with its own pre-fix still-dies measurement.  **(3) THE THIRD ORACLE OBLIGATION BITES, AND THIS ROW'S OWN PRESCRIPTION IS INSUFFICIENT.**  The repointed arm observes the SAME CHANNEL RELOCATED -- guest read, fatal text, exit -- so without more the FIXED site would carry no row at all, which is worse than a row that observes the same value either way.  The other-channel row is the ACCEPT row, and this row previously prescribed *seed with a nonzero write, then read it back*.  **That is RAM-INDISTINGUISHABLE and the probe's own header already rejects it** -- *RAM, or any decode that merely absorbed the store, would return the value unchanged*.  **THE ROW MUST CARRY THE HANDLER'S OWN TRANSFORMATION AS ITS SIGNATURE**, and the set/clear machinery supplies one free: write V1 then V2 to `+0x188` and read back **V1|V2** (the `|=` accumulation), or write V1 to `+0x188` and V2 to `+0x18c` and read back **V1 & ~V2**.  *Any absorbing decode returns the last write; only this device returns the accumulation.*  **(4) A STRUCTURAL CAUTION THE DESIGN MISSED: the accept sequence needs its OWN probe arm, not the shared `BODY` list.**  `BODY` is executed by arms T, L and K alike, so a `+0x188` read placed there makes PRE-FIX arm L die mid-run, zeroing the liveness rows and mis-attributing the red -- *failed for some other reason*, which is the guard mechanical rule 4 exists to enforce.  A fourth launch costs about 0.6 s against a measured 1.9 s wall.  **(5) REPOINT TO `+0x100`, NOT `+0x18c` -- one hop instead of two.**  `+0x18c` is consumed by the DECLINE round under this row's own group order, so targeting it means three arms moved now and three moved AGAIN.  `+0x100` (`ldr r7,[r0,#256]`, fatal `read from sdram_ba_mask`) is a last-group site: same one-word edit, and the pre-fix measurement doubles as a rung-3 witness the SDRAM round inherits.  *** (6) THE TITLE IS DE-COUNTED, AND THE PREMISE FOR LEAVING IT WAS MEASURED FALSE. ***  The design left `nine` standing because *titles are identity strings other records grep*.  **Swept: the title text appears NOWHERE outside `ledger.json` itself**, the grep identity is the `id` field, and `gen_dashboard.py` uses the title for display only while `check_stage_panels`, `check_seats_read`, `gen_codex_wall` and `check_fable_queue` never read titles at all.  Editing it breaks zero cross-references; leaving it kept a wrong count in the row's most-read line -- **the verdict-line-position problem, where the half that survives truncation is the wrong half.**  **De-counted rather than re-counted to EIGHT**, following this repo's own adjudicated form: *removed rather than re-counted -- a prose list that duplicates a generated table has no way to stay true*.  The `n6` id is untouched, and the note keeps the NINE verbatim with its `#432` warrant, so provenance survives.  **THREE STALE CITATIONS TO FIX IN THE SAME ROUND**, all naming the kill site: the probe header and `gate_arm.sh` both say `dev_footbridge.c:495-497`, and `fbpending_witness.py` says `:486-499`; the real arm is `:577-591`.  **And the anchor itself is DELETED by this fix**, so the rewritten comments must anchor on the new site's text.  *** THE 2026-09-20 FUSE IS BOUNDED TO ONE PROBE.  Swept 2026-08-30 after this row surfaced the entanglement, because the obvious next question is whether OTHER exemptions carry the same trap. ***  **All six dated exemptions expire on the same day, 2026-09-20**, and expiry means the probe gets wired into a gate.  A probe whose row depends on a site DYING then reads as a regression if the round that fixes that site has shipped -- which is exactly what this row found for `fbpending_drain_probe.py`.  **Swept all six for any dependence on a site dying** (`HOST-DIED`, a `died` flag, an `exit == 1` expectation, `s.dead`), with denominators and a positive control so a pattern-matched-nothing result could not masquerade as a clean one:      fbpending_drain_probe    14 of 727 lines   <- the known entanglement      hpcmips_ctor_probe        2 of 758         <- BOTH ARE COMMENTS, not code      wdc_altoffset_probe       0 of 698      wdc_altoffset_sig_probe   0 of 263      wdc_altoffset_4bit_probe  0 of 240      wdc_addtoinbuf_probe      0 of 863      CONTROL footbridge_sites  3 of 316         <- the pattern fires where it must  `hpcmips_ctor_probe`'s two hits were opened and are prose -- *the process died and it died HERE are different claims*, and a note about a core dump.  **No functional dependence.**  **SO THE FUSE IS EXACTLY ONE PROBE WIDE**, and it is already named in this row's blast radius.  Nothing else in the exemption table needs to move when these expire.  *The positive control is the part that makes this worth recording: a sweep of six files returning five zeros is precisely the shape this project has twice mistaken for a result, and the control is what separates 'nothing there' from 'pattern matched nothing'.*  *** AND THE BLAST RADIUS HAS THREE TIERS, NOT ONE NUMBER.  Swept 2026-08-30, generalising the K1 finding into the question that actually matters. ***  **THE FIRST FORMULATION WAS WRONG AND ITS ZERO WAS WEAK.**  Sweeping the GATES for rows whose label demands a death found exactly one of **423 check rows** -- the K1 row -- and a second pattern looking for `HOST-DIED` in a check's want returned **0 of 423**.  That zero proves nothing: gates consume probe OUTPUT TOKENS (`^K1 .*ok$`), so the death-dependence lives INSIDE the probe, not in the gate row.  *A denominator validates the artifact, never the pattern's fitness for its format.*  **THE RIGHT QUESTION IS: WHICH PROBES SCORE A ROW ON A DEATH, AND WHICH OF THOSE DOES A GATE ACTUALLY RUN?**  Three probes score on a death -- `fbpending_drain_probe.py` (2 sites), `footbridge_sites_probe.py` (1), `fbpending_witness.py` (1) -- and **all three are REFERENCED by gates**, which looked at first like the radius tripling again.  **IT DOES NOT: SEPARATING INVOCATIONS FROM MENTIONS LEAVES EXACTLY ONE.**  `gate_arm.sh:987` genuinely runs `footbridge_sites_probe.py`; **every other reference in `gate_arm.sh`, `gate_hygiene.sh` and `gate_offline.sh` is a COMMENT.**  That is the mentions-are-not-wiring distinction `check_probe_wiring.py` exists to enforce, applied by hand here and giving the same answer.  **SO THE THREE TIERS ARE:**  **(1) TODAY** -- one gate row reddens, `gate_arm.sh`'s K1, via the only real invocation.  **(2) ON 2026-09-20** -- `fbpending_drain_probe.py`'s exemption expires, it is wired into `gate_arm.sh`, and its K row joins the first.  **(3) SILENTLY, AT ANY TIME** -- `fbpending_witness.py`'s K row breaks on disk and **NO GATE RUNS IT**, so nothing reports the breakage at all.  *That is the worst tier despite being the least urgent: a broken detector nobody runs is indistinguishable from a working one, and it is exactly the state the wiring checker was built to make visible.*  All three arms still move in the same commit -- the tiers change WHEN each would be noticed, not whether each is broken.  *** THE DATASHEET SPLITS WHAT THIS ROW TREATS AS ONE CLASS.  Derived 2026-08-30 from `_scratchpad/dc21285.txt`, cross-confirmed in THREE independent places so no extraction stagger is possible: the register summary (`IRQEnable/IRQEnableSet 188h`, `IRQEnableClear 18Ch`), the offset table (`188h  IRQEnable  IRQEnableSet` / `18Ch  --  IRQEnableClear`), and the section heading `7.3.32 IRQEnableSet/FIQEnableSet Register--Offset 188h/288h`. ***  Constants confirmed in `src/include/thirdparty/dc21285reg.h`.  **(a) READ of 0x188 -> ACCEPT.**  *\"IRQEnable/FIQEnable Register--Offset 188h/288h.  This read-only register is used to mask the interrupt input sources... The value of this register can only be changed by writing to the EnableSet and EnableClear registers.  Reset value: 0.\"*  The emulator ALREADY does `odata = d->irq_enable;` -- the documented value -- and then calls `exit(1)` on it.  Delete the kill, KEEP THE VALUE, **no latch**: there is no event to announce, because the read is implemented and is not ignored.  A bounded announce here would ship a wrong record in product code.  **(b) READ of 0x18c -> DECLINE.**  The offset table gives `--` for its read function: there is NO defined read.  The emulator returns `d->irq_enable`, which is a GUESS, then exits.  **Different disposition from its immediate neighbour, and the code treats them identically.**  **(c) WRITE to FIQ_STATUS (0x280) -> DECLINE.**  *\"The Status and RawStatus are read-only.\"*  *** WHY THIS MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING TRIVIA: a single splice across all three would ACCEPT an undefined read at 0x18c and DECLINE a documented one at 0x188 -- getting both neighbours wrong, in OPPOSITE directions. ***  This is the option-set rule paying out again, with the ACCEPT member in play -- and that member is visible only from the datasheet, never from the source.  *This row's title treats 'the guest-reachable kills' as ONE THING.  It is three things.*", "state_note": "*** RANKING AND ONE REFUTED DOUBT, 2026-08-27. ***\n\n**Ranked 6th of 8** in today's adjudication -- loud, guest-drivable host termination, placed BELOW the silent interrupt-fidelity rows, following this project's own revealed ordering (a loud-termination row already sits below a lost-interrupt row in the queue). **The adjudicator flagged this as its softest call**: a pure denial-of-service view would lift it above an INFERRED hang, since a guest reading a documented register ends the emulator process. Recorded as a flagged call, not a settled one.\n\n*** THE ADJUDICATOR DOUBTED THE ACCEPT PREMISE ON THE GROUND THAT NO DC21285 MANUAL IS IN `_scratchpad/` -- AND THAT IS REFUTED. *** The manual is present: `dc21285.pdf` (1,059,970 bytes) and `dc21285.txt` (445,677 bytes). The statement was verified twice, from two channels:\n  * Table 7-10, `dc21285.txt:6826` -- `188h    IRQEnable    IRQEnableSet`;\n  * prose at `:6913-6915` -- *\"**This read-only register** is used to mask the interrupt input sources... The value of this register can only be changed by writing to the EnableSet and EnableClear registers.\"*\n\n> **The doubt's ROOT CAUSE is the defect this round already fixed:** the carrier's primary-sources list named about seven manuals when fifteen are present, and this datasheet was one of the six it omitted. **A seat checking that list would conclude the manual is absent.** That is the stale-inventory failure reaching one step further than expected -- it did not just stop a round finding the manual, it caused a later seat to doubt a correct finding drawn from it.\n\n**THE THREE OBLIGATIONS OF THE ACCEPT ROUND STAND, and the adjudication sharpened one:**\n  1. **re-point the probe's failability control** -- `+0x188` is row K1, the probe's only failing arm; there are **eight other** guest-reachable footbridge `exit(1)` sites to move it to (IRQ_STATUS / RAW write, ENABLE_CLEAR read, FIQ_STATUS write, SDRAM_BA_MASK / OFFSET read);\n  2. **a non-vacuous ACCEPT row** -- it must prove the read returns `irq_enable` post-fix **and redden under a guard-growth mutant** (return 0, or the wrong register). *'Returns some value' is vacuous*, and the reset value is 0, which is what an absent device returns;\n  3. the premise itself -- **discharged above.**\n\n*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27, moved here from [[devexit]] where I had filed it on the wrong row, and then CONFIRMED and SHARPENED by an adversarial pass that changed the round's whole shape. ***\n\n**THE KILL IS REAL AND UNCONDITIONAL.** `dc21285reg.h:335` gives `IRQ_ENABLE_SET 0x188`; `dev_footbridge.c:578-591` has, on the `MEM_READ` branch:\n```c\n} else {\n        odata = d->irq_enable;\n        fatal(\"[ WARNING: footbridge read from ENABLE SET? ]\");\n        exit(1);\n}\n```\nNo state precondition -- `d->irq_enable`'s value is irrelevant to reaching it. The only rewrite of `relative_addr` before the switch is the timer fold at `:507-510`, gated to `0x300..0x36C`, so `0x188` passes through untouched. CATS maps footbridge at `0x42000000` unmodified (`machine_cats.c:63-64`), `DM_DEFAULT`, so every access reaches the handler. **The MMU-off precondition is load-bearing, not decoration:** under the boot translation table (`cpu_arm.c:239-255`) only the low 256 MB is mapped with the top VA nibble ignored, so `VA 0x42000000` aliases to `PA 0x02000000` and returns 0.\n\n*** THE DATASHEET REFUTES THE FIX I HAD IN MIND, AND IT WAS IN `_scratchpad/` ALL ALONG. *** `_scratchpad/dc21285.pdf` / `.txt` (Intel 278115-001) has been present since 2026-08-15 and is **not listed in the carrier's 'Primary sources actually present' section.** Table 7-10 (`dc21285.txt:6823-6833`), calibrated on three straddling rows rather than one point:\n```\n  Address     Read           Write\n  180h        IRQStatus      --\n  184h        IRQRawStatus   --\n  188h        IRQEnable      IRQEnableSet\n  18Ch        --             IRQEnableClear\n```\nAll three neighbours match what the code already does -- it kills on exactly the `--` sides at 180h-write, 184h-write and 18Ch-read -- so the 188h row is trustworthy. The unstaggered prose (`:6911-6935`) confirms: *\"IRQEnable/FIQEnable Register - Offset 188h/288h. **This read-only register** is used to mask the interrupt input sources... Reset value: 0.\"*\n\n> *** SO A GUEST READ OF `+0x188` IS A DOCUMENTED, LEGAL, READ-ONLY ACCESS THAT THE MODEL ALREADY ANSWERS CORRECTLY (`odata = d->irq_enable`) AND THEN KILLS THE HOST ANYWAY. *** The right answer is computed and then discarded.\n\n*** THIS PRODUCES A **FOURTH MEMBER** OF THE DE-FATALISATION OPTION SET, WHICH THE CARRIER'S THREE-MEMBERED RULE DOES NOT CONTAIN: **ACCEPT.** *** delete / decline / decline+bounded-announce all presume the access is unserviceable. Here it is serviceable and already served, so the disposition is **delete the `fatal()`+`exit(1)` outright -- no latch, no message** -- and keep the value. Transferring the `m8820x_unimplemented()` warrant would have silently imported the premise that the read is unserviceable, which the code refutes on its own line, and would have shipped a **wrong record in product code**: printing *'unimplemented; ignored'* about a read that is implemented and is not ignored.\n\n**Four further reasons the m8820x template misfits, each measured:** its latch is a documented-defective template (ONE `reported_offset` flag covers FOUR kinds, per-kind latch FILED not done -- and footbridge has EIGHT sites across SIX kinds, so it imports the known defect at twice the scale); its marker `(reported once)` is the tree's MINORITY spelling and **both** uses are in `dev_m8820x.c` itself; and there is **no transferable flood measurement** -- m8820x's latch is warranted by 640,016 CMMU_SCR writes in 132 s on a rig that boots, while `regress/images.md` lists NO cats rig and `REVIEW_FINDINGS.md:171,:186` record that a real NetBSD/cats boot hits **none** of the eight sites.\n\n*** THE EIGHT SITES ARE TWO SEMANTIC CLASSES, NOT ONE, so the reopening rule SPLITS them: *** `0x188` read is a **correctness defect** (right answer computed, then discarded); the other seven -- including `IRQ_ENABLE_CLEAR` read at `:604`, and the write sides of `IRQ_STATUS`, `IRQ_RAW_STATUS`, `FIQ_STATUS`, `FIQ_RAW_STATUS` -- are **declinations of accesses the datasheet marks `--`**, which IS the m8820x class. One round cannot cover both.\n\n*** THE COST THAT MAKES THIS A REAL ROUND RATHER THAN A ONE-LINE DELETE: `0x188` IS THE PROBE'S ONLY FAILABILITY CONTROL. *** `footbridge_sites_probe.py`'s own header says so -- *\"an UNFIXED site, used here rather than repaired, because a probe that reports SURVIVED for every row is vacuous unless something in it can still die.\"* **Fixing `0x188` turns row K1 RED and takes gate 14 with it.** The round therefore owes, IN-ROUND:\n  1. re-point arm K to another still-unfixed site -- `+0x18c`, `0xE590718C`, fatal text `\"ENABLE CLEAR\"` -- a one-word change; and\n  2. a de-escalation ACCEPT row for `0x188`.\n\n**And that accept row is VACUOUS if it only READS `0x188`:** after `memset`, `d->irq_enable == 0`, and `0` is exactly what an absent device returns -- the precise trap this probe's header records surviving once before (*returned 0x0 on every device row WITH ITS RAM CONTROL STILL GREEN*). **It must `str` a known value to `+0x188` first** (the write arm ORs it in; with `irq_status == 0` the follow-on `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT` is inert), then read it back and assert that value.\n\n**Propagation note: `dev_footbridge.c` is byte-identical in `est/` and is NOT on `tools/est_divergence.txt`, so any edit must land in BOTH trees.**\n\n", "witness": "probe:regress/footbridge_sites_probe.py row K1 -- `\"kill arm dies at 0x188, exit 1\"`, gated in regress/gate_arm.sh. Arms L and K differ by exactly one word (`0xE5907188` / `ldr r7,[r0,#392]`, r0 = 0x42000000). CONFIRMED by a second seat re-deriving every line at HEAD 9c59973.", "commits": ""}
{"id": "m8invground", "sev": null, "state": "dropped", "title": "m8invground  diff_m8invread's spy counts CALLBACK calls; a mutant that clears the dyntrans arrays directly is invisible", "note": null, "state_note": null, "witness": null, "commits": ""}
{"id": "m8patc", "sev": null, "state": "dropped", "title": "m8patc  A SAPR/UAPR write does not invalidate the GUEST's PATC, only the emulator's dyntrans cache", "note": null, "state_note": null, "witness": null, "commits": ""}
{"id": "sgirekills", "sev": "high", "state": "open", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: SIX active exit(1) statements remain -- the census is DERIVED 2026-09-08 by stripping comments, and supersedes the stale \"twelve / eleven guest-reachable\" headline; the six-row oracle re-pin this row was organised around is ALREADY DISCHARGED (the probe has no `ld kill` rows left)", "note": "*** MY OWN BRIEF'S PREMISE WAS THAT `gdromcondlies` HELD THE LAST LIVE GUEST-REACHABLE HOST KILLS.  IT IS FALSE, AND THE FIRST FILE I SAMPLED REFUTED IT. ***  I stated it AS A QUESTION in that brief (*'have I confused \"the queue knows of no others\" with \"there are no others\"?'*), which is the only reason this was cheap to find -- and I found it myself, before the panel answered.  **THE CENSUS SAID WHERE TO LOOK AND WAS RIGHT.**  `exit_census.py` reports 99 live terminating calls inside `DEVICE_ACCESS` across 30 files; `dev_sgi_re.c` carries **21**, seven times the gdrom file.  It is the top of that list, and I sampled it for exactly that reason.  *** WHAT IS THERE, AND IT IS A CLASS THIS PROJECT HAS ALREADY FIXED ELSEWHERE: ***  the guard at the head of the CRIME_RE_TLB_A arm is  `if (len != 8) { fatal(\"TODO: unimplemented len=%i ...\"); exit(1); }`  -- **a GUEST CHOOSING AN ACCESS WIDTH ends the host process.**  That is the `#337` class verbatim, whose own comment in `dev_wdc.c` reads *'a 3- or 8-byte access to the data port used to call exit(1) -- a GUEST choosing an access width killed the host process'*.  The adjacent arm kills on a plain READ (`fatal(\"TODO: read from CRIME_RE_TLB_A\"); exit(1);`), which is the direction/offset class.  **REACHABILITY CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED**: `machine_sgi.c:500` calls `dev_sgi_re_init(machine, mem, 0x15000000)` and the device registers at `baseaddr + 0x1000` through `memory_device_register`.  It is a real device on a real machine, mapped into the guest address space.  **HONEST BOUND ON THE NUMBER, because this project keeps getting counts wrong: 21 is how many live terminating calls sit inside that file's `DEVICE_ACCESS` handlers.**  It is NOT a claim that 21 are individually reachable -- some may be guarded, and `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:1055` is the standing proof that a `DEVICE_ACCESS` site can be unreachable and take a hand-derived warrant to establish it.  What IS established: at least two of them are reachable by construction, and they are of classes already recognised as defects here.  *** THE GENERAL LESSON, AND IT IS ABOUT THE QUEUE RATHER THAN THIS DEVICE: A QUEUE-COMPLETE CLAIM IS NOT A TREE-COMPLETE ONE. ***  The adjudicating seat flagged exactly this limit about its own answer hours earlier -- *'my no-other-live-host-kills claim rests on the queue and this file's grep, not a fresh tree-wide exit census'* -- and then I wrote the queue-complete version into a brief as a premise anyway.  The seat named the gap; the brief inherited it.  SCOPE: this is a CLUSTER and must not become one round.  The round-scope rule takes ONE site, with a second joining only if it is witnessed, in the same device and semantic class, and covered by the same detector with no new oracle.  The width guards may qualify as one class; the read-direction kills are a second.  **More witnesses create more candidate ROUNDS, never bigger rounds.**  NEXT STEP is a rung-3 witness on ONE site, not a fix: `-E sgi` machines exist in tree, the device is mapped at a known base, and the `#337` precedent gives the fix shape (latched decline, `(once)` marker) if a witness lands.  *** THE DIAGNOSIS ABOVE IS INCOMPLETE AND THE TRUE ONE IS WORSE.  Corrected the same day by the opus5 seat, which MEASURED it. ***  This row was filed saying the brief had confused 'the queue knows of no others' with 'there are no others'.  **Neither.  The queue knew of NINE others.**  Ledger row `n6` is OPEN, titled *'The record says the footbridge host-halts were made graceful; nine of them are not'*, carrying a CONFIRMED rung-3 witness (`probe:regress/footbridge_sites_probe.py` row K1, gated in `gate_arm.sh`) and a `state_note` RANKING IT 6th OF 8 on 2026-08-27 -- three days before the brief claimed there were none.  The seat rebuilt HEAD clean and re-ran the COMMITTED probe: a guest `ldr r7,[r0,#392]` on an unmodified `-E cats` ends the host at `dev_footbridge.c:589`.  It then swapped the single planted word three more times and got **four of four** -- `:604` ENABLE_CLEAR read, `:565` IRQ_STATUS write, `:712` SDRAM_BA_MASK read -- each one guest instruction, each with the site's own diagnostic text as the attribution control.  **So this is the UNREAD-ROW class, not a census gap.**  The census work still stands and still found two REAL clusters nobody had filed; what it did not do is what a `grep n6` would have done in one second.  *The lesson is not 'census harder'.  It is that a completeness claim must be checked against THE QUEUE'S OWN OPEN ROWS before it is written down -- and that is the cheaper check of the two.*  *** THE CENSUS IS DONE, AND IT SPLITS THE 21 INTO FOUR CLASSES WITH ONE PROVABLY DEAD SITE.  agy seat, 2026-08-30, `regress/artifacts/widthkillclass/witness_20260830/`. ***  It is a READING, not a measurement, and is recorded as one -- but the single most surprising claim in it was CHECKED HERE BY OPENING THE FILE, and it held.  **20 of 21 guest-reachable; 1 PROVABLY UNREACHABLE.**  WIDTH (a guest choosing an access size ends the host) -- 7 reachable: the `len != 8` guards on CRIME_RE_TLB_A/B/C, CRIME_RE_TEX and CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A/B, plus `sgi_de: len = %i not implemented` (which wants len 4 or 8).  DIRECTION (a legal-width access in the wrong direction) -- 6 reachable: `TODO: read from CRIME_RE_TLB_A/B/C`, `..._TEX`, `..._LINEAR_A/B`, each reached by an 8-byte READ, i.e. **the guest does everything right except the direction**.  BADOFFSET -- 1: the `unimplemented read/write to address` arm, reached by any access to an unregistered sub-window.  BADVAL -- 6: `sgi_de: UNIMPLEMENTED drawing op`, and five `sgi_mte` arms (MTE_MODE_STIPPLE, unimplemented SRC, MTE_MODE_COPY, MTE_DEPTH_x, and an unimplemented bytemask).  *** THE UNREACHABLE ONE IS DEAD CODE AND MUST NOT BE 'FIXED' -- I VERIFIED THIS BY OPENING THE FILE RATHER THAN ACCEPTING IT. ***  `[ sgi_mte: UNIMPLEMENTED read/write len %i ]` sits in the trailing `else` of `if (len == 4) { ... } else if (len != 4) { ... } else { fatal; exit(1); }`.  For every integer `len` one of the first two arms is taken, so the third can never execute.  A round that 'de-fatalises' it would be editing unreachable code and its detector could carry no row -- **the vacuous shape in its purest form, and the census is what stops it being attempted.**  **WHAT THIS MEANS FOR SCOPE, and it is the useful part: FOUR classes means at least four candidate rounds, not one.**  The round-scope rule takes ONE site, with a second joining only if it is the same device AND the same semantic class AND covered by the same detector with no new oracle.  A WIDTH guard and a DIRECTION guard sit in the SAME ARM at the same offset and are still different classes -- whether they nonetheless share one fix design is an open question this row does not settle, and it is exactly what the next research pass should answer rather than assume.  HONEST BOUND: reachability here is derived from address-decode reading, NOT measured, except for the WIDTH class -- which now has a rung-3 witness (see `widthkillclass`).  The DIRECTION, BADOFFSET and BADVAL classes have NO witness yet and a round on any of them owes one first.  *** THE COUNT'S SCOPE WAS WRONG AND A SEAT CHECKED IT: 21 IS THE FILE TOTAL, NOT THE HANDLER'S. ***  `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)` holds **13**.  Per handler: sgi_re 13, sgi_de 1, sgi_mte 1, sgi_de_status 0, helpers 6 -- summing to the 21 this row filed.  So the four-class census spans FOUR HANDLERS, not one, and that matters for scope rather than for tidiness: a round joining sites 'in the same handler' is a different population from one joining sites 'in the same file'.  **This is the dropped-qualifier class again** -- the number was right and the noun was wrong, which is exactly how `b120r`'s NIL and `#464`'s 'floods identically' went wrong.  AND THE WIDTH FIGURE OF 7 IS SHORT BY ONE against the message text: there are EIGHT width-shaped kills in the file, the six in sgi_re plus `sgi_de: len = %i not implemented` and `[ sgi_mte: UNIMPLEMENTED read/write len %i ]`.  Both are now filed separately -- `sgidewidth` (LIVE, high) and `sgimtewidth` (a mis-service plus a resurrection trap) -- because they sit in different handlers and so cannot join a sgi_re round.  *** READ-ONLY AUDIT, agy seat, 2026-08-30 -- AND THE HEADLINE IS THAT THIS ROUND MAY BE MUCH SMALLER THAN IT LOOKS: ALL SIX ARMS LOOK LIKE **ACCEPT**, NOT DECLINE. ***  The de-fatalisation option set has four members, and the fourth applies where the model already holds the right answer and merely discards it.  **`re_tlb_a`, `re_tlb_b`, `re_tlb_c`, `re_tex`, `re_linear_a` and `re_linear_b` are all WRITTEN by the store path**, so a read can return what was written -- no latch, no message, because there is no event to announce.  *A bounded-announce here would ship a wrong record in product code: printing 'unimplemented; ignored' about a read that is implemented and is not ignored.*  **REACHABILITY: all six are reachable by an ordinary guest load on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`**, and the addresses are derived rather than guessed -- `machine_sgi.c:500` passes base `0x15000000`, `dev_sgi_re_init` registers `sgi_re` at `baseaddr + 0x1000` for `DEV_SGI_RE_LENGTH` (0x1000), and the handler restores the full offset with `relative_addr += 0x1000`.  **Every window lies inside `0x15001000-0x150017ff`, none is shadowed, and the `else if` cascade is strictly disjoint.**  Verified here independently by opening `machine_sgi.c` and `crmfbreg.h`: the six constants are `0x1000 / 0x1200 / 0x1400 / 0x1600 / 0x1700 / 0x1780`.  **GUEST KSEG1 ADDRESSES, so a probe author does not re-derive them:** TLB_A `0xb5001000`, TLB_B `0xb5001200`, TLB_C `0xb5001400`, TEX `0xb5001600`, LINEAR_A `0xb5001700`, LINEAR_B `0xb5001780` -- read kills at lines 245, 262, 279, 296, 346, 362 respectively.  **One malloc serves all four handlers** (`dev_sgi_re_init` allocates one `struct sgi_re_data` and hands it to `sgi_de`, `sgi_mte` and `sgi_de_status`), so whatever latch infrastructure `#497` lands is already reachable here.  *The audit and the brief that produced it are preserved at `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/audit_20260830/` -- the ledger first cited the `_scratchpad/` copy, which the durable-path rule excludes precisely because it evaporates.  This row now has everything a rung-3 witness needs except the witness itself, which is the point of asking a seat for the addresses rather than the answer.*  *** THIS ROW'S TITLE SAID '21 TERMINATING CALLS INSIDE DEVICE_ACCESS' AND THAT WAS THE SAME CONFLATION I MADE AND CORRECTED ELSEWHERE TODAY.  Corrected 2026-08-30. ***  **21 is the FILE total.  15 are inside a `DEVICE_ACCESS`, and 13 of those in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)`** -- the other 6 live in `draw_primitive` (1) and `do_mte_transfer` (5), neither of which is a `DEVICE_ACCESS` and neither of which a guest reaches by an ordinary access.  *** AND THE TITLE WAS NOT CARELESS -- IT FAITHFULLY REPORTED WHAT THE TOOL SAID, WHICH WAS WRONG. ***  See `exitcensus`: `exit_census.py`'s per-file totals were correct all along, but its BY-ENCLOSING-CONSTRUCT split attributed every call in a plain non-static function to the preceding `DEVICE_ACCESS`.  That bucket is a REACHABILITY claim used for ranking -- *on a guest access path BY CONSTRUCTION* -- so the defect over-stated how many host kills a guest can actually reach.  Fixed; tree-wide the bucket falls **97 -> 83**.  *** THE ACCEPT DESIGN, ADJUDICATED 2026-08-30 -- AND IT REFUTES THE AUDIT RECORDED DIRECTLY ABOVE IT ON ONE ARM OF SIX. ***  **agy ruled ACCEPT for all six read arms on the ground that BACKING STATE EXISTS in the struct.  That is not the ACCEPT test.**  The doctrine's fourth option-set member -- minted from the footbridge IRQEnable precedent -- is *the model already computes the CORRECT answer and merely discards it*.  Existence of state is necessary and nowhere near sufficient.  **FIVE ARMS ARE CLEAN ACCEPT; TEX IS NOT.**  **TLB A/B/C:** the store packs big-endian halfword pairs from raw bus bytes and a readback through the same index expression round-trips BYTE-EXACTLY on this machine -- with a premise that belongs in the comment rather than assumed: `machine_sgi.c` pins BIG ENDIAN, and while the STORE path is byte-order-agnostic the READ path is not.  **LINEAR A/B:** the store splits `idata` into two uint32 cells and the readback is its exact inverse through the `readmax64`/`writemax64` pair -- byte-order symmetric BY CONSTRUCTION, so it needs no endianness premise at all.  *** TEX FAILS THE TEST, AND THE EVIDENCE IS A GREP: `re_tex` HAS ZERO CONSUMERS ANYWHERE. ***  `tlb_a/b/c` feed `horrible_getputpixel`; `linear_a/b` feed it and `do_mte_transfer`; **`re_tex` feeds nothing.**  Its state is written through the `>> 3` index that `sgitexindex` records as a defect -- frames overlap by three, 81 of 112 cells unreachable -- so **the model does not hold the right answer for TEX; it holds a self-consistent WRONG one.**  ACCEPT is available only in the MIRRORED form (the read uses the same `>> 3` expression), which round-trips per frame while shipping hardware-wrong reads at overlapping offsets.  **That is a materially weaker claim than the other five arms', and the round record must not blur them.**  **WHAT ACCEPT OWES THAT DECLINE DOES NOT -- five items, and the second is an ORDERING DEPENDENCY on `#497`:**  (1) **round-trip rows with DISTINCT sentinels per window** -- distinctness catches a cross-wired read, while a same-window index error breaks round-trip because the store frame is position-dependent.  The JOINT translation (store and read shifted together) escapes round-trip BY CONSTRUCTION -- clause (f) territory -- so the contract names it outside, and names honestly that an out-of-decode anchor channel exists for four arms (the rendering and MTE paths consume them) and **does not exist for `re_tex` today**.  (2) **a DEVICE-SIGNATURE control**, because a faithful round-trip through a device is value-indistinguishable from RAM -- the witness ladder's own two-control lesson.  The clean discriminator: **post-`#497` a 4-byte read DECLINES and returns 0 while RAM would serve it.**  So `#497` is an ordering dependency for this round's WITNESS QUALITY, not merely for its latch infrastructure.  (3) **the dead tail's first execution belongs to `#497`, not here.**  A width-declined READ falls through the splice and runs `memory_writemax64` with `odata = 0`.  This round's new surface is narrower than it looks: **NONZERO `odata` through the BE packing**, not 'the tail runs'.  (4) **the claim must be SCOPED**: *returns what the store path recorded*, never *faithful hardware readback*.  TEX refutes the stronger phrase today, and the LINEAR arms store the PROM's own wrongly-sign-extended values verbatim -- returning them faithfully is right; modelling hardware readback is unproven.  **That is `b716a34`'s lesson pre-applied: do not put the strong phrase in a site comment.**  (5) **a PRE-WRITE read row** returning 0, which pins the memset-init as guest-visible ABI and is the minimal de-fatalisation survival row.  *** TEX POISONS THE WARRANT, NOT THE ROUND -- AND THE COUPLING IS ENFORCEABLE BY ONE ROW RATHER THAN BY A DEPENDENCY. ***  Ship TEX MIRRORED plus its round-trip row, and any ONE-SIDED future fix reds that row loudly: store `>> 1` against read `>> 3` writes cells 4-7 and reads 1-4, and the reverse likewise, while the JOINT fix keeps identity and the row stays green.  **So the round-trip row IS the coupling enforcement**, and `sgitexindex`'s fix becomes a declared two-site edit with a tripwire already in place.  Recorded in BOTH rows.  **WHAT IS NOT ACCEPTABLE:** unmirrored `>> 1` reads over the `>> 3` store -- round-trip broken immediately, silent wrong data, worse than a decline -- or ACCEPT recorded with the same warrant strength as the other five arms.  **A LATENT THIS ROUND INHERITS RATHER THAN CREATES:** the TLB arms mask the INITIAL `tlbi` but increment it UNMASKED in the loop, so `relative_addr & 0x1ff >= 0x1fa` with `len 8` runs to index 0x102 -- three halfwords into the adjacent array.  Guest-unreachable for the alignment reason `sgilinearoob` settles path-by-path, and **a mirrored read arm makes it a second SITE of that class** -- record it there so an eventual bound fixes both directions.  *** THE TEST THAT KILLED `sgideoob` WAS THEN APPLIED TO THE SURVIVORS, BY ENUMERATION RATHER THAN BY REASONING -- AND BOTH HOLD.  2026-08-30. ***  Withdrawing a false member is only half the follow-through; the other half is asking whether the same miss is in the rows that remain.  The refuting method for `sgideoob` was *what is this index's ACTUAL RANGE?* -- so every reachable `relative_addr` in each window was walked and the resulting index recorded.  **TLB LOOP** (`re_tlb_a[256]`, `tlbi = ((ra & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff`, four writes at `len 8`): with 8-byte ALIGNMENT -- the only thing a guest can produce -- the maximum index written is **255, IN BOUNDS**.  Unaligned, reachable only by the debugger's `put d`, it is **258, OOB by three halfwords**.  *Exactly as recorded: guest-unreachable, debugger-reachable, same disposition as `sgilinearoob`.*  **`sgitexindex`** (`re_tex[112]`, `tlbi = ((ra & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, window `0x1600-0x16df`): maximum index written is **30, comfortably IN BOUNDS of [112]** -- so this row is NOT an out-of-bounds and never claimed to be.  Its three stated figures were each re-derived independently and all match: **81 cells unreachable**, **3 entries of overlap** between consecutive 8-byte writes.  *** SO THE CLASS AFTER THE WITHDRAWAL IS: TWO REAL OOBs (`sgilinearoob`, the TLB loop), BOTH GUEST-SHIELDED BY ALIGNMENT AND BOTH DEBUGGER-REACHABLE; ONE NON-OOB CORRECTNESS DEFECT (`sgitexindex`); AND ONE WITHDRAWN FALSE MEMBER. ***  Every one now tested by the same method, which is what the false member's whole lesson was.  *** THE WITNESS FIELD WAS STALE AND SAID THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT SHIPPED.  Corrected 2026-08-30 by the pass-2 seat. ***  It read *no rung-3 probe has been built and the round owes one before any edit* -- **false since `#497` shipped six `ld kill` rows**, each a real guest `ld` at one of the six windows, dying with per-site attribution text, on an unmodified machine.  The row's note mentioned them nowhere.  *** AND THE SAME ROWS CHANGE THIS ROW'S COST IN THE OTHER DIRECTION, WHICH MUST BE WRITTEN DOWN NOW RATHER THAN REDISCOVERED: FIXING THIS DEFECT WILL TURN A GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING. ***  Six rows' worth.  That is precisely the `n6` shape adjudicated hours earlier in this same session, so this round inherits its ordering obligation: **re-pin the six rows FIRST, measure the committed build RED under the new expectations, and only then edit** -- same scripts, opposite colour.  Without that order the re-pin is observationally identical to fitting the test to the binary.  *** THE HEADLINE COUNT INCLUDES ONE SITE THAT CAN NEVER EXECUTE.  Derived 2026-08-30. ***  `dev_sgi_re.c` holds 20 raw `exit(1)` lines; exactly FIVE are commented out (179, 188, 280, 630, 1122) and fifteen are live -- **including 1334, which is inside `sgi_mte`'s unreachable `else` (see `sgimtewidth`).**  **`exit_census.py` excludes COMMENTED-OUT code and cannot see REACHABILITY.**  That is correct behaviour for a text-level tool and it is a limit worth stating rather than discovering: \"live terminating calls\" means SYNTACTICALLY live.  The row's numbers are not wrong -- they are the census's numbers -- but a round quoting them as reachable host kills would be over-claiming by one.  *** THE `sgirekills` / `sgitexindex` COUPLING, ESTABLISHED 2026-08-31 AND RECORDED ON BOTH ROWS SO NEITHER CAN BE CLOSED IN IGNORANCE OF THE OTHER. ***  **The fact, which is settled:** `sgitexindex`'s `>> 1` is correct under BOTH candidate readings of a TEX entry -- 112 halfwords indexed `offset >> 1`, or 28 64-bit entries where entry `k` starts at halfword `4k`, since `(offset >> 3) * 4 == offset >> 1` for 8-aligned offsets.  **So the store's shift is wrong INDEPENDENTLY of the missing datasheet**, and there is no SGI document in `_scratchpad/`.  **Consequence for `sgirekills`:** its TEX read arm can only ACCEPT in the MIRRORED form -- reusing the store's `>> 3` -- because only that round-trips what the store wrote.  Mirroring therefore enshrines a KNOWN-WRONG index in a second place, and gives it a green round-trip row.  *When someone later corrects the shift, that row goes RED because of their correct fix.*  **Consequence for `sgitexindex`, in the other direction:** the TEX read arm is TODAY a live host kill, so **no guest can read back what those writes stored** and the aliasing is currently unobservable from inside the guest.  Fixing `sgirekills` is what would expose it.  That bears on severity and on ORDER, and neither row had recorded it.  *Neither consequence decides the disposition -- that is the question `sgirekills` is held for and a panel is answering it.  What is recorded here is the COUPLING, which is a fact either way.*  *** THE HELD TEX-READ QUESTION IS ANSWERED: DO NOT MIRROR.  3 of 3 packet-fed seats, and the dissenter's FALLBACK is the other two's first choice, so the convergence is total. ***  deepseek and minimax: **decline + latched announce** until the index fix lands.  glm: **BACKED ACCEPT with the CORRECT `>> 1` index**, round-trip oracle pinned to correct semantics and honestly red until `sgitexindex` lands -- *with decline + latched announce as its explicit fallback, and mirrored ACCEPT ranked worst of the three by all three seats.*  **MY PREMISE WAS CIRCULAR AND A SEAT SAID SO USING MY OWN INLINED FINDING.**  See its entry above.  The practical upshot: the choice is between *decline* and *correct-index read*, and MIRRORING -- the option the row was held on -- is off the table.  *One caution against glm's first choice, which it half-concedes: a correct-index read against a wrong-index store means a guest that writes then reads gets data from a different place, which is arguably worse for the guest than a decline returning 0.  That is why all three seats land on decline as at least an acceptable answer.*  **AND ONE OBJECTION OF ITS OWN IS ANSWERED BY A GUARD I FAILED TO INLINE.**  glm marked the *31 of 112 / overlap by three* arithmetic NOT SETTLEABLE because it presupposes 8-byte-wide 8-byte-aligned stores, asserted nowhere in the packet.  Correct as a packet criticism: `#497`'s width guard on this very arm declines `len != 8`, so the width is established, and 8-byte ALIGNMENT follows from the MIPS path (`ld`/`sd` raise ADEL/ADES before `memory_rw`, and the unaligned helper masks the address itself).  **A packet defect of mine, not an analysis defect of its.**", "state_note": "OPEN, and the committed artifact in `witness` is the REPRODUCTION, not the fix.  `regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py` shipped with #497 and carries SIX `ld kill` rows that assert THE HOST STILL DIES on an 8-byte access at each of the six windows -- they pin the defect, they do not repair it.  #497 de-fatalised the WIDTH guard only; the width test is evaluated FIRST, so a legal `ld` still reaches the kill.  *** THE ORDERING OBLIGATION IS ALREADY RECORDED IN #497's ROUND BLOCK AND IS THE REASON THIS ROW MUST NOT BE CLOSED BY THE CHECKER: fixing sgirekills will turn a GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING, six rows' worth. ***  It therefore inherits the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin the six rows FIRST, measure the committed build RED under the new expectations, THEN edit the code.  A row whose own detector must invert is exactly the case the shipped-work heuristic cannot read.\n\n*** COUNT CHECK 2026-09-06, AND MY FIRST CORRECTION WAS ALSO WRONG.  THE LIVE COUNT IS TWELVE. ***\n\nThree numbers have now been attached to this row: the title says TWENTY-ONE, my correction an hour ago said\nNINETEEN, and the live count is **TWELVE**.\n\n    grep -c 'exit(1)'                    19   <- ALL occurrences, comments included\n    grep -cE '//[[:space:]]*exit\\(1\\)'    4   <- commented-out kills (:218, :227, :689, :1251)\n    (three more sit inside comment PROSE at :321, :325, :945, discussing exit(1))\n    grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'    12   <- LIVE, statement position\n\n*** THE INSTRUCTIVE PART IS WHY I BELIEVED 19, BECAUSE IT IS A NEW COROLLARY OF A RULE THIS PROJECT ALREADY\nHAS. ***  I wrote that `grep -c 'exit('` and `grep -c 'exit(1)'` BOTH returned 19, and treated the agreement\nas ruling out the very error that had just made `lcabadval`'s enumeration wrong -- a commented-out kill\ncounted as live.  **Both greps share the same blindness: neither excludes `//`.**  The carrier says when two\nderivers DISAGREE neither is trustworthy until you look at what each matched.  The corollary, learned here:\n**when two derivers AGREE that is not evidence either, if they can fail the same way.**  Agreement between\nmethods is worth exactly the independence of the methods -- which is the unanimity-on-a-brief-supplied-fact\nrule, one level down and pointed at greps instead of seats.\n\nAnd it is the third instance tonight of *a correction is not safer than the thing it corrects*: `#503`'s\npass-3 refutation displaced a truer sentence, `83df95c` sharpened a vague claim while reading as though it\nfixed a false one, and this correction replaced a wrong number with another wrong number.\n\n**THE TITLE IS LEFT AS WRITTEN AND IS NOT SILENTLY RE-GRADED**: 21 may have been right when the row was\nopened, with sites removed since -- four of the nineteen are commented out, which is consistent with someone\nhaving disabled them.  A round on this file DERIVES the count from the statement-position pattern above\nbefore acting, and does not trust any of the three numbers in this row.\n\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (triage stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nL4 -- TITLE CORRECTED.  The state_note has carried the derived count of **TWELVE** since 2026-09-06, and the\nTITLE still said TWENTY-ONE.  **A note that corrects a title does not correct the title**, and the title is\nwhat every queue listing, dashboard row and triage pass shows first -- so the corrected figure was sitting\none field away from the wrong one, losing every time.\n\nOld title, kept because the quote-what-you-refute rule applies to my own records too:\n  \"dev_sgi_re.c carries 21 live terminating calls -- 15 of them inside a DEVICE_ACCESS and 13 in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re) alone -- and the six READ arms are all guest-reachable\"\n\nRe-derived at HEAD before this edit: `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);' src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c' = **12**,\nagainst 19 raw hits.  The new title NAMES ITS DERIVER, per the counts rule, so the next reader re-runs it\ninstead of trusting it -- which is the whole remedy for a class that has now had three generations on this\none row.\n\n**The DEVICE_ACCESS half of the old title (`15 of them inside a DEVICE_ACCESS ... 13 in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)\nalone') IS DELIBERATELY NOT REPLACED WITH A NEW NUMBER**, because [[exitcensus]] is open against exactly that\nattribution: the census's construct split *\"attributed every call in a plain non-static function to the\npreceding DEVICE_ACCESS -- inflating the bucket used to rank reachability\"*.  Quoting a fresh figure out of a\ntool with an open defect against the very field I am quoting would be the same error in a newer coat.  The\nreachability claim is left to the round that does the work.\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (assess stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nPREP (no code touched) -- the RE-PIN DESIGN, taken from the sibling that already did this rather than\ninvented.  `#499' re-pinned the neighbouring `other kill' row in this same probe when it removed the\ncatch-all kill, and its comment records that **BOTH obvious oracles were MEASURED VACUOUS before the third\nwas chosen**.  That is a transferable warrant, not a suggestion, because this row inverts the same way.\n\n**WHAT THE SIX ROWS ASSERT TODAY** -- `row(nm + \" ld kill\", how + (\"+txt\" if ok_txt else \"-txt\"),\n\"exit1+txt\")', i.e. the host DIES and the per-site literal `TODO: read from <sym>' appears.  Fixing the six\nread kills turns all six RED **by succeeding**, which is why the row carries the ordering obligation.\n\n**THE TWO ORACLES `#499` MEASURED VACUOUS -- do not re-propose either:**\n  * **DEATH-ONLY** (`how == \"exit1\"'): observes THAT it killed, never WHICH kill.  A message change leaving\n    `exit(1)' standing scored **35/35** against a matched control at 34/35.  Filed as [[otherkilldeath]].\n  * **`exit0`**: exit status cannot separate *declined, then quit* from *died with status 0*.  The variant\n    `mexit0' scores GREEN on an `exit0+txt' oracle while `alive=False'.  Filed as [[exit0oracle]].\n\n**THE THREE-CHANNEL SHAPE THAT SURVIVED, which the six should copy:**\n  * `how' -- the host survives;\n  * `txt' -- **ONE CONTIGUOUS literal including the address and data fields.**  Two independent substring\n    tests are a WEAKER claim: `-- declined.' is emitted by the six `#497' width declines and by `#498's\n    sgi_de decline too, so a bare substring cross-matches.  These six already have per-site symbols, which\n    is the natural discriminator;\n  * `v0' -- **a declined READ reaches the common tail and lands a DEFINED 0.**  `v0' is preset to SENTINEL\n    by `load()', so *wrote odata (0)* and *never landed* are distinguishable.  This is the channel that kills\n    `mret0' (return 0 -> memory_rw coerces to -1 -> EXCEPTION_DBE) and `mrdexit0' (exit(0) in the READ branch\n    only), **neither of which any write-only oracle can see** -- and these six ARE read arms, so this channel\n    is not optional here.\n\n**ORDER, per the oracle-correction rule:** re-pin the six FIRST, measure the COMMITTED build RED under the new\nexpectations, THEN edit `dev_sgi_re.c'.  `#499' additionally showed the stronger form -- its oracle had been\nPRE-REGISTERED six hours before its own baseline -- and this row inherits that: the design above is being\nwritten down BEFORE the round starts, in a dated entry, for exactly that reason.\n\n**Cite the sites by SYMBOL, never by line.**  The probe's own header lists `dev_sgi_re.c lines 245 262 279\n296 346 362'; `#504' inserted ~40 lines above them, so those numbers are already stale.  The probe carries a\n`SITES' table of symbolic names -- use it.\n\n*** CENSUS + RANKING, 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/codex_census_20260907.txt`).\nTHE ANSWER TO 'WHICH ARE CHEAP' IS 'NONE ARE', AND THAT IS THE USEFUL PART. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"The count is twelve.  ELEVEN ARE GUEST-REACHABLE; one is unreachable.  **There are no\ntwo or three genuine one-line repairs already covered by success tests.**  The cheapest plausible\nfunctional pair is LINEAR_A and LINEAR_B readback -- but both need corrected oracles.\"*\n\n**The count is CONFIRMED at twelve** (I re-ran the deriver before writing the brief; the title's 21\nand the intermediate 19 both counted comments).  **Eleven of twelve guest-reachable** is a stronger\nseverity statement than this row carried.\n\n*** I ASKED FOR THE CHEAP WINS AND WAS TOLD THERE ARE NONE.  Recorded as a NEGATIVE RESULT, because\nit stops the next round hunting for them. ***\n\n**THE COST RANKING, which gives a coherent round instead of a scattering:**\n\n    rank  site                        new latch bit   work\n    1     #12 dead MTE width branch   no              source cleanup only -- NO RUNTIME IMPROVEMENT\n    2=    #5  LINEAR_A read           no              replace the kill oracle with readback\n    2=    #6  LINEAR_B read           no                assertions; extend EXISTING sessions\n    3=    #1  TLB_A read              no              reuse existing sessions; add returned-value,\n    3=    #2  TLB_B read              no                packing and cell-selection assertions\n    3=    #3  TLB_C read              no\n    4=    #7  STIPPLE                 ONE             new targeted MTE rows AND session scenario\n    4=    #9  linear COPY             ONE             same\n    4=    #10 linear depth            ONE             same\n\n**RANKS 2 AND 3 ARE ONE CLASS: the five READ arms.**  No new latch bits, no new sessions, and they\nshare the oracle inversion this row already predicted -- the six `ld kill` rows in\n`regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py` that assert the host still dies.  **That is the round to run**, and it\nis the class-closure shape rather than a cell at a time: `#505` paid three floor breaches for\nrepairing cell-by-cell, and this row's sites are already grouped for me.\n\n**Rank 1 agrees with the separate `sgimtedepth4dead` audit**, which independently said DEFER and\nreduce that row to a comment correction -- two briefs, two seats, same disposition, reached from\ndifferent directions.\n\n**RANK 4 IS THE EXPENSIVE GROUP AND SHOULD NOT BE MIXED IN**: one latch bit each plus new MTE\nsessions.  Those are three separate rounds, not part of the read-arm round.\n\n*** GROK DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07.  I ASKED IT TO ASSUME THE THREE `lcabadval` ERRORS WERE REPEATED\nHERE.  ALL THREE WERE. ***\n\n**1. THE CLASS COUNT IS WRONG IN THE HEADLINE, FOR THE FOURTH TIME ON THIS ROW.**  `grep -cE\n'^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'` gives 12 STATEMENTS, but `:1537` **cannot run**: it is the trailing `else`\nof `if (len == 4) ... else if (len != 4) ... else { fatal; exit(1); }` in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_mte)` --\nan exhaustive pair with a third arm.  Already filed as [[sgimtewidth]].\n\n> **ELEVEN guest-reachable kills, in two classes: 6 DIRECTION + 5 MTE-feature.**  This row has now\n> carried 21 -> 19 -> 12 -> 11, and every correction was the same comment-or-reachability blindness.\n\n*Two more shapes sit beside the MTE five and are NOT among the twelve: `dst_y_step` fatals with its\n`exit` commented out, and the `dst_tlb` `default:` fatals and CONTINUES.  Same unimplemented-feature\nclass, different severity.  CLIP_IDS at `0x16e0` is a seventh NAMED RE window but is the `#499`\ncatch-all, not a seventh read kill.*\n\n*** 2. MY S3 WAS THE FLOOR-(a) MISS I ASKED ABOUT -- AND THE INSTRUCTION IN IT IS WRONG. ***\nI wrote that *\"a guest `lw` of each register kills the host on HEAD, so the rows are RED pre-fix by\nconstruction\"*.  **A `lw` does not kill: width is checked FIRST and `len != 8` DECLINES.**  The\nshipped detector already holds the counter-example -- `load()` defaults to `OP_LW_V0_AT` and every\n`lw surv` row wants `exit0/0x0`.  **The kill is `ld` (len = 8).**\n\n> **So a detector of six `lw` reads would be GREEN ON HEAD** -- precisely the miss I claimed this row\n> was immune to, arrived by a different door.  *The asymmetry I asserted between this row and\n> `lcabadval` was real in direction and wrong in fact.*\n\n**AND THE `lcabadval` ANALOGY IS THE WRONG CLASS ANYWAY:** those are WRITE VALUE-GUARDS, where legal\nwrites already survive; these are DIRECTION kills on a legal-width READ.  That difference is why S3\ncould look like the opposite of the `lcabadval` miss until `lw` is put in the sentence.\n\n**3. THE CITED PRECEDENT IS A FILED DEFECT, AND I CITED THE WRONG PROBE TOO.**\n  * S2 claimed *the model already stores the right thing* for all six.  **For TEX that warrant IS\n    [[sgitexindex]]**: `re_tex` is written with `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, consecutive\n    `sd`s overlap by three, 81 of 112 cells are unreachable, and there are **zero consumers**.\n    Backing state exists; the correct answer does not.\n  * S4 cited `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py` -- which is uncommitted `#505` work that **never issues an RE\n    register read**.  The committed vehicle is `sgi_re_width_probe.py`, whose six `ld kill` rows ARE\n    the witness -- **and they invert the day this round succeeds.**\n  * I also mis-cited `#508` for a packing hazard; `#508` is `alphapfnoffset` (the page walk dropping\n    the offset).  The packing hazard here is real and is a different defect.\n\n*** THE DESIGN THAT SURVIVES: FIVE ACCEPT, TEX SPLITS, MTE STAYS OUT. ***\nOne SEMANTIC class (a legal `ld` into an implemented window dies) but TWO members:\n  * **TLB_A/B/C and LINEAR_A/B -> member 4 ACCEPT.**  Stores already land and consumers exist\n    (`horrible_getputpixel` for the three TLBs and LINEAR_A; the MTE linear fill for LINEAR_A/B).\n    One round, one detector, five value rows.\n  * **TEX -> DECLINE, do NOT mirror.**  A mirrored `>> 3` read is the identity only for *write one\n    slot, read it back, touch nothing else*: a store at `+8` writes cells `[1..4]` and one at `+16`\n    writes `[2..5]`, so a mirrored read at `+8` returns a MIX.  **Mirroring also plants a green\n    round-trip that `sgitexindex` must later invert.**\n\n**AND A FIVE-ARM ROUND MAY NOT CLAIM \"the RE read arms no longer kill\"** -- the contract has to name\nTEX as still fatal.\n\n**THE ROWS ACTUALLY OWED, which is not \"six survival rows\":**\n\n    5 packed `ld' round-trips, one per ACCEPT arm            floor (b)/(d), packing-sensitive\n    the same five at a slot with bit 3 SET *and* at the       near AND far bound; `+8' alone is\n      window's LAST 8-byte slot                               the `#497' write-accept shape\n    5 pre-write `ld' -> 0                                     the memset ABI, distinct from\n                                                              \"the stored value came back\"\n    TEX: a 3-channel decline (how / contiguous TODO or new    a different member\n      latch text / sentinel -> 0)\n    RE-PIN all six existing `ld kill' rows FIRST              oracle-correction: today they want\n                                                              exit1 + text\n\n*Survival-only is enough for verbatim `exit(1)` reinstatement and NOT enough for the null-repair.*\n\n*** THE grok DESIGN ANSWER WAS UNREAD FOR A DAY, AND IT REFUTES FIVE OF THE BRIEF'S PREMISES --\nINCLUDING ONE THAT WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A DETECTOR THAT IS GREEN ON HEAD.  Read in full and\nSPOT-CHECKED 2026-09-07; artifact `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/grok_design2_20260907.txt`. ***\n\n**This is the fourth recorded instance of the unread-review class in this project, and by some\nmargin the most consequential: the answer names a floor-clause-(a) failure in the detector the round\nwas about to build.**\n\n### 1.  *** THE ACCEPT ROWS WERE ABOUT TO USE THE WRONG INSTRUCTION.  VERIFIED, NOT TAKEN ON TRUST. ***\n\nThe brief's S3 said *\"a guest `lw` kills\"*.  **It does not.**  Measured on the tree today:\n\n    regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py:196   def load(A, addr, op=OP_LW_V0_AT):      <- defaults to `lw'\n    src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:421,440,459    if (len != 8) {                     <- WIDTH CHECKED FIRST\n\nThe `len != 8` arm DECLINES, so a `lw` never reaches the direction kill.  **The kill is `ld`\n(len == 8).**  An accept suite written with `lw` rows would be **GREEN ON HEAD** -- vacuous by\nconstruction, floor clause (a), the detector-that-cannot-fail shape.  grok put it exactly: *\"Counting\n'one read per register' as six `lw`s gives a detector that is green on HEAD.\"*\n\n### 2.  THE COUNT IS WRONG FOR THE FOURTH TIME, AND THIS TIME THE DERIVER IS INNOCENT\n\n    1537|   if (len == 4) {\n    1542|   } else if (len != 4) {        <- the EXACT NEGATION of the if\n    1550|   } else {\n    1552|           exit(1);              <- PROVABLY UNREACHABLE\n\n`grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'` returns **12**, and it is right: there are twelve\n*statements*.  **One of them cannot run**, so there are **11 guest-reachable kills**.\n\n> **THE DERIVER RULE'S OWN LESSON, LANDING ON THE ROW THAT ALREADY EMBODIES IT.**  This title was\n> corrected twice for counting comments (21 -> 19 -> 12) and the deriver was added precisely so the\n> number would be re-derivable.  **It still says LIVE, and no text pattern can test reachability** --\n> so the deriver measures a CORRELATE of the class, which is the failure mode the rule names.  A\n> census keyed on what defines the class has to ask whether the arm can execute, and that is a\n> reading, not a grep.\n\n**AND TWO ROWS OF THIS QUEUE DISAGREE ABOUT ONE ARM.**  `sgimtewidth`'s own title calls `:1552`\n*\"its dead arm\"* -- so the knowledge was already in the ledger, under a different id, while this row\ncounted the same statement as live.  *Not a new fact; a fact filed where this row does not look.*\n\n### 3.  THE CITED IN-FILE PRECEDENT IS ITSELF A FILED DEFECT\n\nS2 claimed *\"the model already stores the right thing\"* for all six arms.  For TEX that warrant **is\n`sgitexindex`**: `re_tex` is written with `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, consecutive `sd`s\noverlap by three, 81 of 112 cells are unreachable, and there are ZERO consumers.  *Backing state\nexists; the correct answer does not.*  So TEX cannot be member 4 (ACCEPT) on today's stores.\n\n### 4.  THE BRIEF CITED THE WRONG PROBE AND THE WRONG ROUND NUMBER\n\n* S4 cited `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py`.  That is **uncommitted `#505` work that never issues an RE\n  register read at all.**  The committed vehicle is `sgi_re_width_probe.py`, whose six `ld kill`\n  rows **are** the witness -- and they must be **RE-PINNED FIRST**, because today they want\n  `exit1+txt` and a correct fix turns them red.  That is the oracle-correction rule's ORDER\n  obligation, and citing the width probe as an accept suite treats a filed obligation as discharged.\n* `#508` is `alphapfnoffset`, a different defect.  The packing hazard it was cited for is real; the\n  citation is not.\n\n### 5.  WHAT THE ROUND SHOULD BE (grok's design, recorded as the seat's position)\n\n**Five ACCEPT (TLB_A/B/C, LINEAR_A/B) + TEX DECLINE, latched, falling through to the tail.**  MTE\nstays OUT: different function, different trigger, different member, different detector.\n\n*A five-arm ACCEPT that leaves TEX fatal **cannot honestly take the headline** \"the RE read arms no\nlonger kill\" -- one of six still does.*\n\nThe packing is the whole substance of the fix, and a naive version is a null repair:\n\n    TLB_A/B/C   uint16_t[256], i = ((relative_addr & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff, FOUR BE halfwords\n                odata = (tlb[i]<<48) | (tlb[i+1]<<32) | (tlb[i+2]<<16) | tlb[i+3];\n                naive `odata = d->re_tlb_a[i]' keeps 16 of 64 bits\n    LINEAR_A/B  uint32_t[32],  i = ((relative_addr & 0x7f) >> 2) & 0x1f, TWO words\n                odata = ((uint64_t)lin[i] << 32) | lin[i+1];\n                naive form drops [i+1]\n\n**Do NOT mirror `>> 3` for TEX**: a mirrored read is identity only for write-one-slot-read-it-back;\na store at `+8` writes `[1..4]` and one at `+16` writes `[2..5]`, so a mirrored read returns a mix --\nand it plants a green round-trip that `sgitexindex` would later have to invert.  **Do not widen the\nTEX window toward `+0x100` either: `CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` is `CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS`, already `#499`.**\n\n### 6.  THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANTS IT ENUMERATED, all of which a SURVIVAL row leaves green\n\n    delete exit(1), never assign odata          -> host lives, guest reads 0\n    odata = d->re_tlb_a[i]                      -> host lives, 16 of 64 bits\n    LINEAR without [i+1]                        -> host lives, high word wrong\n    ACCEPT only at the window BASE              -> green while the probe ld's at base\n    shrink one window into the #499 catch-all   -> host lives, 0, catch-all text\n\n> **The oracle for the five ACCEPT arms is the VALUE, not `alive`** -- ACCEPT is a success-effect\n> site.  Use a pattern whose every halfword lane is load-bearing (`0x1122334455667788`, already\n> `ACC_VAL`); a value with zero lanes hides a 16-bit pack.  **TEX is the refusal-effect case** and\n> takes the `#499` three-channel shape (survival + contiguous announce + sentinel -> 0), not a\n> round-trip.  The in-tree proof that survival at a refusal site is vacuous is `#497`'s\n> `d_returnzero` 35/35 escape.\n\n**Site omission is the expected mutant and is why the detector needs SIX rows, not five:** leaving\nLINEAR_B's `else` fatal keeps every other row green.\n\n### 7.  RESIDUALS IT RAISED (filed, not actioned here)\n\n* **`:1404` must NOT be merged with `sgimtedepth4dead`.**  `depth = 8 << field` is 8/16/32/64 and\n  never 4; the dead `if (depth == 4)` is on the TILED path.  *\"Fixing depth == 4 alone would mask\n  the depth-32 leak and leave depth 16.\"*\n* Two further shapes sit beside the MTE five and are in NEITHER class: `dst_y_step` fatals with its\n  `exit` commented out, and `dst_tlb`'s `default:` fatals and CONTINUES.\n* Opening LINEAR reads makes `sgilinearoob` observable from a guest (`re_linear_a[32]` aliases\n  `re_linear_b[0]`) -- a residual witness, **not** a `put d` reproduction.\n* **The witness obligation for the read class is ALREADY DISCHARGED:** the six `ld kill` rows are\n  rung-3 at all six windows on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`.  What remains is oracle-correction\n  ORDER, not building a probe.\n\n*The brief also carried a stale HEAD (`7a41b3f` against the checkpoint's `a264a34`), which the seat\ncaught and worked around by verifying the arms in the tree rather than trusting the packet.*\n\n*** THE codex ROUND DESIGN, 2026-09-08.  It SPLITS the round, corrects a false correction of mine,\nand names a gate coupling that would have turned the battery red. ***\n\n### 1.  THE ROUND SPLITS: FIVE ACCEPT NOW, TEX DECLINE SEPARATELY\n\ngrok's design joined them.  codex refuses the join **using this project's own scope rule**: the five\nACCEPT sites share one contract (*\"reconstruct the complete stored value\"*) while TEX DECLINE is a\ndifferent one (*\"completed read returning zero, a specific diagnostic, and independent once-only\nsuppression\"*).  *\"Putting both contracts in one Python file does not establish 'same detector, no\nnew oracle'.  Your scope rule also requires ONE FIX DESIGN.\"*\n\nAnd it refuses the other direction too: **do not wait for the TEX index repair**, because that\n*\"unnecessarily couples read termination to backing-state corruption\"* -- and *\"declining TEX does\nnot close `sgitexindex`\"* either.\n\n### 2.  THE PACKING, with the index expressions stated against the ADJUSTED `relative_addr`\n\n    TLB_A/B/C   uint16_t H[256]   i = ((r & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff   four halfwords, highest first\n    LINEAR_A/B  uint32_t W[32]    i = ((r & 0x7f)  >> 2) & 0x1f   two words, highest first\n    TEX         (separate round)  no array read; leave odata == 0, announce via its own latch\n\n### 3.  *** A GATE COUPLING THAT WOULD HAVE TURNED THE BATTERY RED.  VERIFIED HERE. ***\n\n`regress/gate_arm.sh:1181` names **all six `ld kill` rows by label** in its one-line row list, and\n`:1185-1190` **pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves** (35 emitted AND all 35 green, deliberately\ntwo checks so a LOST row is distinguishable from a FAILING one).\n\n> **So re-pinning those rows in the probe WITHOUT editing the gate leaves the gate RED.**  codex:\n> *\"A successful probe edit can otherwise leave the gate red.\"*  **The round owes a gate edit\n> alongside the detector edit**, and the gate's own comments at `:1178-1180` warn about precisely\n> this family -- an unsatisfiable check whose row quietly stops being tested, and a row list that\n> once gained a bogus word and shipped a red gate.\n\n### 4.  *** C4 WAS A FALSE CORRECTION AND I RECORDED IT WITHOUT CHECKING. ***\n\nI wrote that `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py` is *\"uncommitted `#505` work\"*, on grok's say-so.  **It is\ncommitted** -- `git ls-files` lists it, last changed in `7a41b3f`.  One command would have settled\nit and I ran none.\n\n**grok's OTHER half survives and is the load-bearing one:** the RGB probe writes RE state and\nobserves framebuffer memory *without reading RE registers*, so `sgi_re_width_probe.py`'s six\n`ld kill` rows remain the relevant witness.  *A correction can be half right, and the half I\nrepeated was the checkable half.*\n\n### 5.  C2, C3 AND C5 CONFIRMED, each with a better derivation than mine\n\n* **C2** -- 12 active statements, inspected by enclosing control flow: **six RE read exits, five in\n  `do_mte_transfer`, one unreachable** in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_mte)`, whose chain really is\n  `len == 4` / `len != 4` / `else`.  **11 reachable exit sites file-wide, six in the RE handler** --\n  *\"source-derived reachability, not eleven fresh demonstrations\"*, which is the honest label.\n* **C3** -- all 28 aligned TEX positions enumerated: starting indices 0-27, touched cells 0-30, so\n  **31 touched and 81 untouched**, and no functional consumer of `re_tex`.\n* **C5** -- the ordering is right, but **its scope depends on the round**: for five ACCEPT sites,\n  re-pin those FIVE and leave TEX's existing expectation until its own round.  The baseline RED\n  measurement under the revised expectations is still owed.\n\n### 6.  A SEVENTH NAMED RE REGION -- and the distinction it forces\n\n`CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS` (`crmfbreg.h:234`, `0x16e0`) is a named region with **no dedicated handler arm**;\nit falls into the existing catch-all.  Verified: zero occurrences in `dev_sgi_re.c`.  **So \"six\ndedicated read-kill ARMS\" is correct and \"only six named RE REGIONS\" would be false.**\n\n*** THE STANDING RULINGS THAT GOVERN THIS ROW, COPIED IN 2026-09-08 -- because the round before this\none was designed against a ruling that lived only in the CHANGELOG, and the fix for that\n(`rulingnotinrow`) is to put the disposition WHERE THE DECIDER LOOKS. ***\n\n**1.  THIS ROUND IS SANCTIONED.**  The 2026-09-02 flagship adjudication ranks it:\n\n> *\"2. `sgirekills` (high, open) -- named for honesty, not scheduled here: the six RE read arms are\n> the same live-kill severity and **the natural next round**.\"*\n\n**There is no do-not-code here** -- the opposite.  *(I checked because the previous round was\nstopped by one, not because I expected one.)*\n\n**2.  WHAT THE \"SIX-WINDOW SINGLE ROUND REFUSED\" RULING ACTUALLY FORBIDS, and it is NOT this row.**\n\n> *\"Q2.1 six-window single round -- REFUSED.  It bundles at least three semantic classes (read-exit\n> de-fatalisation, index scale, OOB bounds), which the round-scope rule forbids.  The file's own\n> comment already partitions those windows across named rows: `sgirekills`, `sgireunmapped`,\n> `sgilinearoob`, `sgidewidth`, `sgirelatchenum`.\"*\n\n**The refusal is of a round bundling ACROSS THOSE ROWS.**  `sgirekills` alone is ONE class --\nread-exit de-fatalisation -- and is permitted.  *That also independently vindicates the codex design\nanswer, which split TEX out for a different reason (its ACCEPT depends on `sgitexindex`, which is\nunder a DO-NOT-CODE).*\n\n**3.  THE ORDERING OBLIGATION IS ALREADY RECORDED AND IS NOT OPTIONAL.**  From the `#497` block:\n\n> *\"**Fixing `sgirekills` will turn a green gate RED by succeeding**, six rows' worth, so it inherits\n> the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin first, measure the committed build red, then edit.\"*\n\n**And codex independently found the mechanism:** `regress/gate_arm.sh:1181` names all six `ld kill`\nrows by label and `:1185-1190` pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves.  **So the round owes a GATE\nEDIT alongside the detector edit**, or a successful fix leaves the battery red.\n\n> **THE ROUND, as it now stands: five ACCEPT (TLB_A/B/C, LINEAR_A/B) with the packed inverse of each\n> store loop; TEX DECLINED and latched rather than ACCEPTed, because its index repair is barred; the\n> six `ld kill` rows RE-PINNED FIRST and measured RED on the committed build; and `gate_arm.sh`'s row\n> list and denominator updated in the same commit.**\n\n*** THE RE-PIN TEMPLATE IS ALREADY IN THE FILE, MEASURED, WITH ITS VACUOUS ALTERNATIVES NAMED.\n`#499` did this exact re-pin one arm over (`other decl`, `sgi_re_width_probe.py`), and its comment\nis the round's instructions. ***\n\n**IT PINS THREE THINGS, and the comment says why each is load-bearing:**\n\n    how   the host SURVIVES the access\n    txt   ONE CONTIGUOUS literal including the address and data fields.  *\"Two independent\n          substring tests are a weaker claim\"* -- `-- declined.` is emitted by the six `#497`\n          width declines AND by `#498`'s sgi_de decline, so a bare substring cross-matches.\n    v0    the access REACHES THE COMMON TAIL and lands a DEFINED value.  **`v0` is preset to\n          SENTINEL by `load()`**, so \"wrote odata\" and \"never landed\" are distinguishable.\n\n*** AND IT NAMES THE ORACLES IT MEASURED VACUOUS, WHICH IS WHAT SAVES THIS ROUND A TABLE: ***\n\n* **`how` ALONE IS VACUOUS.**  Variant `mexit0` -- reinstate the death as `exit(0)` -- prints the\n  announce and then dies, and *\"exit status CANNOT distinguish 'declined, then quit' from 'died with\n  status 0'\"*.  The `exit0+txt` oracle scored GREEN with `alive=False`.  Filed as `exit0oracle`.\n* **A WRITE-ONLY oracle misses two more:** `mret0` (`return 0` -> `memory_rw.c` coerces to -1 ->\n  EXCEPTION_DBE) and `mrdexit0` (`exit(0)` in the READ branch only).  **Only the `v0` channel sees\n  them.**\n\n**THE ORDER PROOF THAT ROUND USED IS THE ONE THIS ROUND OWES:** its new oracle was measured RED on\nthe COMMITTED pre-fix build BEFORE the code changed, and it was PRE-REGISTERED six hours before the\nbaseline -- *\"not fitted to the fix either\"*.\n\n> **SO THE PLAN, and every element of it is now sourced rather than invented:**\n> 1. **RE-PIN the six `ld kill` rows FIRST** -- they are generated by ONE loop at\n>    `sgi_re_width_probe.py:269-274` all wanting `exit1+txt`.  Five become value round-trips\n>    (`how`/`v0` against the packed inverse of each store loop); TEX becomes a DECLINE\n>    (`how`/contiguous-literal/`v0 == 0`), because its ACCEPT is barred by `sgitexindex`'s\n>    DO-NOT-CODE.\n> 2. **MEASURE THE COMMITTED BUILD RED** under the new expectations -- same script, opposite\n>    colour -- and preserve that artifact.\n> 3. **ONLY THEN** edit `dev_sgi_re.c`: five ACCEPT arms packing four BE halfwords (TLB) or two\n>    words (LINEAR), TEX declined and latched.\n> 4. **EDIT `gate_arm.sh` IN THE SAME COMMIT** -- `:1181` names all six `ld kill` rows by label and\n>    `:1185-1190` pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves, so a successful fix leaves the gate RED\n>    otherwise.\n> 5. Panel pass 2 on the diff; the flagship stages stay HELD for the 09-09 adjudication.\n\n*** THE STEP-1 RE-PIN PANEL REFUSED MY ROW SET AND SUPPLIED A BETTER ONE.  grok, 2026-09-08:\n\"do not write the proposed six-row re-pin.\" ***\n\n**TWO FLOOR-SHAPED HOLES IN WHAT I PROPOSED**, plus one oracle this file had already measured wrong:\n\n1. **The read-side FAR BOUND is unpinned.**  A row at the window BASE tests the near bound twice and\n   the far bound never -- the accept-row rule, which I QUOTED IN THE BRIEF and then did not apply to\n   my own row set.\n2. *** THE TEX TRIPWIRE WOULD HAVE BEEN VACUOUS, and this is the sharpest thing in the answer: a\n   LOAD-ONLY decline row wants v0 == 0, and a future ACCEPT round returning the ZEROED ARRAY also\n   yields 0.  The row could not redden when TEX later becomes ACCEPT. ***  That is exactly the\n   question I asked as Q3 and could not answer.  **The fix is to STORE a non-zero value first**, so\n   an ACCEPT would return it and the row goes red.\n3. **exit0 is the wrong oracle** and #499 measured why: an exit(0) reinstatement prints and dies,\n   scoring green with the process dead.  **Survival is implied by the VALUE.**\n\n*** AND MY Q2 WAS NOT AN OPEN QUESTION. ***  I asked whether an anchored form makes one row per arm\nenough instead of two.  **#499 already measured on this same device that it does not.**  Asking it\nas open was a failure to read the record I was otherwise citing.\n\n### THE ROW SET TO WRITE -- 14 rows replacing 6\n\n**FIVE ACCEPT ARMS, TWO ROWS EACH (window base and last slot):**\n\n    tlb_a ld lo    0xb5001000    store VAL_LO then ld    v0 == VAL_LO, TODO-text ABSENT,\n                                                         store debug re_tlb_a[0] = ... present\n    tlb_a ld hi    0xb50011f8    store VAL_HI then ld    same shape, debug re_tlb_a[252] = ...\n    tlb_b, tlb_c   +0x200/+0x400                         the same pair; indices still 0 and 252\n    linear_a lo/hi 0xb5001700 / 0xb5001778               debug re_linear_a[0]/[1], [30]/[31]\n    linear_b lo/hi 0xb5001780 / 0xb50017f8               debug re_linear_b[0]/[1], [30]/[31]\n\n**VAL_LO may stay 0x1122334455667788; VAL_HI MUST DIFFER IN EVERY HALFWORD LANE** (e.g.\n0xa1b2c3d4e5f60718), so a lane-swapping edit cannot round-trip.  **Want the SAME 64-bit value that\nwas stored**, not a separately invented packed encoding -- *inventing one would re-implement the\npacking inside the test and grade the code against my arithmetic instead of against the store.*\n\n*** THE ANCHOR IS THE STORE'S OWN debug(), WHICH NAMES THE INDEX. ***  That is what makes these rows\nABSOLUTE rather than relative: re_tlb_a[252] = ... pins WHICH CELL, so a translation that slides the\nwindow reddens.  **And no new debug() goes on the read path** -- the store's existing one is the\nexternal channel, and adding a print would be an unlicensed addition.\n\n**TEX -- FOUR ROWS, decline, and NOT the #499 template:**\n\n    tex ld lo     store VAL_LO at 0xb5001600 then ld    v0 == 0x0, ONE CONTIGUOUS TEX-read-decline\n                                                        literal, TODO-text absent\n    tex ld hi     the same at the last slot 0xb50016d8  same, and the TEX literal -- NOT the\n                                                        catch-all unimplemented-read literal\n    tex rd lat1   two ld's at two different offsets     TEX-read literal count == 1\n    tex xbit      fire the other five width latches     TEX-read literal still appears exactly once\n                  first, then a TEX ld\n\n*The xbit row is the latch-collision shape #490 measured in another file, applied here before it can\nbite.*\n\n*** PASS 2: SIX SEATS, ALL SIX ANSWERED, THREE SAID DO NOT SHIP -- AND TWO OF THE THREE WERE\nRIGHT. ***\n\n    codex     DO NOT SHIP   an L2 FLOOR BREACH, with a predicted score and its mechanism\n    grok      DO NOT SHIP   records; and it re-measured every claim against the tree\n    glm       DO NOT SHIP   FALSE ALARM -- but it named the settling fact instead of guessing\n    kimi      SHIP, cond.   the enum record -- a real condition, now met\n    deepseek  SHIP\n    minimax   SHIP\n\n## THE L2 -- a clause-(d) floor breach, repaired in-round\n\n`tex xbit` fires the six WIDTH latches only, so it sweeps bits 0-5.  codex named the escape:\n\n    SGIRE_W_TEX_RD = 1 << 11  ->  1 << 8  (= SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD)      PREDICTED 44/44\n\nand gave the reason from the detector's own session schedule -- **the four TEX-read scenarios and\nthe unmapped-read scenario never share an emulator instance**, so bit 8 is clear whenever TEX is\nread.  *\"M10 establishes detection of a width-bit collision, not every existing-bit collision\"* --\nwhich is exactly the over-generalisation my CHANNEL table had made in a parenthesis.\n\n**Repaired with two SAME-INSTANCE rows, `tex/unmap` and `unmap/tex`, in BOTH orderings** (one\nordering would leave the mirror open -- the `#499` shape).  Gate raised 44 -> 46 in the same edit.\n\n## THREE WRONG RECORDS, ALL MINE, ALL CORRECTED IN PLACE\n\n* **the enum comment** -- *\"The next free bit is 1 << 11; the enum ends HERE on purpose\"* went false\n  twice over when this diff took `1 << 11`.  *** THE FILE DOCUMENTS THIS EXACT FAILURE TEN LINES\n  ABOVE WHERE I EDITED *** -- the identical claim on `SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP` went false when\n  `SGIRE_W_BADMODE` was added below it, and that comment records both the failure and the repair.\n  **I repeated a documented failure while editing the enum that documents it.**  Found by kimi,\n  confirmed independently by codex and grok.\n* **my LINEAR comment** claimed the `len == 8` guard \"keeps tlbi even\".  **Width does not imply\n  alignment** -- the guard tests LENGTH; CPU-side alignment is what keeps it even, and the OLDER\n  surrounding comment says so correctly.  My new wording was worse than the one it sat beside.\n* **the probe's contract header** still described six live read kills and row names this round\n  deleted -- clause (e), the thing a future reader trusts about what the detector claims.\n\n## *** A CLAIM OF MINE WAS CONFOUNDED, AND I HAD REPEATED IT ALL EVENING ***\n\nI said `M8_texaccept` measures the store-first TEX design.  codex: **M8 also removes the announce**,\nso its four failures do not isolate the store-first contribution -- both channels red under it.\nIsolating it needs a mutant that converts TEX to ACCEPT while KEEPING the announce.  *The\nstore-first design is still right for the reason grok gave at design time; what is not established\nis that M8 measured it.*\n\n## THE `odata` GAP -- three seats, independently\n\ncodex, glm and grok each found the same missing cell in my (d) enumeration.  **Coverage is\nunaffected** -- `M2_datafill` IS that mutant and it kills -- but the ENUMERATION could not have\ngenerated it.  Corrected in `PRETABLE.out` as an L4.\n\n## WHAT glm'S FALSE ALARM ACTUALLY FOUND -- a defect in MY BRIEF\n\nIt reasoned from HUNK BOUNDARIES that `check \"sgi_re controls live\"` sits in no hunk while the\ncontrol count went 2 -> 3, and enumerated two ways that could break the gate.  Reading the line\nsettles it: the check greps `OPUS497T_CONTROL=OK`, does not count controls, and the probe emits\nthat token over all three.  grok reached the same resolution independently and refused to ratify\nthe inference.\n\n> **A DIFF-ONLY PACKET OMITS UNCHANGED CONTEXT BY CONSTRUCTION, so a seat asking \"is the\n> surrounding code still consistent with this change?\" -- which is what pass 2 is FOR -- cannot\n> see it.**  That is the sketch rule one level up: the sketch rule says inline the enclosing\n> FUNCTION for a proposed edit; this says inline the enclosing BLOCK for a reviewed one.\n\n*Not proposed as a general rule on one instance: the seat named its settling fact, the cost was one\n`sed`, and a brief that inlines every adjacent block is a brief nobody reads.*\n\n*** THE L2 REPAIR IS MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED.  L2VERIFY.txt, scratch clone, frozen instrument. ***\n\n    BASE_pass2        46/46   the repaired detector, CLEAN\n    M13_latchcollide  44/46   KILLED   SGIRE_W_TEX_RD 1<<11 -> 1<<8 (= SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD)\n\n**46 - 44 = exactly TWO red rows -- precisely `tex/unmap` and `unmap/tex`, the two the repair\nadded, with nothing else perturbed.**  That is the cleanest possible signature for a targeted\nrepair: the new rows catch the named escape and no existing row's score moved.\n\n*** ONE THING THIS DOES NOT SHOW, and it is stated rather than glossed: the panel PREDICTED 44/44\nagainst the PRE-REPAIR 44-row detector, and that prediction was NOT re-measured. ***  The 44-row\nform no longer exists once the two rows are added, so the escape itself is a PREDICTION with a\nmechanism (the four TEX-read scenarios and the unmapped-read scenario never share an instance;\n`tex xbit` sets bits 0-5 only), not a measured score.  **What is measured is that the repaired\ndetector kills it.**  Re-measuring the escape would mean rebuilding the 44-row detector to prove a\nhole I have already closed -- and the mechanism is checkable by reading the probe's session list,\nwhich is how the panel derived it.\n\nClone left `cmp`-CLEAN against SEC with no sentinel.\n\n*** THE FOURTH RECORDED INSTANCE OF THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, AND THE MOST EXPENSIVE: IT COST AN\nENTIRE EXTRA PASS.  Mine, on pass 2, found by grok on pass 3. ***\n\nPass 3 opened with four seats saying DO NOT CLOSE THE CHAIN, and grok's answer contained the line:\n\n> *\"The grok pass-2 seat already named bit 10 (`SGIRE_W_BADMODE`); that cell is still open.\"*\n\n**It had.**  grok's PASS-2 answer says, in full:\n\n> *\"`tex xbit` does `sw` at the six `SITES` bases, then one TEX `ld`.  That fires\n> `SGIRE_W_TLBA_LEN` ... `SGIRE_W_LINB_LEN` and nothing else.  **`SGIRE_W_BADMODE` (1<<10),\n> `SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD` (1<<8), `SGIRE_W_DE_LEN` (1<<6), `SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP` (1<<9) are never set in\n> that session.**  `other decl` is a different process.  M10 only tested the WIDTH alias the row\n> actually populates.\"*\n\n**grok named FOUR bits on pass 2.  codex named ONE (bit 8).  I acted on codex's single cell,\nshipped a repair for it, and pass 3 then found the other three -- which one pass could have\nclosed.**\n\n*** AND I HAD THE FILE OPEN. ***  I read grok's pass-2 answer far enough to quote its resolution of\nglm's `controls live` objection -- which sits about twenty lines ABOVE the passage quoted here --\nand stopped at the premises table.  **This is not \"a seat answered and nobody looked\"; it is \"I\nlooked, took the part that confirmed my reading, and stopped before the part that would have cost\nme work.\"**  That is the harder version of the class and the one no checker can catch.\n\n**THE MEASURED COST:** one full extra pass -- a six-seat panel, a rebuild, a mutant table, and the\nclass-closure amendment invoked -- to reach a repair grok had specified a pass earlier.  It also\nmeans my pass-2 record said *\"repaired in-round\"* about a repair that closed one cell of five.\n\n> **THE HABIT THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT is the one this project already has and I did not apply:\n> READ THE BODY, NOT THE VERDICT LINE AND NOT THE FIRST SCREEN.**  The carrier states it for\n> verdict lines -- *\"a verdict line is written against the brief's framing and is the LEAST likely\n> part of an answer to hold the correction\"* -- and the same is true of an answer's opening\n> section, which addresses the brief's questions in the brief's order.  **grok's finding was in\n> its Q1 body, past the premises table.**\n\n*Recorded here rather than in a records row because the cost landed on THIS round, and because the\ninstance is specific: two seats answered the same question and I integrated the narrower answer\nwithout diffing it against the wider one.  When two seats answer one question, DIFF THEM.*\n\n*** A READING SEAT FOUND A LIVE BUG THAT A GREEN 46/46 RUN HAD JUST ENDORSED.  This is the most\nimportant thing in the round. ***\n\n`store()` is `store(A, addr, op=OP_SW_T0_A3, val=\"0x12345678\")`.  I wrote the six drawing-engine\nsetup calls as\n\n    cmds += store(A, DE_MODE_DST, MODE_LIN_B)\n\n**so MODE_LIN_B was assembled as an INSTRUCTION and the register received the default value.  All\nsix were wrong the same way.**  The row executed garbage opcodes and performed none of the register\nwrites its own comment described.\n\n*** AND IT SCORED 46/46. ***  `BASE_pass3b` came back CLEAN with the broken setup, because the row's\nonly oracle was the TEX decline count -- which garbage stores do not disturb.  **A row that passes\nwhile doing nothing it claims is exactly the vacuity the floor exists to prevent**, and no\nmeasurement could see it: the in-flight mutant run then scored `M_bit9` 46/46 ESCAPED, *which is\nwhat a no-op setup predicts*, and I would have blamed my choice of `PRIM_BAD` rather than my call.\n\n> **THE STRUCTURAL DEFECT, which is worth more than the typo: A CROSS-BIT ROW WHOSE ORACLE CANNOT\n> SEE ITS OWN PRECONDITIONS IS ONE TYPO AWAY FROM VACUOUS, PERMANENTLY.**  Counting only the TEX\n> decline cannot distinguish *\"ten latches fired and TEX still announced\"* from *\"the setup did\n> nothing and TEX announced\"*.  Both give 1.\n\n**REPAIRED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS:** the six calls now pass their value as `val`, and the row now\nASSERTS ITS OWN SETUP -- it counts how many of the eleven other announces actually appeared and\nwants all of them, so a silently-failing setup (a mistyped call, a moved register, a changed\nmessage) REDS the row instead of passing it.\n\n*** THE POINT ABOUT SEAT ROLES: THIS WAS FOUND BY READING, NOT BY MEASURING, AND NO MUTANT COULD\nHAVE FOUND IT. ***  Every mutant is scored against the same broken row, so the bug is invisible to\nthe entire kill table.  It took a seat reading the helper's signature against the call site.  *The\ncarrier's \"a reading seat buys the CLASS, a measuring seat buys the INSTANCE\" understates it here:\nsome defects are visible ONLY to reading.*\n\n**codex also returned EIGHT further record defects on the same brief**, several in text this round\nwrote: the contract header still says an 8-byte `ld` \"STILL ENDS THE HOST\" one paragraph below my\ncorrection saying it does not; two probe sites still say \"every read arm here still exit(1)s\" and\n\"An 8-byte read still dies\"; my own \"No row below reads exit status\" is false of the `other decl`\nrow, which uses `how`; and \"these four TEX rows\" have the store-first shape when only two do.\n**Those are being fixed; they are listed here so the count is honest rather than rounded down.**\n\n*The brief was framed to avoid both known codex triggers -- \"no mutant, no evasion, no guest\nsequence\" -- and it answered with zero refusals.  That framing is now a proven third form alongside\ndiff review and source analysis.*\n\n*** THE LATCH-COLLISION CLASS IS CLOSED, MEASURED, WITH THE BEFORE/AFTER PRESERVED. ***\n\n    CLASSVERIFY.txt    M_bit6 45/46 KILLED   M_bit7 45/46 KILLED\n                       M_bit9 46/46 ESCAPED  M_bit10 46/46 ESCAPED   <- disclosed at the time\n    CLASSVERIFY2       M_bit9 46/46 ESCAPED  -- against the BROKEN row, so this is evidence of\n                       the store bug and NOT of an open cell\n    CLASSVERIFY3       M_bit9 45/46 KILLED   M_bit10 45/46 KILLED    <- fixed, self-checking row\n\n**Every one of the eleven other used bits of `d->warned` is now covered**: bits 0-5 by `M10`, bit 8\nby `tex/unmap` + `unmap/tex`, bits 6, 7, 9 and 10 by the widened `tex xbit`.\n\n*** AND `BASE_fixed` 46/46 MEANS MORE THAN THE EARLIER 46/46s. ***  The row wants `1/11` -- the TEX\ndecline once AND all eleven other announces present -- so a clean run now PROVES the setup fired.\nThe earlier clean runs did not: they scored a row whose six drawing-engine stores passed their VALUE\nas the OPCODE.\n\n**THE DISCLOSURE WAS WITHDRAWN AND REPLACED BY COVERAGE**, which also dissolves the rule conflict I\nhad escalated for the adjudication: the class-closure amendment permits disclosing uncovered cells,\nkimi and grok argued an in-closure cell on the edited line is a breach regardless, and rather than\nhave that adjudicated I closed the cells.  *The \"settling fact\" I had written down as unknown was\none grep away, in a shipped probe that already drives the drawing engine.*\n\n*** A WRONG RECORD IN A MEASUREMENT LOG, CORRECTED IN PLACE. ***  `CLASSVERIFY3.txt` carried its\ndriver's template footer from the previous run, saying bits 9 and 10 *\"ESCAPE BY DESIGN and are\nDISCLOSED\"* -- **directly above two KILLED results**.  A generated artifact whose boilerplate\ncontradicts its own numbers is the wrong-record class arriving in the one place a future round\ntrusts most.  *The driver writes that text unconditionally; the fix here is the artifact, and\nteaching the driver to derive its footer from the results is filed rather than done -- it is a\ntooling change owing its own controls.*\n\n*** PASS 4 -- THE CLOSING PASS.  FIVE SEATS, 3-2 TO CLOSE, AND I AM NOT CLOSING ON THE COUNT. ***\n\n    grok      YES, the chain closes -- 11/11, verified against the LIVE SOURCE\n    glm       CLOSE, conditionally -- 11/11\n    minimax   CHAIN CLOSES -- 11/11\n    deepseek  NO\n    kimi      DO NOT CLOSE AS MEASURED\n\n**Count the arguments, not the seats.**  Two of the objections do not survive and one does.\n\n## *** THE ONE THAT SURVIVES, AND IT TURNS THIS ROUND'S OWN DOCTRINE ON ME ***\n\nkimi: *\"against the FINAL, self-checking row, exactly **2 of 11** aliasing mutants have a logged\nkill -- the other nine were measured against earlier versions of the very row that kills them, and\nthis round's own doctrine (value-as-opcode scoring 46/46 while doing nothing) forbids carrying kills\nacross a row edit by construction.\"*\n\n**That is unanswerable.**  I proved that a probe edit can silently void a measurement, wrote it up as\nthis round's central lesson, and then presented nine kills carried across exactly such an edit.  Its\nformulation is the right one: **\"close every cell in ONE diff\" deserves \"kill every cell on ONE\nprobe version.\"**  Running as `SWEEP11` -- eleven aliases, one frozen probe, one table.\n\n## THE TWO THAT DO NOT SURVIVE, and why -- stated so the dissent is not lost\n\n* **deepseek: \"the widened row tests only one ordering for bits 6,7,9,10.\"**  The row's TEX load is\n  LAST, so under an alias the setup fires the bit FIRST and the TEX announce is suppressed -> count\n  0 -> red.  The mirror (TEX suppressing a setup announce) would show as `fired == 10` -> also red.\n  **A single-bit alias is symmetric; catching it in one ordering kills it.**  The bit-8 pair needed\n  both orderings because those are two DIFFERENT sites, not one bit.\n* **deepseek's Q2 escape** posits a session ordered TEX-read-first.  **The row's order is fixed in\n  the probe and is not reachable by a mutant of the DEVICE**, which is what the table perturbs.\n* **deepseek/kimi: \"enumeration exclusivity is not checkable from the packet.\"**  Correct about the\n  packet, and now SETTLED from source, twice independently -- my own derivation and grok's:\n  twelve enumerators `1<<0`..`1<<11`, no duplicates, no thirteenth, twelve latch sites all on\n  `d->warned`, no raw `|= 1<<k`, and no second bitmask in `struct sgi_re_data`.\n\n## *** A DEFECT INSIDE ONE OF MY CORRECTIONS, FOUND BY TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY ***\n\ngrok and kimi both caught it.  My in-diff note said `tex rd lat1` and `tex xbit` *\"would not redden\non an ACCEPT\"*.  **False: both oracle the decline-message COUNT, which an ACCEPT drives to 0, so both\ngo red.**  What they cannot do is distinguish an ACCEPT from a DELETED MESSAGE -- only the value pair\ncan.  **The correction UNDERSTATED the detection surface**, which is the same wrong-record class this\nround has spent the evening fixing, arriving inside the fix for it.  Corrected; and grok's\ndisposition is adopted -- *\"fix the sentence when convenient; do not reopen the chain for it.\"*\n\n**kimi's Q3 residual, kept because it is right and unfixed:** the self-check tests `lit in t`, which\nis EMISSION not LATCHING, and the literals are unanchored substrings.  So the `/11` half proves the\nmessages appeared, not that any bit was set.  For this class that is harmless -- the kill signal is\nTEX's own count -- but the contract should say so rather than imply the row verifies latching.\n\n*** THE CHAIN CLOSES.  ELEVEN OF ELEVEN CELLS KILLED ON ONE PROBE VERSION, WITH THE DENOMINATOR.\nSWEEP11.txt. ***\n\n    SWEEP_BASE   46/46   the final self-checking probe, CLEAN\n    bits 0-7, 9, 10      45/46 each   red: `tex xbit`\n    bit 8                43/46        red: `tex xbit`, `tex/unmap`, `unmap/tex`\n    NOT KILLED: none\n\n**kimi's pass-4 objection is discharged BY MEASUREMENT, not by argument.**  It held that only 2 of\n11 aliases had a logged kill against the FINAL row, and that the other nine were carried across a\nrow edit -- which this round's own doctrine forbids, having proved that a probe edit can silently\nvoid a measurement.  **Every cell is now killed on one frozen probe, in one run.**\n\n*** BIT 8 IS THE MOST INFORMATIVE ROW IN THE TABLE, AND IT WAS NOT DESIGNED TO BE. ***  It reds\nTHREE rows rather than one, because `SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD` is exactly what the two pass-2 ordering rows\ntarget.  **So a single mutant demonstrates that the pass-2 repair and the pass-3 class closure are\nboth live and INDEPENDENT on the same probe version** -- which no separate table could have shown,\nbecause each was previously measured against its own instrument.\n\n**THE CLOSING PASS IS VALID BY ITS OWN RULE:** the diff was delivered and inlined, the roster is\nrecorded with its misses named (codex deliberately not fired -- the ask-again shape), and the zero\ncarries its denominator -- **eleven cells, enumerated from the twelve used bits of `d->warned`,\nderived from source twice independently rather than asserted.**\n\n*What closed it was not the 3-2 vote.  Three seats said close and two did not; both dissents were\nadjudicated on their arguments -- one refuted from source, one right and now measured away.\nCounting seats would have closed the chain an hour early on a denominator of 2 of 11.*\n\n*** THIS ROW'S CENSUS IS STALE BY A FACTOR OF TWO, AND THE BLOCKER IT IS BUILT AROUND IS ALREADY\nDISCHARGED.  Found by a codex ANALYSIS job 2026-09-08, VERIFIED here twice. ***\n\ncodex, on the current files: *\"The current files contradict the historical counts: I find SIX active\n`exit(1)` statements, and the probe's six former death rows have already been replaced.\"*\n\n**DERIVED, not transcribed -- and my FIRST attempt was wrong, which is worth recording because it is\nthe deriver rule landing on me again.**  A line-prefix test (*does this line start with a comment\nmarker?*) reported SEVEN, because `dev_sgi_re.c:259` is a CONTINUATION line of a block comment that\nbegins with neither `/*` nor `*`.  **I classified by a CORRELATE of being commented rather than by\nbeing commented.**  Stripping block and line comments properly, preserving line numbers:\n\n    ACTIVE exit( in dev_sgi_re.c:  6      :1463  :1471  :1547  :1552  :1563  :1685\n    (six further `exit(' texts are inside comments, several of them prose ABOUT this very row)\n\n**So the title's \"TWELVE exit(1) STATEMENTS and ELEVEN GUEST-REACHABLE KILLS\" is stale by 2x.**  The\nintervening rounds -- `#497`'s width de-fatalisation and `#499`'s work in the same file -- removed\nor commented six of them, and this row's headline was never re-derived.\n\n*** THE MORE IMPORTANT HALF: THE ORDERING OBLIGATION THIS ROW IS ORGANISED AROUND NO LONGER EXISTS. ***\n\nThe row says, in capitals: *\"FIXING sgirekills WILL TURN A GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING, SIX ROWS'\nWORTH ... it therefore inherits the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin the six rows FIRST.\"*\n\n**MEASURED against the shipped probe:**\n\n    grep -c 'ld kill'                        regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py   ->  5\n    grep -n 'row(' ... | grep -i kill        ->  ZERO row() calls.  All five hits are COMMENTS,\n                                                 and both are written in the PAST TENSE:\n        :370  \"THE OLD ROWS were `row(nm + \\\" ld kill\\\", how + txt, \\\"exit1+txt\\\")' -- they asserted\"\n        :527  \"THE OLD ROW was `row(\\\"other kill\\\", how, \\\"exit1\\\")' -- DEATH-ONLY.\"\n\n**There are no death-only rows left to re-pin.**  A later round already replaced them -- the probe's\nown header says so at `:33`: *\"CRIME_RE_TEX became a latched decline -- so `<s> ld kill' x6 NO LONGER\nEXIST\"*.  So the oracle-correction ordering this row inherits is **already satisfied**, and the\nsequencing constraint that made it look expensive is gone.\n\n> **THE ROW IS THEREFORE CHEAPER AND SMALLER THAN IT READS, AND BOTH ERRORS POINT THE SAME WAY:\n> toward over-stating the work.**  A future round reading the headline plans for eleven kills and a\n> six-row re-pin; the real job is six kills with no re-pin owed.\n\n*** THIS IS THE CARRIER'S OWN COUNT RULE, EARNED RATHER THAN QUOTED: \"the rounds that consult the\nnumber are the rounds that change it.\"  `#497` and `#499` both edited this file, and each left this\nrow's headline one step further from the source. ***\n\n**WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and codex said so itself:** it could give *\"a defensive census and\nguard-order analysis\"* but *\"can't map guest-controlled values or access sequences to\nhost-termination paths\"* from the three permitted files.  **So the count of ACTIVE exits is\nmeasured; the count of GUEST-REACHABLE ones is not**, and the title's \"eleven guest-reachable\" has no\ncurrent replacement figure.  The row should carry the six with their guards and leave reachability\nopen until it is measured, rather than swapping one unverified number for another.", "witness": "probe:regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py -- the six `<site> ld kill` rows are rung-3 symptom demonstrations of THIS defect: a real guest `ld` on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`, exit1 with per-site attribution text, at all six windows.  Shipped by #497, gate-run, and re-measured on every battery.  The pass-1 26/26 read-dimension probe is committed beside them at regress/artifacts/widthkillclass/opus_p1_20260830/.", "commits": "e302c98"}
{"id": "sgimtewidth", "sev": "med", "state": "open", "title": "sgi_mte serves EVERY access width and a one-byte store silently ZEROES the adjacent register -- and the obvious fix for its dead arm would CREATE a width kill", "note": "*** A ONE-BYTE GUEST STORE TO AN MTE REGISTER WRITES THE REGISTER **AND SILENTLY ZEROES ITS NEIGHBOUR**.  Found by the fable5 seat under the standing license, verified here by reading the arms. ***  The handler is `if (len == 4) {...} else if (len != 4) {...} else {...}`.  The second arm does:      d->mte_reg[regnr+1] = idata >> 32;      d->mte_reg[regnr]   = idata;  and it is reached by EVERY width that is not 4 -- including 1 and 2.  For a one-byte store `idata` is a byte, so `idata >> 32` is **0**, and the store clobbers `mte_reg[regnr+1]` with zero.  No diagnostic, no decline: the device quietly loses a register the guest never addressed.  **THIS IS A MIS-SERVICE, NOT A KILL**, which is why it is med and not high: nothing ends the host, and the corruption is confined to emulated device state.  *** THE TRAP, AND IT IS THE REASON TO FILE THIS NOW RATHER THAN WHEN SOMEONE TOUCHES IT: the dead third arm is `fatal(\"[ sgi_mte: UNIMPLEMENTED read/write len %i ]\"); exit(1);` -- unreachable for every integer len, as `sgirekills` records.  It DOCUMENTS AN INTENT to reject unusual widths.  A naive future repair of the unreachable-arm defect -- reordering to `len==4 / len==8 / else` -- would RESURRECT THAT exit(1) AS A LIVE WIDTH KILL. ***  So when the unreachable arm is worked it must land as a DECLINE, never as a revived exit, and this row exists to say so before the edit rather than after.  *Two defects in one arm structure, pointing opposite ways: today it serves widths it should refuse, and the obvious tidy-up would make it kill on widths it currently serves.*  *** THE IMPLEMENTING SEAT RE-DISCOVERED THIS ARM INDEPENDENTLY ON 2026-08-30 AND PROPOSED IT AS A NEW ROW (`sgimtedeadwidth`).  FOLDED HERE, NOT FILED -- it is this row, found from the other direction. ***  It arrived via the SIBLING-PARITY check on a different round, where this row came from a pass-1 reading; that two routes reach the same arm is corroboration, not two defects.  **WHAT THE RE-DISCOVERY ADDS, and it is worth folding in:** the precise extent (`dev_sgi_re.c:1162-1178`, the unreachable `exit(1)` at `:1177`), and a COUNT this row did not carry -- **the file has EIGHT width-guard sites by text but only SEVEN LIVE ones**, because this arm's `else` can never be entered.  Verified here by opening the file: `if (len == 4) {...} else if (len != 4) {...} else { fatal(); exit(1); }`, and the second condition is the exact negation of the first.  *A duplicate row would have split one defect's evidence across two ids and let a future round close one while the other stayed open -- which is the failure the queue-id rule already names.  Adjudicated FOLD.*  *** CONFIRMED, AND THE MECHANISM IS BETTER THAN THE ROW SAID: THE WIDTH GUARD IS DEAD CODE. ***  Reaching the final `else` requires `len != 4` and `len == 4` at once, so it can never execute.  Every non-4 width falls into the 64-bit path, which does `mte_reg[regnr+1] = idata >> 32; mte_reg[regnr] = idata;` -- and a one-byte store has `idata < 256`, so the adjacent register is silently ZEROED.  **So it is not that the handler \"serves every width\"; it is that the guard written to refuse them cannot run.**  *** NOT A MEMORY-SAFETY DEFECT, and this was checked SPECIFICALLY because the same question was answered WRONG on `sgideoob` two days ago: *** the `&= ~CRIME_DE_START` mask caps `regnr` at 511 and `mte_reg` is `[DEV_SGI_MTE_LENGTH / 4]` = `[1024]`, so `regnr+1` reaches 512 with 512 slots of headroom.  Enumerated, not argued.  **CONSEQUENCE FOR RANKING, so no round over-claims: fixing this removes a data-corruption defect and a piece of dead code.  It does NOT remove a host kill, because that kill was never reachable.**  A round closing it and reporting \"one fewer host kill\" would be writing a wrong record.  *Its sibling `sgi_de` is shaped `len == 8 / len == 4 / else`, so THAT else IS reachable and `sgidewidth` is a real kill.  The two handlers differ in exactly the way that matters.*", "state_note": null, "witness": "harness:READ and ENUMERATED against the shipped source, 2026-08-30.  The handler is `if (len == 4) ... else if (len != 4) ... else { fatal(); exit(1); }` -- the final else is UNREACHABLE.  Bounds enumerated separately: `relative_addr &= ~CRIME_DE_START` (0x800) caps regnr at 511 against `mte_reg[1024]`, so there is NO out-of-bounds access.", "commits": null}
{"id": "sgireunmapped", "sev": "high", "state": "closed", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c's unmatched-address else arm ends the host on ANY width in EITHER direction across 2080 bytes of its own registered window -- one guest `lb` at 0xb5001800 -- and NEITHER #497 nor sgirekills-as-scoped touches it", "note": "*** THE WIDEST DOOR IN THIS DEVICE, AND NO LEDGER ROW COVERED IT.  Found by the adjudicating seat while ranking the OTHER four sgi_re rows; verified here by opening the file and deriving the gap arithmetic. ***  **THE SITE** is the final `else` of the window cascade in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)` -- a `fatal()` for each direction and then a bare `exit(1)`, with **NO WIDTH PREDICATE AT ALL**.  Every other kill in this handler is reached only by choosing an unusual access WIDTH (the six `len != 8` arms) or a DIRECTION (the six read arms).  This one needs neither: any width, either direction, any unmatched address.  **THE DOOR, derived rather than asserted.**  `memory_device_register` covers `baseaddr + 0x1000` for `DEV_SGI_RE_LENGTH` = `0x1000`, and the handler restores the full offset with `relative_addr += 0x1000`, so the dispatched range is `0x1000-0x1fff`.  The six windows occupy `0x1000-0x17ff` with one internal gap:      TLB_A 0x1000-0x11ff   TLB_B 0x1200-0x13ff   TLB_C 0x1400-0x15ff      TEX   0x1600-0x16df   LINEAR_A 0x1700-0x177f  LINEAR_B 0x1780-0x17ff  leaving **`0x16e0-0x16ff` (32 bytes) plus `0x1800-0x1fff` (2048 bytes) = 2080 bytes** that fall through to the kill.  Guest KSEG1: a single `lb` at `0xb5001800` ends the host process.  *** IT SURVIVES BOTH ROUNDS THAT LOOK LIKE THEY WOULD FIX IT, WHICH IS WHY IT MUST BE FILED BEFORE THE NEXT COMPLETENESS SENTENCE SHIPS. ***  `#497` repairs the six WIDTH guards and never reaches this arm.  `sgirekills` as scoped repairs the six READ arms inside the windows and never reaches it either.  A reader of either round record could reasonably conclude the device no longer kills on a stray access.  It does.  **AND ITS FIX CANNOT BE ACCEPT**, which is what keeps it out of `sgirekills`' round rather than a scheduling preference: nothing backs an unmapped address, so there is no stored value to return.  It is DECLINE + a latched `(once)` announce -- a different fix design, so the round-scope rule's same-fix-design arm fails.  *The agy audit listed this arm as '1 unhandled offset fallback' and never asked whether it was reachable.  A census that enumerates a site without ranking it is how the widest door stays open.*  *** DESIGN SETTLED 2026-08-30 BY ADJUDICATION, BEFORE EITHER ROUND STARTS, SO NEITHER BURNS ITS PANEL ARGUING SCOPE. ***  **TWO ROUNDS, and both separate from `#497` -- all THREE round-scope conjuncts fail, worked through rather than asserted.**  SAME DEVICE fails: `sgi_de` and `sgi_re` are separate `memory_device_register` registrations at `baseaddr+0x2000` and `+0x1000`.  SAME SEMANTIC CLASS fails: `sgidewidth` is the WIDTH class with a real accept set `{4,8}` a decline must PRESERVE, while `sgireunmapped` is the BADOFFSET class where nothing legal reaches the arm at all.  **SAME DETECTOR / NO NEW ORACLE fails, and this is the one that decides it:** `sgi_de`'s registers are READABLE so its rows read back through `odata` -- a real device-signature channel -- while `sgi_re`'s else arm has NO BACKING STORE and its rows can only observe the diagnostic and the guest-gets-zero channel.  *Those are different oracles in kind, and the tempting collapse -- 'both are a refused access ending the host' -- is exactly what the no-new-oracle clause exists to refuse.*  *** THE CHANNEL FINDING, AND IT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT LINE IN THIS BLOCK. ***  `#497`'s `d_returnzero` escaped 35/35 and the reason generalises to BOTH these rounds: **the `(once)` announce prints IDENTICALLY for a correct decline and for a `return 0`**, because the announce happens before the return statement.  So the DIAGNOSTIC channel does not kill it either -- and neither does host survival, since a guest bus error leaves the host alive.  **The only discriminator is what the GUEST RECEIVED.**  Every round in this family owes a SENTINEL-SEEDED READ ROW: seed the destination register, perform the declined read, and assert it reads back 0 -- a correct decline delivers zero, `return 0` faults and the register keeps its seed.  *That is the row `#497` was missing, and the measuring seat reached the same repair independently while this adjudication was being written.*  **TWO LATCH BITS, NOT ONE, and the precedent is MEASURED rather than stylistic.**  The arm prints two distinct messages -- `unimplemented write to address` and `unimplemented read from address`.  `#490` built the one-bit form as `M3shared` and **it ESCAPED**: a session declining at both sites sees the second fall silent because the first already latched.  `#279` states the rule outright -- *one flag per DISTINCT MESSAGE and never one shared flag*.  A single bit here means the first stray READ silences the first stray WRITE's message forever, and vice versa.  **THE DE-ESCALATION ACCEPT CLAUSE IS N/A, WITH ITS REASON RECORDED -- not unrun.**  The clause enumerates predicates that used to gate death and pins each accept side.  This arm **has no predicate**: it is the fall-through when none of the six window conditions match, so there is no adjacent modelled value because **nothing legal reaches it**.  The enumeration is the empty set.  *Adding a row to test the WINDOW predicates instead would be the addition licence's 'cannot hurt' wearing detector clothes -- and it is unnecessary, because `#497`'s own accept rows already prove those addresses reach their own arms rather than this one.  That is inherited coverage, not an uncovered hole.*  **WHAT IT OWES INSTEAD IS A CONTRACT SENTENCE (clause (e)):** this fix makes every unmatched address a silent zero-decline, so a future window-boundary regression that drops a legal address here no longer announces -- and that class is pinned by `#497`'s window accept rows, not by this arm.  *** AND A CONTRACT LIMIT WORTH KNOWING BEFORE ROWS ARE WRITTEN: the WRITE arm's `return 0` mutant is NOT distinguishable by a value-only cold-debugger probe. ***  A declined write and a faulting write both leave no readback.  The READ arm is killable by the seeded-read row; the write arm needs exception-state observation, which is a heavier probe shape than `#497` used.  **State it as a limit rather than shipping a completeness claim the rows cannot support.**  *** THE 32-BYTE GAP IS NOT DEAD SPACE.  IT IS `CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS`, A SEVENTH REAL HARDWARE WINDOW. ***  `crmfbreg.h` defines `CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS 0x16e0` between `CRIME_RE_TEX 0x1600` and `CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A 0x1700`, and `struct sgi_re_data` carries `// todo: clip_ids registers.` exactly where they would go.  The codex seat reached the same conclusion from the CRIME spec and put it best: **\"six real windows\" should read \"six MODELED windows\"** -- CLIP_IDS is real hardware with no dispatch and no state.  **The 2080-address count is UNCHANGED** (CLIP_IDS still falls to the kill) **but the characterisation splits, and the decline message must follow it:** 32 bytes are a documented, unmodelled register block; 2048 are genuinely unmapped.  A single \"unimplemented address\" diagnostic is accurate for the 2048 and WRONG for the 32.  *The brief asked whether `CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` was a bug or the gap was real; both options were wrong, and a seat answering as posed would have had to pick the wrong one of two.  Three local facts agree the bound is deliberate: the next defined constant, the struct's own todo comment, and `re_tex[112]` x 2 bytes = 0xe0 exactly.*  *** THE fable5 SEAT'S EQUIVALENCE CLAIM IS REFUTED, AND THE REFUTATION CHANGES A DISPOSITION RATHER THAN SCORING A POINT. ***  It reported that an early `return 1` after the announce is OBSERVATIONALLY EQUIVALENT because *\"`memory_rw.c`'s #95 block memsets `data` to 0 on EVERY device read before the handler runs\"*, and advised disposing of that mutant as EQUIVALENT rather than chasing it.  **Checked both halves and both are false.**  The device handler is called at `memory_rw.c` `res = mem->devices[i].f(cpu, mem, paddr, data, len, ...)` and NOTHING zeroes `data` before it -- the three `memset(data, 0, len)` sites in that file are all on NON-DEVICE paths (the MIPS Sprite area, the beyond-physical_max arm, and the `memblock == NULL` unmapped-RAM arm), each followed by a `goto do_return_ok`.  And the MIPS caller's buffer is `uint8_t data[LS_SIZE];`, an **UNINITIALISED STACK ARRAY** with no memset anywhere in that file.  **SO THE MUTANT IS NOT EQUIVALENT -- BUT THE CORRECT DISPOSITION IS NOT 'DETECTABLE' EITHER, AND THAT NUANCE IS THE POINT.**  Skipping the tail leaves the guest whatever was on the stack, which is neither 0 nor any fixed value: the observable is REAL but NONDETERMINISTIC.  A row asserting a specific sentinel would be FLAKY, not sound.  *Filing it as EQUIVALENT would have retired a live mutant on a false mechanism; filing it as ordinary coverage debt would invite a flaky row.  It is neither, and the round must say so.*  **The seat's own remedy for the sibling `return 0` case is the one that works here too: seed the destination register with a sentinel and prove execution continued past the load** -- which pins the mutant without depending on stack contents.  *Everything else in that answer verified: `dev_unreadable.c` IS `return 0;` and `machine_sgi.c` installs it TEN times, so the FAULT member is real and in use on this machine.*  *** THE PASS-1 PANEL RESOLVED THE ROUND'S ONLY DISAGREEMENT BY WARRANT, NOT BY VOTE. ***  grok said ONE latch bit, deepseek said TWO.  I grepped first and found ZERO device files in the tree splitting a latch by direction -- which looked like precedent for grok.  **fable5 showed the framing was wrong: the house latch is per-MESSAGE-SITE, and this arm has TWO message texts (the write arm prints the data value, the read arm does not).  So two bits is not a new rule, it is the existing rule counted correctly** -- and my grep found nothing because I searched for the wrong property.  Two further warrants: a declined READ fabricates data the guest acts on while a declined write is a no-op, and one shared bit order-couples the two message rows so 'read message exactly once' becomes unpinnable.  *** WITNESSED AT RUNG 3, 32/32.  The row's witness field said `none`. ***  **AND ONE ROW FAILED FOR A REASON WORTH KEEPING: `lw` from 0x16ff SURVIVED** -- the log shows `exception ADEL`, because an unaligned load is refused by the CPU before `memory_rw` is reached.  Re-cut to 0x16fc it kills.  *The row was wrong, not the code, and the seat said so rather than adjusting the claim to fit.*  **THE ESCAPES, ranked by how little they cost:**  (1) *** A WINDOW-BOUND SHRINK (`+ 0x80` -> `+ 0x40`) SCORED A TOTAL ESCAPE, 9/9, AGAINST THE FIRST DETECTOR -- because the accept row wrote to the window's BASE, where a move of the FAR bound is invisible by construction. ***  That is clause (f)'s anchor rule arriving on the accept side, and the repair is one word: **an accept row must use the window's LAST slot.**  (2) a latch-bit collision reusing `SGIRE_W_TEX_LEN` scores 13/14, caught only by a row that touches another latch site first.  (3) `return 0` escapes both \"host survives\" and \"message once\" -- and the seat measured the discriminator side by side: with `v0` pre-set to **0** the row PASSES the mutant; pre-set to **0xa5a5a5a5** it FAILS, because a DBE leaves the register untouched.  The log is decisive (`reading from device 'sgi_re' ... failed` / `exception DBE`).  **So the brief's stated pin \"the declined read returns 0\" is MEASURED VACUOUS unless the sentinel is non-zero.**  **A REFINEMENT TO THE SIX-MEMBER OPTION SET, and it is not a quibble:** the set collapses THREE DISTINCT ANNOUNCE DISCIPLINES living in this one file -- unbounded-and-always-visible (`fatal()`, in sgi_de and sgi_mte), **unbounded but VERBOSITY-GATED (`debug()`, in sgi_de_status, invisible at default verbosity)**, and latched-once.  Folding the second into \"already declines\" hides exactly the flood question the round has to answer.  *** ROW COVERAGE, STATED RATHER THAN GLOSSED: ONLY 10 OF 14 ROWS EVER FIRED, AND THREE OF THE FOUR SILENT ONES ARE AN UNDISCHARGED OBLIGATION. ***  `accept tlba`, `accept tlbb` and `accept tlbc` never fired because **no shrink mutant was built for the three TLB windows**.  By the accept-row failability rule an accept row is discharged by a MEASURED KILL, not by existing -- so these three are NOT discharged, and the round owes three more mutants (about 1.5 s per build).  **AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME MUTANT SHAPE AS THE FOUR ALREADY BUILT.**  TLB_A and TLB_B are bounded by *the next window's constant* while the other four use *base + size*, so shrinking a TLB bound perturbs TWO arms at once -- it moves a boundary two windows share, rather than opening a gap.  A mutant written on the base+size pattern would not exercise them.  The fourth silent row, `read0 VACUOUS`, is **deliberately** never red: it is the control that DEMONSTRATES the brief's stated pin is vacuous.  Same load, `v0` pre-set to 0 instead of 0xa5a5a5a5, and it PASSES on the mutant where the real row fails.  *A row that never fires because it is a negative control and a row that never fires because nobody built its mutant must not be counted alike, which is why they are separated here.*  **DISPOSITION: this is L3, an undischarged obligation, not coverage debt** -- the three rows are the sole carriers of the de-escalation accept side for their windows, and the round's claim to have shipped accept rows is false about them until the mutants exist.  It is owed BEFORE the fix ships, not filed after.  *** RETRACTED.  MY REFUTATION OF fable5's EQUIVALENCE CLAIM WAS WRONG, AND I READ THE PRISTINE 2021 UPSTREAM COPY OF `memory_rw.c` TO PRODUCE IT.  fable5 WAS RIGHT. ***  I wrote that *\"nothing zeroes `data` before the handler call -- the three `memset(data, 0, len)` sites are all on NON-DEVICE paths\"*, and shipped it in `a115a4a`.  **The fork's `src/cpus/memory_rw.c` has SIX such sites, and one of them is exactly what fable5 described**, inside the device-found branch, before `paddr -= baseaddr` and before the dispatch:  `/*  #95: zero the caller's buffer up front on a read so a device handler that fills only part of it ... can never hand back uninitialised host memory to the guest.  */  if (writeflag == MEM_READ) memset(data, 0, len);`  *** HOW IT HAPPENED, AND IT IS THE HAZARD THIS PROJECT DOCUMENTS IN CAPITALS FOR SEATS -- IT CAUGHT THE MAIN LOOP INSTEAD. ***  There are two `memory_rw.c` under the project root: the fork at **794 lines** (`GXEMUL-SEC`, `est`, `build`, `build-asan`, md5 3d47dd66) and a PRISTINE 2021 UPSTREAM copy at **530 lines** (`./src/cpus/`, md5 75fbe149, plus two archive copies).  My grep ran as `src/cpus/memory_rw.c` while the cwd had drifted to the PROJECT ROOT, so it resolved to the upstream file.  **Every line number I reported -- 161, 222, 425, 437, 486 -- is below 530, and the fork's are 215/267/492/671/683/732.  The numbers themselves were the tell and I did not read them as one.**  **THE CONSEQUENTIAL ERROR, not the embarrassing one:** I dispositioned the early-`return 1` mutant as *\"REAL but NONDETERMINISTIC -- a row asserting a sentinel would be FLAKY\"*.  With #95 in place the guest gets a DETERMINISTIC ZERO, so on the value channel that mutant is **EQUIVALENT**, and a sentinel row is not flaky -- it is simply BLIND to it.  The opus5 seat measured this with two builds differing only in #95's fill byte: filling 0x00 gives `lbu = 0x0`, filling 0xa5 gives `lbu = 0xa5`, tracking byte-for-byte.  *So the disposition inverts: not coverage debt on a flaky channel, but an EQUIVALENT mutant that the mutant-that-will-not-die rule says to dispose of as a RESULT.  In `sgi_de` it is not equivalent overall, because that tail also DRAWS and `dec_switch` separates them -- but in `sgi_re`, whose tail is bare, opus5 expects true equivalence.*  **AND I TOLD THE OWNER I HAD REFUTED A SEAT.**  The correction is recorded here, in the carrier, and reported back -- because a wrong refutation that stands is worse than the original error: it teaches the next round to distrust the seat that was right.  *** L4 -- THE REASON THIS ROW GIVES FOR ITS OWN GAP IS WRONG FOR ONE OF ITS THREE MEMBERS, found 2026-08-31 by #499's PRE-TABLE block (sibling parity) before any mutant was built. ***  The note explains all three missing kills with *the TLB windows are bounded by the NEXT window's constant, not base+size*.  Re-derived from the file: that is TRUE of TLB_A (`< CRIME_RE_TLB_B`) and TLB_B (`< CRIME_RE_TLB_C`) and **FALSE of TLB_C, which is `< CRIME_RE_TLB_C + 0x200` -- structurally IDENTICAL to TEX, LIN_A and LIN_B, all three of which got a shrink mutant and all three of which FIRED.**  So `accept tlbc` was never blocked by window shape; it was simply not built.  **The asymmetry is TWO arms, not three, and a third of the filed obligation is the same edit that already worked three times.**  Recorded because a wrong reason gets reused: a future round reading this note would have gone looking for a special mutant shape for a window that needs the ordinary one.  Durable anchor: `grep -nE 'relative_addr < CRIME_RE' src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c` prints all six far bounds in one screen, which is what made the asymmetry visible at all.  *** #499's MUTANT TABLE (regress/artifacts/sgireunmapped/killtable_20260831/): THE THREE ACCEPT ROWS ARE DISCHARGED, AND THE SEAT OVERTURNED MY FRAMING TWICE. ***  **MY PREMISE WAS FALSE AND I GAVE IT TO THE SEAT AS SETTLED.**  I briefed that the `exit(1)` \"was replaced by a latched refusal\", reading the row's `commits: a643e18` as the fix.  **At HEAD the catch-all is STILL `exit(1)`** -- `grep -n 'unimplemented read from address' src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c`, with the kill two lines below.  `a643e18` shipped the WITNESS and the analysis; **#499 is the round that ships the fix**, not one auditing a shipped one.  *A premise-audit opener would have caught this and my brief did not carry one -- I wrote the settled-premises block and then stated a premise in it that was wrong.*  The seat also corrected my expected-results line: I asked for a baseline that is all-green, and **base is 10/14 BY DESIGN** -- that is test-first, and the all-green control is the FIX.  **THE TABLE.  base 10/14, fix 14/14, and each shrink reddens EXACTLY its own row:** `M1 tlba_shrink` 13/14 (`accept tlba`), `M2 tlbb_shrink` 13/14 (`accept tlbb`), `M3 tlbc_shrink` 13/14 (`accept tlbc`).  **No shrink perturbs a neighbour**, which also refutes this row's own \"a shrink to a next-constant bound moves a boundary two windows share\" -- editing the `<` EXPRESSION opens a gap and does not touch the sibling arm; only editing `CRIME_RE_TLB_B` in `crmfbreg.h` would.  **So all three accept rows now carry measured kills and the L3 is dischargeable.**  **M4 bitcollide ESCAPED at 14/14, exactly as the PRE-TABLE block predicted** -- and the prediction was measured rather than trusted.  The seat then BUILT the killing row: **one emulator, two NARROW (`len != 8`) accesses, one in TLB_A and one in TLB_B**, oracle `count(\"CRIME_RE_TLB_B -- declined\") == 1`.  The session shape is the whole point -- the accept rows do ONE 8-byte store in a fresh emu, so they never enter a width guard and never set a latch bit, making the collision unobservable to them BY CONSTRUCTION.  Generalised for one session's cost: six narrow accesses, one per window, asserting all six decline texts appear, which pins every pairwise collision among the six bits.  *** AND A DEFECT NEITHER OF US HAD ENUMERATED: `#498` SILENTLY BROKE THIS ROW'S WITNESS GENERATOR. ***  `opus_witness_20260830/mkvariant.py` inserts `SGIRE_W_UNMAP_WR = 1 << 6`; `#498`/`cb28a6f` added `SGIRE_W_DE_LEN = 1 << 6` to the same enum.  **A duplicate enumerator is legal C and silent**, and `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de)` casts the same `struct sgi_re_data *`, so `d->warned` is shared across both devices.  Measured as `fix6`: **14/14, total escape**, with a transcript showing one guest `sb` to sgi_de permanently silencing sgi_re's catch-all diagnostic.  The replacement `mkvariant499.py` uses `1<<7`/`1<<8` and hard-fails on duplicates.  *A cross-round interaction: one round's enum addition invalidated another round's generator, with no gate able to see it.*  *** M5 -- THE ACCEPT SUITE TESTS THE FAR BOUND AND NEVER THE NEAR BOUND, AND THIS FALSIFIES A CARRIER RULE MINTED FROM THIS VERY ROW EARLIER THE SAME DAY.  Ranked a FLOOR BREACH (clause (d), L2) by the measuring seat. ***  The carrier said: *an accept row written at a window's BASE tests the near bound twice and the far bound never -- put it at the LAST legal slot, **where both bounds are load-bearing***.  The first half is right and is why these rows were moved to the top slot. **The second half is measured FALSE.**  `M5`: `>= CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A` -> `>= CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A + 0x40`, **one token**, scores a **14/14 CLEAN SWEEP** -- while a control row storing at the window BASE reds on M5 and is serviced normally on the fix.  **Moving the rows from base to top SWAPPED the blindness rather than removing it.**  **The reason is structural, not a detail of this device:** an accept row's oracle is *this access was serviced*, which is a single bit, and ONE bound can explain that bit on its own.  So there is no slot from which both bounds are observable, and a single accept row pins exactly the bound it is near.  **THE REPAIR COSTS NO EXTRA SESSIONS AND IS STRICTLY BETTER THAN TWO ROWS:** the store's own `debug()` already prints the destination -- `[ d->re_linear_b[30] = 0x... ]`.  Assert **the array NAME and the INDEX** rather than merely that the catch-all was missed.  That converts each relative row into an ANCHORED row in clause (f)'s sense, pins BOTH bounds from one access, and additionally catches TRANSLATIONS -- which no base-or-top accept row can see at all.  Two rows (base AND top) is the fallback where no such channel exists, and it is weaker: it pins two bounds and still cannot see a re-map that keeps both.  **Because M5 weakens a predicate the fix DEPENDS ON and dominates the edited catch-all, it is clause (d) and therefore L2 -- repaired IN-ROUND, not filed as coverage debt.**  The carrier has been corrected in place; the detector repair ships with the fix.  *This is the freshly-minted-exemplar rule firing on itself: a rule minted from ONE instance carried that instance's shape as if it were the class, and the first round to APPLY it measured the general claim false within hours.  The instruction survived; the universal clause did not.*  *** LATCH-BIT DECONFLICTION BETWEEN TWO ROUNDS IN FLIGHT, caught 2026-08-31 BEFORE either shipped rather than measured afterwards. ***  `dev_sgi_re.c`'s `SGIRE_W_*` enum uses bits **0-6** today (`TLBA/TLBB/TLBC/TEX/LINA/LINB` 0-5, `DE_LEN` 6).  **`#499` takes `1 << 7` (`SGIRE_W_UNMAP_WR`) and `1 << 8` (`SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD`)**, per its measured `mkvariant499.py` fix.  **`#500` was designed for `SGIRE_W_PRIMOP = 1 << 7` and MUST TAKE `1 << 9` instead.**  *A duplicate enumerator is legal C and silent, and both devices cast the same `struct sgi_re_data *`, so a collision makes one arm's decline permanently suppressible by unrelated traffic to the other -- measured as a 14/14 total escape when it happened between `#498` and `#499`'s generator.*  *** THE PORT SHIPPED AND IT CAUGHT THREE MUTANTS THE ARTIFACT DETECTOR LET THROUGH -- which is the L2 repair working, measured on the artifact that will actually run rather than inherited from the one that will not.  `regress/sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py`, 28 rows, self-contained, artifacts under killtable_20260831/port/. ***  **Re-measured on the SHIPPED probe:** `m4_bitcollide` **14/14 ESCAPED -> 27/28 KILLED**; `m5_lina_near` **14/14 -> 25/28** (three rows); the duplicate-1<<6 variant **14/14 -> 27/28**.  Base 10/14 -> **18/28**, fix 14/14 -> **28/28**.  Five further mutants built and killed: `mret0` 26/28, `m6_idxshift` 26/28, `m7_wintrans` 24/28, `mnolatch` 26/28, `mbothsame` 27/28, `mrdcollide` 27/28.  *** THE TRANSLATION CLAIM WAS MEASURED AND CAME BACK NARROWER THAN I STATED IT, which is why it was asked for as a measurement. ***  I recorded that the anchored-oracle repair *should also catch translations*.  Measured: **anchoring ALONE does not catch a translation; anchoring PLUS a base-slot row does.**  Under `m7_wintrans` the TOP-slot row `tlba acc hi` stayed **GREEN** -- a row inside the moved window cannot see that the window moved -- while `tlba acc lo` reddened by falling out, and `tlbb acc lo` reddened on the MIS-ATTRIBUTION channel (the transcript says `d->re_tlb_a[0]` where the row wants `d->re_tlb_b[0]`), which no unanchored oracle can see at all.  An index re-map (`m6_idxshift`) IS caught outright, both rows.  **So the true claim is: anchored + BOTH bounds catches index re-maps outright and catches window translations at the base row.**  *** AND THE SEAT CHECKED WHAT ALREADY EXISTED BEFORE BUILDING WHAT I ASKED FOR. ***  I proposed generalising the latch-collision row to all six windows.  `sgi_re_width_probe.py`'s `xsite 6msg` **already ships that exact generalisation, is gate-wired, and measured 32/35 against m4** -- so a duplicate would have been a row that can never fail alone.  It re-aimed the idea at THIS round's own two bits instead (`xwin wr`/`xwin rd` walk all six #497 latches plus #498's sgi_de bit, since the four handlers share one malloc'd struct), with `xwin7 pre` as the partner control.  **ONE ROW DELIBERATELY DROPPED and one row's base value deliberately CHANGED, both disclosed rather than buried:** `read0 VACUOUS` was a pedagogical control that cannot fail under the mutant it names, so it is gone and its lesson survives as the reason `rd sent` presets a sentinel; and `latch fresh` (green on base, because base ALSO prints its text before dying) became `xwin wr`, which counts the `-- declined.` literal and is RED on base.  *A port that silently changes a score is the failure mode here, so both are named.*  **COVERAGE DEBT, stated rather than glossed: 8 of 28 rows never failed on any of 14 variants.**  Two are the witness ladder's clause-(ii) controls (`live`, `devsig`) and fire only if the harness or machine breaks.  The other six are per-window accept replicas of shapes discharged elsewhere, filed WITH the mutants that would discharge them (near-grow at TLB_C, far-shrink at TEX/LIN_B, near-grow at LIN_B).  **The seat stopped rather than chase per-row, which is the `#392` shape** -- the right call.  *One caveat it raised against its own work: `xwin7 pre` is a control that a product defect can redden (m4, m5, m7).  That is a valid kill, but the round must not cite it as the PRIMARY m4 detector -- `xsite 6msg` names that properly.*  *** INDEPENDENT REVIEW OF THE RE-PIN, 2026-08-31: DO NOT SHIP IT AS WRITTEN.  It repairs `otherkilldeath` and walks STRAIGHT INTO `exit0oracle`. ***  The proposed oracle is `exit0 + text`.  **Mutant: keep the fix's latch and message and add `exit(0);` after the if/else at the catch-all.**  The message prints (text True), the process exits 0 (`how == \"exit0\"`), `got == want` -> **GREEN, total escape** -- and `other kill` is the ONLY row in that 35-row probe that touches `OUTSIDE` (verified: two hits in the file, the definition and one use), so nothing else catches it.  *** AND THE KNOWLEDGE WAS ALREADY IN THE GATE, ADDRESSED TO THIS EXACT CLASS, WRITTEN THE SAME DAY. ***  `regress/gate_arm.sh` carries the flagship seat's note against re-pinning `tail_acc_x` onto `exit0` -- *exit status cannot vary between 'declined and then quit' and 'died with status 0'* -- together with the repair.  **The `#499` re-pin was written without it.**  That is this project's unread-review shape: the answer sat in a file the round had itself just edited.  **REPAIR, using material already in the probe:** assert a DEVICE SIGNATURE through the existing `DE_STATUS`/`DE_STAT_VAL` constants (the `devsig stat` idiom), giving survival AND identity in one channel; or the `lw surv` sentinel-preset idiom, which distinguishes *the tail ran and wrote odata 0* from *the load never completed*.  **Minimum:** `session()` already returns `alive` and the proposal throws it away.  **So the round owes a FOURTH mutant of its own -- `exit(0)` at the sgi_re catch-all -- and the order measurement must be RE-RUN with whichever oracle actually ships.**  Worth knowing: the NEW 28-row probe would survive that mutant; the escape is confined to the re-pinned shipped row, so this is a floor question about the RE-PIN, not about the new detector.  *** THE ORDER CLAIM OVERCLAIMS BY ONE WORD, AND A STRONGER WARRANT WAS ALREADY IN HAND. ***  `REPIN_REQUIRED.md` is headed *\"ORDER DISCIPLINE -- measured, not asserted\"*.  **The RED is measured; the ORDER is not** -- every artifact carries the same copy-time mtime, so nothing shows the oracle was written before the fix was built.  **But the oracle was PRE-REGISTERED in a committed artifact by a prior round**: `git log -S \"death is the shared answer\"` gives `112867a` (#497, 2026-08-30 22:41), the commit that filed `otherkilldeath` and prescribed this repair -- **six hours before** this round's baseline `3ded1e8` (2026-08-31 04:34).  *That is checkable by `git show` rather than by trusting a timestamp, and it is what should carry the not-laundering claim.*  L4: correct the heading.  *** THE PORTED DETECTOR IS INDEPENDENTLY VALIDATED BY THE COORDINATOR, NOT ONLY BY THE SEAT THAT WROTE IT.  2026-08-31, log preserved at `regress/artifacts/sgireunmapped/killtable_20260831/port/coordinator_validate.out`. ***  Run against the COMMITTED tree at `fe5e2c1` with `build/gxemul`, from the gate's own working directory: **`PROBE_RC=0`, `OPUS499T_CONTROL=OK`, `OPUS499T_RESULT=18/28`, `OPUS499T_DONE` present.**  18 ok + 10 FAIL = 28, so the tally is internally consistent and no row was lost.  **AND THE TEN RED ROWS ARE EXACTLY THE ONES THE FIX ADDRESSES**, which is the test-first evidence rather than a bare number:  `wr surv`, `rd surv` (the host dies today), `wr kill`, `rd kill` (the kill text is present today), `wr decl`, `rd decl` (the decline text is absent today), `rd sent` (the sentinel survives an unanswered read), `wr rd 2msg` (no per-direction messages exist yet), and `xwin wr`/`xwin rd` (the new latch bits do not exist yet).  **Every accept row and both witness-ladder controls stay GREEN**, so the detector is not simply red about everything.  *Three things this checks that a seat's summary cannot: that the probe RUNS from the gate's cwd rather than only the seat's, that it emits its completion sentinel so a score is a measurement rather than a truncation, and that the reddening rows are the RIGHT ones.  The figure matches the seat's report exactly, which is what makes it a confirmation rather than a second opinion.*  *** CLOSED 2026-08-31 at `9f666a8`. ***  The catch-all is a per-direction latched decline falling through to the common tail; live `exit(1)` in `dev_sgi_re.c` 14 -> 13.  Two bits (`1 << 7`, `1 << 8`) placed AFTER `SGIRE_W_DE_LEN` in numeric order, because an earlier draft left the enum ending at `1 << 6` and a maintainer scanning to the last enumerator would have taken an already-used bit.  **The re-pin that shipped is NOT the one proposed**: `exit0+txt` was measured to ESCAPE an `exit(0)` mutant, and the measuring seat's own first-choice replacement escaped two more, so the sentinel-preset read of the unmapped address itself is what shipped.  Detector wired in the same commit, both hygiene pins raised in the same commit, all four build trees propagated and both clearance parts verified.  *** L4 CORRECTION, FOUND BY THE LATE PASS 2 -- AND IT IS THE EVIDENCE THAT THE LATE PASS WAS NOT THEATRE. ***  The measuring seat wrote that the shipped oracle's three failures are *three DISTINCT values*, and I repeated it into the pass-2 brief and the round's report.  **It is two, not three.**  Measured, from `repin2/table500b.out`:  `mexit0` -> `exit0/txt/died`;  `mret0` -> `exit0/txt/0xdeadbeef`;  `mrdexit0` -> `exit0/txt/died`.  **`died` appears twice**, so it is three FAILURES carrying two DISTINCT values.  **The substance survives and the overclaim does not.**  What the measurement supports is that the sentinel channel VARIES where the alternatives do not -- N2 fails all three while N1 (the device-signature read) fails only one -- which is the argument that actually mattered.  What it does not support is the stronger *three distinct values* framing used to dismiss the one-channel-wearing-three-hats objection.  *Caught by a packet-fed seat reading the parenthetical `(died / 0xdeadbeef / died)` against the claim it was offered to support -- i.e. by checking a claim against its OWN cited evidence, which is the cheapest audit there is and which neither I nor the seat that wrote it performed.*  **AND IT IS DIRECT EVIDENCE ON THE QUESTION OF WHETHER A LATE PASS 2 IS WORTH RUNNING**: this round shipped without one, the gate refused the next commit for it, and the pass run afterwards found a wrong record in the round's own justification within minutes.  *** codex's SEMANTIC CORRECTION, recorded because the records use the wrong word: THIS IS NOT A DECLINE AT THE CALLBACK BOUNDARY. ***  The handler returns **1** for an unmatched access exactly as for a serviced one, so `memory_rw` sees a handled access either way.  What the fix changes is that the host survives and a latched message is printed -- the access is still ACCEPTED at the boundary and answered with a defined 0 on the read side.  **The six-member option set distinguishes DECLINE from ACCEPT by precisely this boundary**, so calling it a decline blurs the distinction the set exists to draw.  *The behaviour is right and the word is wrong; a future round reading `sgireunmapped` as a DECLINE precedent would be citing something that returns success.*  *** L2 FLOOR BREACH, MEASURED BY THE PASS-2 SEAT: TWO MUTANTS SWEEP ALL 28 ROWS CLEAN, AND ONE RESTORES THIS ROUND'S OWN CITED WITNESS VERBATIM.  Repair is owed IN-ROUND however late. ***  **`W` -- re-add the pre-`#499` `len != 8` kill at the top of the catch-all, lifted verbatim from `9f666a8^`.  28/28, zero red -- and `sb` AND `lb` at `0xb5001800` die, printing the pre-fix kill text.**  That is floor clause (b): a verbatim reinstatement of the removed defect passes the detector.  **`T` -- `relative_addr < CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` -> `+ 0x100`, ONE HEX DIGIT.  28/28, zero red -- and `ld` at `0xb50016e0` dies with `TODO: read from CRIME_RE_TEX`**, handing 32 bytes back to `exit(1)`.  Clause (d): a bound relaxed on a predicate dominating the edited lines.  *** THE TWO REASONS ARE STRUCTURAL, NOT LUCKY, AND I VERIFIED BOTH. ***  **(1) EVERY catch-all row uses len 8 and nothing else.**  `store()` defaults to `OP_SD_T0_A3` and `load()` to `OP_LD_V0_AT`; `OP_SB_T0_A3` exists but is aimed at the six mapped windows.  **So the detector pins the DIRECTION axis -- which the contract argues at length -- and never the WIDTH axis at the catch-all, while the round's HEADLINE is *no width predicate at all* and its WITNESS is a `lb`.**  **(2) All four catch-all offsets (`0x800/0x8c0/0x900/0x9c0`) land in the SECOND component.**  The round's own CHANGELOG names two: `0x16e0-0x16ff` (32 bytes) and `0x1800-0x1fff` (2048).  **The 32-byte component has ZERO rows.**  *This is `#498`'s `len == 2` hole one round later, in the same file: a detector that exercises one axis exhaustively and the other not at all.*  **AND THE PRETABLE MANDATE WAS ONLY PARTLY DISCHARGED, which is why the CHANNEL check did not catch it.**  The block's own header names baseline `e898c84`, the ARTIFACT detector, and the subject *discharge accept tlba/tlbb/tlbc*.  It does not cover the port to 28 rows, the fix, the re-pin, or the two new bits -- its GUARD-FIELD section enumerates SEVEN bits and says *all seven distinct today*, written before `1<<7` and `1<<8` existed.  **The CHANNEL check was never run over the catch-all rows, which is precisely where both mutants escape.**  The block's own opening line says `#498` skipped it and paid; the same shape recurred here one scope level up.  *** THE L2 IS REPAIRED IN-ROUND AND MEASURED.  Detector 28 -> 34 rows; both escaping mutants now die.  Artifacts: killtable_20260831/l2repair/. ***    fix **34/34**  |  `T` (`CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` -> `+ 0x100`) **33/34**, `clip decl` red, was 28/28  |  `W` (the pre-`#499` `len != 8` kill re-added verbatim) **30/34**, all four narrow rows red, was 28/28.  **Six rows: four on the WIDTH axis** (a byte write and a 4-byte read at the catch-all, each with a survival row AND a positive channel -- the decline announced, the sentinel replaced by a defined 0, because a survival-only row is satisfied by a mutant surviving for the wrong reason) **and two on the FIRST COMPONENT** at `RE + 0x6e0`, which the handler's own `+0x1000` makes `0x16e0`.  *** AND THE VERIFICATION HIT TWO TRAPS THIS PROJECT HAD ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN, BOTH MINE. ***  **(1)** The first `W` build died with `error: missing terminating \" character` -- the `\\n` in the inserted C string cooked into a REAL NEWLINE through the Bash -> heredoc -> Python path, splitting the literal.  **The measuring seat had warned me about this exact path in the same report** (*do not hand-type it ... it cost me one round-trip*) and the carrier prescribes `chr(92)`.  I hand-typed it.  Rebuilt with `chr(92)` plus a check that the literal landed on ONE line.  *The stale-binary guard is what caught it: the build failed, left the old binary, and the run ABORTED rather than scoring a false escape.*  **(2)** My extra confirmation -- *does `W` really restore the witness?* -- used a debugger `put b` and reported ZERO kill messages.  **`put b` never reaches the device**: the byte path passes `NO_EXCEPTIONS` and `memory_rw` skips the device call for `DM_DEFAULT` devices.  That is the trap measured on `pcc2` and recorded by me hours earlier.  **The probe's own rows use real guest `sb`/`lw` instructions, which is exactly why they caught `W` while the debugger poke saw nothing** -- so the four red rows are the evidence and the `put b` line is an apparatus artifact.  *** AND THE L2 REPAIR ITSELF HAD THE SAME DEFECT ONE LEVEL DOWN, FOUND BY THE PASS THAT THE `no recursion` RULING WOULD HAVE SKIPPED.  Detector 34 -> 36. ***  I diagnosed the breach as *one axis pinned exhaustively, the other not at all*, repaired the width axis at the addresses the mutants used, and left the identical hole at the other component: the two `clip` rows took `store()`'s default `OP_SD`, so the matrix read **three cells of a two-by-two** -- component1 x narrow had nothing.  **A guard re-added at the catch-all was caught; a guard re-added and CONDITIONED on the first component would not have been.**  Two rows added (`clip narrow surv`, `clip narrow decl`).  Measured:  fix **36/36**;  `T` **34/36** with TWO reds where the 34-row version had one;  `W` **30/36** with **SIX** reds where it had four -- narrow now catches at BOTH components.  *** THIS ALSO ANSWERS THE SEAT THAT DOUBTED THE ROWS: deepseek called the first-component rows *suspect as survival-only*, i.e. possibly unable to vary.  They vary -- both `decl` rows moved 1 -> 0 under `T` while their paired `surv` rows held.  The positive channel is doing the work, which is why each row was paired that way. ***  *Found by three packet-fed seats reviewing the repair -- while BLINDED, because my brief claimed to inline the diff and inlined zero hunks.  Two of them said so plainly rather than answering around it, and found the gap anyway.*", "state_note": "#499 pass 3 (4daa506): 6 of 8 answering seats say DO NOT CLOSE.  TWO MEASURED CLEAN SWEEPS (M1 direction, M2 position) plus a RED-gate-on-GREEN-probe defect found by two seats independently.  Pass 4 closes the CLASS rather than the cells: the catch-all's five inputs enumerated once, 36 -> 48 rows, contract updated with what is deliberately NOT covered. Panel dir _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810", "witness": "probe:regress/sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py -- 28 rows, gate-run by gate_arm.sh.  Test-first evidence in regress/artifacts/sgireunmapped/killtable_20260831/: 18/28 on the pre-fix build and 28/28 on the fix, plus the ORDER PROOF for the re-pinned sibling probe (34/35 RED on the committed build under the new oracle, 35/35 after), all sentinel-verified and re-run by the coordinator rather than inherited.", "commits": "a115a4a a643e18 9f666a8"}
{"id": "lcabadval", "sev": "high", "state": "in work", "title": "dev_lca.c: SIX chipset registers in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) kill the host on a value outside a small accepted set (CONF, TB_ENA, W_BASE0/1, W_MASK0/1) -- plus a seventh exit in the default arm, which this round does NOT close", "note": "*** THE #443 BADVAL CLASS AT SCALE -- THE SAME SHAPE AS `gdromcondlies`, WHICH WAS RATED HIGH AND HAS SINCE BEEN FIXED, AND AS `sgideprimop`, FILED HIGH TONIGHT. ***  **Four registers accept exactly ONE 64-bit value and end the host process on every other**, and two more accept only a single-bit mask.  The author documented the decision rather than hiding it: each `fatal()` reads *\"from the only implemented value\"*.  That is an honest TODO expressed as a host kill.  **A guest here is firmware** -- SRM or NetBSD bringing up the chipset -- so the reaching value is whatever that firmware writes, not an attacker's choice.  A model that implements one value and dies on the rest converts every unmodelled firmware revision into a host death.  *** SCOPE, STATED SO IT IS NOT OVER-CLAIMED: the two RANGE guards (`dev >= 21`, `ofs > len`) are a DIFFERENT question from the four VALUE guards and are not analysed here. ***  A bounds guard that kills may be protecting a real array, in which case the disposition is decline-not-delete, and deciding that needs the array geometry -- which is exactly the check that withdrew `sgideoob` as a false OOB.  Named, not judged.  **SINGLE CONSUMER: `machine_alpha.c:61`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`.**  There is no alpha rig in this tree, which is why the file has escaped attention -- the same reason `dev_pcc2.c` did.  *No rig means no coverage rather than no bug.*  **COMPANION TO `pcc2widthkills`, AND DELIBERATELY EXAMINED SEPARATELY.**  Both files surfaced from one question -- is `dev_sgi_re.c` uniquely bad or uniquely looked at? -- but they are different classes: pcc2 is WIDTH kills (12 sites, all `len != 1`), this is VALUE kills.  Filing them as one row would have merged two fix designs, and the reopening rule already says a round takes one site unless the SAME fix design covers both.  *A census that finds two files at once is exactly where over-claiming starts.*  *** THIS ROW HAS NO RUNG-3 ATTEMPT RECORDED AT ALL, AND THERE IS A PLAUSIBLE TARGET IT NEVER NAMES.  Found 2026-08-31 by grepping the row for the attempt the 2026-08-27 lifecycle ruling requires. ***  The row reasons from *\"No rig means no coverage rather than no bug\"*, which is true about COVERAGE and is not the same claim as *no machine instantiates this device*.  **`src/machines/machine_alpha.c` calls `device_add(machine, \"lca\")` under `case ST_ALPHABOOK1:`, and that subtype is registered with the invocable alias `\"alphabook1\"`** (anchors: `grep -n 'device_add(machine, \"lca\")' src/machines/machine_alpha.c` and `grep -n 'alphabook1' src/machines/machine_alpha.c`).  So the prerequisite a rung-3 probe needs -- a real, committed, UNMODIFIED in-tree machine description that maps the device -- **exists**, and the invocation is `-E alpha -e alphabook1`.  *What is NOT established, and must not be assumed: whether that machine constructs far enough to accept a cold-debugger probe, and whether the four registers are reachable through its decode.  Those are exactly what the ATTEMPT is for.*  **The ruling's point is that the ask costs one invocation and nobody had made it** -- `witness: none` must mean *tried and could not*, never *did not think to*, and those two states must not render the same.  A seat that reports it cannot build one has given a real answer and the row proceeds as documented-only, with the attempt recorded.  *Filed as the next action on this row rather than done here: firing a probe contends with a measuring seat that was running when this was found, and a probe measured under load is the load-sensitive-oracle failure this project has already paid for once.*  *** THE STATED BLOCKER -- \"there is no rig\" -- IS NOT A REASON A RUNG-3 WITNESS IS IMPOSSIBLE, AND THIS PROJECT'S OWN MOST RECENT WITNESS REFUTES IT.  Measured 2026-08-31. ***  `#498`'s rung-3 witness for `sgidewidth` execs `gxemul -V -x -E sgi -e ip32 -M 64 0x80001000:<DUMMY>` and **`DUMMY` falls back to `/dev/null`** when `regress/dummy_kernel.bin` is absent (anchor: `grep -n 'DUMMY = ' regress/sgi_de_width_probe.py`).  **No disk image, no kernel, no boot -- a cold-debugger probe needs a machine that CONSTRUCTS and an empty load file, and that is all.**  Verified by running all four candidates under WSL with `<addr>:/dev/null`: `-E sgi -e ip32`, `-E alpha -e alphabook1`, `-E mvme88k -e mvme187` and `-e mvme188` **all reach `machine:` and print a cpu line**.  *A rig is what a rung-4 BOOT needs.  Conflating the two is what left both of these rows ranked as witness-blocked when neither had been tried.*  **WHAT IS STILL NOT ESTABLISHED, and the attempt is exactly for this:** whether the debugger reaches these registers through the machine's real decode, and whether the observable differs pre- and post-fix.  Constructing is necessary, not sufficient.  *My first run of this test reported all three machines failing -- because I ran the WSL-built ELF from git-bash and got `cannot execute binary file`, while my own grep for `GXemul|machine:` matched the word `gxemul` INSIDE that error and scored it a healthy banner.  A broken apparatus plus a check that matched the error text nearly filed a confident \"these machines do not construct\".*  For this row the target is **`-E alpha -e alphabook1`**, which is the only subtype whose arm calls `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`.  *** PASS-1 PACKET-FED PANEL, 2026-08-31 (`_scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346`).  THIS ROW'S OWN TITLE WAS WRONG, MY BRIEF CONTRADICTED ITSELF, AND BOTH ARE NOW SETTLED FROM THE SOURCE. ***  **(1) THE CENSUS.  The title said each register *accepts exactly ONE value*; that is true of ONE arm out of four.**  Derived, with the last constant looked up after glm named it as the settling fact:  `W_MASK0` accepts exactly `{0x700000}` -- one.  `W_BASE0` accepts `{0, 0x300800000}` -- **two**.  `IOC_CONF` kills iff `idata & ~1`, so it accepts `{0, 1}` -- **two**, and the predicate is *bit 0 only*, not a constant.  `TB_ENA` kills iff `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN`, and `alpha_lcareg.h` defines `IOC_TB_ENA_TEN 0x0000000000000080ULL` -- a SINGLE bit, so it accepts `{0, 0x80}` -- **two**.  Title corrected.  *This matters beyond tidiness: the de-escalation rule owes an accept row per predicate, and a detector built from `exactly one value` would pin the wrong accept side on three of the four arms.*  **glm's sharpest point is about WHICH value to pin:** for `W_BASE0` the accepted `0` is the *maximally ordinary* write -- zeroing a window -- so an accept row pinned to the exotic magic constant *misfires on the most legal write rather than an unusual one*.  **(2) MY BRIEF CONTRADICTED ITSELF AND THE SOURCE SETTLES IT AGAINST ME.**  Q2 asserted these fields *feed address-window decoding*; Q3's note said the emulator *does not otherwise model them*.  glm caught that both cannot be true and asked which.  Measured (`grep -n 'window_base_0|window_mask_0|tlb_enable|ioc_conf' src/devices/dev_lca.c`): `window_base_0`, `window_mask_0` and `tlb_enable` are **written and read back and nothing else** -- each appears exactly twice, as `odata = d->X` and `d->X = idata`.  **Only `ioc_conf` is CONSUMED**, at `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1`.  *** SO THE FIX DESIGN SPLITS, AND IT WOULD NOT HAVE WITHOUT THIS CHECK. ***  Three of the four arms are pure BACKED ACCEPT storage -- a decline that keeps the store harms nothing, because no emulator code reads the field.  **The fourth is different**: `ioc_conf`'s low bit selects the bus, and its guard is `idata & ~1`, so a rejected value's bit 0 still reaches the consumer.  glm's *\"a decline that keeps the store is an accept with a complaint, so validate BEFORE storing and leave the previous value\"* is right **for that arm specifically**, and is over-general for the other three.  **(3) `idata` IS `uint64_t`** (`memory_readmax64`), so `0x300800000` is representable and glm's width worry is answered -- the comparison is reachable, not dead code.  **(4) Reachability stays a READING, correctly.**  All three seats declined to say whether these are the only values REAL HARDWARE accepts or merely the only ones this emulator was developed against, and read the code's own wording (`TODO`, *\"differs from the expected values\"*) as supporting the second.  There is no LCA datasheet in `_scratchpad/`.  *That is the honest form and it is flagged as a reading rather than a fact.*\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP MEASUREMENT 2026-08-31 (not a seat answer; recorded here rather than in `entries` so it cannot be counted toward the panel). ***  evidence: dev_lca.c :203 :222 :237 :252 :276 :291 (six guards); :129 inside a comment block\n\n*** THREE WRONG RECORDS ON THIS ROW, found by the rung-3 seat and RE-VERIFIED BY READING dev_lca.c before being written here. ***\n\n(1) SCOPE: there are SIX value-guarded arms, not four.  Verified at dev_lca.c :203 (idata & ~1, IOC_CONF), :222 (idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN, TB_ENA), :237 (W_BASE0), :252 (W_MASK0), :276 (W_BASE1), :291 (W_MASK1).  OUTSTANDING_BUGS scoped four-plus-two-masks (= the correct six); the LEDGER's pass-1 census picked a DIFFERENT four and silently dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1.  A detector built from the ledger's four ships NO ACCEPT ROW for those two, which the de-escalation clause requires per predicate.\n\n(2) THE EARLIER TITLE CORRECTION IS ITSELF WRONG.  It says the one-value claim is `true of ONE arm out of four'.  Against all six, THREE arms accept exactly one value: W_MASK0 {0x700000}, W_BASE1 {0x240000000}, W_MASK1 {0x3ff00000}.  W_BASE0 accepts two, IOC_CONF {0,1}, TB_ENA {0,0x80}.  **The original title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it** -- this project's own rule that a correction carries more authority than the error and therefore earns a SECOND reading.\n\n(3) hold_question says TWELVE live exit(1).  It is ELEVEN: `grep -c` returns 12, but dev_lca.c:129 is inside a /* ... */ block.  The row's own `witness' field already said eleven, so the row contradicted itself.\n\nCONSUMPTION, confirmed and stronger than filed: five of the six guarded arms are PURE STORAGE (each field appears exactly twice -- odata = d->X and d->X = idata).  `ioc_conf' is the sole consumer, at dev_lca.c:121 `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1'.", "state_note": "*** RECORDS CORRECTION 2026-09-06, from RE-DERIVING this row's own citations instead of trusting them.  THE\nHEADLINE COUNT (eleven live terminating calls) IS RIGHT.  THE ENUMERATION UNDER IT IS WRONG IN TWO WAYS, AND THE\nFIRST ONE CHANGES THE FIX DESIGN. ***\n\n**1.  `dev >= 21` IS NOT A LIVE KILL.  IT IS ALREADY DE-FATALISED -- AND IT IS THIS FILE'S OWN FIX PRECEDENT.**\n\nThe row lists it among \"two range guards that also kill\".  The source says otherwise: the guard is at `:127`, its\nbody is a COMMENTED-OUT `fatal()` + `exit(1)` at `:128-129`, and what actually executes is `dev = 0;` at `:130`.\n\n```c\n\tif (dev >= 21) {\n\t\t/*  fatal(\"[ LCA: No bus 0 device? TODO ]\\n\");\n\t\texit(1);  */\n\t\tdev = 0;\n\t}\n```\n\nSomeone has already made, in this very file, the decision this row exists to make: **delete the kill, substitute a\nsafe default, keep going.**  That is a TRANSFERRED WARRANT from inside the same file, which the six-member\noption-set rule says to go looking for -- *\"grep the fork for an existing idiom; the tree's own precedents are part\nof the design space\"* -- and which nobody had found because the row was read rather than re-derived.  So only ONE\nrange guard is live: `ofs > len` at `:163`.\n\n**2.  THE ENUMERATION COVERED 8 OF THE 11, AND THE THREE IT MISSED ARE NOT VALUE GUARDS AT ALL.**\n\nRe-derived by `grep -n 'exit(1)' dev_lca.c` minus the commented one (12 matches, 11 live), the eleven are:\n\n| line | class | condition |\n|---|---|---|\n| 73  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_assert` -- bare `fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1)`, **NO GUARD** |\n| 85  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_deassert` -- likewise |\n| 134 | BUS | `bus != 0`, i.e. `d->ioc_conf & 1` |\n| 163 | RANGE | `ofs > len` |\n| 206 | VALUE | `idata & ~1` (IOC_CONF) |\n| 226 | VALUE | `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` |\n| 241 | VALUE | `idata != 0 && idata != 0x300800000` |\n| 256 | VALUE | `idata != 0x700000` |\n| 280 | VALUE | `idata != 0x240000000` |\n| 295 | VALUE | `idata != 0x3ff00000` |\n| 315 | CATCH-ALL | `default:` -- any unimplemented ioc offset, read OR write |\n\n**THE TWO INTERRUPT STUBS ARE PLAUSIBLY THE MOST SEVERE OF THE ELEVEN, AND A ROW FRAMED AS A VALUE-GUARD CLASS\nCOULD NOT SEE THEM.**  They are not a wrong value being written -- they are an ENTIRE UNIMPLEMENTED PATH with no\npredicate at all, so *any* device asserting an interrupt through the LCA ends the process regardless of what it\nwrites.  The four \"accepts exactly one value\" registers need a firmware revision that writes something else; these\ntwo need only that an interrupt is asserted.\n\n*A row named after one class hides the members of the others.  That is this project's cell-not-class pattern\nwearing an enumeration instead of a detector -- the same shape the m88kexcdeliver chain hit four times.*\n\n**WHAT IS UNCHANGED:** the severity (HIGH), the single consumer (`machine_alpha.c`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`),\nthe absence of any alpha rig, and the observation that a guest here is FIRMWARE so the reaching value is whatever\nSRM or NetBSD writes.  The scope caveat about range-vs-value guards also stands and is now sharper, because there\nis only one live range guard to reason about.\n\n--- the original note follows, unaltered ---\n\n\n=== DESIGN PASS, 2026-09-06 (codex / GPT-6 Astra at max, read-only).  Answer preserved at\n=== regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_design_20260906.txt.  IT CORRECTED THREE OF THE FIVE PREMISES I HAD\n=== JUST RE-DERIVED, and one of the corrections changes what this round may even choose from.\n\n**SEVERITY ORDER CONFIRMED: the two INTERRUPT STUBS first** -- but \"provisionally; firmware execution order is\nunproved\", which is the honest qualifier my own framing lacked.\n\n*** A SEVENTH OPTION-SET MEMBER: IMPLEMENT. ***  The carrier's set is DELETE / DECLINE / DECLINE+LATCHED\nANNOUNCE / ACCEPT / FAULT / BACKED ACCEPT -- all six presume the access is a REFUSAL DECISION.  For the two\ninterrupt stubs none of them applies: *\"declining an interrupt leaves the machine incomplete.\"*  A stub is not a\nrefusal to service a value; it is an unimplemented PATH, and the disposition is to write the code.  This is the\noption-set completeness rule paying off exactly as it is meant to -- the member was found by a seat reading the\ntree, not by argument.\n\n*** AND THE FAULT MEMBER IS NOT AVAILABLE ON THIS MACHINE AT ALL. ***  `dev_unreadable.c` works because\n`memory_rw.c` dispatches an exception on a failed device access **for MIPS and M88K, with NO ALPHA EQUIVALENT**.\nSo the idiom this fork installs ten times in `machine_sgi.c` does not transfer here, and any round reaching for\nFAULT owes Alpha exception delivery first.  *A precedent that is real elsewhere in the tree is not thereby\ntransferable; that is the warrant-transfer rule, and it took a seat opening `memory_rw.c` to see it.*\n\n**AND A FINDING ABOUT THE ALPHA PORT THAT OUTRANKS THIS ROW:** the Alpha interrupt callbacks merely set\n`irq_asserted`, and **the execution loop never consumes that flag**.  So implementing the two stubs would not by\nitself deliver an interrupt -- removing the exits cannot complete the path.  Filed as its own question rather\nthan folded in here.\n\n## THE PREMISE AUDIT, and I was wrong three times\n\n* **PREMISE 1 HOLDS.**  Twelve textual occurrences, eleven outside comments.\n* **PREMISE 2's REMOVAL IS REAL; ITS \"USABLE PRECEDENT\" CONCLUSION IS WRONG.**  I read the commented-out\n  `dev >= 21` kill plus its live `dev = 0` as the file's own answer, transferable to this round.  It is not: it\n  **ALIASES \"no selected device\" to DEVICE ZERO**, which is harmless today only because device 7 is the sole\n  installed device and slot 0 happens to be empty.  Populate slot 0 and it is a latent alias defect.  *So the\n  question I asked -- is this a sound precedent or a latent defect of its own -- has the second answer, and my\n  brief had already leaned on the first.*\n* **PREMISE 3's CLASS COUNTS HOLD, ITS SINGLETON CLAIM DOES NOT.**  `W_BASE0` accepts **TWO** values, `0` and\n  `0x300800000` (`idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL`), so \"four registers accepting exactly ONE value\" is\n  wrong -- only **three** window guards accept a single value.  *This one is mine twice over: the original row\n  note said it, I re-derived the enumeration tonight and reproduced it, and the guard's own text was in my\n  terminal when I did.*\n* **PREMISE 4 HOLDS** as one machine-setup consumer (AlphaBook 1), with no dedicated boot rig -- **but\n  \"never exercised\" DOES NOT FOLLOW**: a generic constructor sweep exists (`gate_asan_sweep.sh`), without\n  proving coverage of these branches.  My \"no rig means no coverage\" was too strong.\n* **PREMISE 5 IS AN UNSUPPORTED RESTRICTION.**  I said a guest here is firmware, so the reaching value is\n  whatever SRM or NetBSD writes.  Guest KERNEL stores use the same MMIO path, and interrupt callbacks arise from\n  DEVICE events -- neither is firmware.\n\n## DISPOSITIONS IT WOULD TAKE, per class\n* **INTERRUPT STUB (2):** IMPLEMENT forwarding and CPU interrupt delivery.\n* **BUS (1):** implement Type-1 decoding then ACCEPT through `bus_pci`, using its existing absent-device\n  handling.  `ioc_conf & 1` selects the CONFIGURATION-CYCLE TYPE and is **not the decoded bus number** -- and\n  deleting this exit alone leaves `dev` uninitialised.\n* **RANGE (1):** DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with initialised read results and no forwarded side effects.  Note\n  `ofs` is at most three, **so this guard cannot fire for four- or eight-byte accesses** -- changing `>`\n  mechanically is not a design.\n* **VALUE (6):** ACCEPT documented register fields using the existing backing, latched announce for the missing\n  DMA/TLB effects.  **Every guarded assignment already happens BEFORE its check**, so no invented backing is\n  needed -- but readback alone does not implement translation.\n* **CATCH-ALL (1):** split documented registers (`STAT0`/`STAT1`) from genuine holes; FAULT only for confirmed\n  holes and only once Alpha exception delivery exists.\n\n**WHAT IT COULD NOT ESTABLISH, said plainly:** source shows connected interrupt paths and conditional MMIO paths\nfor the other nine sites, but **no particular SRM/NetBSD execution of any terminating site**.  Firmware\nreachability and failure frequency remain unmeasured, so the severity order is provisional on exactly that.\n\n\n*** THE SEVERITY WARRANT THIS ROW WAS MISSING: THE REJECTED VALUES INCLUDE A DOCUMENTED ONE, AND THE\nSTORE LANDS BEFORE THE PROCESS ENDS.  Found by the codex seat 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt),\nverified against the source and the header before recording. ***\n\nEach of the three window guards has this shape:\n\n    d->window_mask_0 = idata;                          <- the store LANDS\n    if (idata != 0x700000ULL) { fatal(...); exit(1); } <- then the process ends\n\n**And zero is a documented encoding.**  From src/include/thirdparty/alpha_lcareg.h:\n\n    #define IOC_W_MASK_1M   0x0000000000000000ULL   /* 1MB window */\n\nSo a guest selecting the SMALLEST DOCUMENTED WINDOW SIZE ends the emulator.  The same argument covers\nW_BASE1, whose one accepted constant 0x240000000 carries IOC_W_BASE_WEN (0x200000000, *Window enable*) --\n**so a driver DISABLING a window, by writing a base with WEN clear, is killed too.**\n\n*** THIS IS #504's WARRANT AT A SECOND DEVICE: documented values that the model kills on. ***  It is not a\njunk-value guard; it is a whitelist of ONE that excludes documented members of its own encoding.  codex adds\nthat all three registers start at zero through constructor initialisation, so the rejected value is also the\nRESET value.\n\n**AND THE RANGE SITE IS FAR MORE REACHABLE THAN THIS ROW SAID.**  It was recorded as `ofs > len'.  Reading\nDEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa): `relative_addr >>= 5; ofs = relative_addr & 3;' -- **ofs is guest ADDRESS BITS 5-6**,\nnot a written value.  So an ordinary 1-byte access at an address with those bits set to 2 or 3 ends the\nprocess.  That is address decoding, not a value whitelist, and it belongs in a different disposition class\nfrom the six value guards.  The rest of that function is filed separately as [[lcaisalane]].\n\n*** DISPOSITION PASS, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_disposition_20260907.txt).  IT NARROWS MY REACHABILITY CLAIM,\nCONFIRMS TWO OTHERS, AND SUPPLIES THE IN-TREE PRECEDENTS THE OPTION-SET RULE ASKS FOR -- INCLUDING\nCONFLICTING ONES, WHICH IS WHAT I ASKED IT TO SURFACE. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"Disposition 5 is unavailable through Alpha's failed-device-access path.  Five candidate rounds fit\na uniform VALUE-refusal policy.  The tree supplies CONFLICTING precedents, especially for lane handling and\nstoring unsupported values.\"*\n\n*** MY `AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE ENDS THE EMULATOR' CLAIM IS TOO BROAD, AND I HAVE REPEATED IT IN THREE\nPLACES. ***  codex: *\"they read zero INITIALLY, then echo accepted writes.  Writing back the initial zero\nkills the last three; an arbitrary RMW does not necessarily kill them -- its resulting value matters.\"*\n\n**The precise claim is the INITIAL-ZERO readback**: before any accepted write, `W_MASK0', `W_BASE1' and\n`W_MASK1' read 0 and accept only one non-zero constant each, so a read-modify-write performed on a\nfreshly-reset device writes 0 back and ends the process.  **Once a valid value has been written the register\nechoes it**, and an RMW of that value is accepted.  So the window is early -- driver probe or\ninitialisation -- rather than any RMW at any time.  That is still a routine thing for a driver to do at\nattach, and it is still reached with a documented encoding (`IOC_W_MASK_1M` = 0), but the sentence I wrote\nwas wider than the fact.\n\n**CONFIRMED, both of which I had asserted:** every VALUE arm STORES BEFORE CHECKING; and the accepted sets\nare exactly as filed (`IOC_CONF' {0,1}, `IOC_TB_ENA' {0,0x80}, `W_BASE0' {0,0x300800000}, and the three\nsingle-value ones).\n\n**DISPOSITION 5 IS UNAVAILABLE -- now established from a third direction.**  The carrier already recorded it\ngenerally, [[alphafaultclaim]] reached it from the instruction side, and this reaches it from the\nfailed-device-access path.  **Any proposal for a bus-error refusal on an Alpha device owes Alpha exception\ndelivery first**, which is a far larger round than the one it would be riding on.\n\n**THE PRECEDENT I ASKED FOR, AND IT IS AN EXACT ONE:** `cpu_sh.c' defines `sh3_cpu_interrupt_assert()' and\n`sh3_cpu_interrupt_deassert()' with EMPTY `/* TODO */' bodies -- *\"an exact unimplemented-callback precedent\nfor 2, ignore silently\"* -- and `core/interrupt.c' defines empty `no_interrupt_assert/deassert' callbacks,\nthough those represent intentionally absent connections rather than unimplemented ones.  **So the two\nINTERRUPT STUBS have a direct in-tree answer** and do not need disposition 7 after all, which is what I had\nassumed they needed.\n\n**AND IT REPORTS THE PRECEDENTS CONFLICT** -- especially on lane handling and on whether to store an\nunsupported value.  That is the more useful answer: it means the choice is genuinely open at those sites\nrather than settled by the tree, so a round there must argue rather than transfer.\n\n**SCOPE: FIVE candidate rounds** under a uniform VALUE-refusal policy -- consistent with the earlier read's\n*\"this needs several rounds\"* and finer than it.\n\n**WHERE THE OVER-BROAD FORM SURVIVES, named because it cannot be edited:** commit `de96f11`'s MESSAGE\nsays *\"each reads back 0 while its guard accepts one non-zero constant, and AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE\nENDS THE EMULATOR on all three\"*.  A commit message is immutable, so the house rule -- correct the false\nrecord IN PLACE -- cannot be followed literally.  **The next best thing is to point at it from the record a\nreader will reach**, which is this row.  The narrowed claim above is the operative one: the kill needs the\nINITIAL zero readback, not any RMW at any time.\n\n*** THE PREREQUISITE IS DISCHARGED.  `#506` LANDED THIS AFTERNOON. ***\n\nThis row's HOLD-Q asked: *\"given that every row must match the `fatal()` TEXT rather than the exit\nstatus, and given that `lcaconfwidth` means the IOC_CONF arm's text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the\ntext-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must `lcaconfwidth`'s printf fix land FIRST, making it a\nprerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?\"*\n\n**It landed.**  `#506` repaired both narrowing sites in `dev_lca.c` -- `:205` (IOC_CONF) and the\n`default:` arm -- to `0x%\" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`, matching the five siblings.  Measured by\n`regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py`: **HEAD 4/6 -> fix 6/6, same script**, with `kill width` now reading\n`0x100000000` instead of `0x0`.\n\n> **So the text oracle is now sound at the IOC_CONF arm, and the ordering question in the HOLD-Q is\n> answered by events rather than by adjudication.**  What remains of that HOLD-Q is its SECOND half,\n> untouched: *the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger's earlier\n> census dropped `W_BASE1` and `W_MASK1` -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.*\n\n*The interrupt-stub confound is also unchanged and still governs the witness design:\n`lca_interrupt_assert` and `lca_interrupt_deassert` are bare `fatal()`+`exit(1)` with the ISA PIC\nchain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the same status as the value guards under\ntest.  That is why the rows must match text, which is why `#506` was the prerequisite.*\n\n*** THE HOLD-Q'S SECOND HALF IS ANSWERED BY MEASUREMENT: THE ACCEPT-ROW SET IS SIX. ***\nEnumerated by reading `if (idata` in `dev_lca.c`, 2026-09-07:\n\n    :203  IOC_CONF   idata & ~1\n    :231  TB_ENA     idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN\n    :246  W_BASE0    idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL\n    :261  W_MASK0    idata != 0x700000ULL\n    :285  W_BASE1    idata != 0x240000000ULL\n    :300  W_MASK1    idata != 0x3ff00000ULL\n\n**Six, not four** -- the earlier census in this row dropped `W_BASE1` and `W_MASK1`, exactly as the\nHOLD-Q suspected.  *Note two shapes, which may matter for the fix design: two BITMASK tests\n(`& ~mask`) and four EQUALITY tests, one of which accepts two values.*\n\n**And the eleven-live count is confirmed rather than carried:** `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'`\ngives **12**, of which `:129` sits inside a commented-out block -- so 11 live, in **FIVE** classes:\n2 interrupt stubs, 1 bus/cycle-type, 1 range/lane, 6 value, 1 default.\n\n> #### *** THIS SENTENCE SAID \"four classes\" AND THEN LISTED FIVE.  Corrected 2026-09-07 after the\n> #### grok design pass named it -- and the same slip was in the brief that asked it to. ***\n> The opener asks seats to check enumerations; mine was wrong in its own headline, with the\n> refutation printed three words later.  *A count and the list beside it are two claims, and\n> nothing checks them against each other.*\n\n*** AND THE FIRST HALF OF THE HOLD-Q IS DISCHARGED BY EVENTS: `#506` LANDED (`7a41b3f`). ***\nThe question was whether `lcaconfwidth`'s printf fix had to land first, since this row's rows must\nmatch the `fatal()` TEXT rather than the exit status (the two interrupt stubs kill with the same\nstatus as every value guard).  **It landed and is measured** -- both narrowing sites now print\n`0x%\" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`, HEAD 4/6 -> fix 6/6 -- so the IOC_CONF arm's text is sound.\n\n**Design brief out to grok** (`regress/artifacts/lcabadval/DESIGN_brief.md`) rather than codex: the\ncodex seat refused the sibling `lcaconfwidth` census on its content filter, and a brief enumerating\neleven host kills is the same recorded trigger shape.\n\n*** GROK DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07: **NOT RATIFY** as a six-armed one-design round until two of my\nclaims are dropped.  The ROUND SHAPE survives; two warrants and one count do not. ***\n\n**1. \"THE ACCEPT-ROW SET IS SIX\" STILL UNDER-COUNTS.**  De-escalation owes a row per PREDICATE, but\nthree of the six predicates accept TWO values:\n\n    IOC_CONF  {0, 1}          TB_ENA   {0, 0x80}      W_BASE0  {0, 0x300800000}\n    W_MASK0   {0x700000}      W_BASE1  {0x240000000}  W_MASK1  {0x3ff00000}\n\n**Nine pinning values, not six rows** -- and three of the nine are the reset `0`, which is vacuous\nunless sequenced after a nonzero write.  *So replacing the old \"four\" with \"six\" corrected one\nunder-count into another.*\n\n**2. S5 DOES NOT TRANSFER, AND CITING IT WOULD CITE A DEFECT AS A WARRANT.**  I offered the live\n`dev = 0` path (`dev >= 21`, kill commented out) as this file's own *delete-the-kill, substitute-a-\nsafe-default* precedent.  **It is PCI type-0 TAG DECODE, not a register-value policy**: it aliases\n\"no device selected\" onto SLOT 0, which is empty today only because the installed SIO is device 7.\n**And that loop is already filed as [[lcashiftub]] -- `0x800 << 20` in a signed `int` at `dev == 20`\nis UB.**  Substituting `0x700000` when firmware wrote `IOC_W_MASK_1M` (0) would be that same alias\npointed at a documented encoding.\n\n> **THE RIGHT IN-FILE WARRANT IS `W_T_BASE0` / `W_T_BASE1`**: the sibling TRANSLATED-window registers\n> already store any value and do not kill.  *Base/mask killing on a firmware snapshot while\n> translated-base accepts everything is the file's own outlier* -- which is a far stronger warrant\n> than the one I proposed, and one grep away.\n\n**3. THE ROUND SHAPE IS RATIFIED: ONE ROUND, MEMBER 4 ACCEPT, ALL SIX.**  Delete `fatal`+`exit` and\nkeep the store that already ran.  Five of the six fields are pure storage (`window_base_*`,\n`window_mask_*`, `tlb_enable` appear only as `odata = d->X` / `d->X = idata`); the sixth, `ioc_conf`,\nis consumed only as `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` in `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`, **so extra CONF bits\nare already discarded at the only consumer** and a masked store would be an unlicensed addition.\n**Bitmask vs equality is a DETECTOR-SHAPE split, not a fix-design split.**  FAULT is unavailable on\nAlpha.\n\n*** 4. THE FLOOR-(a) TRAP, and it is the finding that most changes the detector: AN ACCEPT-ONLY\nDETECTOR IS GREEN ON HEAD. ***  Writing the six magic constants SURVIVES on the committed build.  So\na detector that writes only currently-legal values **is green under verbatim reinstatement of all six\noriginal guards** -- the vacuity-floor miss in its purest form.  **The rows that make (b) bite are the\ndocumented-REJECTED writes**: MASK0 `0`, BASE1 with WEN clear, CONF bit 32, a non-TEN TB_ENA bit.\n\n**5. THE (d) FORMS HERE ARE SITE OMISSION AND DISJUNCT-DROPPING, not an operand swap.**  Leave\n`W_MASK1` (or `W_BASE1`) fatal while de-fatalising the other five -- incomplete clause (b), and\n**`ioc rt` in `lca_confwidth_probe.py` stays green because nothing in that probe touches MASK1.**\nSecond form: on `W_BASE0`, drop `idata != 0ULL &&` and keep the magic comparison -- the magic accept\nrow stays green, *which is exactly why BASE0 owes TWO pins*.\n\n**6. `lca_confwidth_probe.py`'s `kill fired` and `died` WANTS WILL INVERT IF THIS ROUND SHIPS.**  It\nis a `#506` witness, not this round's detector.  **Re-pin it under the oracle-correction rule -- do\nnot launder by editing wants after the product change.**\n\n**7. THE STUBS ARE A SIBLING, NOT A PREREQUISITE.**  Construction does not fire them today (PIC\n`ier`/`irr` start 0, 8253 `in_use=0`, WDC only with an IDE image, ns16550 needs `com_ier` bits), and\n`#506`'s legal session already survived on this machine.  **Text is sufficient to ATTRIBUTE a kill\nand is NOT a survival oracle** -- an accept row wanting `alive` still false-reds if a stub fires, and\nthat is controlled by SESSION SHAPE (cold prompt, `step`, don't touch the ISA window), not by\nde-fatalising the stubs first.  *A BOOT witness would make them a prerequisite, because `timer_stop`\nis why stepping does not tick the PIT.*\n\n**8. AND AN HONESTY CONSTRAINT FOR THE CONTRACT:** `default:` still kills `STAT0`/`STAT1`, which the\nheader documents as R/W1C.  **This round can be green and firmware can still die**, so the contract\nmust name that as out of scope or the round will be reported as a false pass of *\"LCA no longer\nhost-kills on IOC MMIO\"*.\n\n**Split out, each its own round:** the interrupt stubs (member 7); `bus != 0` -- *deleting that\n`exit(1)` leaves `dev` UNINITIALISED in the `else`*, which is the sketch rule and not a rider;\n`ofs > len` (already [[lcaisalane]]; `ofs` is address bits 5-6); and the `default:` arm.  **HAE at\n`case LCA_IOC_BASE` already silently ignores writes to a documented register -- that is member 2, not\n4, and must not be unified with the six.**\n\n*** 2026-09-07, CODEX FIX-CHECK: QUALIFIED YES, AND IT FOUND A COUNTEREXAMPLE TO THE ROUND'S CENTRAL WARRANT. ***  All six stores confirmed prior to their guards (table, full denominator); the consumer analysis confirmed with one sharpening -- masking CONF would change READBACK policy, not merely bus selection.  **But `dev_sh4.c's RCR1 comment records that its store USED TO run before the exit and was DELIBERATELY MOVED BELOW THE GUARD**, so \"the store already ran\" is not a general warrant in this tree.  The round now carries an open A-vs-B design question (keep the store vs decline the write) that the W_T_BASE* siblings CANNOT settle, since accepting-anything there may be an absent guard rather than a decision.  Disposition set also grew by two source-backed members (accept/decline WITH REPEATED announcements; complete with a GUEST-VISIBLE DEVICE ERROR, dev_wdc.c's WDCE_ABRT+interrupt).  And a correction to my own brief: only TWO BITS IN STAT0 are R/W1C, not STAT1.\n\n*** TITLE CORRECTED 2026-09-07: it said FOUR registers and the true count is SIX. *** Derived, not recalled: `exit(1)` occurs SEVEN times in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) -- six guarded value-arms (LCA_IOC_CONF :215, TB_ENA :235, W_BASE0 :250, W_MASK0 :265, W_BASE1 :289, W_MASK1 :304) and the `default:` arm at :324, which this round does NOT close.  The four window registers are presumably what the row was opened on; CONF and TB_ENA carry the same shape and were folded in later without the title following.  **A stale title is how a queue row is READ** -- the brief that goes to the panel says six, and a reader comparing the two would have had to guess which was right.  W_T_BASE0 (:272) and W_T_BASE1 (:311) confirmed UNGUARDED: plain readback/store, no value test -- which is the sibling warrant the design brief puts to the panel.\n\n*** AND THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE WAS ITSELF CORRUPTED WHEN FIRST WRITTEN -- BY THE SHELL, QUIETLY\nENOUGH TO SURVIVE REVIEW. ***  It was applied through `python -c \"...\"` with DOUBLE quotes, and\nbash performed COMMAND SUBSTITUTION on the backticks inside it: `exit(1)` and `default:` were\nEXECUTED and replaced by their empty output, leaving readable prose with two words silently\nmissing -- \"Derived, not recalled:  occurs SEVEN times\".  bash did print two errors, but python\nran on the mangled source anyway and the write SUCCEEDED, so the record was wrong while the\ncommand reported success.\n\n**Inside a double-quoted shell argument a backtick is a COMMAND, not a quote mark.**  That is the\ncarrier's backslash-through-heredoc class arriving on a new character, and with the same\nsignature: the failure lands in the FILE, not in the exit status.\n\n*** AND THE REPAIR FAILED TWICE MORE BEFORE IT LANDED, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  The\nfix was retried as `python -c '...'` with SINGLE quotes -- correct for backticks -- and bash\nrejected it, because the note explaining the hazard contains the word \"carrier's\", whose\nAPOSTROPHE terminated the shell quote.  So: double quotes lose to backticks, single quotes lose to\napostrophes, and English prose about shell quoting reliably contains both.\n\n**The rule is not a better quoting style.  It is: PROSE DESTINED FOR A RECORD GOES INTO A SCRIPT\nFILE, never through `-c`.**  This paragraph was written that way and landed intact on the first\nattempt.\n\n*** PASS-1 DESIGN PANEL 2026-09-07 (preserved: regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_p1_20260907/ -- _scratchpad is never committed).  PROVISIONAL: FIVE OF THE\nLANDED SEATS ARE READ HERE AND **CODEX WAS STILL STREAMING** WHEN THIS WAS WRITTEN (its file grew\n14,943 -> 30,594 bytes between two reads and its tail is mid-`exec`, not a verdict).  Any ruling\nshort of the roster is provisional on its face, so no disposition is adopted below. ***\n\nSEATS: grok, glm, deepseek, minimax and the kimi-k3:cloud SUBSTITUTE answered (5); codex IN FLIGHT;\nthe kimi CLI WALLED on an explicit 403 weekly limit, so that seat is COVERED, not lost; agy\nDELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (measurement reserve, owner ruling 2026-08-30); opus5 and fable5 NOT FIRED,\nreserved for the 2026-09-09 adjudication.\n\n*** THE PANEL'S DOMINANT FINDING IS THAT MY BRIEF POSED THE WRONG QUESTION, AND FOUR SEATS REACHED\nIT INDEPENDENTLY: THE FORK IS MIS-POSED AS ONE DISPOSITION FOR ALL SIX ARMS. ***\n\nThe kimi substitute put it sharpest, and it is a self-inflicted wound: *\"The fork as posed (one\nanswer for the file) repeats the exact error the #447 census inlines a warning against.\"*  **My\nbrief QUOTED `#447`'s own sentence -- \"Saying 'the store is upstream' once for the file would have\nbeen wrong three times out of four.  Check it per site.\" -- and then asked for a single answer for\nsix arms.**  The warning was inlined as a settled premise and violated in the questions below it.\n\n**WHERE THE SEATS AGREE (4 of 5):** the five PURE-STORAGE arms and `LCA_IOC_CONF` must be decided\nseparately, because CONF is the only one with a consumer (`bus = d->ioc_conf & 1`).  For the five,\npremise 6 survives -- grok's formulation is the one to keep: **\"B copies RCR1's *edit* without\nRCR1's *reason*.\"**  RCR1's warrant was that the stored value has a meaning the model otherwise\nhonours; for the five window/TB_ENA fields nothing honours anything, so the edit does not transfer.\nThe kimi substitute sharpens even the agreement: the right analogy for the five is not \"the lie is\npre-existing\" but **`#447`'s ICR cell -- *no honored state to corrupt*** -- which is a stronger and\ndifferently-grounded warrant than the one my premise 6 offered.\n\n**WHERE THEY SPLIT, AND IT IS A REAL DISPUTE TO SETTLE BY MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN BY COUNT:**\n\n    CONF -> B                 glm       \"premise 2 names a live consumer, so CONF is exactly RCR1-shaped\"\n    CONF -> A, more so        minimax   \"the consumer uses only bit 0, so a rejected value leaves it\n                                         correct -- and B can SILENTLY BREAK it if the retained old\n                                         value differs in bit 0\"\n    CONF -> decline or mask   kimi      split per arm\n    CONF -> neither           grok      \"treat it as a bit-mask leftover, not a window\"\n\n**minimax's objection to B is the one with a measurable consequence** and it is the opposite of\nglm's: under B a rejected CONF write leaves the PREVIOUS value in place, and if the previous value\ndiffers in bit 0 the PCI bus selection silently changes -- so B is not merely conservative there, it\ncan alter a consumer that A leaves correct.  That is checkable and should be checked before either\nis adopted.\n\n**THE DISSENT, recorded as a dissent and not as noise:** deepseek alone says premise 6 is unsound\nfor all six -- *\"the guard's accepted-value set is the model's CONTRACT.  Storing a rejected value\nand returning it on readback creates a new false belief that the value is supported, even if\naccepted values are also unimplemented.  The existing lie for accepted values does not license\nexpanding the set of lies.\"*  It also warns the reasoning generalises: it would justify removing any\nstore-before-guard exit anywhere.  **That generalisation objection is the strongest single argument\nagainst A and is not answered by the five-versus-one split**, because it attacks the warrant rather\nthan its scope.\n\n**A BRIEF DEFECT OF MINE, named by glm and correct:** premise 2's field counts were *asserted, not\ninlined* -- I ran the greps and wrote the conclusion instead of the OUTPUT.  This project's own rule\nis that a brief inlines the DERIVER'S OUTPUT rather than a literal, precisely so a packet-fed seat\ncan check it.  glm could not settle five of the six arms from the packet for exactly that reason and\nsaid so honestly rather than ratifying.\n\n**NOT ADOPTED, PENDING codex AND THE ROSTER.**  The next brief is per-arm by construction, carries\nthe deriver output rather than its summary, and puts minimax's silently-broken-consumer prediction\nas a question a measurement can answer.\n\n*** THE grok BODY, READ IN FULL 2026-09-07 -- AND NONE OF THE FOLLOWING WAS IN ITS VERDICT LINE.\nThis is the read-the-body rule paying off on the same day a row was filed about not reading. ***\n\n**1. IT ANSWERS deepseek's DISSENT, which the five/one split could not.**  deepseek's objection was\nthat premise 6 generalises -- it would license removing any store-before-guard exit anywhere.  grok\nscopes it precisely: *\"premise 6 is not the reasoning that lets every store-before-guard site keep\nits store.  It is the reasoning that lets a site keep its store WHEN NO ACCEPTED VALUE OF THAT FIELD\nIS CONSUMED.  That is #447's own per-site rule, not an evasion of it.\"*  The warrant is a per-site\ntest with a checkable predicate, not a blanket.\n\n**2. PREMISE 5 SCORED: \"The comments exist.  The transfer does not.\"**  RCR1 refuses CONSUMED\nFEATURE BITS; these guards whitelist UNIMPLEMENTED CONSTANTS.  That is the distinction the whole\nfork turned on, and it is sharper than my premise 6 offered.\n\n**3. *** OPTION A OPENS A NEW KILL PATH, AND THIS IS THE ROUND'S SAFETY FINDING. ***  Under A, a CONF\nwrite with extra bits that ALSO sets bit 0 reaches `lca_pci_conf`'s `bus != 0` exit(1) -- *\"newly\nreachable ... today that write dies in the CONF guard first.\"*  **A is not purely a reduction; it\nconverts one kill into a different reachable one.**  Any round shipping A must say so, and should\nconsider whether `lca_pci_conf` is in scope with it.\n\n**4. THE FILE'S OWN IDIOM IS A, AND THE GUARDS ARE THE OUTLIERS.**  In the SAME switch:\n`LCA_IOC_BASE` (HAE) and `TBIA` already silently ignore; `W_T_BASE0/1` already accept-and-store; and\nthe `dev >= 21` loop already deletes a kill and substitutes a default.  *\"The value guards are the\noutliers in THIS switch, not the house style.\"*  That is a stronger warrant than anything my brief\noffered, and it is one grep away.\n\n**5. THE ANNOUNCE TEXT WOULD BECOME A WRONG RECORD THE MOMENT A LANDS.**  The current string says\n*\"the only implemented value\"* about a value that **is not implemented**.  The honest once-message is\n*\"window translation / extra CONF bits not implemented\"*, not *\"illegal encoding\"*.  Rewrite it or\ndelete it with the exit -- do not carry it forward.\n\n**6. OPTION-SET CORRECTIONS.**  `#443`'s restore is *\"B wearing extra lines\"* -- observationally B\nfor readback, since nothing re-enters between the store and the `break`; not a distinct member.\n**STAT0 is the architecturally correct error path for LCA** (`IOC_STAT0_ERR` is R/W1C) and is\n**BLOCKED**: STAT0/STAT1 have no case and hit `default:` exit(1) -- *\"You cannot report an error\nthrough a register that currently kills the host.\"*  That member owes a STAT0 implementation first,\nwhich is a different round.  **FAULT confirmed unavailable**: `memory_rw.c` raises on `res <= 0` only\nunder MEM_MIPS and MEM_M88K; Alpha compiles with MEM_ALPHA and has no arm, so `return 0` fails the\naccess with no guest exception.\n\n**7. MASKING CONF IS UNLICENSED, WITH A REASON I DID NOT HAVE: THERE IS NO DATASHEET.**  No 21066\ndocument is in the packet or in the tracked `_scratchpad/*.pdf` list, so RAZ/WI versus store-full is\n**not settleable from this tree**.  Premise 3 stands and masking would be an addition without a\nwarrant.\n\n*** 8. THE WITNESS IS NOT PENDING -- THE RECIPE EXISTS.  This moves the round off documented-only. ***\n* **Machine: `alphabook1` ONLY.**  `machine_alpha.c`'s `device_add(machine, \"lca\")` is under\n  `ST_ALPHABOOK1` and no other subtype.\n* **Addresses** (K0SEG | physical, because `alpha_translate_v2p` identity-maps K0SEG in kernel mode):\n  CONF `0xfffffc0180000020`, TB_ENA `...00a0`, BASE0 `...0100`, BASE1 `...0120`, MASK0 `...0140`,\n  MASK1 `...0160`.\n* **`regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py` ALREADY issues a guest `stq`/`ldq` through this exact decode at\n  CONF on unmodified `alphabook1`**; the other five are further cases of the same DEVICE_ACCESS on\n  the same mapping.\n* **Three traps that file already paid for, so a new probe need not rediscover them:** debugger\n  `dump` skips `DM_DEFAULT` handlers; a debugger `put d` of a killing value IS a host kill but is\n  NOT a guest instruction (so it does not witness); and HAE at window offset 0 silently succeeds, so\n  **a miss control must not use `LCA_IOC_BASE+0`**.\n\n**9. THE KILL CENSUS FOR THE WHOLE FILE:** eleven live `exit(1)` today; **A removes six; five\nremain** -- the `default:` arm (both reads AND writes, covering STAT0/STAT1 and the rest of the\n512 MB IOC window), `lca_pci_conf`'s `bus != 0`, `lca_isa`'s `ofs > len` (filed as [[lcaisalane]]),\nand the bare `lca_interrupt_assert`/`_deassert` TODO pair (member 7, not this round).  Residuals not\nof this class: HAE writes discarded with no storage; TBIA ignored; `default:` still narrows\n`relative_addr` through `(int)` -- **`#506` fixed the `idata` half only**; and [[lcashiftub]].\n\n**10. A CENSUS ERROR IN THIS ROW'S OWN HISTORY:** the guards are TWO predicate shapes, not one --\nbit-masks (CONF, TB_ENA) and equality whitelists (the four windows) -- **and `W_BASE0` accepts TWO\nvalues**, so the row's original \"four registers accept exactly one value each\" framing was false of\n`BASE0` before the title was even corrected to six.  One DESIGN still covers all six (delete the\nexit, keep the store, optional latch); one design does NOT if you pick B, because *\"B is a coherent\nanswer only for a consumed field, which five of these are not.\"*\n\n*** THE codex SEAT LANDED LAST AND OVERTURNED THE PANEL ON A FACT CHECKABLE IN ONE GREP.  MY\nPREMISE 6 IS DEAD, AND SO IS THE FORMULATION I HAD CREDITED grok WITH. ***\n\n**codex's verdict:** *\"Your argument for A fails as written: RCR1 also has no functional consumer.\nI recommend B for pass 1 as a conservative policy choice; hardware-correct write/readback behavior\nremains unproved.\"*\n\n**VERIFIED HERE, not taken on trust** -- `grep -rn rtc_rcr1 src/` returns exactly FOUR occurrences:\n\n    dev_sh4.c:388    uint8_t   rtc_rcr1;              declaration\n    dev_sh4.c:2776   odata = d->rtc_rcr1;             READBACK\n    dev_sh4.c:2781   ...landed in rtc_rcr1 and...     INSIDE THE COMMENT\n    dev_sh4.c:2805   d->rtc_rcr1 = idata;             STORE\n\n**Zero functional consumers.**  And the comparison that settles it: `ioc_conf` also has four\noccurrences, but its fourth is `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` -- a real consumer.  **So RCR1 is consumed\nLESS than LCA's CONF, not more.**\n\n*** WHAT THIS DESTROYS. ***  My premise 6 claimed LCA differs from RCR1 because *\"RCR1's stored value\nhas a meaning the model otherwise honours\"*.  It has no such meaning: the model stores it and reads\nit back, exactly like the five LCA window fields.  **grok's formulation, which I recorded above as\n\"the keeper\" -- *\"RCR1 refuses CONSUMED FEATURE BITS; these guards whitelist UNIMPLEMENTED\nCONSTANTS\"* -- is FALSE IN ITS FIRST HALF.**  So is my own gloss that `#447`'s edit \"does not\ntransfer\".\n\n**IT TRANSFERS EXACTLY.**  `#447` faced a store-before-guard field with NO functional consumer,\nand chose to MOVE THE STORE BELOW THE GUARD.  That is the LCA shape, arm for arm.  The tree's own\nprecedent is therefore evidence FOR B, which is what codex, deepseek and (for CONF) glm all said.\n\n*** AND THE SURVIVING ARGUMENT IS THE ONE deepseek RAISED FIRST AND codex SHARPENED: THE GUEST IS\nITSELF A CONSUMER OF READBACK. ***  codex: *\"Previously, a rejected write ended execution before\nsubsequent guest code could observe that value.  A creates that observation and allows the guest to\nmake further decisions from it.  Existing incomplete behavior for accepted settings does not\nestablish that these newly admitted executions are equally harmless.\"*  That argument is untouched by\nthe consumer census, because it does not depend on a MODEL consumer at all -- and it is precisely\nwhat `#447`'s comment means by *\"the guest writes CIE|AIE, reads CIE|AIE back, and believes the RTC\nwill interrupt it.\"*\n\n**TALLY, and the argument decides it rather than the count:** codex -> B; deepseek -> B; glm -> B for\nCONF and not settleable for the five; kimi substitute -> split per arm; grok -> A; minimax -> A.  But\ngrok's and minimax's A both rest on the consumed/unconsumed distinction, **and that distinction is\nnow measured false.**  Count the arguments, not the seats: the load-bearing premise under the A\nposition has been refuted by a grep.\n\n**THIS IS THE THIRD REVERSAL IN THIS ROUND'S DESIGN AND IT COST NOTHING, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE\nHOLD DISCIPLINE.**  No code is committed; the round has produced adjudication text and zero product\nchanges.  The earlier records above -- premise 6 \"sound for five arms\", grok's formulation as \"the\nkeeper\" -- are left standing WITH this correction beneath them rather than silently edited, because\na future reader needs to see that the panel's majority reasoning was overturned and by what.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOW OWED BEFORE ANY FIX: the ARM-BY-ARM design, with the RCR1 precedent read as\nSUPPORTING B, and codex's readback-consumer argument answered rather than assumed away.  The\nquestion that remains genuinely open is minimax's measurable one -- under B a rejected CONF write\nretains the previous value, and codex adds that W_MASK0/W_BASE1/W_MASK1 RESET TO ZERO, which is NOT\nin their accepted set, so \"retain the last accepted value\" is not even well-defined at reset.  That\nis a concrete defect in B as stated and it needs an answer (retain? force the reset value? decline\nonly the guarded bits?) before B can ship. ***\n\n*** AND THE SHARPEST LESSON IS NOT THE REVERSAL, IT IS HOW MY GLOSS BEAT MY OWN PRIMARY SOURCE. ***\n\nThe brief inlined #447s RCR1 comment VERBATIM.  That comment argues purely from READBACK:\n\n    the guest writes CIE|AIE, reads CIE|AIE back, and believes the RTC will\n    interrupt it.  It never will.\n\n**It never claims a model consumer.**  The consumed/unconsumed framing is MINE -- premise 6 glossed\nit as RCR1s stored value having a meaning the model otherwise honours -- and grok adopted my\ngloss rather than the quoted text sitting directly above it, producing B copies RCR1s edit\nwithout RCR1s reason.  codex went to the source, grepped the field, and found the gloss false.\n\n> **INLINING THE PRIMARY SOURCE DOES NOT PROTECT A BRIEF FROM A WRONG SUMMARY PLACED BESIDE IT.**\n> A seat reads the summary as the QUESTION and the quote as SUPPORTING MATERIAL, so a false gloss\n> steers the answer even when the refuting text is on the same page.  This projects premise-audit\n> rule says to state load-bearing premises AS QUESTIONS; the refinement is that a premise which\n> RESTATES an inlined quotation is the most dangerous kind, because it looks already verified.\n\n*The cheap defence is the one codex used and I did not: when a premise characterises what some\nother code does, GREP THAT CODE.  `grep -rn rtc_rcr1 src/` is four lines of output and it was\navailable to me before the brief went out.*\n\n*** codex, `#511` PASS 2, CHALLENGES THE \"GRACEFUL DEGRADATION PRECEDENT\" THAT BOTH LCA ROWS LEAN\nON. ***\n\n*\"`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s fallback selects device zero and forwards the access through\n`bus_pci_setaddr`.  **That is an ALIASING DECISION, not by itself proof of harmless degradation.**\nThe comment's 'transferable warrant' needs its own justification; the shift repair does not establish\nit.\"*\n\nThe `dev >= 21` arm -- commented-out `fatal()`/`exit(1)` with `dev = 0;` substituted -- has been\ncited repeatedly here, including by [[lcabadval]], as the file's own in-tree warrant for deleting a\nhost kill.  **codex's point is that substituting device ZERO is not a decline: it silently redirects\nthe access to a DIFFERENT DEVICE.**  Whether that is harmless is a separate question nobody has\nasked, and \"someone already did it in this file\" is not the same as \"it is right\".\n\n> **A TRANSFERRED WARRANT NEEDS ITS OWN JUSTIFICATION, NOT JUST A PRECEDENT.**  This is the\n> option-set rule's own caution turned around: grepping the fork for an existing idiom finds what the\n> tree DOES, which is evidence about practice and not about correctness.\n\n*Not resolved here.  Recorded on both rows because both cite it, and any round that leans on it owes\nthe justification codex is asking for.*\n\n*** THE THIRD OPTION-SET MEMBER IS NAMED, AND THE TWO-OPTION FRAMING WAS REALLY A ONE-OPTION\nFRAMING.  codex, 2026-09-08; the two load-bearing claims VERIFIED here before recording. ***\n\n**VERDICT: C -- decline the out-of-set write, PRESERVE the previous register value, announce once.**\n\n### 1.  A AND B OVERLAP.  There was never a choice between them.\n\n*\"Your reconstructed A and B overlap: both store the written value and continue.  With the existing\nreads retained, B describes SHADOW STORAGE WITH READBACK, not a write-only register.  It supplies no\ndistinct state-transition policy.\"*  **So the panel that argued A-versus-B was arguing about one\noption under two names** -- the option-set-completeness failure in its purest form, and this row had\nit for two rounds.\n\n### 2.  *** WHAT `#447` ACTUALLY DOES IS DECLINE-AND-PRESERVE, AND I HAD IT BACKWARDS. ***\n\nThe precedent was cited as \"a register with no consumer, where the write is accepted\".  The\nimplementation says otherwise -- **the store is in the ELSE branch**:\n\n    if (idata & (SH_RCR1_CIE | SH_RCR1_AIE)) {\n            /* bounded diagnostic */\n    } else\n            d->rtc_rcr1 = idata;\n\n*\"Unsupported writes preserve the previous value.  They are NOT accepted into backing storage.\"*  And\nthe reason recorded in that comment is a READBACK concern -- echoing interrupt-enable flags would\nsuggest functionality the model does not provide -- so **absence of a consumer neither disqualifies\ndecline-and-preserve nor argues for accepting everything**: readback is guest-observable on its own.\n\n### 3.  *** `ioc_conf` HAS A FUNCTIONAL CONSUMER.  VERIFIED. ***\n\n`dev_lca.c:121` is `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;`, feeding the PCI decode.  **The row's \"no functional\nconsumer\" premise is false for CONF**, whatever it is for the other five.\n\n> **AND IT TIES TWO HIGH ROWS TOGETHER.**  `lcaconfbus1kill` is precisely about that consumer: a\n> write of `0x1` makes `bus == 1` and arms the `bus != 0` host kill.  `0x1` is INSIDE this arm's\n> accepted set `{0, 1}`, so **C does not protect against it and must not be described as if it\n> does.**  The two rows are separate defects on one register and both need fixing.\n\n### 4.  THE RESET QUESTION IS DISSOLVED, NOT ANSWERED -- and the reason improves the fix.\n\n*\"The predicates govern WRITES, not all possible stored states.\"*  Initialization assigns zero with\nno guard; reads return the stored value with no guard; **and the write assigns BEFORE the guard is\nevaluated.**  So the model can return zero at reset while refusing a later write of zero:\n*\"that asymmetry is established; it is not a logical contradiction.\"*\n\n    register  writes the guard accepts        reset 0 passes?\n    CONF      {0, 1}                          yes\n    TB_ENA    {0, 0x80}                       yes\n    W_BASE0   {0, 0x300800000}                yes\n    W_MASK0   {0x700000}                      NO\n    W_BASE1   {0x240000000}                   NO\n    W_MASK1   {0x3ff00000}                    NO\n\n*** MY OWN ADDITION, from verifying the store-order claim at `:233` vs the guard at `:236`: BECAUSE\nTHE STORE HAPPENS FIRST, SIMPLY DELETING THE `exit(1)` LEAVES THE BAD VALUE STORED. ***  That is not\na weaker version of C -- it is a DIFFERENT OBSERVABLE STATE, readable by the guest through the\nexisting read arm.  **C requires moving each store behind its guard, exactly as RCR1 does.**\n\n### 5.  THE SIBLING EVIDENCE SUPPORTS BOTH POLICIES AND SELECTS NEITHER\n\nInside this same switch:\n\n    LCA_IOC_BASE, LCA_IOC_TBIA      discard writes; reads return 0; success\n    LCA_IOC_W_T_BASE0/1             store writes and return them on reads\n    default:                        diagnose and TERMINATE, no backing storage\n\n*\"Thus the sibling evidence supports BOTH discarding writes and retaining shadow state.  It does not\nselect a universal policy.\"*  **A useful negative result: the grep-the-fork rule found the member but\ncannot settle the policy here**, because the file is internally inconsistent.\n\n*`default:` is explicitly OUT of this round -- it has no previous value to preserve, so C is not even\nexpressible there.*\n\n### 6.  THE CENSUS SURVIVES ITS AUDIT\n\nEleven live explicit `exit(1)`: the six value arms at **248, 268, 283, 298, 322, 337**, plus\n**73, 85, 167, 196, 357**.  A bare `exit(` grep returns thirteen; two are comments (the explanatory\nmention and the disabled call whose live fallback is `dev = 0`).  *This is the row's third census and\nthe first to survive.\n\n*** CORRECTION, WITHIN THE HOUR AND BEFORE THIS PROPAGATED: \"THE THIRD OPTION-SET MEMBER\" IS WRONG.\nTHERE IS NO THIRD MEMBER.  THE OPTION SET WAS ALREADY RIGHT AND **MY BRIEF CORRUPTED IT**. ***\n\n`tools/pipeline/fable_queue.md` \u00a77i states the fork exactly as the earlier panel left it:\n\n    A  delete fatal()+exit(1) from all six arms, KEEP the store that already ran\n    B  DECLINE -- move the store BELOW the guard, as #447 did for SH4's RCR1\n\n**The queue's B IS codex's C.**  Decline-and-preserve was already ON the table, correctly described,\nwith the `#447` precedent already attached to it.  My `DISPOSITION2_brief.md` reconstructed B as\n*\"treat the register as write-only backing state\"* -- which is NOT what B says -- and that\nreconstruction is what made A and B overlap.\n\n> **SO THE OPTION-SET FAILURE WAS IN THE BRIEF, NOT IN THE ROW.**  The row had two genuinely\n> distinct members; the brief collapsed them into one and then asked the panel for a third.  *A\n> brief that mis-states the options gets a panel arguing about the wrong set* -- which is the\n> option-set rule's own warning, arriving from the direction it does not usually name: not a MISSING\n> member, an ALTERED one.\n\n**codex flagged this itself and I read past it:** *\"If the earlier panel called decline-and-preserve\n'B,' its letter assignment differs from your reconstruction.  The quoted verdict alone cannot\nresolve that historical naming.  Its implemented precedent is unambiguous.\"*  **It hedged the exact\nerror, derived the right disposition anyway from the `#447` source, and I recorded its answer under a\nletter that does not exist in this row's own queue entry.**\n\n*** WHAT SURVIVES THE CORRECTION, and it is nearly all of it: ***\n\n* **The DISPOSITION is unchanged and now has two independent derivations** -- the earlier panel\n  reached decline-and-preserve as B, and codex reached it from `#447`'s implementation without\n  trusting my framing.\n* **The `#447` reading is new and load-bearing**: the store sits in the ELSE branch, so unsupported\n  writes PRESERVE the previous value.  *The brief had that backwards.*\n* **`ioc_conf`'s consumer at `dev_lca.c:121` is new**, and it ties this row to `lcaconfbus1kill`.\n* **The store-before-guard order at `:233`/`:236` is new**, and it is what makes A and B differ in\n  OBSERVABLE STATE rather than only in tidiness.\n* **The reset-coherence dissolution and the accepted-set table are new.**\n* **The census survived a third audit.**\n\n**What does NOT survive is the sentence \"the third option-set member is named\".**  Nothing was\nmissing.  *Recorded in place rather than deleted, because a correction that hides what it corrects\nis how this row got to three reversals in the first place.*\n\n*** A FACT THIS ROW'S QUESTION DOES NOT CARRY, ESTABLISHED 2026-09-08 FROM THREE INDEPENDENT FILES:\nOPTION-SET MEMBER 5 (FAULT) IS NOT AVAILABLE ON ALPHA. ***\n\nThis is an Alpha machine, and the adjudication of this row weighs dispositions from the standing\noption set.  **FAULT looks available and is not**, which is the option-set-completeness problem\ninverted -- not a missing member, a PHANTOM one.\n\n    memory_rw.c        the device-failure dispatcher has MIPS and M88K exception arms and\n                       NO ALPHA ARM                          (found via dev_lca.c, `lcaisalane`)\n    memory_alpha.c     no MM-fault dispatch exists at all    (found via `alphapteprot`)\n    cpu_alpha_palcode  ALPHA_KENTRY_MM and ALPHA_MMCSR_* are DEFINED and have NO CONSUMERS;\n                       kentry[] is written by wrent and initialised to a dummy PAL, never\n                       dispatched                            (found via `alphatbinegsel` Q3)\n\n**Three briefs reached this from three directions, none of them asked about it.**  And a fourth\nseat priced it: choosing FAULT obliges building delivery first -- a PAL frame,\n`kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a redirected PC.  *\"That is\na new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.\"*\n\n> **SO ANY ANSWER TO THIS ROW THAT REACHES FOR FAULT IS REACHING FOR A ROUND THAT DOES NOT EXIST\n> YET.**  It remains a legitimate ANSWER -- but it is an answer with a prerequisite, and the\n> prerequisite is large.  Recorded here so the adjudication prices it rather than assuming it.\n\n*** THE NOTE ABOVE UNDERSTATED THE SCOPE, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART: FAULT IS NOT\n\"UNAVAILABLE ON ALPHA\".  IT IS AVAILABLE ON **MIPS AND M88K ONLY**, FOR EVERY ARCHITECTURE IN THIS\nTREE.  Derived 2026-09-08 by opening the dispatcher. ***\n\n`src/cpus/memory_rw.c`, the device-failure path -- the whole of it:\n\n    if (res <= 0 && !no_exceptions) {\n            debug(\"[ %s device '%s' addr %08lx failed ]\");\n    #ifdef MEM_MIPS\n            mips_cpu_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION? EXCEPTION_IBE : EXCEPTION_DBE, ...);\n    #endif\n    #ifdef MEM_M88K\n            m88k_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION ? M88K_EXCEPTION_INSTRUCTION_ACCESS\n                                                           : M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS, 0);\n    #endif\n            return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED;\n    }\n\n**TWO `#ifdef` ARMS.  There is no ARM arm, no Alpha arm, no PPC arm and no SH arm** -- every other\narchitecture reaches `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` with no exception delivered at all.\n\n> **SO OPTION-SET MEMBER 5 IS A PHANTOM ON EVERY ARCHITECTURE BUT TWO**, and a round on an ARM, SH,\n> PPC or Alpha device that reaches for FAULT is reaching for a round that does not exist yet.\n\n*Found because I wrote the OPPOSITE into a brief.  Assessing `fbfiqsilent` -- an ARM footbridge row\n-- I told the seat that \"member 5 FAULT may genuinely be available here, `memory_rw`'s\ndevice-failure path has an ARM arm.\"  It replied that the claimed arm **is absent from this tree**,\nand opening the file confirms it.  **I was one brief away from spreading a false availability claim\nacross the ARM queue**, having just corrected three rows for the Alpha version of it.*\n\n*** THE 21066 MANUAL IS LOCAL NOW, AND IT PUTS A NUMBER ON THIS ROW FOR THE FIRST TIME -- BUT THE\nNUMBER IS MY DERIVATION AND IS OUT WITH A PANEL, NOT ESTABLISHED. ***\n\n**QUOTED, `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt:9572-9578`, read locally:**\n\n> *\"The window mask register IOC_W_MASK provides a mask corresponding to bits <31:20> of an\n> incoming PCI address.  The size of each window is programmed to be from 1 MB to 4 GB ...  Table\n> 6-6 shows the supported values of IOC_W_MASK register bits <31:20>; **other values are\n> unsupported**.\"*\n\n**Table 6-6 (`:9581-9598`) has THIRTEEN rows** -- a doubling run from 1 MB (`0x000`) to 4 GB\n(`0xfff`), each a contiguous run of low ones.\n\n**MY DERIVATION, flagged as mine:** the register value is the field shifted left 20, so the fork's\ntwo accepted constants are\n\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK0   accepts ONLY 0x700000     ->  field 0x007  ->    8 MB   legal, 1 of 13\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK1   accepts ONLY 0x3ff00000   ->  field 0x3ff  ->    1 GB   legal, 1 of 13\n\nand the legal set is exactly `(f & (f + 1)) == 0`, verified over all thirteen.\n\n> **IF THAT IS RIGHT, the model terminates the host on TWELVE DOCUMENTED-LEGAL VALUES PER MASK\n> REGISTER -- 24 across the two -- and a guest programming any window size other than 8 MB / 1 GB\n> is doing something the hardware manual explicitly supports.**  That is a much sharper severity\n> statement than *\"rejects values outside a small accepted set\"*, because it names the denominator.\n\n*** IT IS NOT RECORDED AS ESTABLISHED, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. ***  The load-bearing step is my own\narithmetic, done in one sitting, on a table extracted by `pdftotext` -- and this project's rule is\nthat an extracted table can stagger names against values.  **Table 6-6 LOOKS self-calibrating (a\ndoubling bit-run beside a doubling size run, thirteen against thirteen), but the calibration rule\nsays one point is only valid for a UNIFORM stagger and you cannot tell which kind you have from one\npoint.**  So it went out to a panel with *\"check the premises, not just the derivations\"* and with\nthe derivation shown step by step:\n\n    regress/artifacts/lcabadval/MANUAL_brief.md   file-reading seats: codex, grok -- also asked to\n                                                  read S6.4 for the OTHER FOUR arms and to give a\n                                                  count WITH ITS DENOMINATOR per register\n    regress/artifacts/lcabadval/PACKET_brief.md   packet-fed seats: glm, deepseek, minimax, kimi --\n                                                  Table 6-6 inlined verbatim so they can check the\n                                                  arithmetic without file access\n\n**WHAT IS STILL OPEN, and the brief asks for each explicitly:** the manual enumerates legal values\nfor W_MASK and (as far as I read) NOT for W_BASE, so the two registers may need different\ndispositions; `0x300800000` and `0x240000000` are 34-bit values and I have not established how wide\nthese registers are or what bits above 31 mean; and the more dangerous error direction is a real\nquestion -- terminating on a legal value, versus accepting a value the model's own address decode\nthen does not honour.\n\n*** THE PANEL ANSWERED: SIX SEATS, MY COUNT CONFIRMED -- AND THE ONE SEAT THAT DISSENTED MADE THE\nDEFECT BIGGER, NOT SMALLER. ***\n\n    codex     CONFIRMS 12/13 per register, 24/26 register-value pairs, and Table 6-6 IS\n              self-calibrating -- it parsed all thirteen rows and checked the adjacent runs\n    glm       \"the S3 arithmetic is right -- I re-derived every step and found no defect\"\n    kimi      \"your derivation is right\": shift, both fields, the count, the invariant\n    minimax   \"the S3 derivation is correct\"\n    grok      answered, 14,818 b\n    deepseek  CORRECT ONLY IF bits <19:0> must be zero -- AND THAT PREMISE IS NOT ESTABLISHED\n              BY THE TEXT I QUOTED\n\n*** deepseek IS RIGHT, AND codex SETTLED IT FROM THE OTHER END. ***  My quote enumerates bits\n<31:20> and says nothing about <19:0>.  codex read the preface: **RES fields are type `X` --\nIGNORED ON WRITES, unpredictable on reads, NOT must-be-zero** (`21066_66a_hrm.txt:955`, `:985`).\n\n> **SO THE GUARD IS WORSE THAN \"ACCEPTS ONE OF THIRTEEN\".  IT TESTS THE WHOLE 64-BIT OPERAND\n> AGAINST A SINGLE LITERAL -- `if (idata != 0x700000ULL)` -- WHILE THE HARDWARE DEFINES A 12-BIT\n> FIELD AND IGNORES THE REST.**  A guest writing `0x7000ff`, functionally identical on real\n> hardware, terminates the emulator.  *deepseek said so in as many words: \"if lower bits are\n> ignored, the number of legal register values is much larger than 13 per mask, and the code is\n> even more restrictive.\"*\n\n**AND IT SUPPLIED THE TRAP A NAIVE FIX WOULD HIT:** the invariant must be applied to the SHIFTED\nFIELD.  `(idata & (idata + 1)) == 0` on the full value **REJECTS THE LEGAL `0x700000`**, because\n`0x700000 & 0x700001 == 0x700000`.  The guard is `f = idata >> 20; (f & (f + 1)) == 0`.\n\n*** FOUR MORE CORRECTIONS FROM codex, EACH NARROWING OR SHARPENING A CLAIM OF MINE. ***\n\n* **MY OWN TABLE DROPPED A ROW.**  `MANUAL_brief.md` reformatted Table 6-6 into three columns and\n  **lost `0000 0001 1111` = 32 MB** -- twelve rows under a sentence saying thirteen.  The PACKET\n  brief has all thirteen.  *A transcription error in the brief that asks others to check my\n  transcription.*\n* **ALIGNMENT AND NON-OVERLAP, which my quote stopped one paragraph short of** (`:9618`, S6.4.9):\n  bases must align naturally to window size and windows must not overlap.  **With the two accepted\n  bases held FIXED, window 0 admits only the four sizes through 8 MB and window 1 the eleven\n  through 1 GB** -- so in that configuration the guards reject 3/4 and 10/11.  All thirteen sizes\n  are supported; changing size can require changing the surrounding configuration.\n* **`IOC_CONF` IS A TWO-BIT FIELD, values 0..3, so `idata & ~1` rejects 2 of 4** defined settings --\n  I had quoted the guard without checking the field width.\n* **`IOC_TB_ENA` IS NOT A FUNCTIONAL DEFECT AT ALL: TEN has exactly two states and BOTH are\n  accepted -- 0/2.**  Its only defect is the ignored-bit one.  *Recorded because a round that\n  \"fixes six arms\" would be overclaiming.*\n\n*** THE SHARPEST SINGLE FINDING, AND IT IS NOT MINE: THE MANUAL REQUIRES INITIALISING BOTH BASE\nREGISTERS TO ZERO (S6.4.14, `:10788`), AND `LCA_IOC_W_BASE1` REJECTS EXACTLY THAT. ***  A guest\nfollowing the manual own documented initialisation sequence terminates the emulator.  That is not a\ncorner value -- it is the prescribed setup.\n\n**Also established:** the registers are **64-bit, quadword access only** (S6.4 excludes longword,\n`:10175`); and **all six arms STORE THE VALUE BEFORE TESTING IT** (`d->window_mask_0 = idata;`\nprecedes the guard), so device state is already updated when the process dies.\n\n**DISPOSITION IS NOT SETTLED HERE.**  deepseek proposes accept-with-backing-storage for the values\nthe manual or existing code already treats as legal, plus a `(once)` decline otherwise, and warns\nagainst expanding the W_BASE set without evidence since the manual enumerates none.  That is a\ndesign question for the round, and the round must not silently widen an accept set.\n\n*** THE FIX DESIGN IS IN, AND IT QUALIFIES THREE OF THE ASSESS PANEL'S FINDINGS -- ONE OF THEM A\nCONCLUSION I HAD ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN. ***\n\n**VERDICT (codex):** *preserve masked register state; validate `W_MASK` FIELDS; remove the\n`W_BASE` magic-value whitelists; leave TEN's two-state policy alone but correct `TB_ENA`'s\nreserved-bit guard.*  **\"This fixes register handling, not DMA translation\"** -- the scope\nsentence the round must carry.\n\n* *** `TB_ENA` IS NOT EXEMPT AFTER ALL, AND MY RECORD SAID IT WAS. ***  I recorded *\"IOC_TB_ENA is\n  NOT a functional defect at all -- TEN has exactly two states and both are accepted (0/2)\"*.  That\n  is right **only for normalised TEN values**: the arm's other bits are `RES`, type `X`, so the\n  existing whole-operand guard **still rejects writes the hardware ignores**.  Leaving the arm\n  untouched would contradict the very answer given to Q2.  *A per-field finding restated as a\n  per-arm exemption -- the dropped-qualifier class again.*\n* **ALL SIX REGISTERS ARE WRITE-ONLY, not RW.**  The manual makes reads of IOC write-only registers\n  UNPREDICTABLE, so **preserving readback is a useful existing emulator convention, not a hardware\n  requirement.**  A round that \"accepts with backing storage\" should say which of the two it is\n  doing.\n* **AND A PRECISION CORRECTION TO HOW I STATED THE RES RULE:** *\"`RES` alone does not mean ignored\n  -- the decisive evidence is each register table's type `X`, interpreted through the\n  conventions.\"*  My record leaned on \"RES fields are type X\" as one fact; it is two, and only the\n  second carries the ignored-on-write conclusion.\n* **`IOC_CONF` has four REPRESENTABLE field encodings**, and calling them four *supported PCI\n  operating modes* goes beyond the evidence -- the HRM also defers to the PCI specification's\n  configuration-cycle encoding restrictions.  So the \"2 of 4 rejected\" figure is a count of\n  representable encodings, not of legal modes.\n\n**Table 6-6's thirteen entries and the rest of the numerical premises are re-confirmed.**\n\n*** THE SHARP FINDING IS NOT THE OFFSET COUNT.  IT IS THAT TWO DOCUMENTED REGISTERS HAVE NO CASE,\nAND ONE OF THEM IS THE ERROR-STATUS REGISTER A DRIVER READS AFTER A FAILED PROBE.  2026-09-08. ***\n\nA codex ANALYSIS job (the construction-shaped brief is refused; see [[codexcyberflag]]) enumerated\nthe `lca_ioc' region and its stream ended before a verdict.  Its numbers were then VERIFIED here\nrather than taken:\n\n    memory_device_register(..., \"lca_ioc\", LCA_IOC_BASE, 0x20000000, ...)   512 MiB\n    switch (relative_addr + LCA_IOC_BASE)                    NO shift, NO mask before the switch\n    named cases: 10        0x20000000 - 10 = 536,870,902 byte offsets reach the shared `default:'\n\n**That count is arithmetically right and it is the WEAKER half of the finding.**  Most of those\noffsets are not registers at all, and the carrier's own rule says a domain is itself a premise: the\nAlpha alignment gate has not been analysed here, so the count of REACHABLE ACCESSES is smaller than\n536 million by an unmeasured factor.  It is recorded with its deriver and NOT as a severity claim.\n\n*** THE STRONGER HALF, from comparing the HEADER's register list against the switch's cases: ***\n\n    LCA_IOC_HAE        +0x000   HANDLED  (via `case LCA_IOC_BASE:' -- HAE and BASE are the same\n                                          address; see the correction below)\n    LCA_IOC_CONF       +0x020   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_STAT0      +0x040   *** NO CASE ***   Status 0\n    LCA_IOC_STAT1      +0x060   *** NO CASE ***   Status 1\n    LCA_IOC_TBIA       +0x080   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_TB_ENA     +0x0a0   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_BASE0/1  +0x100/0x120   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK0/1  +0x140/0x160   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_T_BASE0/1 +0x180/0x1a0  HANDLED\n\n**TWO of the twelve documented registers have no case, and they are STAT0 and STAT1.**\n\n> *** AND STAT0 IS EXACTLY THE REGISTER THE 21066 MANUAL SAYS RECORDS A FAILED PCI ACCESS.\n> `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt' S6.2.18 (PCI Master Abort): an unanswered read \"returns arbitrary\n> data with error status to the CPU, which causes an instruction exception\", plus an internal\n> interrupt and ERR/CODE/CMD/ADDR logging -- and that log is STAT0. ***\n>\n> **So the ordinary driver error-recovery sequence -- probe a PCI slot, take a master abort, then\n> read the status register to find out what happened -- reaches `fatal()' + `exit(1)'.**  That is\n> not an exotic value or an unusual address.  It is what a driver does when a probe fails.\n\n*** AND IT PUTS THIS ROW ON THE SAME GUEST CODE PATH AS `#515' / [[lcaconfbus1kill]], WHICH MATTERS\nFOR ORDERING RATHER THAN FOR SCOPE. ***  `#515` makes an absent-device config access return the\nempty-slot signature instead of terminating the host.  A driver that then consults STAT0 to check\nfor the error the manual says accompanies it lands here.  **The two rows are adjacent steps of one\ndriver sequence** -- which is an argument for scheduling this row next, and NOT an argument for\nfolding it into `#515` (different handler, different semantic class, different detector).\n\n*** A CORRECTION TO MY OWN CHECK, made before it could mislead: THE FIRST RUN REPORTED **THREE**\nUNHANDLED REGISTERS AND NAMED `LCA_IOC_HAE' AMONG THEM.  THAT IS WRONG. ***  `alpha_lcareg.h`\ndefines `LCA_IOC_HAE` as `LCA_IOC_BASE` -- the same address -- and the switch carries\n`case LCA_IOC_BASE:`, so HAE is handled under the other name.  **My script matched cases by NAME\nwhen the thing that decides handledness is the VALUE.**\n\n> That is this file's own deriver rule landing on me: *\"BEFORE WRITING A DERIVER, ASK WHAT MAKES A\n> MEMBER A MEMBER.  If the answer is a property the regex does not test, the deriver is measuring a\n> CORRELATE, and a correlate's error is invisible because the output is still a plausible number.\"*\n> A by-name census of a switch whose labels are aliases returns a plausible, wrong answer -- and it\n> inflated the finding by 50% in the direction that would have made the row look worse.\n\n**STILL OPEN and deliberately NOT answered here:** whether the two `default:` arms (read and write)\nare symmetric, what the write side does with `idata`, and what the manual says STAT0's reset value\nand semantics are.  The codex job was cut off before Q3-Q5 and its remaining questions stand.\n\n*** THE codex RE-FIRE LANDED WITH A VERDICT, AND IT REFUTED ONE OF MY OWN PREMISES. 2026-09-08. ***\n(The first firing could not resolve the manual -- a repo-relative path, when `_scratchpad/` sits one\nlevel ABOVE the git root -- and said so plainly instead of guessing.  A brief defect, not a seat\nfailure.  Re-fired with the absolute path.)\n\n**MY Q4 ASKED whether the READ and WRITE `default:` arms are symmetric.  codex: the question has no\nsubject.**\n\n    VERDICT: \"The claimed default-arm asymmetry is false: `lca_ioc' has ONE SHARED terminating\n             `default:'.\"\n\nCHECKED: `awk '/DEVICE_ACCESS\\(lca_ioc\\)/,/^}/' | grep -c 'default:'` = **1**.  I had assumed two\narms because the sibling handler `lca_pci_conf` splits by `writeflag`; this one does not.  *A brief\nthat asks about an asymmetry invites an answer about which side is broader, and the honest answer\nwas that there is one side.*\n\n*** THE COUNT, WITH THE ALIGNMENT QUALIFICATION I HAD NOT ASKED FOR AND WHICH THE CARRIER'S OWN\nDOMAIN RULE DEMANDS. ***\n\n    registration     0x180000000, length 0x20000000 = 536,870,912 bytes, ending 0x19fffffff\n    recognised       10 relative offsets: 0x000 (BASE, also HAE), 0x020, 0x080, 0x0a0,\n                     0x100, 0x120, 0x140, 0x160, 0x180, 0x1a0\n    reach default    536,870,902 byte offsets      (= 0x20000000 - 10)\n    QUADWORD-ALIGNED  67,108,854                   <- the honest figure for ordinary accesses\n\n**codex volunteered the alignment split rather than letting the big number stand**, noting the\nhandler adds the base *without shifting, masking, or checking alignment*, and that the permitted\nfile set could not establish what the dispatcher actually delivers.  It also cites the manual (S6.4)\nrequiring quadword IOC accesses -- so the 67 million figure is the one an ordinary driver can reach,\nand unaligned delivery is not needed to make the point.\n\n*** TEN ACTIVE `exit(1)` CALLS IN THIS ONE FILE, enumerated by codex and consistent with what this\nsession has separately measured: ***\n\n    2   the interrupt callbacks -- UNCONDITIONAL on entry, no predicate at all   -> [[lcairqstubkill]]\n    1   the `lca_isa' guard `ofs > len'\n    6   `lca_ioc' value guards\n    1   the shared `lca_ioc' default\n    ---\n    (plus the one `#515' / [[lcaconfbus1kill]] has just REMOVED from `lca_pci_conf')\n\n*** THE `lca_isa' GUARD IS REACHABLE, BUT ONLY BY A BYTE OR WORD ACCESS -- and codex's phrasing was\ncorrectly qualified where a careless reading of it would not be. ***  It said the manual's\n*\"ordinary longword and quadword accesses cannot satisfy `ofs > len'\"*.  Enumerated here rather than\ninferred, since `ofs = relative_addr & 3` is 0..3:\n\n    len >= 4 (longword, quadword)   ofs > len is UNSATISFIABLE          -- codex is right\n    len == 1 (byte)                 ofs = 2 or 3  ->  REACHABLE\n    len == 2 (word)                 ofs = 3       ->  REACHABLE\n\n**So it is not a dead guard; it is a guard no CONFORMING access reaches.**  Three (ofs, len) pairs\nreach it, all of them sub-longword.  *Worth stating precisely because \"the manual says accesses are\nquadword\" is an argument about what a good driver does, not about what the model will accept -- and\nthe model accepts a byte access and then terminates the host.*\n\n**SCOPE, so this row does not silently swallow its neighbours:** the interrupt callbacks are\n[[lcairqstubkill]] and are separately witnessed; the `lca_isa` guard is a DIFFERENT handler with a\ndifferent reachability story and should be its own row rather than folded in here.  This row remains\nthe six `lca_ioc` value guards plus the shared default.", "witness": "probe:regress/artifacts/lcabadval/witness_20260909/HEAD_74daefd.out -- rung 3, 14/20 on the committed build (six legal-value rows RED with their own diagnostics), 20/20 on the fix with the same script, then 64/64 with the detector rows", "commits": ""}
{"id": "lcaisalane", "sev": "high", "state": "in work", "title": "dev_lca.c DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa): THREE defects in eleven lines -- an off-by-one guard that lets a bus-hitting read return all zeros, a discarded downstream failure result, and an uninitialised byte consumed on that failure", "note": null, "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  The lane-semantics half was raised by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt) with an\nenumerated table; the third defect I found by reading the function while checking that table.\n\n    ofs = relative_addr & 3;                       <- guest address bits 5-6\n    if (ofs > len) { fatal(...); exit(1); }        <- `>' where `>=' was meant\n    ...\n    uint8_t byte;                                  <- never initialised\n    cpu->memory_rw(..., &byte, 1, MEM_READ, ...);  <- result DISCARDED\n    for (i = 0; i < len; i++)\n            data[i] = i == ofs ? byte : 0x00;\n\n**1. OFF-BY-ONE.**  The guard is `>' rather than `>=', so ofs == len passes.  codex's table, re-derived:\n\n    len=1  ofs {0,1} accepted      len=4  ofs {0,1,2,3} accepted\n    len=2  ofs {0,1,2} accepted    len=8  ofs {0,1,2,3} accepted\n\nFor (len,ofs) = (1,1) and (2,2) the loop never reaches i == ofs, so **the access performs a real downstream\nread and returns ALL ZEROS**.  The guest is told the device answered zero when that lane was never asked.\n\n**2. THE READ PATH DISCARDS THE FAILURE RESULT** that the WRITE path returns -- codex's observation, and the\nasymmetry is visible with the two branches side by side.\n\n**3. `byte' IS UNINITIALISED and is consumed if that discarded read failed.**  This is the FOURTH instance\nof the uninitialised-consumption family recorded on 2026-09-07, alongside [[sgirebufuninit]],\n[[sgigbeconvover]] and #95's incomplete class closure.  **#95 zeroes device READ buffers, so a\ndevice-served read is covered; a read that FAILS before reaching a handler is not** -- the same gap\nsgirebufuninit turns on.\n\nMED rather than HIGH: the wrong-lane read is a correctness defect with no host death and nothing disclosed\nbut a stale stack byte.  The exit(1) in the same function is [[lcabadval]]'s range site, graded there.\n\nNO WITNESS.  Reachability of the (1,1)/(2,2) pairs is established by reading; nothing shows a guest driving\nthem.\n\n*** codex ASSESS 2026-09-08.  I DELIBERATELY DID NOT TELL IT WHAT THE THREE DEFECTS WERE, and\nwarned it not to manufacture three.  It found SIX items and said the filed count is wrong. ***\n\n*\"Four behavioral issues, one conditional initialization gap, and one minor format mismatch.  **The\nfiled 'three' overstates the initialization claim.**\"*\n\n## *** THE TRAP: THE OBVIOUS ONE-CHARACTER FIX MAKES IT WORSE ***\n\n    dev_lca.c:193    if (ofs > len) {          <- equality PASSES\n\nOn equality the write selects byte zero via `ofs % len` and the read never places the downstream\nbyte anywhere -- **but the downstream read HAS OCCURRED and may have side effects.**  The guard\nshould be `ofs >= len`.\n\n**BUT the rejected-lane arm is `exit(1)` at :196, so changing only `>` to `>=` EXPANDS the\ntermination.**  *That is the same shape as `alphavmkill`, where deleting a kill would have published\na bogus 4 TB mapping: the boundary repair and the disposition repair are one design or neither.*\n\n**AND IT BOUNDED THE CLAIM RATHER THAN INFLATING IT:** *\"This is NOT a demonstrated buffer\noverrun\"* -- positive-length writes use modulo and the read loop stays within `len`; the modulo is\npart of the boundary behaviour, not an independent OOB.\n\n## THE IDIOM CENSUS -- five files, grepped rather than argued\n\n* **the same function's WRITE arm already returns the downstream result** -- the strongest precedent\n  for repairing its read arm;\n* both siblings (`lca_pci_conf`, `lca_ioc`) initialise `odata = 0`, and the config handler has a\n  non-terminating `dev = 0` fallback;\n* `eagle` and `footbridge_isa` initialise read results, and footbridge warns on unsupported writes\n  without exiting -- *but footbridge is not an equivalent sparse-lane mapper*;\n* `DEV_RAM_MIRROR` is the closest FORWARDING precedent: passes `PHYSICAL`, returns the nested result;\n* `wdc` -- ISA-connected -- already declines and latches for unsupported widths.\n* **no second implementation of LCA's sparse-lane packing exists in the fork.**\n\n## SIX ITEMS, each with disposition, bounded severity, and its observable\n\n    1 lane boundary        member 3   MED source-only   downstream access occurrence + payload\n                                                        (readback zero ALONE cannot distinguish)\n    2 process exit         member 3   MED pending witness -- and ALREADY RECORDED under lcabadval\n    3 lost read failure    member 7   LOW/documented    handler return status, writeback suppression\n    4 initialisation gap   member 7   LOW/documented    definedness analysis; normal readback is NOT\n                                                        an established channel\n    5 physical forwarding  member 7   MED conditional   which backing is accessed; bootstrap masks it\n    6 diagnostic format    member 7   LOW portability   no runtime text difference established\n\n**Member 5 FAULT is unavailable**, confirmed for the third time this session from a different file:\n`memory_rw.c`'s device-failure dispatcher has MIPS and M88K arms and **no Alpha arm**, and\n`ALPHA_KENTRY_MM` is defined and unused.\n\n## *** THE DUPLICATE I ASKED IT TO LOOK FOR -- AND THERE IS ONE ***\n\n*\"The original `lcabadval` scope explicitly EXCLUDES range-guard analysis, while its later note\nrecords this exact termination site.  **Extract or reassign that site explicitly rather than counting\nit twice.**\"*  `lcaconfbus1kill` and `lcairqstubkill` remain separate; their severity and witnesses\ndo not transfer here.  **This is an open obligation on both rows and is not discharged by recording\nit here.**\n\n## SCOPE: SEVERAL ROUNDS\n\nBoundary + termination are **one** design (same guard site).  Read completion is another; physical\nforwarding another.  *\"Sharing a function -- or a prospective test executable -- does not establish\none semantic class and one oracle.\"*\n\n## NO WITNESS EXISTS AND NONE IS CHEAP\n\n*\"I found no in-tree regression exercising the sparse `lca_isa` handler.\"*  The config probes cover\nother handlers, and `lca_irqstub_probe.py` **accesses the downstream ISA mapping directly, bypassing\nthis shim.**  So every item here is a documented source candidate until someone drives the shim.\n\n**FIX DESIGN IN (codex, 2026-09-10): next LCA round after #518 closes.  Witness first (len-1 access at ofs 2/3); implement per the design; rows per its Q3 table.**\n\n**WITNESSED 2026-09-10 (Opus): 13/16 on HEAD.  SEVERITY RAISED med -> high on the measured lane mapping: ofs == port & 3, so a byte read of the console UART's LSR (0x3fd) returns ZERO with a false success and a byte access to LCR/MSR/scratch ends the process -- every 8250 driver polls LSR.  Fix design ready (codex, 2026-09-10): NEXT LCA round.**", "witness": "probe:regress/lca_isalane_probe.py -- rung 3 (Opus), 13/16 on the committed build: a byte read at lane 1 answers zero with a false success, lanes 2 and 3 end the process; green post-fix with the same script", "commits": null}
{"id": "alphavmkill", "sev": "med", "state": "open", "title": "memory_alpha.c: a guest virtual address of 0xfffffffd80000000 ends the host process -- fatal() + exit(1) on a specific vaddr, inside the not-yet-implemented VM path", "note": null, "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, found while checking [[alphapfnoffset]]'s reachability rather than by any seat.\n\n    if (vaddr == 0xfffffffd80000000ULL) {\n            fatal(\"Alpha kernel virtual memory seems to want to be used. Not yet implemented.\\n\");\n            exit(1);\n    }\n\n**A guest-chosen virtual address ends the emulator process.**  It sits inside the `ptbr == 0' bootstrap arm,\nso it is reached BEFORE virtual memory is configured -- i.e. early in a boot, on an address the kernel\nchooses, not one a user program supplies.\n\nIt is the same de-fatalisation class this project has worked through repeatedly in devices, arriving in a CPU\nmemory path where nobody has been looking.  **The disposition is NOT obvious, which is why this is filed\nrather than fixed:** declining leaves the guest with a wrong translation and it will fault or hang somewhere\nless legible, so this may be one of the rare sites where a loud stop is genuinely better than continuing --\nand if so the round's product is a RECORD saying so, plus a latch, not a removal.\n\nMED: reachable by construction, but only on a machine whose Alpha VM support is already declared incomplete\nseven TODOs over.\n\n*** CLUSTER SCOPING, codex, 2026-09-08 (ev: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_verify_20260908.txt).\nSIX SEPARATE ROUNDS -- no merger has sufficient evidence. ***\n\n    one fix design : replace the terminal path with the architecturally appropriate outcome\n    one oracle     : that outcome is observed for the affected address class\n\n**The ordering ruling matters more than the grouping:** codex put **`alphaswpctxreg` FIRST**, on the\nground that it is the only one with verified behaviour AND a decisive witness (a returned register\nvalue), and it labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential\nseverity\"* -- which is the rung-versus-severity distinction the ladder already insists on, arrived at\nindependently.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED A GROUPING HYPOTHESIS I HAD FLOATED: \"the claim that `alphapteprot` prevents all\nguest paging is not established by this review.\" ***  I had suggested the page-walk row might be a\nprerequisite for testing the others; that is not supported, and scheduling on it would have been\nscheduling on an assumption.  `alphafaultclaim` and `alphavmkill` may be reconsidered together only\nafter proving they share a file, one fault-handling repair and one fault-delivery oracle --\n*\"correcting a comment and preventing termination do not establish that grouping.\"*\n\n*** grok ASSESS 2026-09-08 -- the sixth and last Alpha row.  THE CLUSTER IS NOW FULLY ASSESSED. ***\n\n**THE CLAIM IS TRUE BUT NARROWER THAN FILED: it holds for kernel mode with PTBR == 0 on that exact\nVA.**  There are TWO hits on `0xfffffffd80000000` in `memory_alpha.c` and **only one terminates**;\nthe other is a bare `fatal(\"AYONA3\n\")` -- a debug string left in the tree.  `fatal()` only prints;\nthe termination is the `exit(1)` immediately after, with nothing recovering in between.\n\n## THE CENSUS I ASKED FOR, and it changes the framing\n\n| walker | unmodelled / stub region | ordinary translation miss |\n|---|---|---|\n| Alpha | this one VA: `fatal`+`exit(1)`; other PTBR==0 VAs **ACCEPT** identity | `fatal` + **`return 0`**, no guest exception (`#194` removed the abort here) |\n| MIPS | -- | `mips_cpu_exception`, `return 0` |\n| ARM | unimplemented descriptor type: `fatal`+`exit(1)` | `arm_exception`, `return 0` |\n| PPC | 64-bit translation: `fatal`+`exit(1)` | `ppc_exception`, `return 0` |\n| M88K | internal inconsistency: `exit(1)` | `m88k_exception`, `return 0` |\n| SH | unimplemented kernel VA: `fatal`+`exit(1)` (**honours FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS**) | `sh_exception`, `return 0` |\n\n**The kill is an outlier in BOTH in-file directions** -- it is neither the PTBR==0 siblings' ACCEPT\nnor the walk's own `return 0`, which this project already chose over host death in `#194`.\n\n## *** ACCEPT IS REFUTED ON A PREMISE, AND THIS IS THE SHARPEST PART OF THE ANSWER ***\n\nDeleting the `exit(1)` looks like member 4, because `*return_paddr` is already computed above it.\n**But that value is the comment's own \"UGLY hack\": a K0SEG mask applied to a K1SEG address**, giving\na physical address near 4 TB against a default 128 MB of RAM.  Data then takes the unmapped-physical\npath -- **zeros returned, writes dropped, and the access reported SUCCESSFUL.**\n\n> *\"That is the silent bogus map the landmine exists to refuse.\"*\n\n**So a bare de-fatalisation here would be WORSE than the kill it removes**, which is the opposite of\nthis queue's usual shape and is exactly the kind of thing the option-set rule exists to surface.\n\n## DISPOSITION: member 3, DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, `return 0`\n\n* **5 FAULT is architecturally right and NOT AVAILABLE.**  Choosing it obliges building delivery\n  first: a PAL frame, `kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a\n  redirected PC.  **That is a new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.**  *My brief's caution\n  that FAULT may be unavailable on Alpha is confirmed, with the bill itemised.*\n* **7 IMPLEMENT does not apply** -- its tell is \"no predicate, whole path stubbed\"; this is a\n  one-address predicate, and K1SEG translation IS the walk once PTBR is set.\n* **2 silent DECLINE drops the only signal the author planted.**\n\n## SCOPE: STILL SIX SEPARATE ROUNDS\n\nI asked whether the shared missing MM-dispatch now meets codex's bar for merging this with\n`alphafaultclaim`.  **It does not:** *\"missing MM-dispatch is a CARRIER, not a shared repair.\"*  A\ncommon blocker is not one fix design and one oracle.\n\n## SEVERITY: **med**, explicitly BELOW `alphatbinegsel`\n\nRaised from unset to `med` on grok's argument: this needs a guest already executing privileged code\nwith PTBR == 0 at one specific address, whereas `alphatbinegsel` is a documented primitive silently\nunder-flushing during ordinary operation.  *The ranking is the seat's and I am adopting it rather\nthan substituting my own.*", "witness": null, "commits": null}
{"id": "alphaintdeliver", "sev": "med", "state": "open", "title": "Alpha has NO interrupt delivery -- #516's decline is the disposition, this is the SUBSYSTEM it declines into.  cd.alpha.irq_asserted is written (cpu_alpha.c:218,:223) and read nowhere; cpu_dyntrans.c delivers for FIVE architectures and has no Alpha arm; memory_rw.c's failed-device branch dispatches only for MIPS and M88K.  Implementing it is dyntrans dispatch + PALcode entry + the IPL check -- its own round.  NOT a host-kill and lower than the kills it grew from: Alpha interrupts have never worked on this tree, #516 only stopped the host dying.  No guest that NEEDS delivery has been measured; the row claims the mechanism is absent, not that a guest is blocked.", "note": "Filed 2026-09-09 from the #516 adjudication (option-set member 7, IMPLEMENT), so the missing feature is TRACKED rather than buried inside a fixed kill -- the anti-laundering move the adjudication named.", "state_note": null, "witness": "none", "commits": null}
{"id": "wdcaltoffleg", "sev": "med", "state": "open", "title": "The THREE altoffset WDC differentials as ONE instrument -- wdc_altoffset_probe.py and the two that `import wdc_altoffset_probe as W' (_sig, _4bit): one module, one rig (-E evbmips -e malta, the battery's own _images/wdc-scratch-64m.img), one media family (the `c' in ciR: is a PREFIX -- MEASURED, the same image attaches as an IDE CD-ROM).  OWED: (a) the parent's `ok' -- already a complete verdict over 18 explicit expectations -- gets an exit status, FIRST QUESTION being whether a nonzero exit breaks the two-leg driver; (b) _4bit's single-leg form is an ORACLE CHOICE (its verdict is definitionally between-leg -- 'its whole point is that it DIFFERS by leg', :216), so the round chooses the fix-side absolute and says so; (c) _sig's `sig' oracle is NON-DISCRIMINATING (unreadable and unrelated values both read as 'declined') and is REDESIGNED, not bolted onto.  Each of the three wiringcliff exemptions is re-dated naming THIS row and DELETED in the same edit as its wiring.", "note": "Filed 2026-09-09 by the wiringcliff assess ruling (rev 2), from the Opus seat's measured 3 + 1 split.", "state_note": null, "witness": "none", "commits": null}
{"id": "sgimtelabelcells", "sev": "med", "state": "in work", "title": "sgi_mte_fill_probe.py (#509) is BLIND AT TWO OF THREE LABELS: every transfer row drives dst_tlb == MTE_TLB_A, so `if (depth == 32 && dst_tlb == MTE_TLB_A)' and `&& dst_tlb != MTE_TLB_C' each score 14/14 against a 14/14 base (MEASURED, killtable3_20260909/), while a guest-only control shows the CI-arm defect #509 closed LIVE at B and C under them.  RULED FLOOR by 7g-1 (the selector flows into dst_mode, the operand the repair reads), so L2 in a follow-up commit under #509: two rows (B x 32, C x 32 on the `fill top' channel), the table re-run with both mutants added (must go 12/14 or worse), the contract amended, and the added comment's `every tiled transfer' universal narrowed or rowed.  A changed instrument owes its table.", "note": "Filed 2026-09-09 by the 7g-1 ruling: predicted by PRETABLE.out before table 1, measured by the Opus seat, graded floor on the data-flow discriminator.", "state_note": "\n\n**SHIPPED 1ce8296 as the #509 follow-up.**  The L2 floor breach is closed by measurement; the repair owes its own pass on the diff (repair-chain rule), batched with the next panel window; state `in work' until that pass and the regress phase.", "witness": "detector:regress/sgi_mte_fill_probe.py -- 16 rows; M_tlbA 14/16, M_tlbAB 15/16 where both swept the 14-row instrument (regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/repair_20260909/TABLE.out)", "commits": "1ce8296"}
{"id": "multilabelfloor", "sev": "low", "state": "closed", "title": "CLASS CENSUS owed by the 7g-1 ruling: which shipped detectors sit at a multi-label switch block where the selector's value flows into an lvalue the repair path reads or writes (the sgimtedepth4dead shape), and row only one label?  Each such detector is short by (labels - 1) rows on the guard operand's body-tested partition; a block whose selector touches nothing the body reads is clause (e) and owes a sentence, not rows.  Deriver: for each de-fatalisation/repair row with a kill table, read the enclosing switch and the repair's operands.  A reading task for a file-reading seat; no measurement until a candidate is named.", "note": "Filed 2026-09-09 from the 7g-1 ruling (Q3, the class consequence).  Not a sweep: name candidates, then measure one.", "state_note": "\n\n**CLOSED 2026-09-10 on the codex census: measured ZERO further instances across 31 kill-table rounds (82 tables).  The one positive, #509, is carried by `sgimtelabelcells'.  No sweep was needed because the census found nothing to sweep; the discriminator stays in the carrier's SIBLING PARITY row for future rounds.**", "witness": "harness:census -- regress/artifacts/multilabelfloor/codex_census_20260909.err (31 rounds / 82 tables walked; 0 further positives)", "commits": null}
{"id": "sgimtetlbdefault", "sev": "low", "state": "open", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c do_mte_transfer(): the `switch (dst_tlb)' default: arm is a guest-drivable UNLATCHED fatal() -- dst_tlb values 3/6/7 come straight from a guest MTE_MODE write (the DST_BUF field), one message per transfer, unbounded.  A READING by the Opus seat during the sgimtelabelcells table, not measured.  Fix shape settled by the file's own ten-bit SGIRE_W_* latch enum and its `own bit, never shared' precedent; the measurement is one session (fill_at(A, RE_TLB_A, 3 << 2)).  Not a host kill (fatal() returns); floodclass member.", "note": "Filed 2026-09-10 from the Opus seat on the sgimtelabelcells repair.", "state_note": null, "witness": "none", "commits": null}
{"id": "sgiretlbshiftub", "sev": "med", "state": "open", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c horrible_getputpixel: the tile-base shift `re_tlb_[abc][tile_nr] << 16' is a SIGNED shift of a promoted uint16_t, and every tile pointer the model accepts as VALID (bit 15 set) overflows INT_MAX -- undefined behaviour on the only path that works", "note": null, "state_note": null, "witness": null, "commits": null}

