SGIRE_STATE_NOTE OPEN, and the committed artifact in `witness` is the REPRODUCTION, not the fix.  `regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py` shipped with #497 and carries SIX `ld kill` rows that assert THE HOST STILL DIES on an 8-byte access at each of the six windows -- they pin the defect, they do not repair it.  #497 de-fatalised the WIDTH guard only; the width test is evaluated FIRST, so a legal `ld` still reaches the kill.  *** THE ORDERING OBLIGATION IS ALREADY RECORDED IN #497's ROUND BLOCK AND IS THE REASON THIS ROW MUST NOT BE CLOSED BY THE CHECKER: fixing sgirekills will turn a GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING, six rows' worth. ***  It therefore inherits the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin the six rows FIRST, measure the committed build RED under the new expectations, THEN edit the code.  A row whose own detector must invert is exactly the case the shipped-work heuristic cannot read.

*** COUNT CHECK 2026-09-06, AND MY FIRST CORRECTION WAS ALSO WRONG.  THE LIVE COUNT IS TWELVE. ***

Three numbers have now been attached to this row: the title says TWENTY-ONE, my correction an hour ago said
NINETEEN, and the live count is **TWELVE**.

    grep -c 'exit(1)'                    19   <- ALL occurrences, comments included
    grep -cE '//[[:space:]]*exit\(1\)'    4   <- commented-out kills (:218, :227, :689, :1251)
    (three more sit inside comment PROSE at :321, :325, :945, discussing exit(1))
    grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\(1\);'    12   <- LIVE, statement position

*** THE INSTRUCTIVE PART IS WHY I BELIEVED 19, BECAUSE IT IS A NEW COROLLARY OF A RULE THIS PROJECT ALREADY
HAS. ***  I wrote that `grep -c 'exit('` and `grep -c 'exit(1)'` BOTH returned 19, and treated the agreement
as ruling out the very error that had just made `lcabadval`'s enumeration wrong -- a commented-out kill
counted as live.  **Both greps share the same blindness: neither excludes `//`.**  The carrier says when two
derivers DISAGREE neither is trustworthy until you look at what each matched.  The corollary, learned here:
**when two derivers AGREE that is not evidence either, if they can fail the same way.**  Agreement between
methods is worth exactly the independence of the methods -- which is the unanimity-on-a-brief-supplied-fact
rule, one level down and pointed at greps instead of seats.

And it is the third instance tonight of *a correction is not safer than the thing it corrects*: `#503`'s
pass-3 refutation displaced a truer sentence, `83df95c` sharpened a vague claim while reading as though it
fixed a false one, and this correction replaced a wrong number with another wrong number.

**THE TITLE IS LEFT AS WRITTEN AND IS NOT SILENTLY RE-GRADED**: 21 may have been right when the row was
opened, with sites removed since -- four of the nineteen are commented out, which is consistent with someone
having disabled them.  A round on this file DERIVES the count from the statement-position pattern above
before acting, and does not trust any of the three numbers in this row.


